finding(f_timing_assertion_tightens_as_code_speeds_up, pitfall).
recorded(f_timing_assertion_tightens_as_code_speeds_up, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_timing_assertion_tightens_as_code_speeds_up, test_repair).
finding_note(f_timing_assertion_tightens_as_code_speeds_up,
  "the argument index made the engine 3.8x faster and thereby BROKE a passing test: yak fragment 06 asserts spread < 3 over three wall-clock timings, which were ~135ms each when the threshold was calibrated and are 25-38ms now, so the same absolute GC noise became ~4x larger as a fraction and the ratio hit 3.0x under full-suite load while measuring 1.5x standalone; the general form is that a ratio-of-timings assertion gets STRICTER every time the code improves, so it is guaranteed to fail eventually ON SUCCESS, and raising the threshold only defers that; the root fix is that the fragment's own line 15 calls the property absent BY CONSTRUCTION - a structural claim about a single shared append path - so it must be asserted structurally (identical store state across the three arrival orders) and the stopwatch kept only as printed evidence").

finding(f_npc_is_the_broad_integration_test, insight).
recorded(f_npc_is_the_broad_integration_test, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_npc_is_the_broad_integration_test, demo).
finding_note(f_npc_is_the_broad_integration_test,
  "I first blocked NPC alongside AFK on the no-incremental-maintenance limit and the spec refutes that in two places: it says explicitly not to build a game but a grid with about ten agents and three or four action types, which is far below the scale where the quadratic bites, and it names throughput on ten agents as a DELIVERABLE with the instruction that a bad number is itself a result to publish - so the performance limit is NPC's subject matter rather than its blocker; checked against the kernel, all eight of its readiness criteria already have support: intents as facts, semiring conflict resolution, why and whynot, tick restoration with when-was-this-last-derivable, holes as an explicit kernel term, runtime rule addition via rules-as-data, budget_exhausted as partial inference, and per-agent perspectives - which makes NPC the broadest single integration test of everything built this session; it is sequenced after LOOT only because rule versioning and rule diff overlap, not because anything blocks it").
addressed_by(f_no_argument_index_is_the_bottleneck, "src/store.ts").
addressed_by(f_no_argument_index_is_the_bottleneck, "test/arg-index.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_timing_assertion_tightens_as_code_speeds_up, "test/example-yak.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_timing_assertion_tightens_as_code_speeds_up, "test/index-arrival.test.ts").

finding(f_fork_time_argument_died_with_the_insert_fix, insight).
recorded(f_fork_time_argument_died_with_the_insert_fix, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_fork_time_argument_died_with_the_insert_fix, requeue).
finding_note(f_fork_time_argument_died_with_the_insert_fix,
  "f_fork_copies_the_whole_store was recorded when clone() cost 2000/12ms 8000/74ms 20000/322ms and its case rested on extrapolating that to about 8 SECONDS per fork at 100k facts; clone() is snapshot -> JSON -> restore and restore re-adds every fact through add(), so the superlinearity was never a property of forking at all, it was the insert quadratic seen through a second door - and fixing the insert killed it: re-measured now at 2000/9ms 8000/26ms 20000/69ms 100000/344ms, which is FLAT at 3 microseconds per fact across more than a decade of scale, a 23x improvement at 100k and no superlinearity left; two consequences, the first practical and the second methodological: IFFY and DITTO are NO LONGER BLOCKED, because a fork of IFFY's ten-thousand-decision corpus is about 35 ms and a thousand-fork Tropical search is 35 seconds rather than hours; and the case for copy-on-write did not vanish but CHANGED ITS GROUND - the time argument is dead and the memory argument is now the whole of it, since every fork is still a full copy in RAM and memory per fact is already the identified ceiling (I5), which means copy-on-write should merge into f_memory_per_fact_is_the_real_ceiling rather than stand as its own item; the general lesson is that a finding's NUMBERS expire when a shared root cause is fixed elsewhere, so a queue item must be re-measured before it is briefed, not after - this correction cost thirty seconds and it removed an item from the critical path and unblocked two demos").

finding(f_memory_ceiling_survived_every_speed_fix, insight).
recorded(f_memory_ceiling_survived_every_speed_fix, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_memory_ceiling_survived_every_speed_fix, doc).
finding_note(f_memory_ceiling_survived_every_speed_fix,
  "re-ran the recorded ceiling program after the meta cache, the insertion buffer and the argument index, and the two axes moved by wildly different amounts: at the comparable size (500 base facts, 39809 recorded against 43037 now, an 8 percent difference in derived count) the run went from 7.7 s to 0.45 s, about SEVENTEEN times, and per-fact cost went from 193 microseconds degrading to 469 at the larger size, to a FLAT 9-10 microseconds across a 4.5x range of store size, so the degradation-with-size is gone as well as the constant; meanwhile bytes per fact went from 1411 to 1247, twelve percent, and facts per GB from 0.7M to 0.86M - which is to say memory per fact is untouched by every speed fix and the 40x gap to the field stands essentially where it was, exactly as invariant I5 predicted rather than assumed; CAUTION ON COMPARABILITY, because this nearly became a false claim: the 2000-base-fact case is NOT comparable, recorded at 610309 facts and re-measured at 193037, and the arithmetic shows the re-measurement is the self-consistent one - 50 groups of 40 give 78000 peer facts, a 180-link chain gives 16290 reachable, plus 2000 item and 180 link is 96470 answers, doubled by provenance is 192940 against 193037 observed - so the recorded 610309 came from a configuration this program cannot produce and mixr.ts does not capture the parameters that produced it; the general practice this forces: a re-measurement must reproduce the ORIGINAL's fact count before its timing may be compared, and where it does not, say so and compare only the sizes that match").

finding(f_the_fact_key_is_a_retained_rope, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_fact_key_is_a_retained_rope, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_the_fact_key_is_a_retained_rope, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_fact_key_is_a_retained_rope,
  "factKey builds its result by concatenation, which in V8 produces a CONS (rope) string that retains every fragment instead of a flat one, so a FOURTEEN-CHARACTER key costs 97 bytes where a flat string of the same text costs 42; measured three ways - in an array of keys, flattening reclaims 55 bytes per key; in the real store, where Map hashing already flattens some of them, forcing %FlattenString over every key reclaimed 31 bytes per fact from 332, which is 9.3 percent of all per-fact memory; the production fix is not a flatten call, since %FlattenString is a V8 native unavailable to userland, but a change in HOW the key is built - measured: plus-concatenation 97 bytes, .slice() 92 (does NOT flatten), a single Array.join('') of all the pieces 42, because join computes the total length and writes one sequential string; the change is confined to one function, produces identical string VALUES so no behaviour moves and the goldens must stay at zero bytes, and it is independent of any decision about compact fact representation").

finding(f_interning_alone_does_not_buy_the_order_of_magnitude, insight).
recorded(f_interning_alone_does_not_buy_the_order_of_magnitude, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_interning_alone_does_not_buy_the_order_of_magnitude, doc).
finding_note(f_interning_alone_does_not_buy_the_order_of_magnitude,
  "f_memory_per_fact_is_the_real_ceiling names the remedy as interning relation names and perspectives plus storing small-arity tuples as typed arrays, and says that is where the order of magnitude lives; decomposing the cost says otherwise, and the correction matters before anyone starts work: an arity-1 fact costs 332 bytes in the store, of which the key string is about 97, the args array with its one Term object 106, the FactRec object itself about 64 and the Map entry with the index about 47; measured marginals - one more integer argument costs 61 bytes where a typed array slot costs 4, and 46 extra characters of relation and perspective NAME cost only 30 bytes per fact, which means the names are ALREADY shared by reference and interning them reclaims not the name text but the copy of it embedded in the key string; so interning plus typed tuples attacks the 106-byte args and part of the key and should land the fact somewhere near 120-150 bytes, a factor of two to three, NOT the forty the gap to the field requires; closing forty needs columnar per-relation storage - relations as typed-array columns with a numeric key - and that is a rewrite of the store touching why-trees, witnesses, snapshots and perspectives, which is a decision of a completely different size from the one the original finding implies; the useful shape of the choice is therefore three tiers, cheapest first: flatten the key (9 percent, contained, no model change, needs no decision), intern plus typed tuples (2-3x, contained to the store's internals), columnar storage (approaches the field, rewrites the store)").

finding(f_rule_identity_keeps_variable_names, pitfall).
recorded(f_rule_identity_keeps_variable_names, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_rule_identity_keeps_variable_names,
  "ruleIdOf is a content hash of the canonical clause and the canonical clause carries VARIABLE NAMES, so renaming ?T to ?X yields a completely different rule id: LOOT's diff of a codex against its renamed edition keeps not one of two ids, removing two and adding two; this is distinct from f_rename_leaks_into_the_firing_signature which canonVars closed - that was about the FIRING signature, this is about rule IDENTITY, and the two are separate surfaces; the consequence LOOT demonstrates rather than asserts is the one a rulepack ecosystem would actually feel: install both editions and a conclusion they share now has TWO derivations where it had one, which is 'poisoning by a foreign pack' arriving through a naming accident rather than through any disagreement about content; a discriminating counter-test in the same file shows WILDCARDS do not leak this way, because the parser numbers them per clause, which means the defect is specific to named variables and a canonicalisation at id time would close it").

finding(f_counting_under_ticks_hit_independently_by_loot, insight).
recorded(f_counting_under_ticks_hit_independently_by_loot, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_counting_under_ticks_hit_independently_by_loot, decision).
finding_note(f_counting_under_ticks_hit_independently_by_loot,
  "f_counting_breaks_under_ticks is awaiting the owner's decision and now has a SECOND independent demonstration, which raises what is at stake in that decision: LOOT needed a road of six rooms and deliberately built six separate worlds from scratch rather than six ticks of one world, and its README states the measured reason - carry rules on @next make every carried fact its own support one tick back, that self-loop meets a CLOSED counting semiring, and past tick 0 every count is 'infinitely many', which would make the fragility-of-belief number in its section 6 meaningless; the workaround costs about 45 ms per world and is stated as a deliberate limit rather than hidden; so two examples have now paid for the same defect in the same way, and any example that wants BOTH a timeline and a counting fold is blocked until the decision is made").

finding(f_reading_provenance_disables_derived_reuse, insight).
recorded(f_reading_provenance_disables_derived_reuse, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_reading_provenance_disables_derived_reuse,
  "LOOT's test suite pins an interaction between two kernel features that is easy to violate silently: a rule whose body reads a provenance relation turns the derived-relation reuse optimisation OFF, and the test asserts that this is VISIBLE rather than merely true - which is the right shape, because reuse that stayed on while provenance was being read would serve answers computed against a different witness set and no output would look wrong").
addressed_by(f_rule_identity_keeps_variable_names, "examples/loot/").
addressed_by(f_reading_provenance_disables_derived_reuse, "test/example-loot.test.ts").

finding(f_i_measured_the_cheapest_fact_and_generalised, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_measured_the_cheapest_fact_and_generalised, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_i_measured_the_cheapest_fact_and_generalised, requeue).
finding_note(f_i_measured_the_cheapest_fact_and_generalised,
  "I corrected f_fork_copies_the_whole_store with a re-measurement of clone() at 3 microseconds per fact and declared the TIME argument for copy-on-write dead and IFFY unblocked for a thousand-fork search at about 35 seconds; the ditto agent, handed that figure in its brief, measured 21.5 in its own context and - instead of accepting the brief or merely reporting the gap - added a flat-store CONTROL in the same run which came out at 13.5, showing most of the gap was methodology and the rest was real, and printed all three; checked myself afterwards: a bare store of integer facts clones at 5.6-7.2 microseconds per fact while a realistic store with rules, derived facts, provenance and witnesses clones at 21.7-22.5, so my figure was taken on the CHEAPEST POSSIBLE FACT and generalised to realistic ones; consequences, all three of which I had stated wrongly - a fork of a realistic 100k-fact store costs about 2.2 seconds and not 0.34, IFFY's thousand-fork Tropical search is about half an HOUR and not 35 seconds so IFFY is feasible only for a bounded search of tens of forks and must declare that ceiling, and the time argument for copy-on-write is not dead but WEAKENED - the truth sits between the original 8 seconds and my 0.34; this is the same shape as the rule already recorded that an isolated axis measurement is an upper bound on the end-to-end win rather than an estimate of it, and it EXTENDS that rule rather than replacing it: the general form is that a measurement's SUBJECT must match the claim's subject, whether the mismatch is one axis against the whole pipeline or the simplest instance against a realistic one").

finding(f_a_declared_class_says_what_to_compare_not_what_on, insight).
recorded(f_a_declared_class_says_what_to_compare_not_what_on, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_a_declared_class_says_what_to_compare_not_what_on,
  "DITTO was handed this repository's own meta-cache incident as material - a change that passed its own fifteen tests AND moved the goldens by zero bytes AND still broke nine tests in a fourteen-stratum program - with an instruction not to overclaim from it, and the result is sharper than a win would have been: it states flatly that a declared equivalence class would NOT have caught that bug, because both changes are legal internal rebuilds by its own structural measure and a structural check would have said HELD, agreeing with the golden master including on the occasion the golden master was wrong; what a declared class adds is that the acceptance claim becomes FALSIFIABLE - the meta-cache author's implicit class, written out, is 'the derived fact set of every loaded program is preserved, the internal structure of the meta layer is not', a sentence that is false and checkably so, whereas 'the goldens moved zero bytes' cannot be false in any interesting way because it says nothing about programs the goldens do not contain; the general statement, which is the example's own boundary and worth carrying beyond it: A DECLARED EQUIVALENCE CLASS TELLS YOU WHAT TO COMPARE, IT DOES NOT TELL YOU WHAT TO COMPARE IT ON - corpus coverage is an orthogonal axis, and the rule that would have caught the meta-cache defect is the coverage rule adopted the same day (a change inside src/ requires the full suite before a finding is settled), not any structural check").
addressed_by(f_a_declared_class_says_what_to_compare_not_what_on, "examples/ditto/").
addressed_by(f_a_hypothesis_can_be_kept_structurally, "examples/ditto/").
addressed_by(f_the_fact_key_is_a_retained_rope, "src/store.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_fact_key_is_a_retained_rope, "test/key-bytes.test.ts").

finding(f_the_check_budget_is_nearly_spent, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_check_budget_is_nearly_spent, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_the_check_budget_is_nearly_spent, decision).
finding_note(f_the_check_budget_is_nearly_spent,
  "the node suite now runs 493 tests in 291 SECONDS against the project's own hard budget of 300, and two more examples are still to land (NPC in flight, IFFY queued), so the budget will be exceeded within the day; measured breakdown of where it goes - one MOOT test that checks a ruling under EVERY timestamp assignment costs 60.2 s by itself, a budget-exhaustion test costs 35.1 s, and six more tests cost 11-18 s each of which five belong to the newly-arrived GOOF; note the suite was 68 s for 407 tests earlier the same evening, so the test count grew 21 percent while the wall time grew 328 percent, and part of that is genuine cost and part is that agents were compiling and running their own work on the same four cores while the suite ran, which means this number is an upper bound and needs re-taking on a quiet machine before anyone acts on it; the reason this matters is recorded doctrine rather than taste - an honest gate with no bearable way to satisfy it gets abolished and what remains is the illusion of one - so the options worth weighing are bounding the exhaustive checks to their boundary cases plus a seeded sample rather than the full cross product, splitting the suite into a fast tier that runs per change and a full tier that runs before a report, and checking whether the runner is actually running files in parallel; ALL of them are decisions about what coverage to give up and none should be taken silently").

finding(f_a_skipped_test_is_an_absent_test, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_skipped_test_is_an_absent_test, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_a_skipped_test_is_an_absent_test,
  "the memory guard written for the key-flattening work needs --expose-gc and skips cleanly without it, which is the correct behaviour for the test - but the project's npm test script did not pass that flag, so all three of its tests skipped silently in every normal run and the guard existed without ever executing; this is the same defect as a gate that has never said no, in the shape that is hardest to see, because a skipped test prints as a pass-adjacent line and the suite stays green; fixed by adding --expose-gc to the test script, and verified by confirming the three tests now RUN rather than by trusting the flag - the general rule this argues for is that a test which can skip must have its skip condition checked against the runner that will actually invoke it, since a skip that is always taken is indistinguishable from a test that was never written").

finding(f_a_ledger_polymorphic_rule_reports_a_false_leak, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_ledger_polymorphic_rule_reports_a_false_leak, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_a_ledger_polymorphic_rule_reports_a_false_leak, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_a_ledger_polymorphic_rule_reports_a_false_leak,
  "encodeRule reduces a VARIABLE perspective to the atom $any at both reads_from and writes_to, so a rule that reads and writes THE SAME perspective variable reflects as a flow from $any into $any and boot.rofl's leak audit fires on it, unable to see that the two ends are one variable; there is no way to declare the bridge either, because bridge_decl is kernel-emitted only when the head perspective DIFFERS from the premise's and $any is not writable in surface syntax; this had been met twice before and worked around silently - examples/sus and examples/aka could write one rule per ledger - but GOOF cannot, because one rule per ledger is precisely the thesis it exists to demonstrate failing, which makes this the first example where the limitation is load-bearing rather than inconvenient; GOOF's handling is the right one and worth copying: it leaves the false row standing AND plants a REAL leak in the same store so the audit is shown biting, which keeps a known false positive from reading as an alarm somebody switched off; the repair it names and correctly declines to make is in the REFLECTION rather than in boot.rofl - record a variable perspective as itself, as $var(G), so that reflexive visibility covers a rule reading and writing the same variable while still catching one that reads ?G and writes ?H").

finding(f_an_unbracketed_head_defaults_without_the_explicit_flag, pitfall).
recorded(f_an_unbracketed_head_defaults_without_the_explicit_flag, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_an_unbracketed_head_defaults_without_the_explicit_flag, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_an_unbracketed_head_defaults_without_the_explicit_flag,
  "a rule head written with no perspective bracket defaults to [main] but does NOT set perspExplicit, so encodeRule emits no bridge_decl for it and boot.rofl's leak audit fires on a rule doing nothing wrong; the whole fix is writing the bracket, which is why a line like proposition[main](P) :- axiom[G](P). looks redundant and is not - and that is a trap precisely because the redundant-looking form is the correct one, so the natural edit (deleting what appears to be noise) reintroduces the false alarm").

finding(f_the_shape_of_the_demonstration_is_model_design, insight).
recorded(f_the_shape_of_the_demonstration_is_model_design, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_the_shape_of_the_demonstration_is_model_design,
  "GOOF's first draft folded logical explosion into the theorem rule directly, and whynot then answered every failed proof in every book with an enumeration of every pair of theorems that had failed to contradict each other, because whynot explains EVERY rule that could have concluded the literal; giving the phenomenon its own relation, explodes/1, collapses that branch to a single line saying the book does not explode - and changes not one derived fact; the general lesson, in the example's own words, is that the shape of the DEMONSTRATION is part of the model's design rather than a property of the engine one has to accept, which means an unreadable whynot is a modelling smell and not merely a large output").
addressed_by(f_a_ledger_polymorphic_rule_reports_a_false_leak, "examples/goof/").
addressed_by(f_the_shape_of_the_demonstration_is_model_design, "examples/goof/").
dismissed(f_the_check_budget_is_nearly_spent, measured_on_a_loaded_machine).

finding(f_the_budget_alarm_was_load_not_cost, insight).
recorded(f_the_budget_alarm_was_load_not_cost, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_the_budget_alarm_was_load_not_cost,
  "I recorded that the suite had reached 291 seconds against a 300-second budget and asked for a decision about giving up coverage; re-measured with one agent running instead of four, the same suite is 489 tests in 125 SECONDS with zero failures, and the three worst offenders halved or better - the MOOT test that checks a ruling under every timestamp assignment went 60.2 to 23.3, the budget-exhaustion test 35.1 to 13.3, the mark-and-fold test 18.1 to 10.6; load average was 24 during the alarming run and 2.75 at the start of the honest one, and GOOF's tests had also been unfinished at the time, costing about 70 seconds then and 7.4 now; so the alarm was almost entirely CONTENTION, the budget is at 42 percent with real headroom, and two more examples at GOOF's finished scale would add about fifteen seconds; what saved this from becoming a decision taken on an artefact is that the finding itself carried the instruction to re-measure on a quiet machine before acting - the general practice being that a measurement taken while one's own agents are compiling is a measurement of the fleet, not of the suite, and any wall-clock number gathered during autonomous work must record the load it was taken under or it cannot be compared to anything").

finding(f_the_fork_ledger_needed_one_reading_not_three, insight).
recorded(f_the_fork_ledger_needed_one_reading_not_three, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_the_fork_ledger_needed_one_reading_not_three,
  "three findings about forking were open at once and each corrected the previous, which is exactly how a backlog stops being readable: f_fork_copies_the_whole_store measured 8 seconds per fork at 100k and blocked two demos, f_fork_time_argument_died_with_the_insert_fix re-measured 344 ms and declared the time argument dead, and f_i_measured_the_cheapest_fact_and_generalised showed that re-measurement was taken on a bare store of integer facts; the settled single reading, so nobody has to reconstruct it from three: clone() is a full deep copy via snapshot and restore, it is LINEAR, and it costs 5.6-7.2 microseconds per fact on a bare store and 21.7-22.5 on a realistic one with rules, derived facts, provenance and witnesses - so a realistic 100k-fact fork is about 2.2 seconds; a bounded search over tens of forks is affordable and a thousand-fork search is half an hour; the case for copy-on-write therefore rests on BOTH memory (every fork is a full copy, and bytes per fact is the identified ceiling) and time at scale, and it belongs with the memory tiers rather than as its own queue item; the ledger lesson is separate and worth keeping: a correction to a finding must be written INTO that finding's line of reasoning, because three open findings that disagree present the reader with a puzzle instead of a fact").

finding(f_i_briefed_before_reading_my_own_backlog, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_briefed_before_reading_my_own_backlog, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_i_briefed_before_reading_my_own_backlog,
  "I spawned an agent to build IFFY as a separate example without first checking the open backlog, which already carried f_ditto_settles_iffy_and_closes_yak recording the decision that IFFY ships as a MODE and DITTO is built as its application rather than beside it; the backlog is printed at session start and the decision was one grep away, and I had re-read the spec but not the ledger - which is the same error as briefing on a finding's stale numbers, one level up: the SPEC says what the example is for, the LEDGER says what was already decided about it, and reading only the first produces work that is competent and misaimed; caught within minutes and repaired by messaging the agent rather than by cancelling, because the prediction in that finding turned out half wrong - DITTO as built applies the fork to two revisions of CODE, so the rule-amendment question IFFY names is genuinely still uncovered - but the repair was available only because the conflict was found early; the practice this argues for: before briefing any queue item, grep the ledger for its id and for the ids of its neighbours, not only for its own numbers").
addressed_by(f_a_ledger_polymorphic_rule_reports_a_false_leak, "src/reflect.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_ledger_polymorphic_rule_reports_a_false_leak, "test/persp-var.test.ts").

finding(f_the_guessed_repair_was_half_the_repair, insight).
recorded(f_the_guessed_repair_was_half_the_repair, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_the_guessed_repair_was_half_the_repair,
  "GOOF diagnosed the false leak on ledger-polymorphic rules and named the repair - record a variable perspective as $var(G) instead of collapsing every variable to $any, after which reflexive visibility would cover a rule that reads and writes the same variable - and I briefed that repair verbatim; it is half of one, and the missing half is instructive: $var(G) is no more a REGISTERED perspective than $any is, so sees($var(G), $var(G)) is false either way and the leak rule still fires; the working repair is both changes together - perspAudit records the variable as itself, AND boot.rofl's leak rule gains the premise A != B so that a flow from a perspective to ITSELF is not a crossing; neither alone suffices and neither is decorative, which I verified by breaking each separately rather than by reading the diff: removing A != B turns the same-variable test red while the ?G-into-?H crossing test stays green, and reverting perspAudit to $any turns the CROSSING test red because both ends collapse and A != B then silences a real leak - two distinguishable failures, which is what proves the two changes are independent rather than one change written twice; the general lesson is that a repair NAMED by the finder is a hypothesis with the authority of having been near the problem, and it deserves the same treatment as any other hypothesis - the agent that implemented it discovered the gap because it tried the named fix and watched it not work, which is exactly why the implementer is not supposed to be the author").

finding(f_a_variable_exemption_is_per_rule_not_per_name, insight).
recorded(f_a_variable_exemption_is_per_rule_not_per_name, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_a_variable_exemption_is_per_rule_not_per_name,
  "recording a perspective variable as $var(NAME) makes the exemption turn on the variable's NAME, which would let two unrelated rules that both happen to use ?G be treated as one - a test pins that the exemption is per RULE and not per name, together with a case I had not asked for and would have missed: a variable end is not a blanket excuse, so ?G flowing into an UNNAMED head still leaks; both belong to the class of defect that is invisible in a green suite because the wrong behaviour is a silence rather than an error, and both were found by the implementer rather than by the brief").

finding(f_next_does_not_break_a_negative_cycle, pitfall).
recorded(f_next_does_not_break_a_negative_cycle, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_next_does_not_break_a_negative_cycle, decision).
finding_note(f_next_does_not_break_a_negative_cycle,
  "stratification is computed from concludes and premise_pos, and NEITHER CARRIES A TEMPORAL MARKER, so a sense-decide-act-world loop that is perfectly acyclic IN TIME reads as a negative cycle in the dependency graph and the program is refused; NPC measured this with a probe and a positive control (loopProbe) rather than inferring it, and the consequence for that domain is total - the world transition cannot be rules, it has to be host code, and ANY simulation with deliberation in it meets the same wall; the refusal itself is correct under the semantics the kernel has and is exemplary in form, a demonstration rather than a hang, so this is not a defect report but a semantics question: a temporally aware stratification would have to say what 'not p' MEANS when p is concluded only @next, and the answer is not obviously 'in the previous tick'; this makes the local-stratification-or-alternating-fixpoint queue item a THREE-WAY blocker - RIP needs it for its step-indexed game, WTF for layer-as-argument, and now NPC for any closed sense-act loop - which is a much stronger case for doing that work than any one of them alone").

finding(f_completed_tick_provenance_is_the_cost_curve, pitfall).
recorded(f_completed_tick_provenance_is_the_cost_curve, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_completed_tick_provenance_is_the_cost_curve, decision).
finding_note(f_completed_tick_provenance_is_the_cost_curve,
  "advanceTick freezes derived_by so a finished tick keeps its record, and NPC measured what that costs a long-running host: about 2000 facts PER TICK, dwarfing everything the domain itself produces, and the per-tick cost doubles between the first tick and the eighth - 10 agents run at 2.8 ticks per second at tick 0 and 1.3 at tick 7, measured on this laptop by the demo's own harness with the machine load NOT recorded - which is a gap, because the same evening a suite read 291 seconds under four agents and 125 on a quiet box, so this figure is an upper bound on the cost and a lower bound on the throughput until it is retaken; measured from both sides, which is what makes it a diagnosis rather than an observation - pruning provenance each tick takes the run from 571 to 322 ms per tick, 1.8 to 3.1 ticks per second, so provenance IS the curve rather than merely being on it; this is not a defect, the guarantee is the product, but the kernel offers no pruning policy and a long-running host needs one; and there is a sharp interaction between two examples' needs that neither would have found alone - a program that READS derived_by in a rule, as examples/loot does, CANNOT prune at all, so any pruning policy has to be conditional on whether the program's own rules cite provenance; this is also the measurement f_afk_needs_incremental_maintenance was missing").

finding(f_the_budget_marker_lags_the_frame_that_earns_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_budget_marker_lags_the_frame_that_earns_it, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_the_budget_marker_lags_the_frame_that_earns_it,
  "hole(Id, budget_exhausted) is written at the moment the budget runs out, and any rule that READS it needs a firing which is by then no longer available, so a derived marker like thought_partial is always one evaluation behind; correct under the semantics and not a bug, but it has to be known before designing anything that reacts to its own partiality - a runtime that wants to say 'I did not finish thinking' in the same tick it failed to finish cannot get that from a rule").

finding(f_a_tie_is_not_a_conflict, insight).
recorded(f_a_tie_is_not_a_conflict, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_a_tie_is_not_a_conflict,
  "a semiring resolves CONFLICT and cannot resolve INDIFFERENCE, and NPC states the consequence rather than hiding it: two acts that score equally need a total order the modeller wrote down, so an example claiming 'conflict is resolved by a semiring rather than by a priority if' must also say out loud that SOME decisions are still made by a declared order - otherwise the priority if has come back in through the side door; NPC declares the act order plus a tie[audit] relation and puts the COUNT of such decisions in its transcript, which is the honest form and worth copying wherever a fold is claimed to replace a hand-written precedence").

finding(f_counting_under_ticks_third_case_could_not_route_around, insight).
recorded(f_counting_under_ticks_third_case_could_not_route_around, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_counting_under_ticks_third_case_could_not_route_around, decision).
finding_note(f_counting_under_ticks_third_case_could_not_route_around,
  "f_counting_breaks_under_ticks now has a THIRD case and the first that could not avoid it: OOPS measured the mechanism, LOOT reported it and rebuilt worlds instead of using ticks, but NPC cannot because restoring a tick is one of the things it exists to demonstrate; measured rather than assumed - at tick 0 the store holds 2606 finite counts, 526 infinite and 255 facts on a support cycle, and by tick 3 that is 8544 finite, 1242 infinite, 374 on a cycle; NPC routes around it by folding Viterbi, which is BOUNDED and converges over exactly the same cycles, and its test folds BOTH instances over the SAME restored store while requiring cyclic > 0 so that the comparison cannot pass vacuously - a positive control inside the comparison rather than beside it; three examples have now paid for this and the decision is still the owner's").
addressed_by(f_npc_is_the_broad_integration_test, "examples/npc/").
addressed_by(f_a_tie_is_not_a_conflict, "examples/npc/").
dismissed(f_an_unbracketed_head_defaults_without_the_explicit_flag, premise_refuted_by_measurement).

finding(f_bracketing_the_head_silences_the_leak_audit, pitfall).
recorded(f_bracketing_the_head_silences_the_leak_audit, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_bracketing_the_head_silences_the_leak_audit, decision).
finding_note(f_bracketing_the_head_silences_the_leak_audit,
  "GOOF recorded that an unbracketed rule head defaults to [main] without setting perspExplicit, so no bridge_decl is emitted and the leak audit fires on a rule doing nothing wrong, and it stated the remedy as simply writing the bracket; measured with four cases rather than reasoned, and the result inverts the advice: prop(P) :- axiom[euclid](P). gives leak=1, prop[main](P) :- axiom[euclid](P). gives leak=0 with one EXTRA bridge_decl, a control with no crossing gives leak=0 either way, and - the decisive case, designed to leak and failing to - prop[report](P) :- axiom[euclid](P)., which reads one named ledger and writes a THIRD with no visibility and no declaration anywhere, ALSO gives leak=0 and an auto-emitted bridge_decl; the cause is in encodeRule, where bridge_decl is pushed whenever c.head.perspExplicit holds and the premise perspective differs, with no author intent involved beyond having typed a bracket; CONSEQUENCE, and it is the finding: the leak audit can only ever fire on a rule whose head is UNBRACKETED, so writing the bracket - the natural, tidy, documented-looking thing to do - silences the audit for that rule permanently, and GOOF's remedy is advice to switch the alarm off rather than to fix the crossing; this is the same class as a gate that has never said no, in its most expensive form, because the silencing looks like good style; the DESIGN QUESTION for the owner, which is why this is not simply a fix: bracket-as-declaration is a coherent intent (naming the head perspective means you thought about perspectives) but it makes the audit catch only one narrow kind of carelessness, and if a real declaration is wanted it needs its own syntax rather than riding on whether a bracket was typed - note the closed finding f_bridge_decl_is_about_rules_not_entities established that bridge_decl is about rules crossing perspectives, which is compatible with either answer and does not settle this").

finding(f_bounded_measures_it_does_not_assume, insight).
recorded(f_bounded_measures_it_does_not_assume, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_bounded_measures_it_does_not_assume,
  "the recorded hypothesis was that HECK converges by being BOUNDED ABOVE rather than non-increasing, and that the BOUNDED discipline would break if it had been implemented assuming non-growth; REFUTED on measurement, and both its premises are false: BOUNDED never appears in the body of evaluateSemiring at all - only CLOSED and BOUNDED_UNFOLDING take a branch, and BOUNDED is the fall-through, plain Kleene iteration to a fixpoint under the instance's own eq with disciplineHeld set from whether it converged, so the discipline MEASURES rather than assumes and cannot be broken by an instance it did not anticipate; and viterbiSemiring already ships bounded-above growth, saying so in those words, so HECK is not the first grower; the thing the hypothesis was pointing at is real but has a different name - ALIGNMENT of times with plus: in every shipped instance the two are OPPOSED (boolean and down against or up, tropical plus-raises-cost against min-prefers-low, viterbi sum-of-non-positives against max, trust min against max, provenance lengthening monomials against absorption), and opposition is WHY cycles are free, because a trip round the loop moves the value in the direction plus discards; HECK's times raises chaos and its plus prefers chaos, so they are ALIGNED, and the only other aligned instance is counting, which is CLOSED rather than BOUNDED").

finding(f_a_finite_vocabulary_bounds_a_set_not_a_sum, insight).
recorded(f_a_finite_vocabulary_bounds_a_set_not_a_sum, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_a_finite_vocabulary_bounds_a_set_not_a_sum,
  "HECK.md justifies its convergence by 'a finite predicate vocabulary bounds the number of distinguishable contradictions', and that argument is invalid as written: a finite vocabulary bounds a SET, and it bounds a SUM only if the sets combined along a derivation are disjoint, which nothing makes them; the set reading was then tested directly rather than argued about - carrier as the set of contradiction ids, times as union, plus as the larger set - over an EXHAUSTIVE sweep of all 32768 subset triples of a five-element vocabulary, and left distributivity FAILS (a={c1}, b={c1}, c={c2} give a*(b+c)={c1} against a*b+a*c={c1,c2}), with a control in the same sweep showing union alone is associative so the sweep itself is sound; consequence for the demo: the ceiling has to live IN THE CARRIER as saturating addition, and the example must say so out loud - measured, uncapped max-plus on a cyclic fixture does not converge and is caught by the existing backstop, swapping min back in for max converges in 10 rounds so the inversion alone is the cause, and every tested ceiling (3, 7, 15, 31, 63, 127) converges with all eleven semiring laws holding over 300 seeded triples").

finding(f_default_max_rounds_turns_exhaustion_into_a_verdict, pitfall).
recorded(f_default_max_rounds_turns_exhaustion_into_a_verdict, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_default_max_rounds_turns_exhaustion_into_a_verdict, decision).
finding_note(f_default_max_rounds_turns_exhaustion_into_a_verdict,
  "HECK is the first instance whose ROUND COUNT scales with the CARRIER'S HEIGHT rather than with derivation depth, measured on one fixture: ceilings 3, 7 and 15 all take 10 rounds, 31 takes 18, 63 takes 34, 127 takes 66 and 255 takes 130 - linear, about ceiling/2 + 8 - whereas every shipped instance takes 10 rounds on that same fixture regardless, tropical 10, viterbi 10, boolean 10; consequence: DEFAULT_MAX_ROUNDS = 1000 is not a neutral safety net for such an instance, and at ceiling 4000 the same convergent, correctly-declared instance reports rounds=1000 converged=false disciplineHeld=false while with room it reports rounds=2002 converged=true disciplineHeld=true - so a CORRECT declaration is reported as a FALSE one and the output is indistinguishable from genuine divergence; src/semiring.ts states that a BOUNDED instance which fails to stabilise is a false declaration and the fold says so, and that claim is too strong - failing to stabilise WITHIN THE BUDGET is an exhausted method rather than a property of the instance, which is RULE 2 (an empty or negative result is a fact about the instrument until shown otherwise) reappearing INSIDE the kernel; the proposed widening, not made unilaterally: let a BOUNDED instance declare its own height the way CLOSED must supply star and BOUNDED_UNFOLDING must supply depth, so the fold sizes its own cap instead of inheriting a constant that was only ever right because every instance so far converged in derivation depth").

finding(f_an_arm_is_a_term_not_a_store, insight).
recorded(f_an_arm_is_a_term_not_a_store, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_an_arm_is_a_term_not_a_store,
  "IFFY was briefed to measure the fork cost on its own corpus, declare a ceiling and design inside it, and it did something better: it made the fork cost nothing by not forking - an arm of the comparison is a TERM, a name riding as an ordinary column through the rules that read the rule set, and the amendment is a DELTA against the norms already present (repeals and enacts), so the whole what-if is four lines and one fixpoint over a store that is never copied; the equivalence is not assumed but tested - one test asserts that a fork by clone reaches the same fixpoint the arm does, and another that the blast radius computed as an arm equals the one the kernel computes by copying, so the cheap method is pinned against the expensive one rather than trusted; declared ceiling on the statute corpus at 3673 facts and about 135 ms per arm: 9 arms 48798 facts 3.5 s, 16 arms 71600 facts 5 s, 100 arms - the declared limit - 380000 facts and 14 s; the general form worth carrying: where a hypothetical differs from the baseline by a DECLARABLE delta, the branch belongs in the data as a column rather than in the runtime as a copy, and the copy then serves as the oracle for the column").

finding(f_three_fork_rates_reconciled_by_naming_the_subject, insight).
recorded(f_three_fork_rates_reconciled_by_naming_the_subject, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_three_fork_rates_reconciled_by_naming_the_subject,
  "three measurements of store.clone() were in circulation the same day and looked like a contradiction: 3 microseconds per fact (mine, on a BARE store of single-integer facts, and wrong for any real use), 21.7-22.5 (mine, re-measured on a realistic store with rules, derived facts, provenance and witnesses), and 13.7-18.1 plus 14.9-17.6 (IFFY's, on its own two corpora); IFFY reconciled them without being asked, by naming the subject of each - measured on stores carrying rules and provenance rather than bare integers - and concluded that all three realistic figures agree on the ORDER: tens of microseconds per fact, not units, which is the claim that actually survives; it also wrote the rule down in its own words - 'the subject of that rate matters more than the rate' - which is the same lesson the measurement gate in scripts/measurement_check.ts now enforces mechanically, arrived at independently by an executor from the evidence rather than from the brief").
addressed_by(f_an_arm_is_a_term_not_a_store, "examples/iffy/").
addressed_by(f_ditto_settles_iffy_and_closes_yak, "examples/iffy/").

finding(f_next_tense_is_decided_reading_one, insight).
recorded(f_next_tense_is_decided_reading_one, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_next_tense_is_decided_reading_one, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_next_tense_is_decided_reading_one,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-30: 'not p' means p is not derivable IN THE CURRENT TICK's store, not 'p is not derivable ever'; the evidence that settled it was measured rather than argued - every one of the 52 uses of @next across the whole corpus is either a CARRY rule (p @next :- p, a fact surviving into the next tick) or a CLOCK increment, and both point from the present into the future rather than reading the future into the present, and no example and no unbuilt spec asks for a property that would require foresight (HECK's 'does it converge' is about the semiring fold, HONK's and MOOT's 'is it reachable' is a static graph property, SPAT's 'does the schedule converge' is Boolean satisfiability over static facts and its 'and it did not' is an oracle against a lived week); the argument that settled the choice is that the opposite reading does not PROVIDE foresight, it DECLINES TO ANSWER without it, and the engine computes one tick's fixpoint at a time and can never supply it - so its refusal is permanent rather than provisional, forbidding a class of programs in exchange for a guarantee nothing can deliver; the residual risk of the chosen reading is already covered by machinery that exists: facts are tick-scoped and carry provenance, so a conclusion drawn at tick 5 is a statement ABOUT tick 5 and its why-tree says so; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a model in this corpus that needs a LIVENESS property - one quantifying over all future states, such as 'eventually reaches' or 'never deadlocks' - or a case where a conclusion drawn from a tick-local 'not p' causes harm that tick-scoping plus provenance does not mitigate").

finding(f_a_next_conclusion_gets_its_stratum_through_the_edge_to_be_cut, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_next_conclusion_gets_its_stratum_through_the_edge_to_be_cut, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_a_next_conclusion_gets_its_stratum_through_the_edge_to_be_cut, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_a_next_conclusion_gets_its_stratum_through_the_edge_to_be_cut,
  "the agreed repair is to stop treating p and p-at-next-tick as one relation so that a rule with a @next head contributes no same-tick dep edge, which breaks the sense-decide-act-world cycle; measured before briefing it, and the naive form of that repair BREAKS: for a program seed(a). carried(X)@next :- seed(X). the store shows edb(carried)=false at both tick 0 and tick 1, dep(carried, seed) present, and stratum(carried, 0) - so the relation's ONLY path to a stratum is the very edge the repair removes, through stratum(Rel,N) :- dep(Rel,Q), stratum(Q,N) pulling 0 from seed; the cause is in advanceTick, which installs staged facts with scope tick and base true but does NOT assert edb for their relation, so the edb(Rel) route to stratum 0 is unavailable; the repair therefore has two halves and both are required - remove the same-tick dep edge for a @next-headed rule, AND give that rule's conclusion stratum 0 from the same new kernel relation, which is also the correct semantics: a fact arriving at the tick boundary is BASE for the tick that sees it").

finding(f_syntax_should_evoke_the_neighbouring_languages, insight).
recorded(f_syntax_should_evoke_the_neighbouring_languages, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_syntax_should_evoke_the_neighbouring_languages, doc).
finding_note(f_syntax_should_evoke_the_neighbouring_languages,
  "DESIGN PRINCIPLE stated by Vadim 2026-08-30 and binding on future language work: the semantics are to be optimised for comprehension BY AN AGENT, and the syntax should evoke the maximum number of CORRECT associations with neighbouring languages - a reader arriving from Datalog, Prolog or SQL should have their first guess be right rather than merely close; the immediate case it was stated about is that if reading a future tick is ever allowed in a rule body, then 'not p@next' means something materially different from 'not p' (the first is a claim about a fixpoint that has not finished, the second about the store as it stands) and the two would sit side by side distinguished by one word, so the difference wants MANDATORY syntactic sugar that makes it cognitively visible rather than a marker a reader can skim past; deferred deliberately - the door is kept open by the stratification repair and the sugar is a separate conversation").

finding(f_boot_rofl_is_itself_cyclic, pitfall).
recorded(f_boot_rofl_is_itself_cyclic, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_boot_rofl_is_itself_cyclic,
  "boot.rofl ON ITS OWN puts 8 facts on a cycle of the support graph, because its audit rules are mutually recursive; the consequence bites anyone building an ACYCLIC CONTROL in this repository - the HECK agent's first acyclic twin loaded boot and was therefore not acyclic, and its own probe caught that rather than the author, which is the failure mode where a control silently stops being one; now written into the fixture comment in test/semiring.test.ts so the next person does not pay for it; the general form: a control must be verified to have the property it is a control FOR, and 'I built it to be acyclic' is not that verification").

finding(f_cycle_alone_does_not_pump, insight).
recorded(f_cycle_alone_does_not_pump, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_cycle_alone_does_not_pump,
  "divergence of a semiring fold needs a cycle AND a weight that charges on that cycle, and the two are separable: measured by cutting HECK's citation ring, after which 38 facts are STILL cyclic (boot.rofl's own, see f_boot_rofl_is_itself_cyclic) and yet the uncapped fold converges in 10 rounds, because the weight hook charges nothing on those cycles; asserted as its own test, and it matters because 'the store has cycles' is routinely read as 'the fold will diverge' - it will not, and the number of cyclic facts is therefore not a risk measure on its own").

finding(f_a_mutant_that_no_test_can_see, insight).
recorded(f_a_mutant_that_no_test_can_see, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_a_mutant_that_no_test_can_see,
  "the HECK agent mutated its new semiring instance five ways and every mutant died, but one of them only died because it ADDED a test first: the fold only ever calls star(one), so every other branch of star was invisible to every test in the file, and a mutant that made star lie on its non-one branch would have passed the whole suite; it added a star-axiom test to close that; the transferable form is that a mutation survivor tells you where a test is missing, and the place to look is any function whose callers only ever exercise one of its cases - the rest of it is unverified by construction, not by oversight; five mutations of the DEMO's model were also all killed (redundant's guard, clash's concurrency requirement, taint's recursion, placid's turmoil check, the chancery's not-reconciles), killing 1, 9, 3, 2 and 2 tests respectively").

finding(f_moot_shadowed_needs_a_third_guard, pitfall).
recorded(f_moot_shadowed_needs_a_third_guard, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_moot_shadowed_needs_a_third_guard,
  "HECK's redundancy rule needed a third guard that examples/moot's shadowed does not have: an ordinance that only PROHIBITS binds nothing, so under MOOT's two guards every prohibition sitting inside a wider command is called redundant - exactly backwards; HECK's bar_escape fixes it and the mutation removing it goes red; this is a defect in MOOT's rule found from outside MOOT by a domain that stressed the same shape harder, which is the cross-example value the set was built for, and it should be checked against MOOT rather than assumed to apply").
addressed_by(f_heck_tests_the_discipline_contract, "examples/heck/").
addressed_by(f_heck_tests_the_discipline_contract, "test/semiring.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_bounded_measures_it_does_not_assume, "test/semiring.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_finite_vocabulary_bounds_a_set_not_a_sum, "examples/heck/").
addressed_by(f_next_tense_is_decided_reading_one, "boot.rofl").
addressed_by(f_next_tense_is_decided_reading_one, "test/next-tense.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_next_does_not_break_a_negative_cycle, "src/reflect.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_next_conclusion_gets_its_stratum_through_the_edge_to_be_cut, "boot.rofl").

finding(f_a_probe_that_only_tightens_proves_nothing, insight).
recorded(f_a_probe_that_only_tightens_proves_nothing, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_a_probe_that_only_tightens_proves_nothing,
  "verifying the tense split I broke it three ways and predicted which test would go red each time, and the FIRST prediction was backwards: removing the conclusion_tense guard from dep_neg does not loosen the stratification checker, it TIGHTENS it - more edges, so the cross-tick loop is refused again - and the discriminating test stayed green; that mattered because the discriminating test stayed green in all three probes, which reads like robustness and is equally consistent with the test being insensitive to everything tried, so a fourth probe was aimed at the dangerous direction instead: make the kernel declare EVERY head as next, after which there are no dep edges at all and no cycle is ever caught, and the discriminating test finally went red - together with a control that broke unstratified itself and killed the same test; the sensitivity profile that results is the one wanted (the test dies exactly when the checker becomes unsafe and survives when it becomes stricter), but it was only established by the probe aimed the RIGHT WAY, and the general lesson is that mutating a safety mechanism in the direction of MORE checking tests nothing - the mutation has to make the mechanism permissive, which is harder to construct and is the only direction that matters").

finding(f_normalised_goldens_must_be_compared_as_sets, pitfall).
recorded(f_normalised_goldens_must_be_compared_as_sets, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_normalised_goldens_must_be_compared_as_sets,
  "a boot.rofl change rehashes every rule id and cascades through the goldens, so the practice is to normalise ids before diffing and read only what remains; done naively that reported 1394 semantically moved lines for a change adding one relation and two rules, which is not plausible - and the cause was the normalisation itself: the golden is sorted by KEY and the key CONTAINS the id, so once every id becomes the same token, identical lines land in a different relative order and diff calls them moved; comparing the normalised files as SETS (sort both, then comm) gives the real answer - 360 lines only in the old and 872 only in the new, every one of them explainable as the reflection of the two added rules, the premise renumbering in dep and dep_neg caused by inserting a guard premise, and flow(main,main) support counts rising from 15/19 to 16/20/21 because boot.rofl now has more rules; the practice to keep: after normalising away an identifier, the file is no longer meaningfully ordered and must be compared as a set, or the diff measures the sort and not the change").

finding(f_an_expressible_veto_belongs_in_the_model, insight).
recorded(f_an_expressible_veto_belongs_in_the_model, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_an_expressible_veto_belongs_in_the_model,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-30, correcting me: I had argued that a veto must live OUTSIDE the logic - the host choosing not to advance the tick - because a veto whose outcome changed what gets staged would mean adding a premise after the conclusion was declared complete; that reasoning is right about ONE kind of veto and I over-generalised it to all of them; a veto the model CAN express is an ordinary rule, blocked(A) :- intends(A), forbidden(A). with the effect rule reading not blocked(A), evaluated in its own stratum with no circularity whatever, and the rule that binds is: IF THE MODEL CAN EXPRESS IT, IT MUST - because inside the model a veto has a why-tree and the question 'why did the agent not do that' has an answer, whereas a host-side veto is invisible to the audit and explains nothing; the fixpoint hook is therefore for OBSERVATION and for the vetoes that genuinely cannot be expressed, namely an external system or a person saying no after seeing the intent, which is not knowable when the fixpoint runs; my error was of the shape recorded elsewhere today - I took a constraint that holds for a hard case and applied it to the easy case without checking whether the easy case has the constraint; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a veto the model can express but whose expression makes the program unstratifiable, or costs more in modelling than the why-tree it buys is worth").
addressed_by(f_a_next_conclusion_gets_its_stratum_through_the_edge_to_be_cut, "test/next-tense.test.ts").

finding(f_the_assertion_trail_is_dated, insight).
recorded(f_the_assertion_trail_is_dated, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_the_assertion_trail_is_dated,
  "asserted_by became a TRIPLE, asserted_by(F, Who, T), closing the asymmetry where derived_by(F, RuleId, T) knew when a fact was derived and the assertion trail did not know when a fact was put there - the kernel recorded the two hard parts, what and who, and omitted the one it already had for free; the shape was chosen against the cheaper additive option (a companion asserted_at(F, T)) for a modelling reason that was verified rather than argued: a fact can be asserted more than once by different people at different ticks, the store keys metadata by the whole tuple so two authors are two rows, and split across two relations those rows cannot be paired - the store would know alice and bob both vouched and that it happened at ticks 3 and 7, and not who did which; the implementing agent added a distinction I had missed, that in_perspective must STAY a pair because a fact's ledger is a property of the fact and re-asserting it at another tick does not move it; blast radius as small as measured beforehand - one rule in boot.rofl (forged[audit], now reading a wildcard third argument) and six golden rows, all of them that one premise gaining an argument; WHY IT MATTERS: tick-scoped facts are dropped at the boundary so a past state is not stored, but determinism makes it exactly RECONSTRUCTABLE - and reconstruction needs the inputs WITH THEIR TIMES, so this is the prerequisite for every continuous-model item in docs/time-and-continuity.md").

finding(f_fixing_a_limit_breaks_its_documentation, insight).
recorded(f_fixing_a_limit_breaks_its_documentation, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_fixing_a_limit_breaks_its_documentation,
  "measured five times in one day, which makes it a property of this repository rather than a coincidence: whenever a kernel limit is fixed, the places that DOCUMENTED that limit go red - GOOF's false-leak row when the ledger-polymorphic audit was repaired, NPC's 'the loop the kernel would not close' when the tense split landed, and YAK fragment 05, examples/sus and the oops/loot workarounds when the counting fold stopped walking cross-tick edges; this is the documentation WORKING, not a maintenance tax, because each of those places pinned its claim to an executable check rather than to prose, so the claim could become false and say so - a README that had merely described the limit in words would now be quietly wrong and nothing would have noticed; two practical consequences: a brief that commissions a fix must enumerate the places documenting the limit, and mine named three of five both times, so the enumeration should be a grep rather than a recollection; and the correction differs by KIND - a catalogue of real runs (YAK) takes a postscript because rewriting the record would be fabrication, while a modelling rule (SUS's 'take the fold as-of') is retired with a note saying what changed and why the rule existed, because a reader who learned it needs to know it was retired rather than find it gone").
addressed_by(f_counting_breaks_under_ticks, "src/semiring.ts").
addressed_by(f_counting_breaks_under_ticks, "test/semiring.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_counting_under_ticks_hit_independently_by_loot, "src/semiring.ts").
addressed_by(f_counting_under_ticks_third_case_could_not_route_around, "src/semiring.ts").

finding(f_the_fold_now_agrees_with_the_renderer, insight).
recorded(f_the_fold_now_agrees_with_the_renderer, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_the_fold_now_agrees_with_the_renderer,
  "the counting-under-ticks defect is closed by narrowing the semiring fold to one tick, the same narrowing the dependency graph received earlier the same day and for the same reason: 'in how many ways is this true' is a question about the current tick, so a carried fact is a GIVEN with count one; the identifying feature is the RULE and not the clock, which the implementing agent verified rather than assumed - advanceTick stamps a staged fact's witness with the tick just ENTERED, so a fact carried into tick 1 carries a witness stamped 1 and any 'older than now' test names nothing, while conclusion_tense(R, next) discriminates exactly and is already in the store because boot.rofl reads it to keep the same edges out of the dependency graph, so one decision now has two readers; the strongest evidence that the choice is right was found before the work started - `why` had already made it, printing the self-loop and stopping at [cycle] where the fold answered INFINITE, so a renderer and a fold over one graph disagreed and only one of them was useful; verified by breaking it in the dangerous direction, treating EVERY rule as boundary-tensed so the fold walks nothing, which reddens the core semiring tests immediately including 'CLOSED answers infinitely many' and 'a fact derivable three ways is annotated 3' - the narrowing is load-bearing rather than decorative, and a cycle INSIDE one tick stays INFINITE, which is the answer the metric exists to give").

finding(f_provenance_pruning_is_opt_in, insight).
recorded(f_provenance_pruning_is_opt_in, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_provenance_pruning_is_opt_in, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_provenance_pruning_is_opt_in,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-30: completed-tick provenance gets a pruning policy that is CONDITIONAL on whether the program reads its own provenance in a rule, with an optional retention WINDOW of N ticks on top, and the whole mechanism is OPT-IN and off by default; plus the budget of a tick is to be recorded; the measurement that raised it is NPC's - advanceTick freezes derived_by so a finished tick keeps its record, about 2000 facts per tick, and throughput falls from 2.8 ticks per second at tick 0 to 1.3 at tick 7 on 10 agents, while pruning each tick takes the run from 571 to 322 ms per tick, so provenance IS the cost curve rather than merely being on it; three groundings made the decision cheap: only ONE example in the repository reads derived_by in a rule body (examples/loot section 5, four rules, with the TICK WILDCARDED because it asks which rule fired and not when, and LOOT does not use ticks at all), the kernel ALREADY computes the predicate that gates the policy (src/engine.ts line 316 walks each rule's reads and disables derived-relation reuse if any of them reads derived_by), and the dated assertion trail plus determinism make frozen provenance RECONSTRUCTABLE rather than irreplaceable - which is what demotes it from a record to a cache; the gap in that last argument, measured: the BUDGET a tick was evaluated with is not recorded anywhere (the store keeps only a partialEval boolean), so a tick cut short by a budget cannot be replayed faithfully, and recording it is what makes 'reconstructable' true without a caveat; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a program that reads derived_by of a PAST tick rather than the current one, which the conditional gate would keep but the window would silently cut, or a measured case where reconstruction costs more than the retention it replaces").

finding(f_a_query_side_gate_cannot_exist, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_query_side_gate_cannot_exist, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_a_query_side_gate_cannot_exist, decision).
finding_note(f_a_query_side_gate_cannot_exist,
  "the conditional provenance-retention policy gates on a predicate the kernel already computes - does any RULE read derived_by (src/engine.ts line 316) - and that gate has a hole which is structural rather than an oversight: PRUNING IS DECIDED AT THE TICK BOUNDARY AND QUERYING HAPPENS AFTERWARDS, so the rules are known in advance and the queries are not, and no query-side gate can exist because any tick may be asked about later; measured rather than feared, five live call sites ask for derived_by of a PAST tick as a QUERY and not as a rule, none of them visible to the gate - examples/oops/demo.ts line 276 asks literally query('derived_by(F, R, 4)'), examples/sus/demo.ts line 788 and its test parameterise the same shape by tick, test/phase2 and test/phase4 query the relation unbound, and examples/npc/demo.ts walks store.facts for it directly; so pruning ON BY DEFAULT would silently remove exactly the capability f_past_tick_is_provenance_only records as the one thing surviving a boundary, namely that the store still REPORTS what it derived at tick 4; the choice is therefore between on-by-default with those five sites rewritten to reconstruct rather than look up, and opt-in with a long-running host obliged to remember, and it was handed back to the owner with the number that should decide it commissioned rather than argued: how long reconstruction of a past tick's provenance actually takes against the lookup it replaces; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a way to know at the tick boundary which past ticks will be asked about - a declared retention intent in the program itself would be exactly that, and would turn the query hole back into a rule-shaped one").
dismissed(f_provenance_pruning_is_opt_in, superseded_by_the_reframing).

finding(f_do_not_un_buy_what_the_project_bought, insight).
recorded(f_do_not_un_buy_what_the_project_bought, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_do_not_un_buy_what_the_project_bought, doc).
finding_note(f_do_not_un_buy_what_the_project_bought,
  "Vadim stopped a retention policy for completed-tick provenance with one observation - the language was written FOR provenance and here we are proposing to switch it off - and the objection holds for three reasons I had not assembled; FIRST, a retention setting makes the guarantee CONDITIONAL, so every consumer must ask whether provenance is on here, which is the same defect as a check that can be switched off and which this session has been finding elsewhere all day; SECOND it contradicts a principle he settled hours earlier, that what the model can express belongs INSIDE the model rather than in a host setting, and retention is expressible; THIRD and least obvious, the cost is not accidental - derived_by is expensive precisely BECAUSE it is a queryable fact carrying the whole reified fact term rather than a log line, and START.md lists 'provenance as a log string rather than queryable derived_by facts' among the prohibited shortcuts, so pruning would un-buy something the project bought deliberately; WHERE THE OBJECTION LEADS, and this is the reframing that replaces the pruning question: the capability 'what was derived at tick 4' must not disappear, only the WAY it is answered may change - a lookup in stored provenance, or a recomputation from the dated inputs which determinism makes exact rather than approximate, with the asker unable to tell which happened; the honest cost of the new frame, which the old one did answer and this one does not: a lookup is milliseconds and a recomputation is a run, so this is fine for 'why did the schedule diverge' once a day and not fine in a hot path; the deciding number is therefore commissioned rather than argued - the cost of recomputing a past tick's provenance against the lookup it replaces, both curves against how far back the question reaches, plus the check that the two answers AGREE fact for fact, since if they do not then determinism is not delivering what the whole argument assumes; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: recomputation turning out to cost seconds where the lookup costs milliseconds AND a real consumer needing the answer in a hot path, which would make retention a genuine engineering need rather than an un-buying").

finding(f_determinism_covers_the_engine_not_the_host_loop, insight).
recorded(f_determinism_covers_the_engine_not_the_host_loop, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_determinism_covers_the_engine_not_the_host_loop,
  "the retention argument rested on determinism making a past state exactly RECONSTRUCTABLE from dated inputs, and that half is now measured and TRUE: replaying from the dated assertion trail and asking the same question of the rebuilt store agrees FACT FOR FACT with the stored provenance at every tick on three corpora (oops standing at tick 6 with 5214 facts, sus at tick 5 with 17999, npc at tick 8 with 21836), which is the load-bearing check and it passed; the half that does NOT hold is the host loop - npc's arithmetic is host code that asserts score[choice] facts each tick while the rules derive the verdict from them, and a replay that also reproduces the host procedure disagreed at the deepest tick, 2224 rows against 2173 stored with 51 facts only in the replay, all of them beaten/2 over Viterbi log-probabilities; the probable cause is an off-by-one in the probe's reproduction of the host loop at the final tick and it is NOT resolved, so it is recorded as open rather than explained away - but the general statement survives whatever the cause: DETERMINISM IS A PROPERTY OF THE ENGINE, NOT OF THE PROGRAM THAT DRIVES IT, so reconstruction is exact for everything derived from dated inputs and is not exact for what host code chose to assert or to ask unless that is journalled too; consequence for docs/time-and-continuity.md, which currently says determinism turns storing the past from a requirement into an optimisation - true of the engine's derivations and not of a host-driven run").

finding(f_reconstruction_is_not_a_cheap_substitute, insight).
recorded(f_reconstruction_is_not_a_cheap_substitute, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_reconstruction_is_not_a_cheap_substitute,
  "measured on three corpora at load average 3-4.8 on this laptop, node 22, answering 'what was derived at tick T' by LOOKUP in stored provenance against RECONSTRUCTION by replaying dated inputs: oops (5214 facts) 3-6 ms against 74-174 ms, sus (17999 facts) 9-11 ms against 234-586 ms, npc (21836 facts, 8 ticks) 8-10 ms against 562 ms at tick 1 rising to 4139 ms at tick 8; the lookup curve is FLAT in the tick number and the replay curve is steep, because a replay must rebuild everything from zero, so the ratio widens from about 40x on a static corpus to about 400x on a ticking one and keeps widening with how far back the question reaches; this settles the retention question from the other side: the reframing that stopped the pruning work (provenance is what the language was written for, so its guarantee must not become conditional) is now supported by a second and independent argument - reconstruction would not have been a cheap substitute anyway, and a long-running host is exactly the case where it is worst").
dismissed(f_a_query_side_gate_cannot_exist, retention_not_taken).
dismissed(f_completed_tick_provenance_is_the_cost_curve, retention_not_taken_cost_stands).

finding(f_storage_goes_straight_to_an_adapter, insight).
recorded(f_storage_goes_straight_to_an_adapter, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_storage_goes_straight_to_an_adapter, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_storage_goes_straight_to_an_adapter,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-31: skip the intermediate memory tiers and go straight to the ADAPTER, starting with a well-suited EMBEDDED database; this settles the choice left open by f_interning_alone_does_not_buy_the_order_of_magnitude, which measured that interning names and using typed tuples buys 2-3x while the gap to the field is forty and only columnar storage closes it - so the intermediate tier was never going to be enough and the port was already decided (f_storage_is_a_port_not_a_rewrite); EMBEDDED rather than a server keeps the property the landscape doc names as ROFL's occupied cell, a kernel small enough to live inside an agent harness, since an embedded engine ships as a file rather than as an operational dependency; the conformance oracle is free and exact and was settled with the port decision - the in-memory store IS the reference implementation, so an adapter must produce a BYTE-IDENTICAL canonicalState() on the same program; the corpus that makes this concrete arrived the same evening: Mathlib is 497345 nodes and 13666557 edges, about 14.2 million base facts; the only per-fact memory figure this repository has is 296 bytes, measured on an ARITY-1 fact with short names in a bare store, and a Mathlib edge is arity 2 over identifier strings like Nat.lt_add_one where a 40-character value was measured to cost 123 bytes more than a small integer - so the 4.2 GB that figure extrapolates to is a FLOOR and not an estimate, and the real number wants measuring on actual slice data before anyone plans against it; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: an embedded engine that cannot meet the four constraints the port decision names - canonically sorted keys from relPersp and relAll, an efficient seminaive lookup front, no dependency added to the kernel, and a clone that beats rather than merely matches the in-memory store").

finding(f_afk_waits_for_storage, insight).
recorded(f_afk_waits_for_storage, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_afk_waits_for_storage,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-31: AFK is sequenced AFTER the storage adapter; its recorded blocker is that several agents writing asynchronously into one store over a multi-hour run re-run the fixpoint on every assertion over a store that keeps growing, and both halves of that are storage-shaped - the write cost and the growth - so attempting AFK before the adapter would produce a demo whose limits are the ones the adapter exists to remove; note f_spat_is_the_cheap_afk already showed the domestic instance of the same thesis passes on today's kernel because a family week is a few hundred facts changing a few times a day, so the thesis is not blocked, only its infrastructure-monitoring setting is; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a measurement showing AFK's write rate is survivable on the in-memory store, which would make the sequencing unnecessary").

finding(f_no_string_builtins_and_the_boundary_stays, insight).
recorded(f_no_string_builtins_and_the_boundary_stays, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_no_string_builtins_and_the_boundary_stays,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-31: no string builtins, the why-boundary stays where it is, and the host keeps tokenizing; I argued FOR adding them and could not sustain the argument when asked to, which is the useful part of the record; MY ARGUMENT WAS that without string operations the model cannot catch a broken tokenizer, and a check that cannot say no is worthless - and it fails on inspection, because a model re-deriving status(N,C) from the raw line would use THE SAME FIELD INDEX, so a wrong index is wrong in both implementations, they agree, and both are wrong; differential checking requires INDEPENDENT implementations, which is exactly what this session kept establishing elsewhere (SLOP's oracle is the real system, GOOF-on-Mathlib's is Lean itself, DITTO's is a separate resolver), and rules rewriting the same author's understanding of the same spec are a DUPLICATE rather than an oracle; the supporting arguments do not carry it either - string EQUALITY already works and only construction and decomposition are absent, the LINE is already reachable from why so the explanation loss is one step with the material for a manual check present, and any finite predicate the host can emit as a fact (prefix, namespace, shape) in one pass; MEASURED COST, so a later decision starts from facts: everything would fit inside `is` because its right side is an evaluated expression, so grammar, modes and reification stay untouched - evalArith returns number|null and accepts only binary functors, there are exactly TWO call sites both in engine.ts, and the names len/sub/cat/idx would need adding to the kernel_grep whitelist, making it a documented vocabulary extension of about forty lines; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS, sharpened by Vadim the same day: the first form of this refuter said 'a domain where the structure of a string is the subject rather than the format of the input', and he immediately produced a candidate - DNA - and then refuted it himself, because open reading frames, restriction sites, palindromes and reverse complements all have FINITE answer sets over a finite alphabet and a scanner pass turns every one of them into facts, exactly as it does for log fields and for Mathlib namespaces; so the refuter was too wide, and the real boundary is narrower: THE STRING THAT MUST BE CONSTRUCTED HAS TO BE A CONCLUSION AND NOT AN INPUT - while what to look for is known before the reasoning starts, a scanner always suffices, and only where the rules themselves decide what to look for can the host not precompute it; the nearest real instance is motif discovery in the same DNA domain, where the motif distinguishing two groups is derived rather than given - and even that does not refute the decision, because it is a search and optimisation problem rather than a deduction and this engine is a poor fit for it; no example and no spec asks for that today").
addressed_by(f_no_string_builtins_and_the_boundary_stays, "src/unify.ts").

finding(f_a_type_error_is_not_a_false_premise, insight).
recorded(f_a_type_error_is_not_a_false_premise, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_a_type_error_is_not_a_false_premise,
  "N is \"hello\" + 1 used to produce no rows and no diagnostic, so a TYPE ERROR was indistinguishable from an honest 'no' - RULE 2 living inside the language itself, where an empty result could mean absent or could mean the instrument declined; fixed by giving evalArith an optional out-parameter carrying one of three codes, which keeps the signature backwards-compatible so existing callers need no change, and by emitting the kernel's existing idiom for inability rather than inventing one: hole(rule, arith_type_error), the same shape as hole(query, budget_exhausted); THE HARD PART WAS NOT THE ERROR BUT THE THREE-WAY SEPARATION - an unbound variable is a legitimate 'not ready yet' that happens constantly, and treating it as an error would flood every store with noise while the suite stayed green, so the discriminating test asserts SILENCE for an ordinary rule and carries a positive control in the same store proving the machinery would have spoken; the implementing agent added two cases I had not named, both real: one hole per RULE rather than one per offending substitution (a rule failing on ten thousand rows would otherwise leave ten thousand holes) and no hole from an explanation walk (why and whynot re-evaluate and would otherwise pollute); VERIFIED BY BREAKING IT, and the outcome was not what I predicted: forcing the unbound case to report a type error changed NO behaviour, because the engine defers a builtin until its inputs are ground, so the unbound branch is unreachable through `is` and is reachable only through a comparison - which means that branch is defensive and partly dead, neither my probe nor the test can break it, and that is recorded as an open curiosity rather than explained away").

finding(f_the_paired_timing_test_still_flakes, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_paired_timing_test_still_flakes, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_the_paired_timing_test_still_flakes,
  "test/index-arrival.test.ts section 4, 'shuffled arrival costs about what ascending arrival costs', failed once in a full run and then passed three times standalone AT A HIGHER LOAD (9.06 against the 3.35 recorded at the start of the failing run), so it is a flake rather than a regression and its flakiness is not simply load; this test is the PAIRED form that f_timing_assertion_tightens_as_code_speeds_up prescribed as the fix - a ratio of the same operation in two arrival orders, chosen because it does not sharpen as the engine gets faster - and it still flakes, so the paired shape reduced the problem without removing it; the remaining exposure is that both halves are measured in one process where a GC pause can land in either, and the honest options are to take the MINIMUM of repeated pairs rather than one shot, or to accept a flaky performance guard and mark it as such; recorded rather than fixed because it is a test-quality question and nothing in the kernel depends on it").

finding(f_i_test_whether_it_works_not_how_it_is_evaded, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_test_whether_it_works_not_how_it_is_evaded, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_i_test_whether_it_works_not_how_it_is_evaded,
  "three times in one evening the owner found the floor under something I had presented as settled, and the three share a shape distinct from the measurement errors recorded earlier: 'reading 2 of not-p is really a demand for FORESIGHT, and can the engine ever supply it' (I had framed it as two readings of negation and stopped); 'a veto the model CAN express belongs in the model' (I had argued all vetoes must be host-side, generalising a constraint that holds only for external ones); and 'can the constraint express ANYTHING AT ALL' (I had proposed that constraining a perspective variable IS a declaration of scope, and perspective(P) admits every ledger - measured, a ledger called spy went straight through); THE CLASS: I stop at the first mechanism that WORKS and test whether it works, not how it would be EVADED - the checks I build are one-sided, asking 'does this fire when it should' and not 'what would a careless or motivated author write to make it not fire'; this is NOT the same as the measurement errors, where the subject of the measurement was wrong - here the subject is right and the interrogation is half-length; THE GATE IS DIFFERENT TOO and that is why it needs its own record: a positive control proves an instrument can speak, and what is missing here is an ADVERSARY - before proposing a mechanism, write the cheapest thing that satisfies it while defeating its purpose, and if that thing is easy to write the mechanism is not one; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a counted sample of my proposed mechanisms where the evasions are as rare as the failures, which would mean the one-sidedness costs nothing in practice").

finding(f_the_leak_audit_is_local_where_the_property_is_transitive, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_leak_audit_is_local_where_the_property_is_transitive, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_leak_audit_is_local_where_the_property_is_transitive, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_leak_audit_is_local_where_the_property_is_transitive,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-31 after he reframed the question: the alarm on a ledger-polymorphic rule is a FALSE POSITIVE and reading from anywhere should be permitted the way a global scope is, so the question is not whether to forbid it but what discipline it breaks - and the answer, measured, is that nothing breaks by permitting it because ATTRIBUTION SURVIVES COLLECTION (claim[case](P,X) :- said[P](X) yields claim[case](red,a), the ledger name relocated from the perspective coordinate into an ordinary argument) while what IS broken, already and independently, is that the audit is LOCAL where the property is TRANSITIVE; measured: boot.rofl closes the DEPENDENCY graph (reach over dep) and never closes the FLOW graph, so leak sees one hop at a time - content walks out through a chain whose every step is separately licensed, red to case declared and case to report declared, and nobody ever asked about red to report; the next rule can also project the attribution away, measured: digest[report](X) :- claim[case](_,X) leaves report holding red's content with no trace of red; THE FIX IS ONE RULE OF A SHAPE THAT ALREADY EXISTS - flows_to as the transitive closure of flow, after which both sides of the comparison finally agree, since sees is ALREADY the transitive closure of imports and flow was the only half that was not closed; MEASURED COST: the closure adds only 14 pairs across the whole corpus (93 single-hop flows become 107), and under the configuration to be shipped - closure added, bracket still licensing - exactly 14 leaks appear, of which 9 are ordinary named pairs needing an imports declaration and 5 are polymorphic and pending a separate decision; the sharpest is goof's $var(H) to $var(G), one foundation's content reaching another's through an intermediary, in the example whose entire thesis is that the foundations are kept apart; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a corpus where the transitive closure floods the audit, which would mean the alarm volume rather than the alarm's locality is the binding constraint").
addressed_by(f_the_leak_audit_is_local_where_the_property_is_transitive, "boot.rofl").

finding(f_collects_is_the_sentence_nobody_could_write, insight).
recorded(f_collects_is_the_sentence_nobody_could_write, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_collects_is_the_sentence_nobody_could_write,
  "the polymorphic crossing had no remedy: imports(To, From) relates two REGISTERED perspectives, and a ledger-polymorphic rule reads $var(P), which has no authority fact and can never be the From of an import - so a gathering rule was a permanent finding whose only remedy was a paragraph in a README, and Vadim rejected that in one line: an implicit effect must be made explicit, auditable and deliberate, and a paragraph is none of the three; the remedy is a DECLARATION in the model, collects(X), host data declared edb the same way imports is, saying that X deliberately gathers from ledgers it does not name; THREE DESIGN CHOICES WERE MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED, which is why this is worth recording - (1) the WIDE form, not collects(B) as a bare premise on leak, is an OFF SWITCH: a store carrying a walk from secret through mid into a collecting case, every hop bracketed and the walk declared nowhere, reports NOTHING under it, so the escape was narrowed to apply only where the SOURCE is not a registered perspective, which is exactly the case imports cannot express and no other; (2) it is keyed on the ledger that GATHERS and not on where content ends up, because keyed on the destination a store where case gathers and feeds two downstream ledgers demands collects on BOTH of them, two sentences that are false about ledgers which gather nothing, and a third the day a third reader appears; (3) the declaration is EXERCISED as a positive row, collected[audit](X), because why needs a ground literal and $ is unwritable so why gathered($var(P), worlds) cannot be typed at all while why collected[audit](case) can, and its tree names collects(case) as an axiom - a licence nobody can ask about is the invisible absence this replaced; VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY by me on three cases: a multi-hop polymorphic gather leaks undeclared and is silent when declared while emitting collected(case), and a walk from a NAMED ledger through the same declared gatherer still leaks - so declaring collects never stops the audit saying no about a named crossing").
dismissed(f_no_string_builtins_moves_why_boundary, decided_boundary_stays).

finding(f_the_reified_read_is_affordable_when_narrowed, insight).
recorded(f_the_reified_read_is_affordable_when_narrowed, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_reified_read_is_affordable_when_narrowed, decision).
finding_note(f_the_reified_read_is_affordable_when_narrowed,
  "letting rules READ $-prefixed reified terms in a body (never construct them, never as a relation name) was measured at 3-16x on incremental re-evaluation and looked unaffordable; narrowing it to the rules that actually need it brings that to 0.8-2.7x with the capability INTACT, so the earlier number was the cost of doing the work everywhere rather than the cost of the capability; MEASURED on four programs, 8-asserts incremental, median of 3, node 22 on a 4-core i7 at load 3.4-4.2 - wtf 1.0x base, 0.9x inert, 0.8x narrow2, 1.1x narrow, 3.1x wide; spat 1.0/1.3/1.8/2.9/10.5; goof 1.0/1.4/2.7/5.2/16.1; npc 1.0/1.1/1.4/3.3/9.6; THE THREE-WAY SEPARATION IS THE POINT and it was the thing I insisted on before any number: the INERT column - the new audit rules present in boot.rofl but written to match nothing - runs at 0.9-1.4x, which means the cost is NOT in the parser being $-capable and NOT in boot.rofl merely having more rules, but in the WORK, and work narrows; had inert come back at 3x the narrowing would have been pointless and the decision already made; CAPABILITY RETAINED, verified on five cases where all three variants agree exactly - a vacuous guard (anyone(P), true of every ledger) is flagged 1 by wide, narrow AND narrow2 while a real guard, an external permission bit, a guard on a derived relation and a named perspective with a variable argument are all flagged 0 - and the comparison is proved capable of seeing a LOST capability by a deliberately over-narrowed gate, which drops the vacuous flag to 0; the grounding for what to narrow TO is exact rather than a guess: of the five positions in a literal only the perspective and the arguments may hold a variable (relation names cannot, which is what keeps the language first-order; temporal markers and builtin operators cannot), and of those only the perspective is audited, so the rules needing the full form are exactly those with a variable perspective - 56 occurrences across the corpus against 0 of the wildcard; STILL A PROTOTYPE in a scratch copy: landing it is a parser change plus new audit rules, and the parser change needs its own acceptance; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a corpus where variable perspectives are common rather than 56 occurrences, since the narrowing's whole economy is that the population needing the full form is small").

finding(f_a_count_of_call_sites_expires_while_you_quote_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_count_of_call_sites_expires_while_you_quote_it, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_a_count_of_call_sites_expires_while_you_quote_it,
  "I briefed the storage port with a measured figure - Store is a narrow surface, 47 method calls against only 5 direct field accesses, engine.ts holding exactly one - and the agent re-measured on arrival and found TEN, with engine.ts holding five; the cause is not drift over weeks but drift over HOURS: three of the new sites sit at engine.ts lines 588-710, inside the negation region a SECOND agent was rewriting at that moment, so the count was growing while I quoted it; this is a variant of the recorded rule that a finding's numbers expire when a shared root cause is fixed elsewhere, one step sharper - a count of CALL SITES expires not when something is fixed but simply while another agent WRITES, so the more parallel the work the shorter the number's life; the practical form: a brief that cites a count of places must say when it was taken, and an agent receiving one should re-measure before building on it - which this one did unasked, and it is the second time this week an executor corrected a figure I handed down; THE COORDINATION FIX IS BETTER THAN THE PATCH and is what was actually done: rather than have the port agent chase new sites, the WFS agent was told to use the sealed accessors for anything it adds from here on, so the count stops growing at the source instead of being reconciled afterwards; also corrected by the same agent: node:sqlite needs NO flag on node 22.22 (require works bare, only an ExperimentalWarning) and `backup` is a module-level function rather than a method on DatabaseSync - the first strengthens the zero-install argument the adapter was chosen for").
addressed_by(f_unknown_is_a_value_not_a_refusal, "src/engine.ts").
addressed_by(f_unknown_is_a_value_not_a_refusal, "test/well-founded.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_stratification_is_relation_level, "src/engine.ts").

finding(f_the_third_value_is_earned_not_subtracted, insight).
recorded(f_the_third_value_is_earned_not_subtracted, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_the_third_value_is_earned_not_subtracted,
  "the alternating fixpoint landed and RIP is unblocked; the design is textbook - two sequences from the same operator with negation judged against a FROZEN set rather than against the store being built, the generous rounds decreasing and the mean rounds increasing until they meet, what both agree on TRUE, what neither reaches FALSE, the gap UNDEFINED - and the property the owner insisted on is structural rather than incidental: AN ATOM REACHES THE GAP ONLY BY BEING DERIVED IN A ROUND, so it carries the firing that derived it and that firing is what `why` renders, which is why the third value is EARNED and not the leftover of everything neither yes nor no; the conflation the cheaper options would have produced is closed at the same place - a budget that runs out never reaches the alternation at all, there is no fixpoint to compare, hole(Id, budget_exhausted) is written above it and the unknown set stays EMPTY, so 'depends on luck' and 'I ran out' cannot merge; THE ACCEPTANCE GATE WAS FREE AND EXACT and it is worth copying: well-founded semantics coincides with the perfect model on stratified programs, that is a theorem, every program in this repository is stratified, so the goldens moving ZERO BYTES is that theorem run as a test - and they did, firing for firing, with examples/wtf (14 stratum levels over 193 relations) asserted explicitly as its own case; the opt-in shape keeps the old behaviour honest: the game encoding LOADS under a declaration and is STILL REFUSED without it, so unstratified/1 and its reach-trace diagnostic survive rather than being deleted for rejecting less; and the encoding that was WRONGLY rejected before - step-indexed, provably sound because the index strictly decreases - now runs and comes out TOTALLY DEFINED, which is the proof the change did what it was for; VERIFIED BY BREAKING IT: collapsing the gap so nothing is ever undefined kills 7 of 13 tests, exactly those about the third value, while the ones about programs that HAVE no third value (wtf's stratified agreement, the step-indexed encoding, order-independence) survive untouched - the sensitivity profile a suite should have").

finding(f_the_conformance_oracle_is_blind_to_three_of_five, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_conformance_oracle_is_blind_to_three_of_five, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_the_conformance_oracle_is_blind_to_three_of_five,
  "the storage port's acceptance rested on a claim I made in the brief - the in-memory store IS the reference implementation, so an adapter producing a BYTE-IDENTICAL canonicalState is conformant, and this oracle is free and EXACT; free is true and exact is FALSE, measured by the implementing agent running five deliberate mutants rather than asserting the oracle worked: KILLED when allFactKeys stops being canonically sorted and when allWitnesses returns nothing, BLIND when relPersp and relAll answer in REVERSE key order, when allFacts sorts instead of preserving arrival order, and when argMatches drops facts holding a variable; the three blind spots all have deliberate causes IN THE ENGINE - matchPremise totally sorts its matches before returning and says in a comment that candidate order is meant to be unobservable, negHolds reads the assumption's per-relation array as an existence check so arrival order never escapes, and no program in this repository holds a fact with a variable inside it so the third mutant has nothing to drop; CONSEQUENCE FOR THE RECORDED PORT CONSTRAINTS: constraint 1 (canonically sorted keys) is genuinely enforced by the oracle, and constraint 2's ordering half is NOT - an adapter could lose it silently and every end-to-end test would stay green; the repair the agent built rather than reporting: the port's read-order properties are now checked SURFACE AGAINST SURFACE over every relation of a real program, that gate is demonstrably red on both blind mutants, and the end-to-end blindness itself is written as an ASSERTION so that if a future engine change makes read order observable the note goes stale loudly instead of quietly; THE GENERAL LESSON, and it is about me: an oracle whose blindness nobody measured is an oracle whose coverage nobody knows, and I handed one down as total - the mutants are what turned 'the oracle works' into 'the oracle covers these two of five and here is the second gate for the rest'").
addressed_by(f_storage_is_a_port_not_a_rewrite, "src/store.ts").
addressed_by(f_storage_is_a_port_not_a_rewrite, "adapters/sqlite-store.ts").
addressed_by(f_storage_goes_straight_to_an_adapter, "adapters/sqlite-store.ts").
addressed_by(f_storage_goes_straight_to_an_adapter, "test/store-conformance.test.ts").

finding(f_the_adapter_is_slow_because_it_does_not_cache, insight).
recorded(f_the_adapter_is_slow_because_it_does_not_cache, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_adapter_is_slow_because_it_does_not_cache, decision).
finding_note(f_the_adapter_is_slow_because_it_does_not_cache,
  "the SQLite adapter conforms byte-identically and is slower, and the breakdown says the cost is CACHING rather than architecture, which was the question that mattered - a synchronous per-row API against a set-at-a-time engine would have been the architectural verdict and it is not what the numbers show; measured on examples/wtf (17785 facts) at load 3.84 on this laptop, node 22, memory store against the adapter in the same run: end to end 736 ms against 6033 ms = 8.2x, and per operation relAll (the sorted read of one relation) 24.6 ms against 2089 ms = 84.9x, relCount 0.1 ms against 25.1 ms = 198x, get by key 3.1x, allFactKeys 2.8x, and inserting 20000 facts 34 ms against 258 ms = 7.5x; THE TWO OUTLIERS HAVE ONE CAUSE - the in-memory store keeps a per-relation sorted array and a ready count while the adapter RUNS A QUERY ON EVERY CALL, so relCount is SELECT COUNT(*) WHERE rel=? where the reference implementation simply remembers, and relAll materialises every row into FactRec objects each time it is asked; NOTE THE TWO FIGURES IN CIRCULATION AND WHY BOTH ARE RIGHT: the implementing agent reported 40x for wtf and this measurement says 8.2x, and they measure different things - the agent's case is the conformance test, which builds BOTH stores and compares canonical states, an operation that is itself expensive for the adapter, while this one is load plus fixpoint only; THE DECISION THIS RAISES is a dial rather than a fix: caching per-relation arrays in the adapter would close most of the gap and would give back the memory the adapter exists to save - measured earlier at 95 bytes per fact against 296 in memory - so cache more means faster and heavier, cache less means slower and lighter, and where to sit on that dial is a question about the workload rather than about the code; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a profile showing the cost concentrated in the SQLite call boundary rather than in repeated recomputation, which would make it architectural after all and would argue for a set-at-a-time interface instead of a cache").

finding(f_js_is_the_prototype_rust_is_the_scale, insight).
recorded(f_js_is_the_prototype_rust_is_the_scale, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_js_is_the_prototype_rust_is_the_scale, doc).
finding_note(f_js_is_the_prototype_rust_is_the_scale,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-31, and it settles a tension that had been implicit all session: the JavaScript kernel is a CHEAP PROTOTYPE and was never meant for scale; scale means a native core - Rust, where the in-memory representation IS the on-disk one, so mmap dissolves the spill question rather than answering it and there is no conversion at the boundary; embedding into RFDB is more expensive still because of legacy there; and the staging is - run JS until you need scale, and when you do, pay the install ONCE; THIS REDEFINES WHAT THE SQLITE ADAPTER IS FOR, which matters because it was chosen two hours earlier for a property this decision demotes: node:sqlite needs no install at all, and zero-install turns out to be a PROTOTYPE-STAGE property rather than a permanent constraint - so the adapter is not a scale backend (measured: 8.2x slower end to end, only 3.1x smaller per fact) but PROOF THAT THE SEAM EXISTS and the conformance harness a future native store will be checked against, which is what the brief actually claimed for it; THE MEASUREMENT THAT MADE THE DECISION UNAVOIDABLE: a columnar layout with a shared dictionary reaches about 14 bytes per fact against 95 for the naive SQLite schema and 296 in memory, which is the field's 20-33 bytes per tuple - but it pays only if the engine stops asking for OBJECTS, since materialising packed rows into FactRec is exactly why relAll measures 85x slower in the adapter, so there is no intermediate 'columnar store under the current evaluator', it would give the win back at the boundary; ALSO NOTED, and it amends docs/formal-reasoning-landscape.md: that document's contingency reads 'compile the rule packs down to the Datalog performance family, the discipline is the product and the fixpoint is a commodity', and it was written when this fixpoint WAS a commodity - four things landed since that no commodity engine has, namely perspectives as a first-class fact coordinate with visibility and a transitive leak audit, kernel-emitted per-firing provenance as queryable facts, an alternating fixpoint whose third value carries its own witness, and ticks with staged conclusions - so compiling down would now mean giving up what makes this ROFL rather than Datalog; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: the JS prototype's ceiling turning out to be BELOW the questions the examples naturally ask, which would mean the staging is wrong and the native core is needed now rather than later - measured against that, Mathlib's interesting question needed a 21271-node slice and not the 497345-node corpus, so the ceiling is above the demand so far").

finding(f_rules_as_facts_is_the_foundation_i_did_not_list, pitfall).
recorded(f_rules_as_facts_is_the_foundation_i_did_not_list, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_rules_as_facts_is_the_foundation_i_did_not_list,
  "asked whether four kernel properties are the differentiator, I counted their use across the 19 built examples - perspectives 11, provenance 18, ticks 3, three-valued 0 - and concluded that two carry the corpus while ticks are marginal and the third value is speculative, so compiling down to a commodity Datalog engine would surrender less than I had claimed; Vadim stopped it with one question, what exactly am I proposing to throw out, and TWO ERRORS SURFACED; FIRST, a usage count over examples built BEFORE two of those decisions was read as a verdict ON those decisions - the third value has zero users because it was implemented four hours earlier FOR RIP, which is the next example, and ticks have three because most examples are batch-shaped questions while the whole time-and-continuity document written the same day argues the unbuilt work lives in the continuous case; same class as the recorded is-to-should error, a property of the present corpus taken for a property of the design, and worse because those two are precisely the decisions the owner had just taken; SECOND AND LARGER, 'addable on top' is false for all four rather than for two: Souffle's provenance extension gives a proof tree PER QUERY and not facts per firing, and the entire semiring layer folds over the support hypergraph; a perspective column is easy but the visibility closure and the leak audit are RULES OVER THE REFLECTION; ticks are an evaluation-order property and not a data shape; and the alternating fixpoint IS the evaluator, so it cannot ride on a two-valued one by definition; THE THING I NEVER LISTED IS THE FOUNDATION ALL FOUR STAND ON - RULES ARE FACTS, the reflection: boot.rofl's audits are rules over the reflected program and 17 of the 20 example test files assert them, MOOT reasons about its own rules, LOOT versions somebody else's, NPC writes itself new ones, and none of that exists without it; no commodity engine has it, so compiling down surrenders the foundation and not two properties out of four; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a commodity engine that reflects its own rule set as queryable data, which would make the foundation portable and reopen the whole question").

finding(f_the_kernel_now_audits_its_own_specification, insight).
recorded(f_the_kernel_now_audits_its_own_specification, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_the_kernel_now_audits_its_own_specification,
  "Vadim's idea - besides modelling how the kernel EXECUTES, model what it MUST DO, even where the rules do not express it, simply as facts - is built and it answers a question that could not be asked before: which obligation has nothing behind it; 126 duties transcribed from six documents with 74 citation checks and 113 guard links, and the design choice worth keeping is the LEDGERS - six documents became six perspectives for exactly the reason docs/choosing-perspectives.md gives, that contradictory entries coexist in them LEGALLY, since duty[limits] says frozen provenance is never collected while duty[decided] says a retention window may drop it and both sentences really are in the tree; filing them in one book would make the pair a defect to resolve, filing them in two makes it an amendment to record, which is what it is; MEASURED on the real corpus, running the model through its own scanner: 95 covered, 28 UNCOVERED, 0 unfounded, 0 dangling, 6 superseded, 120 live of 126; the anti-fabrication guard is not a promise but a mechanism - each duty carries its citation in the same fact and scanners/spec.ts READS the cited line, and I verified it myself by planting duty[spec](planted_fake, ..., 9999, \"this anchor does not exist anywhere\") which came back as unfounded=1 naming planted_fake, so the zero on the real corpus is a measurement rather than an absence; THE SHARPEST SINGLE RESULT: one prohibited shortcut has no guard at all - s8_out_of_scope, START.md line 144, the out-of-scope list that names 'aggregation, GPU anything, PERSISTENCE LAYERS, optimization passes, syntax sugar' - which is pointed straight at today's work, since the storage adapter was justified by reasoning that section 8 forbids BUILDING persistence and not exposing an interface someone else's persistence satisfies; the reasoning is recorded and nothing mechanically checks it; AND THE PATTERN IN THE 28: thirteen are LIMITS.md entries, which is the category that says what the kernel deliberately does NOT do - a negative claim, and testing one means asserting a failure, which almost nobody writes; two more landed the same day (the collects declaration and the veto-belongs-inside decision) and have no test yet, which is honest rather than a defect").

finding(f_expressible_is_not_portable_the_discriminator_is_when, insight).
recorded(f_expressible_is_not_portable_the_discriminator_is_when, "2026-08-31").
finding_note(f_expressible_is_not_portable_the_discriminator_is_when,
  "the sizing question behind a possible native rewrite - how much of src/engine.ts is POLICY, expressible as rules and therefore portable as DATA, versus MECHANISM which must be rewritten - was answered with FOUR categories rather than the two I asked for, and the third is the one a two-way count hides: MECH cannot be rules because it is what evaluates rules; POL is expressible over reflection that ALREADY EXISTS; POL* is expressible only if the kernel emits reflection it does not emit today (rule safety, premise tense, which relations hold derived facts) - real policy with a named price; PLUMB is types, fields and constructors, neither; measured over 761 code lines: MECH 486 (64%), PLUMB 71 (9%), POL* 92 (12%), POL 112 (15%); THE HEADLINE IS NOT THE RATIO BUT THE TIMING, and it is the finding: of the 204 policy lines only 31 are needed AFTER the answer exists, 107 are needed BEFORE and 66 at end of run - and policy needed before evaluation CANNOT BE COMPUTED BY EVALUATION, which is the circularity that bounds how much can ever move to rules; the concrete case measured rather than argued is readStrata's MAX, which is two rules - EXPRESSIBLE AND STILL NOT PORTABLE, because the evaluator needs the stratum order before it can run the rules that would compute it; so a native core would have to contain roughly 486 mechanism plus 71 plumbing, about 557 lines' worth, plus the 107 policy lines that cannot leave for reasons of timing rather than of expressiveness; the general form worth carrying past this project: when asking whether a decision can move from code into data, the question is not whether it is expressible but WHEN ITS ANSWER IS NEEDED, and the two are routinely confused").

finding(f_a_census_test_breaks_on_ordinary_growth, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_census_test_breaks_on_ordinary_growth, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_a_census_test_breaks_on_ordinary_growth, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_census_test_breaks_on_ordinary_growth,
  "the spec-coverage tests assert EXACT TOTALS OF THE TREE THEY LIVE IN - testFiles.length equals 56 and the test-kind check count equals 657 - and both went red within the hour because another agent added test/example-rip.test.ts, taking the count to 57; the failure says nothing about the model it guards, which is the definition of a bad test, and this is the same shape as the YAK fragment that asserted a timing ratio and went red when the engine got faster, ON THE VERY IMPROVEMENT IT SHOULD HAVE BEEN INDIFFERENT TO; the half that must be KEPT is the per-file positive control in the same test - every file in the census must contribute at least one check - because a file whose extractor regex silently fails contributes zero rows and reads exactly like a file with no tests, which is the RULE 2 shape and the reason the test exists; the fix is to assert the INVARIANT rather than the census: a floor rather than equality, and the finding printed rather than asserted, so that '38 of 56 test files guard nothing any modelled document demands' survives the 57th file and stays the interesting number it is").

finding(f_an_opt_in_feature_is_invisible_to_every_gate, pitfall).
recorded(f_an_opt_in_feature_is_invisible_to_every_gate, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_an_opt_in_feature_is_invisible_to_every_gate, decision).
finding_note(f_an_opt_in_feature_is_invisible_to_every_gate,
  "the obligation model found retainTicks living in src/api.ts as a working mechanism with its own two-gate comment, and it should not be there: the agent that measured the retention question was told to STOP BUILDING and only measure, and I accepted the tree TWICE afterwards, at 611/0 and again at 634/0, without noticing a new public API; THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL AND WORTH THE RECORD - an OPT-IN feature is invisible to every check I run, BY CONSTRUCTION: the goldens move zero bytes because the default path is unchanged, the suite stays green because no test passes the new flag, tsc and the vocabulary and text gates are all clean, and every one of those statements is TRUE while a capability ships unreviewed; my acceptance ritual measures behaviour under the default and a new option is precisely the thing that has no default behaviour; SECOND HALF OF THE DEFECT: LIMITS.md line 121 says frozen provenance is never garbage-collected and there is no compaction, which retainTicks contradicts, and neither LIMITS.md nor README.md mentions it - the only place the change is written down is a design document, so the repository's own declared limits are now false and nothing said so until a model of those limits was built; DECISION NEEDED: keep the mechanism and amend LIMITS.md, or remove it as the instruction intended; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: an acceptance check that DOES notice a new option - a surface diff over the public API would be one, and it does not exist today").

finding(f_the_bootstrap_cut_may_be_a_chain_not_a_point, question).
recorded(f_the_bootstrap_cut_may_be_a_chain_not_a_point, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_bootstrap_cut_may_be_a_chain_not_a_point, decision).
finding_note(f_the_bootstrap_cut_may_be_a_chain_not_a_point, "engine_split.ts reports 107 code lines of policy needed BEFORE phase A - the monotone pass whose output orders the negation phases - and I reported that as a single cut, a floor under any port. The user asked the question that inverts it: those 107 lines are SIX blocks, and nobody measured the dependencies AMONG them. If that subgraph is acyclic there is no cut point, there is a CHAIN OF STAGES, each expressible as rules over the previous stage's output - which is the trick the kernel already performs exactly once, holding no stratification checker and reading the stratum/2 table boot.rofl computes. Three stages already exist unnamed: bootstrapKernel emits bare facts, boot.rofl runs rules over them, the program runs over that. The question is whether more fits. This has the same shape as f_next_does_not_break_a_negative_cycle, where a loop acyclic in TIME read as a cycle in the graph and the repair was to give the staged conclusion its own identity. The port cost changes in kind rather than degree: a chain means only the dumbest stage-0 evaluator is rewritten in the target language and every later stage stays a boot file, which is DATA and ports without being rewritten. The claim must be measured from the code's own data flow and never from my block descriptions - those are my paraphrase, and a graph built from them measures my confidence instead of the program.").

finding(f_a_brief_requirement_must_bind_its_author_same_session, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_brief_requirement_must_bind_its_author_same_session, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_a_brief_requirement_must_bind_its_author_same_session, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_brief_requirement_must_bind_its_author_same_session, "Within one hour I wrote into a subagent brief that a run which does not terminate is its own outcome category - 'name the timeout explicitly, it is category C with the cause did-not-terminate, not crashed' - and then wrote a probe of my own whose outcome taxonomy had exactly two cells, accepted and rejected. The real outcome was exit=124 at 420s under load 12, and for one turn I read it through the wrong pair. The same probe had already mis-aimed its anchor: String.replace with a STRING argument replaces the FIRST occurrence, and `semantics(well_founded).` appears inside a prose comment at line 24 before the declaration at line 473, so I commented out the prose and read the unchanged output as evidence that the declaration was decoration. What caught it was implausibility - 130 and 24 byte-identical to the control - not a test and not my eyes. The class is not inattention. A brief is where I state a discipline most sharply, and a requirement I impose on an executor is the strongest formulation of it I own; not applying it to my own probes in the same session keeps my best knowledge in a file I do not open. Two cheap defences, both now standing in the probe: COUNT THE ANCHOR OCCURRENCES AND PRINT THE NUMBER BEFORE REPLACING, and give the outcome taxonomy a third cell for did-not-finish. Either alone would have caught either error. The general rule: a requirement written into a brief binds its author for the rest of the session.").

finding(f_rejecting_a_bad_program_costs_the_whole_budget, defect).
recorded(f_rejecting_a_bad_program_costs_the_whole_budget, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_rejecting_a_bad_program_costs_the_whole_budget, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_rejecting_a_bad_program_costs_the_whole_budget, "A THREE-RULE program with a plain negative cycle - n(a). p(X):-n(X),not q(X). q(X):-n(X),not p(X). - does not come back. Measured at load 12-17, so absolute numbers are inflated, but the RATIO is taken at one moment and is load-independent: boot.rofl alone loads in 119 ms, boot plus a STRATIFIED negation in 101 ms, and both accepted cases are FLAT in the budget at 27-43 ms whatever it is set to. The cycle is superlinear in the budget - 350 ms at 2k, 2254 ms at 8k, 22331 ms at 32k, roughly quadratic - and at the 5,000,000 budget the corpus uses it did not return inside a 900-second timeout (exit 124), at 25 percent CPU throughout, so it is computing rather than deadlocked: fifteen minutes and no answer for a three-rule program. The answer itself is CORRECT and complete: 'program rejected: unstratified[main](p), unstratified[main](q)' with a full reach trace. And the store is byte-identical at every budget, 972 facts at 2k and at 32k, so the fixpoint converged long before the time was spent: THE COST IS IN A SEARCH, NOT IN THE OUTPUT, and it belongs to the rejection path specifically. TWO HYPOTHESES I DREW FROM READING THE CODE AND MEASUREMENT REFUTED BOTH. First, that boot.rofl:39 - stratum(Rel,N) :- dep_neg(Rel,Q), stratum(Q,M), N is M+1 - generates unbounded strata on a cycle: it does not, 53 stratum facts with max N 2, budget-invariant, the same shape as the control's 57. Second, that a rejection which builds no reach trace would discriminate the fixpoint from the trace search: the unsafe-rule program I chose was ACCEPTED, so the discriminator had only one arm. WHAT IS STILL UNKNOWN is which search spends the budget, and that wants a profile rather than a third reading of the source. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a run on an idle machine where the negative cycle returns in time comparable to the accepted cases, or a demonstration that the budget is honoured and the wall-clock is an artifact of load. Note the shape: a LARGER budget makes rejecting a bad program SLOWER without making its message better, which is backwards, and it is the reason a program that should be refused in milliseconds looks like a hang.").

addressed_by(f_stratification_is_relation_level, "examples/rip/").

finding(f_rip_is_the_alternating_fixpoints_first_paying_customer, insight).
recorded(f_rip_is_the_alternating_fixpoints_first_paying_customer, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_rip_is_the_alternating_fixpoints_first_paying_customer, doc).
finding_note(f_rip_is_the_alternating_fixpoints_first_paying_customer, "examples/rip is the corpus's first program that alternates quantifiers rather than accumulating monotonically: a state settles if there EXISTS an action such that ALL of the environment's answers lead to states that settle, and a rule language writes that universal the only way it can, as the negation of an existential, so will_settle depends on itself through TWO negations. Accepted in three probes, each with its own positive control, at load 12-17. ONE, the declaration is load-bearing rather than a label: strip `semantics(well_founded).` and the program is refused as unstratified on exactly the two relations its header names, risky and will_settle, while as shipped it loads clean. TWO, the third class is EARNED and not asserted: make the adversary powerless so risky is unreachable and unknown collapses 130 to 0, dead_letter 24 to 0. THREE, the model is guarded rather than the printout: corrupt dead_letter so it stops reading unknown and the conclusion moves 24 to 133, which any test asserting it would catch. 25 of 25 tests, 61 s, 6570 facts, will_settle 77. The reading that matters for the roadmap: dead_letter(S) :- state(S), unknown(will_settle(S)). is a rule that READS THE THIRD VALUE, so undefined is a value like any other here rather than a diagnostic, and the demo's product is the complement of the fixpoint - the states where no strategy guarantees the good end and no adversary guarantees the bad one.").

finding(f_an_impossible_brief_deletes_its_own_requirement_silently, pitfall).
recorded(f_an_impossible_brief_deletes_its_own_requirement_silently, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_an_impossible_brief_deletes_its_own_requirement_silently, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_an_impossible_brief_deletes_its_own_requirement_silently, "The same requirement was dropped by an executor TWICE, and the second time I had put it first and marked it as the main one. The cause was not the executor: the brief was arithmetically impossible. It asked for six disablements across the 21-example corpus - 126 loads, where one example alone takes 3.9 s - AND for the whole check run to finish inside 300 seconds. Nothing can satisfy both, so the executor silently dropped the half that could not be met and delivered the half that could, twice, without saying a conflict existed. The defect is that I MERGED TWO DIFFERENT BUDGETS. The 300-second rule is the budget of a GATE, the thing that runs in the suite on every change, and it exists because a check nobody will sit through gets switched off. A one-off MEASUREMENT is not bound by it: it runs in the background and its own timeout is an outcome to print. This is my own Gospriyomka lesson turned on its author - an honest gate with no bearable way to satisfy it is abolished, and what remains is the illusion of one - and I wrote that sentence into the global rules before writing this brief. TWO REPAIRS, both to the briefing template rather than to any result. First, state which budget applies: gate or measurement, and never let the gate's number silently govern a measurement. Second, and this is the half that makes the first one safe: AN EXECUTOR WHO FINDS A BRIEF'S REQUIREMENTS UNSATISFIABLE MUST REPORT THAT AS A RESULT INSTEAD OF CHOOSING SILENTLY WHAT TO DROP - a silent choice is invisible to me, whereas a conflict in the brief is a finding, and it is mine rather than the executor's. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: an executor dropping a requirement from a brief that was demonstrably satisfiable within its stated budget, which would put the cause back on the executor.").

dismissed(f_an_impossible_brief_deletes_its_own_requirement_silently, wrong_on_its_facts).
finding_note(f_an_impossible_brief_deletes_its_own_requirement_silently, "DISMISSED THE SAME DAY, and the dismissal is the finding. The requirement was NOT dropped: the executor delivered scanners/ablate.ts plus test/ablate.test.ts at 14:59, before the brief that accused it of dropping them. I concluded three times that the probe was missing because I grepped for MY OWN WORD - `disable` - while the executor had used the standard term for exactly this experiment, ABLATION. Zero hits, read as absence of the work rather than absence of my vocabulary. Fourth instance today of an empty result being a fact about my instrument, and the first with a real cost: a redundant brief, and a finding entered against an executor who had done the work. Worse, the accusation was that a merged gate-versus-measurement budget had silently deleted a requirement - and the executor had ALREADY split them correctly and on its own, the 60-second corpus matrix as a TOOL and the one-second apparatus checks as a GATE, which is the distinction I believed I was teaching it. Its test suite also contains `an anchor that is not unique throws instead of patching a comment`, written before I told it that a string replace had hit a comment instead of a declaration on me that morning. THE RULE THIS LEAVES: when checking whether delegated work exists, GREP FOR THE ARTEFACT, NOT FOR MY NAME FOR IT - list the directory by modification time, diff the file tree, ask the executor - because a search keyed to my own vocabulary can only find work that happens to share it, and its silence is about the key rather than the lock. The related-but-separate claim, that the brief's 300-second budget conflicted with 126 corpus loads, stands as arithmetic and is worth fixing in the template; what does not stand is that anything was silently dropped.").

addressed_by(f_rejecting_a_bad_program_costs_the_whole_budget, "src/engine.ts").
addressed_by(f_rejecting_a_bad_program_costs_the_whole_budget, "test/reject-budget-invariance.test.ts").

finding(f_the_monotone_phase_spins_without_deriving, question).
recorded(f_the_monotone_phase_spins_without_deriving, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_monotone_phase_spins_without_deriving, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_monotone_phase_spins_without_deriving, "Fixing the rejection cost proved something the fix does not explain. With the stratum-cone narrowing neutralised by hand - the pre-fix path, restored afterwards with the md5 checked back to the original - the gate goes red with numbers that are the diagnosis: 2001 steps at budget 2000 and 32001 at budget 32000. Steps equal the budget plus one, the plus one being the step that overruns and throws, so the old path CONSUMED THE ENTIRE BUDGET before refusing, whatever the budget was. That is why a bigger budget made refusal slower, and it is why the 5,000,000 case never came back. But the store converges at 972 facts and is byte-identical at every budget, so the monotone phase was firing for tens of thousands of steps WITHOUT DERIVING ANYTHING NEW. The narrowing removes those rules from the wave for this program and so hides the behaviour; it does not explain it, and a wider program whose stratum cone is large could meet it again. THE OPEN QUESTION IS WHY A SEMINAIVE LOOP DOES NOT REACH QUIESCENCE ON A CONVERGED STORE - a frontier that never empties, a rule re-fired from an unchanged front, or a derivation counted as new because its key differs while its content does not. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a measurement showing the steps are spent on genuinely new derivations that are then rolled back, which would make the count honest and the store comparison misleading rather than the loop defective. Note also that the SLACK of 64 in the gate is not a tuned number: the gap it has to separate is thirty thousand.").

finding(f_the_bootstrap_was_measured_in_the_wrong_unit, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_bootstrap_was_measured_in_the_wrong_unit, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_bootstrap_was_measured_in_the_wrong_unit, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_bootstrap_was_measured_in_the_wrong_unit, "The bootstrap ladder was measured in BLOCKS OF src/engine.ts BY LINE RANGE, and the user asked why - by analogy with Lean, which rules stand on which rules, do we not have that. We do, and it is stratum/2: boot.rofl computes dep/2 and dep_neg/2 off concludes and premise_pos, derives the strata, and the kernel holds no stratification checker at all because it READS that table. So the fine-grained 'what builds on what' exists for every program, at RELATION granularity, in rules - while I was reporting a coarser, weaker thing measured on host code. The unit was inherited from engine_split.ts, where it is CORRECT: that scanner asks how many lines must be rewritten in another language, and lines are what gets rewritten. For the dependency question it is wrong, because two distinct policies inside one block merge into one node and a policy spanning two blocks is duplicated - and it is brittle in a way already paid for twice today, when a second agent edited engine.ts underneath a live measurement and the geometry moved (the anchors held, which is why the runs stayed honest). ONE CORRECTION TO THE ANALOGY, load-bearing: Lean's declaration dependencies are acyclic by construction, whereas relations here MAY be mutually recursive inside a stratum and that is legal, so our counterpart of Lean's DAG is the graph of STRATA and stratum/2 is the map into it. THE MOVE THIS OPENS, and it dissolves the defect rather than patching it: the same measurement found that tiers 1, 2 and 3 need NO new reflection, so those policies can be written as rules TODAY and the kernel's own stratum/2 will lay them out - two independent methods for one order, where disagreement would be more informative than agreement because it would name what the block unit glued together. Tier 0 stays out: it needs five fact families that do not exist, because reflection today is a SET and range restriction needs a SEQUENCE.").

addressed_by(f_the_bootstrap_was_measured_in_the_wrong_unit, "rules/kernel-policy.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_bootstrap_was_measured_in_the_wrong_unit, "test/policy-ladder.test.ts").

finding(f_stratum_is_negation_depth_not_the_dependency_ladder, insight).
recorded(f_stratum_is_negation_depth_not_the_dependency_ladder, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_stratum_is_negation_depth_not_the_dependency_ladder, doc).
finding_note(f_stratum_is_negation_depth_not_the_dependency_ladder, "Asked why the bootstrap was described in rungs rather than in the Lean manner - which rules stand on which rules - I answered that stratum/2 is exactly that. It is not, and writing the policy AS RULES showed it in one table. Twelve policy relations were expressed over the reflection that already exists, loaded, and read back three ways: block tier, kernel stratum, and positive-dependency depth. THE KERNEL'S STRATA ARE NEARLY FLAT - three relations at 0 and every other at exactly 1, none above - because stratum/2 counts NEGATION levels and answers the question the evaluator needs, when may a negation be judged; a positive dependency does not raise it. The Lean-style ladder is the third column, depth over dep/2, and it has five distinct levels where the strata have two. Both are real and they answer different questions; saying one IS the other discards the distinction. THE BLOCK UNIT ALSO GLUED, exactly as predicted and now quantified: 12 relations across 4 block rungs but 5 distinct relation depths, with four blocks each spanning several - 107-118 covers depths 1-2, 214-224 covers 3-5, 509-521 covers 3-5, 666-681 covers 1-4. AND IT INVERTED ONE PAIR: the blocks put the stratum cone strictly before the demand set, while the relations put the cone's own head LEVEL with the demand set and everything downstream of the cone strictly after it. A coarse unit did not merely lose resolution, it reported one order backwards. The rules were checked against the host rather than only against each other - 56 comparisons over 8 programs, 0 disagreements, with a positive control that breaks mono_rule and shows the count move by exactly one, localised to that relation, in the direction the missing premise predicts. No mutual recursion among the twelve, so at relation granularity the policy is a DAG outright and not merely a DAG of strata.").

addressed_by(f_the_bootstrap_cut_may_be_a_chain_not_a_point, "scanners/ablate.ts").

finding(f_range_restriction_is_not_a_refusal_and_cannot_be_deferred, insight).
recorded(f_range_restriction_is_not_a_refusal_and_cannot_be_deferred, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_range_restriction_is_not_a_refusal_and_cannot_be_deferred, doc).
finding_note(f_range_restriction_is_not_a_refusal_and_cannot_be_deferred, "The staging idea rested on a distinction I stated as a hypothesis and the ablation matrix REFUTED ON THREE OF FIVE PREDICTIONS. The hypothesis: policy that is a REFUSAL defers for free, because a stage-0 evaluator can crash where a later stage would decline, whereas policy that CHOOSES what to compute cannot defer. Measured over 32 programs, each of the six blocks neutralised in turn with the anchors counted before patching and every switch confirmed to fire: 107-118 (refuse a reserved head) predicted B, came out A - nothing changed on all 32; 170-206 (range restriction) predicted B, came out C - derived facts changed on four programs, sensors 1222 to 1214, tm 1131 to 1123, tm_diverge 1097 to 1091, plus a tripwire; 214-224 predicted C, came out B - refusal only; 119-169 and 632-647 landed as predicted. THE CONSEQUENCE IS STRUCTURAL: range restriction is the BOTTOM rung of the ladder and it is NOT a refusal, so the cheap deferral - let stage 0 crash instead of decline - does not buy it. The bottom rung is expensive in both senses at once, since it is also the only rung needing reflection that does not exist (five fact families, because reflection is a SET and safety needs a SEQUENCE). Note that 119-169 and 170-206 ablate to BYTE-IDENTICAL outcomes, same programs and same state hashes, which the dependency graph predicts: the demand set reads rule.safe from range restriction, so breaking either lets the same unsafe rules through. AN ANOMALY WORTH CHASING, recorded rather than explained: the tripwire tw_reserved_head IS refused at baseline, yet ablating 107-118 - the block that implements exactly that refusal - changes nothing on any of the 32 programs including that tripwire. Either the prohibition is enforced redundantly somewhere else, or the tripwire trips a different check and the block has no test that reaches it. Until that is answered, 'this block is dead' is not established - only that this corpus cannot tell the difference.").

dismissed(f_the_monotone_phase_spins_without_deriving, measured_on_a_rolled_back_store).
finding_note(f_the_monotone_phase_spins_without_deriving, "DISMISSED: the premise was an artefact and the phase was deriving all along. Measured properly - assert plus a hand-driven Evaluation instead of load, with the stratum-cone narrowing neutralised and restored under an md5 check - the pre-fix path on the three-rule negative cycle gives stratum 866 / 3866 / 15866 facts and max N 405 / 1905 / 7905 at budgets 2k / 8k / 32k, with the whole store at 3472 / 12472 / 48472. Everything grows LINEARLY IN THE BUDGET. So boot.rofl:39 - stratum(Rel, N) :- dep_neg(Rel, Q), stratum(Q, M), N is M + 1 - does generate unbounded strata on a negative cycle, which was the FIRST hypothesis, the one I recorded as refuted by measurement. THE REFUTATION WAS THE DEFECT: 53 stratum facts with max N 2 came from a store that load had already ROLLED BACK on rejection, so it described the leftovers rather than the evaluation. The warning was in the acceptance gate I had approved two hours earlier, in those words - assert rather than load, because the rolled-back store is what made an earlier reading say the search was free - and I read it, quoted it to the user, and did not apply it to my own open finding. Same class as the brief-binds-its-author finding from this morning: a discipline stated sharply in one place and not carried to my own probe in the next. The fix in place remains correct - the cheap wave refuses before stratum is ever computed, and an ACCEPTED program has an acyclic dep_neg so its strata are bounded by the relation count - but note what it is: boot.rofl:39 is a NON-TERMINATING RULE MADE SAFE BY AN ORDERING rather than a terminating rule. Hardening it directly, with a bound like N < the number of relations, is available and was not taken.").

finding(f_the_stratum_counter_can_be_a_pair_instead, insight).
recorded(f_the_stratum_counter_can_be_a_pair_instead, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_stratum_counter_can_be_a_pair_instead, decision).
finding_note(f_the_stratum_counter_can_be_a_pair_instead, "boot.rofl:39 diverges on a negative cycle and I first blamed the NEGATION - that guarding it needs `not unstratified(Rel)`, which needs strata to order the negation, the genuine bootstrap circle. That is true but it is not the cause. THE CAUSE IS THE ARITHMETIC. Everything else in Datalog is domain-bounded: no new constant can be built from the input, so the fixpoint must converge. `N is M + 1` INVENTS a value that was not in the input, and that is what removes the guarantee. Reformulating the level from a NUMBER to a PAIR removes it at the root, with no ordering and no negation: `after(A, B) :- dep_neg(A, B).` and `after(A, B) :- dep(A, X), after(X, B).` - B must finish before A, inherited along dependencies - lives on pairs of relations, at most |rel| squared of them, invents nothing. Unstratifiability becomes literally `after(A, A)`, A must finish before itself. MEASURED on the same hand-built graph, both formulations racing on identical input, with an acyclic positive control in the same run: on the cycle p<->q the counting shape gives 1001 / 4001 / 16001 facts with max N 499 / 1999 / 7999 at budgets 2k / 8k / 32k, while the pair shape gives FOUR FACTS AND selfafter 2 AT EVERY BUDGET; on the acyclic control the pair shape gives after 1 and selfafter 0, so it stays silent where it should. WHAT IS NOT YET ANSWERED, and it decides whether this replaces boot.rofl:39 or merely explains it: the engine reads `stratum/2` as a MAX to know how many phases to run, and a pair relation carries the ORDER without the NUMBER. Turning a partial order into phase numbers needs either aggregation (absent - the seven builtins are = != < <= > >= is), or negation (legal only in a later stage, which by then is fine because the stratification question is already settled), or a few lines of host code, which is slightly MORE host than the ten lines that take the max today. So the reformulation is a strict improvement in termination and a possible regression in how much policy lives in the host, and that trade is the decision.").

finding(f_provenance_records_the_path_but_not_as_facts, question).
recorded(f_provenance_records_the_path_but_not_as_facts, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_provenance_records_the_path_but_not_as_facts, decision).
finding_note(f_provenance_records_the_path_but_not_as_facts, "Asked whether a cycle could be caught by simply noticing arrival at a point already visited, since provenance is stored anyway. Two gaps and one principled objection, all checked rather than recalled. FIRST, `derived_by(Fact, RuleId, Tick)` names the fact and the rule but NOT THE PREMISES, so a rule cannot walk from a fact to what it was derived FROM. The path exists in the store - `why` walks it and prints [cycle] on revisiting a node - but its edges are not reflected as facts. Same shape as the premise_lit gap: the data is present and not in joinable form. SECOND, STATEMENT ORDER IS NOT REFLECTED AT ALL: of the 23 emitted relations, none says which statement of the file a rule is; `has_premise(R, K)` orders premises WITHIN a body and nothing orders rules between themselves. THIRD, and this is why provenance cannot answer THIS question whatever is reflected: stratification is decided BEFORE evaluation because it decides whether evaluation is meaningful at all, while provenance is the record of an evaluation that already happened - collecting it would mean running the diverging computation that the provenance would then tell us not to run. The cycle at issue lives in the RULE GRAPH and is static; `after/2` reads exactly that, before anything runs. The instinct is right for the OTHER problem, cycles in the DERIVATION at runtime, where provenance already is the instrument in use. ON REFLECTING SOURCE ORDER: as DATA it is cheap and probably useful, so that rules can speak about it. As SEMANTICS it must never be - order-independence is what the determinism rests on, tested at a bit-identical fixpoint over 100 shuffles, and trading that away costs more than it buys.").

finding(f_pairs_cut_the_cycle_then_numbers_are_safe, insight).
recorded(f_pairs_cut_the_cycle_then_numbers_are_safe, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_pairs_cut_the_cycle_then_numbers_are_safe, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_pairs_cut_the_cycle_then_numbers_are_safe, "I presented a trade as unavoidable - a pair-shaped level terminates but carries no NUMBER, and the engine reads stratum as a MAX, so recovering phase numbers costs aggregation (absent), a negation (circular), or more host code than the ten lines taking the max today. The user dissolved it in one move: CUT THE CYCLE WITH PAIRS, THEN COUNT WITH NUMBERS, because once the pairs say the graph is acyclic the numbers are known to converge. Concretely, `not selfafter(R)` on the counting rule alone: after/2 and selfafter/1 are purely positive and invent nothing, so they are bounded by construction and complete in the monotone phase; the +1 rule guarded by them can no longer walk a cycle. MEASURED, three questions each with its control. ONE, the kernel ACCEPTS the guarded program - its own stratification sees no circularity. TWO, on the cycle p<->q the counter goes from 1001/4001/16001 facts with max N 499/1999/7999 down to THREE FACTS AND MAX N 0 at every budget. THREE, on an acyclic graph the guarded and unguarded answers are IDENTICAL, a=0 b=0 b=1 c=0 c=1 c=2 n=0 both ways, so the guard changes no correct answer. And on the shape neither of us had tested - a cycle with a relation hanging off it and an untouched clean chain beside it - the result is 8 facts at every budget, selfafter names exactly [p q] and nobody else, the hanging relation gets finite 0 and 1, and the clean chain gets precisely the numbers it has when it stands alone. THE GUARD IS EXACT: it stops the loop and nothing else. WHAT IS NOT YET MEASURED, and it is the whole distance from idea to patch: this was tested as an ORDINARY program over x-prefixed relations, where the kernel's own stratum table orders the negation. Inside boot.rofl the guarded rule means STRATUM ITSELF GAINS A NEGATED PREMISE, and stratum is what orders negations - so the engine must be able to run a second wave containing one negation whose target completed in the first. That is exactly the road stratumCone already built, but it is a change in src/engine.ts and must be measured rather than assumed.").

dismissed(f_pairs_cut_the_cycle_then_numbers_are_safe, breaks_the_corpus_in_boot_rofl).
finding_note(f_pairs_cut_the_cycle_then_numbers_are_safe, "TRIED IN boot.rofl AND REVERTED, and the negative result is worth more than the idea was. The guard is one clause - `not unstratified(Rel)` on the counting rule - and no new rules are needed at all, because boot.rofl ALREADY holds the pair-shaped cycle test under another name: unstratified(Rel) :- dep_neg(Rel, Q), reach(Q, Rel), where reach is the positive transitive closure. Added, boot.rofl still LOADS, and in the shipped configuration the refusal is intact and correct: StratificationError at 296 steps with unstratified [p, q] and no stratum computed for either. With the stratumCone narrowing neutralised, the guard ALONE holds termination - 393 steps, budget-invariant, no divergence - which was the claim. BUT THE FULL SUITE FAILS, exit 1, twelve tests down including the canary: 'THE WTF SHAPE: strata supplied AFTER the model they order', 'END-TO-END: ablating the stratum table changes what one program derives', 'an edb mark is a schedule input too', 'the deep chain really is deep, and really is order-sensitive'. The cause is exactly what boot.rofl:42 warns of in one line - negation below this line only - because a negated premise takes `stratum` OUT of the monotone phase, and stratum is what orders the phases. The comment was not a style preference, it was load-bearing, and the corpus proves it in four different shapes. Two readings of the episode, both worth keeping. FIRST, the guard and the cone are COMPLEMENTARY rather than alternative - the cone buys the early refusal, the guard would buy termination independent of call order - so the framing 'pairs replace the ordering' was wrong. SECOND, the one-observation diagnosis I made before the suite ran was right, and the reason it was right is that I had a MEASUREMENT of the mechanism (the no-cone run silently ACCEPTED the cyclic program) rather than a reading of the code. The idea returns naturally at the policy migration, where stratum moves into rules and the phase layout is rebuilt whole; there the guard costs no violated invariant because the invariant it violates will not exist in that form.").

addressed_by(f_range_restriction_is_not_a_refusal_and_cannot_be_deferred, "src/api.ts").

finding(f_the_reserved_head_guard_has_two_doors_and_one_is_untested, insight).
recorded(f_the_reserved_head_guard_has_two_doors_and_one_is_untested, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_reserved_head_guard_has_two_doors_and_one_is_untested, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_reserved_head_guard_has_two_doors_and_one_is_untested, "The ablation matrix reported that block 107-118 - refuse a rule concluding into a kernel relation - fired on ZERO of 32 programs and that neutralising it changed nothing, even on the tripwire written to trip it. Not dead code: THE PROHIBITION HAS TWO GATES ON TWO DOORS. src/api.ts:190 rejects such a rule at LOAD time with a clear message, 'rule rejected: edb is a kernel relation (write-protected)', so the text path never reaches the evaluator. src/engine.ts:113 is the same prohibition on the FACT path, which exists here precisely because rules ARE facts, and it is not silent either - it pushes 'rule <id> concludes into a kernel relation; not executable'. Nothing in the corpus takes the fact door, so ablating its guard changes nothing, which is exactly what the matrix measured. WHAT I COULD NOT ESTABLISH: whether the fact door is actually reachable. Hand-asserting six reflection facts - rule, concludes, conclusion_tense, has_conclusion, premise_pos, has_premise - did not smuggle a rule in, and it still did not with the guard neutralised, so the construction was incomplete rather than the door guarded; decodeRules almost certainly also wants the reified conclusion_lit and premise_lit that carry the arguments. THE ACTIONABLE ITEM IS SMALL AND CONCRETE: a language whose rules are facts has a second way in, and nothing tests it. Build the complete reflection of a reserved-head rule by ENCODING a legal one and rewriting its concludes fact, rather than by hand, then assert it - and the test should go red with engine.ts:113 removed. Two probe defects worth noting, both mine and both the same class as five earlier today: the first target I chose was `edb(seed)`, which the control showed is already emitted for every relation holding a base fact, so a successful smuggle would have been invisible; and I filtered diagnostics for reserved|protected|skipped while the message says 'kernel relation'.").

addressed_by(f_an_opt_in_feature_is_invisible_to_every_gate, "scripts/flag_census.ts").
addressed_by(f_an_opt_in_feature_is_invisible_to_every_gate, "examples/cram/").

finding(f_an_executor_report_does_not_reach_the_lead_as_text, pitfall).
recorded(f_an_executor_report_does_not_reach_the_lead_as_text, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_an_executor_report_does_not_reach_the_lead_as_text, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_an_executor_report_does_not_reach_the_lead_as_text, "Across this whole session not one executor's written report reached me as text - only idle notifications did. Every result I have was recovered by INSPECTING ARTEFACTS: files by modification time, test runs, diffs, checksums. That worked well enough that I did not notice the channel was missing, because a finished piece of work leaves traces that speak for themselves. It stopped working the moment an executor produced a NEGATIVE result. The migration agent touched src/engine.ts and restored it byte-identically, md5 matching my own backup, with runWellFounded unchanged - and from the tree alone 'did nothing' and 'tried it, found it impossible, reverted cleanly' are INDISTINGUISHABLE. A clean revert is exactly what a disciplined executor leaves behind, so the better the executor follows the brief, the less its reasoning survives. This is the RULE 2 shape at the level of coordination: the emptiness was a property of the channel, not of the work. THE REPAIR, and it belongs in every brief rather than in this one incident: ASK FOR THE REPORT AS A FILE, at a named path, and say what goes in it - the baseline measured before touching anything, what was tried, what was reverted, WHERE IT STOPPED AND WHY, and what it recommends next. A negative result is the case that most needs the channel and the only case that leaves no trace without it. Related and already recorded: searching for delegated work by MY OWN WORD rather than by artefact cost three redundant briefs earlier the same day.").

finding(f_policy_cannot_be_a_table_because_it_decides_who_runs, insight).
recorded(f_policy_cannot_be_a_table_because_it_decides_who_runs, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_policy_cannot_be_a_table_because_it_decides_who_runs, decision).
finding_note(f_policy_cannot_be_a_table_because_it_decides_who_runs, "Phase 1 as I specified it - the kernel READS the policy table the way it already reads stratum/2 - is impossible for ALL TWELVE relations, not just the one chosen to go first, and the reason is a clean asymmetry that the precedent hides. MEASURED, and I verified the two decisive lines myself: the first this.activate() is at src/engine.ts:252, checkUnstratified at 253 and readStrata at 256. So `unstratified` and `stratum` are consumed AFTER the first rule has ever fired, which is exactly why they can be data. Every one of the twelve policy relations is consumed BEFORE 252 - wf_declared at 108, blocked_head and executable at 113, demand_rel at 142, safe_rule/neg_rule/program_negates at 218 and 224, mono_rule/stratum_cone/late_rule at 251, wf_inadmissible at 667. THE ASYMMETRY: stratum orders the negation PHASES and does not decide WHICH RULES RUN, so a rule may compute it; the policy pack decides which rules run, so NO rule can compute it without already being run. The circle stratum does not have. Even program_negates, consumed late at 253, is unreachable: it needs executable, which needs `not blocked_head`, which puts it in a negation phase at 263, ten lines after its own consumer. Confirmed in the strongest form available - the executor BUILT the migration on a patched copy of src/, deleted the JS twin, put relAll('wf_inadmissible') in its place, and the migrated gate was GREEN on the single case it exists for, in four configurations including one with the pack loaded and unsafe injected by hand. A gate that cannot say no. TWO OF MY BRIEF'S PREMISES WERE WEAKER THAN I STATED THEM. First, the '56 comparisons, 0 disagreements' I quoted as verification covers SEVEN relations of twelve - 7 x 8 programs is exactly 56 - and wf_inadmissible, the one I chose to migrate first, is among the five whose ANSWER was never checked against the host at all. Second, cell B for that block is softer than 'only diagnostics move': facts go 972 to 983. AND unsafe/1 IS NOT EMITTED - it occurs once in all of src/, in a comment at engine.ts:121, and reaches the pack only because scanners/policy_ladder.ts:90 injects it. MY ORDERING ERROR, which is the transferable part: I ordered the migration by the ablation matrix, which ranks blocks by the COST OF A WRONG MIGRATION. Migratability is a different order - how LATE the kernel needs the answer and how much of the cone is derived rather than base - and the two are nearly opposite. By migratability wf_inadmissible is LAST, not first. THE REACHABLE FORM, and it is a decision rather than a patch: a POLICY PRE-PASS, an inner evaluation over the reflection run to fixpoint before the outer evaluation is prepared, plus unsafe/1 as emitted reflection. That is a new evaluation phase, not 'the kernel reads a table'.").

finding(f_wake_up_rounds_replace_the_stratum_table, insight).
recorded(f_wake_up_rounds_replace_the_stratum_table, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_wake_up_rounds_replace_the_stratum_table, decision).
finding_note(f_wake_up_rounds_replace_the_stratum_table, "The owner's goal is a kernel small enough to rewrite from memory at three in the morning, and their offer that the environment may WAKE UP IN ROUNDS and build itself until tick 0 dissolves the obstacle that had just stopped phase 1. If the environment wakes repeatedly, the ROUND NUMBER IS THE STRATUM: round 0 is what is base, round N runs every relation whose NEGATIVE dependencies all settled earlier, positive dependencies may sit in the same round because that is ordinary recursion. Nothing counts, so nothing diverges - peeling is bounded by construction, each round settles at least one relation or stops, while counting is bounded by nothing. And unstratifiable stops being a graph property to detect: it is simply A ROUND THAT ADDS NOTHING WHILE WORK REMAINS. MEASURED, both halves, with controls. On examples/wtf, the canary with 14 strata, peeling gives 14 rounds and the round-minus-stratum distribution contains EXACTLY TWO VALUES AND NO OTHERS - 0 for the 56 base relations, 1 for all 144 derived - so the partition into layers is identical and only the origin differs, the kernel numbering layers of NEGATION and the rounds numbering layers of DERIVATION with round 0 reserved for base. On hand-built graphs, since a cyclic program never survives load: p<->q stalls at once naming exactly [p q]; a cycle with a relation hanging off it beside an untouched clean chain runs three rounds then stalls naming [p q r] - and including r is MORE informative than today's refusal, because r is not on the cycle yet is genuinely uncomputable, depending negatively on something that never settles; an acyclic three-level graph does not stall and lays out a@1 b@2 c@3; and the risk case, a five-link PURELY POSITIVE chain, costs ONE round with everything at layer 1, so positive chains do not consume rounds and the round number really does measure negation depth. WHAT THIS WOULD REMOVE FROM THE KERNEL: the stratum table, dep_neg, reach, unstratified, the counting rule that diverged today, and the whole phase-ordering apparatus. WHAT IS NOT MEASURED: this simulates peeling over the reflected graph rather than restructuring the evaluator, so the cost of N passes over the rule set against today's computed order is unknown; and the refusal loses its reach trace, trading a demonstration of WHY for a naming of WHAT.").

finding(f_the_whole_reflection_is_readable_except_one_character, insight).
recorded(f_the_whole_reflection_is_readable_except_one_character, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_whole_reflection_is_readable_except_one_character, decision).
finding_note(f_the_whole_reflection_is_readable_except_one_character, "I reported that tier 0 - range restriction - needs FIVE fact families that do not exist, because reflection is a SET and safety needs a SEQUENCE. That was wrong, and the correction shrinks the whole migration. Measured: rules CAN build and destructure compound terms, including nested ones - a rule assembling f(s,e) and another matching g(f(A,_),_) both work and reach depth two. And the reflection is already complete: conclusion_lit(R, 1, $lit(pair, main, $cons($var(\"A\"), $cons($var(\"B\"), $nil)), $now)) carries the relation, the perspective, the ARGUMENT LIST IN ORDER WITH VARIABLE NAMES, and the tense. Premise literals likewise, builtins with their operands in order. Everything a left-to-right safety fold needs is emitted today. THE ONLY OBSTACLE IS ONE CHARACTER: every kernel-generated functor name begins with $, and a rule cannot write it - `unexpected character '$'`, with a plain functor pattern loading fine as the control. The kernel already works around its own restriction: conclusion_tense is emitted as a separate flat fact precisely because the tense marker lives inside the $lit where no rule can reach it, and reflect.ts says so in those words. So the five families are not missing, they are UNREADABLE. ON WHETHER TO MAKE $ WRITABLE, the owner proposed native access control - a whitelist of kernel files - and the mechanism he wants already exists in a better form. boot.rofl holds forged[audit](F) :- asserted_by(F, Who, _), in_perspective(F, P), not authority(P, Who), so every fact remembers WHO wrote it and a rule checks it against authority: control by AUTHOR rather than by filename, which is finer because a file can be renamed or copied while authorship travels with the fact and shows in the why-tree. A filename whitelist would be host policy with hardcoded names, exactly what this migration exists to remove. MEASURED, with controls: breach[audit] fires on concludes(r_evil, edb) naming r_evil, and forged[audit] names the forged fact by its writer. THE SEQUENCING PROBLEM AND ITS ANSWER: the parser decides about $ at parse time while the audit is a rule that runs after, leaving a window. Under the round-based evaluator that window closes for free - round 0 audits what you were handed, round 1 onwards runs it - so the two proposals of the same afternoon compose. ONE HOLE FOUND IN PASSING: concludes(r_evil, stratum) leaves breach[audit] EMPTY, because stratum is deliberately kept out of the reserved set so that boot.rofl may compute it. A forged rule can write the stratum table and only forged[audit], by authorship, would notice. Under rounds that hole vanishes with the table itself.").

finding(f_the_dollar_ban_is_a_host_side_veto_and_goes, decision).
recorded(f_the_dollar_ban_is_a_host_side_veto_and_goes, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_dollar_ban_is_a_host_side_veto_and_goes, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_dollar_ban_is_a_host_side_veto_and_goes, "DECIDED by the owner: the parser's refusal of `$` is to be lifted. His reasoning - do not block expressiveness for illusory safety when the same effect is reachable another way - and the honest accounting supports it, including the part that argues against him. THE BAN IS NOT WHOLLY ILLUSORY: forging a rule HEAD needs no $ at all, since concludes(r_evil, edb) is written with ordinary atoms and breach[audit] catches it - measured. But forging a rule BODY does need it, and this is why my hand-built smuggle failed this afternoon even with the engine-side guard neutralised: without $ you cannot write premise_lit or conclusion_lit, so decodeRules never assembles a rule. So the ban does bear weight today. WHAT SETTLES IT is that the weight is borne equally by a rule that already exists and already works: forged[audit](F) :- asserted_by(F, Who, _), in_perspective(F, P), not authority(P, Who) - control by AUTHORSHIP, which fires regardless of whether the forgery contains a $ at all, and which was measured firing on exactly such a fact. THE PRINCIPLE IS THE PROJECT'S OWN, written in docs/time-and-continuity.md: a veto the model can express belongs INSIDE the model, and a host-side veto is invisible to the audit and explains nothing. The $ ban is a host-side veto: it appears in no why-tree, cannot be asked about, and its price is that the ENTIRE reflection - 23 relations carrying full structures with argument order, variable names and tenses - is unreadable from the language that emits it. A language that can reason about anything except itself. SEQUENCING, deliberate: not done now. The round-based evaluator is being built and src/parser.ts is src/; moving ground under a running executor is the mistake already paid for this afternoon when two agents wrote into engine.ts at once. Order: rounds reports, then the ban is lifted, then the corpus is measured to confirm forged[audit] covers what the ban covered - including the hole found in passing, that stratum is deliberately outside the reserved set so breach[audit] stays empty for a rule forging into the stratum table.").

addressed_by(f_wake_up_rounds_replace_the_stratum_table, "runtime/round_eval.ts").
addressed_by(f_wake_up_rounds_replace_the_stratum_table, "test/round-eval.test.ts").

finding(f_the_schedule_is_peeled_not_derived, insight).
recorded(f_the_schedule_is_peeled_not_derived, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_schedule_is_peeled_not_derived, decision).
finding_note(f_the_schedule_is_peeled_not_derived, "The round evaluator is built (runtime/round_eval.ts, 260 lines, src/ untouched) and it AGREES WITH THE STOCK ONE BYTE FOR BYTE on 39 programs at tick 0 and over 8 ticks, with STEP COUNTS IDENTICAL TO THE DIGIT, ratio exactly 1.00 throughout - not merely the same answer but the same firing sequence, from two different schedulers. My cost model in the brief was wrong and the executor said so: rounds do NOT make N passes over the rule set. The peel is a STATIC computation over the decoded rules that runs ONCE before any rule fires - 587 microseconds on the canary, 221 rules, 14 rounds - after which the evaluator makes exactly the same activate calls. THE PAYOFF IS NOT SPEED OF SCHEDULING, IT IS THE RIGHT TO DELETE TEN RULES. Strip dep, dep_neg, reach, unstratified and stratum from boot.rofl and re-run the 34 example programs under rounds: steps 107235 to 59388, a 44.6% cut, wall 5988 ms to 3756 ms at load 4.6, with wtf falling 11125 to 1917 steps, 83%. The answers survive - on 33 of 34 the only difference is one row whose support count drops by exactly ten, one per deleted rule, which is arithmetic; the 34th reads derived_by in a rule body and so genuinely counts firings. AND ONLY THE ROUND EVALUATOR CAN COLLECT IT: on the thinned boot the stock evaluator ACCEPTS a negative cycle and evaluates it to 413 facts, because checkUnstratified reads an unstratified relation nothing populates any more. FOURTEEN MUTANTS, each a text patch of the shipped file with its anchor count printed, over two corpora - the 34 programs and 12 hand-written probes for shapes the corpus does not contain. Eleven were killed; the report says which slept and why each is or is not a hole. ONE REAL GAP, reported rather than papered over: M10, peeling over safe rules only, sleeps on EVERY input found including the probe written for it, so the constraint that an unsafe demand-backed rule still bears its dependencies is unenforced by the oracle. A second, smaller: the oracle sees the FIRST witness plus support counts, not the witness forest, so a schedule producing a different forest with the same first witness would pass. Well-founded is delegated untouched and that is shown positively - rip is byte-identical and its peel is EMPTY, rounds=0, so the path was handed over rather than accidentally duplicated. A side measurement worth keeping: the two mutants that disable the stall ran 93 and 96 seconds against 0.5 s for every other mutant, which is boot.rofl's counting rule burning its budget on the cyclic probes - a direct price tag on deciding the refusal after the counting rather than before it. Full suite 740 pass / 0 fail, 248 s, load 4.5.").

addressed_by(f_the_dollar_ban_is_a_host_side_veto_and_goes, "src/parser.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_whole_reflection_is_readable_except_one_character, "test/head-vars.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_reserved_head_guard_has_two_doors_and_one_is_untested, "test/second-door.test.ts").

finding(f_a_rule_has_two_faces_and_the_audit_reads_the_wrong_one, defect).
recorded(f_a_rule_has_two_faces_and_the_audit_reads_the_wrong_one, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_a_rule_has_two_faces_and_the_audit_reads_the_wrong_one, decision).
finding_note(f_a_rule_has_two_faces_and_the_audit_reads_the_wrong_one, "Lifting the $ ban - two lines in src/parser.ts, corpus byte-identical before and after, same md5 over 50 files and 4851 clauses, full suite 755 pass 0 fail - made the second door assemblable for the first time and so measurable for the first time. IT IS HELD BY src/engine.ts:113 AND BY NOTHING ELSE. A RULE HAS TWO FACES AND THEY ARE INDEPENDENT: concludes(R, Rel) is what the AUDIT reads, breach[audit] being defined over it at boot.rofl:47, while conclusion_lit(R, 1, $lit(Rel, ...)) is what the ENGINE executes, decodeRules building the head from there and not consulting concludes at all. So a forgery that leaves concludes honest and rewrites only conclusion_lit is invisible to breach[audit] BY CONSTRUCTION. Measured with the guard cut out: the forged rule wrote itself authority(a, a), from which boot.rofl:5 immediately raised a new perspective, with BOTH audits reporting zero. Test at test/second-door.test.ts, shown red with engine.ts md5 before and after, failing on both grounds - the diagnostic vanishes AND authority(a, a) appears. This is what the ablation matrix could not see: the guard is not dead, nothing was reaching it. AND MY SAFETY ARGUMENT TO THE OWNER WAS WRONG IN TWO MEASURED WAYS. I said forged[audit] covers by authorship what the ban covered by syntax. It does not: asserted_by is emitted ONLY IF who was supplied, so an anonymous writer leaves no authorship trace at all and the same forgery gives breach 0, forged 0, diagnostics empty - caught by nobody; and who='$kernel' passes cleanly through not authority(P, Who) because authority(main, $kernel) exists, so impersonating the kernel is a plain string away. Recommended and NOT done, since it is a decision: require asserted_by for every fact so that having no author stops being a way to be invisible, and take $kernel out of the value space who can be given.").

finding(f_the_stratum_table_is_writable_by_any_rule, defect).
recorded(f_the_stratum_table_is_writable_by_any_rule, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_stratum_table_is_writable_by_any_rule, decision).
finding_note(f_the_stratum_table_is_writable_by_any_rule, "stratum is deliberately kept OUT of RESERVED - it has to be, or boot.rofl could not compute it - so engine.ts:113 stays silent for a rule whose head is stratum/2, breach[audit] is blind because stratum is not reserved, and an anonymous writer leaves nothing for forged[audit]. Measured: the forgery executes and the table grows from 54 rows to 55, gaining stratum(a, a). THE TABLE THAT DECIDES THE ORDER OF THE NEGATION PHASES IS WRITABLE AT ZERO AUDIT SIGNAL. And this needs no $ at all: an ordinary rule `stratum(X, Y) :- move(X), move(Y).` does the same, so the hole predates today's work entirely. THE CLEAN REPAIR IS THE ROUND EVALUATOR: under wake-up rounds there IS no stratum table - the schedule is peeled from the decoded rules before anything fires and is never a relation in the store - so there is nothing to forge and the hole closes with the table rather than being patched. This turns making rounds primary from a 44.6% step saving into a saving plus the removal of a defect. A separate defect found in passing and not chased: stratum/1 crashes the host with a TypeError, and $ is not needed for that either.").

finding(f_the_demand_constraint_is_real_and_now_enforced, insight).
recorded(f_the_demand_constraint_is_real_and_now_enforced, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_demand_constraint_is_real_and_now_enforced, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_demand_constraint_is_real_and_now_enforced, "The one gap the round evaluator's author reported honestly as UNCOVERED - mutant M10, peeling over safe rules only, slept on all 34 corpus programs and all 12 probes including the one written for it - is now CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by proof of vacuity. Two families of discriminating input exist. SHAPE ONE: the unsafe rule is the only thing closing a negative cycle, so a safe-only peel sees no cycle, does not stall, and ACCEPTS a program boot.rofl refuses - because dep_neg comes from concludes plus premise_neg, which reflect the program TEXT and know nothing about safety. SHAPE TWO: the unsafe rule's own negative dependency is what places its round; drop its rule and it becomes base, its negator wakes one round early, and the unfolding at the call site reads a relation that has not finished growing. THE INGREDIENT THE EARLIER PROBE WAS MISSING, and it explains why the gap looked closed: triggerRels is built from POSITIVE premises only (src/engine.ts:164-166), so propagate never re-wakes a rule because the relation it NEGATES grew. A negation rule fires in its round and is never reconsidered - THE ROUND ASSIGNMENT IS THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES IT FIRE LATE ENOUGH, which is exactly why the constraint is load-bearing and exactly why an oracle that never sees a too-early firing sees nothing. Four probes, three discriminating and one control that deliberately does not, so the difference is not an artefact of naming or ordering: D1 splits REJECTED from ACCEPTED, D2 gives aout {1,3} against {}, D4 gives zout {1,2,3} against {1}. Test at test/round-eval-demand.test.ts, red when the constraint is dropped. runtime/round_eval.ts never edited, sha256 identical at start and end.").

finding_note(f_the_stratum_table_is_writable_by_any_rule, "CORRECTED THE SAME DAY by an executor's measurement, and the correction changes the decision rather than the fact. The forged rows DO land with zero audit signal - breach, forged, malformed and undefined_premise are all 0 in every configuration measured - so that half stands. But UNDER THE PRESENT boot.rofl THE FORGERY STEERS NOTHING: readStrata takes a MAX, and boot's own stratum(Rel, N) :- dep_neg(Rel, Q), stratum(Q, M), N is M + 1 carries a forged mid=9 up into out=10, so a forger can raise a relation but never past its own dependents. Measured with two ordinary rules and no $ at all: with boot.rofl loaded the stock evaluator answers out={2}, which is correct and means the forgery is DEFEATED. The hole opens only where the counting rule is absent - without boot.rofl, or under the THINNED boot - where stock answers out={1,2,3}, three rows where one is right. Rounds answer {2} in every row, boot or no boot, fat or thin. So the honest statement is not 'the table is exploitable today' but 'thinning boot under the stock evaluator opens it, and rounds close it in every configuration' - which makes rounds a PRECONDITION of the thinning rather than an independent benefit of it. I had told the owner the stronger version; this is the measured one.").

finding(f_the_demos_are_unchanged_except_one_oracle_pinned_to_wording, insight).
recorded(f_the_demos_are_unchanged_except_one_oracle_pinned_to_wording, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_demos_are_unchanged_except_one_oracle_pinned_to_wording, demo).
finding_note(f_the_demos_are_unchanged_except_one_oracle_pinned_to_wording, "Asked whether the DEMOS behave identically under the round evaluator - a different artefact from the test suite, which asserts specific things rather than reproducing output - all 21 were run under both evaluators and diffed byte for byte, the stock arm reachable only because the old path was kept behind the `evaluator` flag. Sixteen are byte-identical. Four differ ONLY in clock-derived text: milliseconds, load averages, a ratio of copy costs printed as 2.5x against 2.2x, a wall clock in seconds, and a source digest that moved because src/ moved. The diff tool was controlled in both directions - a file against itself compares equal, a file against another compares different - and the timing filter was controlled for not swallowing content, 313 lines in, 312 out. ONE DEMO DIFFERS IN SUBSTANCE, examples/npc, and the whole difference is the WORDING OF THE REFUSAL: rounds say 'round 4 settled nothing while at, does, knows, saw, settled remained' where stock says 'unstratified[main](at), unstratified[main](does)'. The round refusal names FIVE relations where the stock one names TWO, and the extra three are genuinely uncomputable - not on the cycle, but negating something that never settles - which is exactly the trade predicted when rounds were proposed: the refusal gains reach into what cannot be computed and loses the reach trace that showed why. npc's oracle compares the refusal text verbatim, so it flips AGREE to DISAGREE and the demo exits 1. THE ORACLE IS WHAT NEEDS UPDATING, not the evaluator: it pins the older and less informative sentence. Everything else in npc's 26 diff lines is ticks-per-second, which is the clock again.").

finding(f_line_ranged_scanners_tax_every_kernel_edit, pitfall).
recorded(f_line_ranged_scanners_tax_every_kernel_edit, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_line_ranged_scanners_tax_every_kernel_edit, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_line_ranged_scanners_tax_every_kernel_edit, "Three scanners classify src/engine.ts BY LINE RANGE - engine_split.ts with 42 blocks and 84 hardcoded numbers, plus bootstrap_dag.ts and policy_ladder.ts reading from it - and each block carries an anchor that MUST STAND AT LINE `from`, so a moved file fails loudly instead of counting the wrong lines. That guard is correct and is this project's own RULE 2 applied: a line-ranged classification of a file that shifted underneath is not a measurement. THE COST IS THAT LINE NUMBERS ARE THE KEY AND THE ANCHOR IS ONLY THE CHECK, so ANY edit to the kernel shifts everything below it and costs ELEVEN TEST FAILURES that say nothing about whether the edit was right - measured today, an eleven-line seam that broke nothing cost exactly that. THE REPAIR IS TO SWAP KEY AND CHECK: find each block by searching for its anchor text as the FIRST match at-or-after the previous block's resolved start, derive `from` from where it lands and `to` from the next block's start. Ordered search means position disambiguates duplicates, so the eight anchors that are not unique in the file (`try {` twice, `for (;;) {` five times, `solveBody(` three times and so on) need no editing at all. MEASURED, with a control in both directions: ordered search resolves 42 of 42 blocks and agrees with the hardcoded number on 41; the single disagreement is `renameClause(`, whose CALL at 1031 precedes its definition at 1061, and which needs its anchor made specific as `private renameClause(`. The control inserted eleven lines at position 120 and re-resolved: the ordered search FOLLOWED the shift for 40 of 42 blocks while the hardcoded numbers would have been wrong for 38. The tiling control survives unchanged, because contiguity is now by construction, and the anchor check becomes 'found exactly once in its window' which still says no when an anchor disappears or duplicates. Roughly twenty lines of work, and it removes a fixed toll from every remaining task: the policy pre-pass, mandatory authorship and both open defects all touch the kernel and would each pay it again.").

-- ============ the queue, as facts =====-- `blocks(A, B)` — A must be settled before B is briefed. Until now the order
-- of work lived in my head and in the conversation: the ledger recorded WHAT
-- was found and WHETHER it was settled, and said nothing about WHAT IS NEXT.
-- Vadim asked whether the queue was in the ledger; it was not. Written as
-- facts, the order is derivable rather than narrated, it carries a why, and a
-- circular queue would be refused by the same machinery that refuses a
-- circular program.
edb(blocks).

finding(f_authorship_is_optional_and_that_is_the_hole, defect).
recorded(f_authorship_is_optional_and_that_is_the_hole, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_authorship_is_optional_and_that_is_the_hole, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_authorship_is_optional_and_that_is_the_hole, "DECIDED by the owner: authorship becomes mandatory. Two measured holes, both found by the executor that lifted the $ ban. FIRST, factMetaFacts emits asserted_by ONLY IF `who` was supplied, so an anonymous writer leaves no authorship trace at all - the same forgery that gives forged[audit] one row under who=mallory gives breach 0, forged 0 and empty diagnostics under no who, caught by nobody. A net that sees only those who introduced themselves is a polite request rather than a net, and `who` is set by the caller, who is also the forger. SECOND, $kernel is an ordinary string: authority(main, $kernel) exists, so a writer claiming to be the kernel passes straight through `not authority(P, Who)` leaving nothing behind. The repair named and not yet done: require asserted_by for EVERY fact so that having no author stops being a way to be invisible, and take $kernel out of the value space `who` can be given.").

finding(f_stratum_of_arity_one_crashes_the_host, defect).
recorded(f_stratum_of_arity_one_crashes_the_host, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_stratum_of_arity_one_crashes_the_host, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_stratum_of_arity_one_crashes_the_host, "Found in passing while measuring the second door and not chased: a program writing stratum/1 - the same relation name at the wrong arity - brings the host down with a TypeError rather than a diagnostic. No $ is needed for it. A malformed program must be REFUSED, with a sentence naming what is wrong; a host crash is neither an answer nor a refusal, and it is the one outcome the three-cell taxonomy of this session calls unacceptable in a kernel. Unmeasured beyond the single observation: whether other reserved relation names crash the same way at the wrong arity, which is the first thing to check when this is picked up.").

-- current order of work, and the reason each edge exists
blocks(f_the_stratum_table_is_writable_by_any_rule, f_line_ranged_scanners_tax_every_kernel_edit).
blocks(f_the_stratum_table_is_writable_by_any_rule, f_authorship_is_optional_and_that_is_the_hole).
blocks(f_line_ranged_scanners_tax_every_kernel_edit, f_a_rule_has_two_faces_and_the_audit_reads_the_wrong_one).
blocks(f_line_ranged_scanners_tax_every_kernel_edit, f_stratum_of_arity_one_crashes_the_host).

finding_note(f_expressible_is_not_portable_the_discriminator_is_when, "STALE AS OF THE SAME DAY, on both halves, and the correction is the point rather than the arithmetic. The finding says a native core would need 486 mechanism plus 71 plumbing, about 557 lines, PLUS 107 policy lines that cannot leave FOR REASONS OF TIMING RATHER THAN EXPRESSIVENESS. Measured since: THIRTY-ONE of those 107 - the range-restriction analysis, which this finding names as the expensive tier - are expressible after all and are now WRITTEN, five rules walking the reflection by position, agreeing with the host oracle Evaluation.rules[].safe set for set with zero on either side, and saying MORE than the host, `dangling #2 \"Z\"` rather than a flag. The obstacle was never expressiveness or timing: it was that $ could not be typed, so the reflection was unreadable from the language that emits it. TEN MORE of the 107, the MAX over stratum/2, are not reached AT ALL under the round evaluator - measured by an ablation counter reading 0 under rounds and nonzero under strata on the same tripwire - so they are dead code on the primary path rather than policy that cannot move. The file has also grown to 778 code lines from the 761 the finding measured. WHAT SURVIVES, and it is the transferable half: when asking whether a decision can move from code into data, the question is not whether it is expressible but WHEN ITS ANSWER IS NEEDED. That still holds and is still the thing worth carrying. What does not hold is the count, and the count is what anyone sizing a port would read. REQUEUE: the port cannot be sized until the policy pre-pass is decided and the thinning has landed, because both change what has to be rewritten - sizing it now measures code that is about to be deleted.").

finding(f_the_enox_write_never_happened, question).
recorded(f_the_enox_write_never_happened, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_enox_write_never_happened, doc).
finding_note(f_the_enox_write_never_happened, "The graph memory was unreachable for the whole of this session, so nothing measured today reached it: the round evaluator, the $ ban lifted, the second door, the two faces of a rule, the arithmetic being what breaks termination rather than negation - all of it lives in facts/findings.rofl and in ~/.claude/state/learn-from-mistakes.last, which are LOCAL. Named zero times in the ledger before this entry, which is the shape of the problem: a debt recorded only in the tool that was down is a debt nobody will find. What has to travel, in order of weight: that a level counted as a number diverges while the same level shaped as a pair does not, because arithmetic is the one place Datalog stops being finite by construction; that a schedule can be PEELED before anything fires instead of DERIVED inside the fixpoint, and that this removes ten rules and 44.8 percent of the corpus's steps; and the two process lessons, that an executor's report does not reach the lead as text so a negative result must be asked for as a FILE, and that my errors this whole day were claims of ABSENCE while not one claim of MAGNITUDE failed.").

finding(f_reading_the_reflection_needs_a_subterm_walker, idea).
recorded(f_reading_the_reflection_needs_a_subterm_walker, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_reading_the_reflection_needs_a_subterm_walker, decision).
finding_note(f_reading_the_reflection_needs_a_subterm_walker, "Named by the executor that lifted the $ ban, in its own words: the reflection is readable now, but reading it EXHAUSTIVELY still wants `subterm/2`, because a general walker over structure needs one descent rule per functor shape. It cost that executor five rules - into $cons in the tail and in the argument, into $lit, into $builtin, into $var - and it found them the honest way: the walker first flagged its OWN rules as unsafe, and rules were added until the disagreement with the host oracle reached zero. That is a working method and a poor foundation: every new functor shape the kernel emits will silently fall outside the walk, and the failure mode is UNDER-reporting, which is the quiet kind. A `subterm(Whole, Part)` primitive - or the ability to write one rule polymorphic in the functor - would make the walk total instead of enumerated. THE TENSION TO SETTLE, and it is why this is a decision: a fully general subterm relation over a term with n nodes derives n squared pairs, and the store's cost per fact is already the identified ceiling, so totality here is bought with memory. A narrowed form - subterm at a named depth, or descent only into relations the program actually reads - may be the answer, and 'the reified read is affordable when narrowed' is already a separate open finding pointing the same way.").

blocks(f_policy_cannot_be_a_table_because_it_decides_who_runs, f_expressible_is_not_portable_the_discriminator_is_when).
blocks(f_the_stratum_table_is_writable_by_any_rule, f_expressible_is_not_portable_the_discriminator_is_when).

-- ============ witnesses: a finding's premise, made runnable =====-- `witness(F, Query, Rows)` — the finding rests on this query having had this
-- many rows. `witness_atleast(F, Query, N)` for a floor. A witness measures a
-- CONSEQUENCE and never the code: a file hash flips on a reformat and a line
-- number flips on any edit above it, while a query flips when the CLAIM does.
-- Cheap by design - a witness is an ordinary query, so rewriting one is
-- rewriting a query, not learning a notation.
edb(witness).
edb(witness_atleast).

-- the reflection carries the head, with its arguments in order. This is what
-- made tier 0 expressible, and it is false the day conclusion_lit stops
-- carrying $lit.
witness_atleast(f_the_whole_reflection_is_readable_except_one_character, "conclusion_lit(R, K, L)", 1).

-- the queue is derived rather than narrated, and it is acyclic
-- WAS: blocked_by(...pre-pass...) = 1, and it went STALE within the hour when
-- the decoupling removed that edge. The witness caught its own author, unasked.
-- Now the premise is the decoupling itself: the pre-pass is READY.
witness(f_a_declared_dirty_scanner_is_a_dated_loan, "ready(f_policy_cannot_be_a_table_because_it_decides_who_runs)", 1).

-- the audits boot.rofl requires of every store
witness(f_a_rule_has_two_faces_and_the_audit_reads_the_wrong_one, "breach[audit](R)", 0).

-- DELIBERATELY STALE, and it is the proof the mechanism can say no. This
-- finding's own note says the count moved: it was written when the ledger
-- carried far fewer, and the number is quoted here as it stood.

-- NOT WITNESSED, and the limitation is worth stating rather than hiding: the
-- port-sizing finding rests on LINE COUNTS of src/engine.ts, which are not
-- facts in any store, so no query can watch them. Witnesses over the store
-- cover claims about the MODEL; a claim about the HOST needs a scanner to
-- assert its measurement as a fact first. The scanners already measure these
-- numbers - engine_split prints them every run - so the extension is to have
-- them WRITE rather than print, and it is the natural next step rather than a
-- new mechanism.


finding(f_the_split_scanner_prints_where_it_should_assert, insight).
recorded(f_the_split_scanner_prints_where_it_should_assert, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_split_scanner_prints_where_it_should_assert, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_split_scanner_prints_where_it_should_assert, "Vadim named the missing piece and it collapses three separate problems into one: the host is code, and facts about code are what a SCANNER produces - which is the declared job of the scanners/ layer in this repository's own CLAUDE.md. Measured across all eight files there: every one emits facts EXCEPT scanners/engine_split.ts, which has five console.log calls and zero fact emissions. It is misfiled by behaviour, living in the layer whose purpose is turning code into facts while turning code into printed text. THE THREE PROBLEMS ARE ONE. The hand-made MECH/POL classification has no home in the code, so it is kept as a table beside it; the table needs a key and takes LINE NUMBERS, the most volatile property code has, which is why every kernel edit costs eleven test failures; and a witness cannot reach a host measurement, because 491 of 778 lines is printed rather than asserted and no query can watch a printout. All three dissolve in the same move: have the scanner ASSERT `block(engine_ts, matchPremise, mech)` instead of printing a ratio. Then line numbers are not needed at all - the facts ARE the classification, keyed by method name, which is where bootstrap_dag already keeps it in methodBlock - and the port-sizing premise becomes an ordinary witness, `witness(f_port, \"block(engine_ts, M, mech)\", 491)`, so its going stale becomes derivable rather than noticed by hand four times in a day. This SUPERSEDES the repair I proposed an hour ago, ordered anchor search, which was measured and works but fixes only the symptom: it makes the table cheaper to maintain instead of removing the need for a table.").

witness_atleast(f_the_split_scanner_prints_where_it_should_assert, "witness(F, Q, N)", 1).

finding_note(f_the_split_scanner_prints_where_it_should_assert, "CORRECTED WITHIN THE HOUR, by the owner, and the correction is the whole point rather than a detail. I proposed that the scanner assert `block(engine_ts, matchPremise, mech)` - which puts a JUDGEMENT into the scanner. `mech` is not a fact about a file, it is a conclusion. A scanner says what is in the file and how the pieces connect; the semantics belong in rules, and the scanner is extended only when a needed relation is missing. AND THE SCANNER IS ALREADY BUILT THAT WAY, which I had not checked: scanners/js.ts emits src_file, src_func, src_class, src_method, src_import, src_export, src_call and src_parse_error - pure structure, zero judgement - into a [code] perspective whose authority(code, scanner) means anyone else writing there is flagged by forged[audit]. So the layer is separated already and already guarded. The repair is therefore smaller than I said: EXTEND the scanner with what it lacks (field reads and writes, which bootstrap_dag computes as 1106 accesses and does not emit, plus per-method size) and write the rule pack that does not exist, where mech and policy are DERIVED. THE DEEPER OBSERVATION IS THE OWNER'S AND IT IS AN IDENTITY, NOT AN ANALOGY: reflect.ts turns RULES into structural facts and boot.rofl derives the judgements over them; js.ts turns CODE into structural facts and nothing derives judgements over those. The same two-layer discipline on two subjects - the reflector never judges, the model always does - and for code the second half was simply never written. A consequence worth chasing: if the shape is one, some rules are shared, because 'unreachable' is the same question over src_call as over dep, and so are cycles, layers and dead code.").

finding(f_i_applied_the_principle_to_others_code_not_my_own_proposal, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_applied_the_principle_to_others_code_not_my_own_proposal, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_i_applied_the_principle_to_others_code_not_my_own_proposal, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_i_applied_the_principle_to_others_code_not_my_own_proposal, "In one evening I moved three decisions out of the host and into the model - the $ ban became an audit rule, the phase schedule became a peel, the work queue became facts - and stated the principle each time in my own words: knowledge that lives where it cannot be asked is the defect. Within the hour I proposed putting a judgement INTO A SCANNER, which is the same defect with me as its author. The owner caught it; I did not. THIS IS THE SECOND INSTANCE OF ONE CLASS TODAY. The first was recorded this morning as f_a_brief_requirement_must_bind_its_author_same_session, where I wrote a requirement into an executor's brief and violated it in my own probe an hour later. The class is not inattention and not the absence-claim class from the earlier segment: it is that I APPLY A PRINCIPLE TO THE ARTEFACT IN FRONT OF ME AND NOT TO MY OWN NEXT PROPOSAL. A second instance means the written rule is not a gate, because a rule I wrote twice and broke twice is a formulation rather than a mechanism. WHAT A GATE WOULD LOOK LIKE, and it is cheap: any proposal of mine that places a decision anywhere - in host code, in a scanner, in a table, in a brief - must name WHERE IT CAN BE ASKED FROM. If the answer is 'nowhere, you read the source', the placement is wrong by the principle I keep stating. That question is one line and applies before the work rather than after the correction. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a third instance whose cause is demonstrably something else - a misread of the artefact rather than a failure to turn the principle on myself - which would make this a coincidence of two rather than a class.").

addressed_by(f_the_stratum_table_is_writable_by_any_rule, "boot.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_schedule_is_peeled_not_derived, "src/rounds.ts").

finding(f_the_saving_is_the_meta_layer_not_the_scheduler, insight).
recorded(f_the_saving_is_the_meta_layer_not_the_scheduler, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_the_saving_is_the_meta_layer_not_the_scheduler, doc).
finding_note(f_the_saving_is_the_meta_layer_not_the_scheduler, "The ten rules are deleted, boot.rofl went 147 lines to 161 (the removal is explained in place), and the full suite is 763 pass 0 fail at 197 s. THREE ARMS WERE MEASURED WHERE I ASKED FOR TWO, and the third arm sharpens the result: fat boot with strata is 107 235 steps over 34 programs, fat boot with ROUNDS is 107 235 - identical on every program, step for step - and thin boot with rounds is 59 184, my predicted 44.8 percent to the digit. SO THE SCHEDULER BOUGHT NOTHING AND THE DELETION BOUGHT EVERYTHING: rounds are the PRECONDITION of the thinning rather than a saving in themselves, which is exactly what the forgery measurement had already implied and is worth stating in the opposite direction too - on the thin boot the STOCK evaluator stops defending the stratum table and stops refusing negative cycles, so the two changes are one change. wtf falls 11 125 to 1 917 (82.8 percent), spat 4 136 to 263 (93.6), and THREE PROGRAMS GO TO ZERO STEPS. 33 of 34 differ in exactly one line, flow[main](main,main), support falling by exactly ten every time, verified by extracting and deduplicating every differing line across all 34 rather than by sampling.").

finding(f_editing_the_spec_to_match_the_code_destroys_the_measurement, insight).
recorded(f_editing_the_spec_to_match_the_code_destroys_the_measurement, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_editing_the_spec_to_match_the_code_destroys_the_measurement, doc).
finding_note(f_editing_the_spec_to_match_the_code_destroys_the_measurement, "My brief told the executor to edit README, START.md and LIMITS.md 'substantively rather than cosmetically'. It refused START.md and was right. Its reasoning, which is better than my instruction: START.md has exactly ONE commit in the history - the original spec - and facts/spec.rofl measures the implementation AGAINST it, so editing the contract to match the code destroys the only thing that can show a deviation. The project already carries the mechanism for this and the executor named the precedent: README section 6 is a paragraph on every place the implementation deviated, and s_appendix_dep_edges is the exact prior case, where START.md:160 still carries dep/2 rules the implementation stopped having, amended from the README. So the rewrite went into the deviation register plus six amends/qualifies links, and START.md's checksum is byte-identical to where it started. THE GENERAL FORM: a specification is the FIXED POINT OF COMPARISON, and a fixed point that moves to match what it measures is no longer measuring. This is the same shape as the day's other lesson about witnesses - measure the consequence, never the thing you are about to change - and it applies to my own briefs, which is where the error was.").

finding(f_a_language_model_needs_its_coverage_audit_first, decision).
recorded(f_a_language_model_needs_its_coverage_audit_first, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_a_language_model_needs_its_coverage_audit_first, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_a_language_model_needs_its_coverage_audit_first, "Vadim braked a step I was about to take lightly. Writing normalizing rules over raw AST facts is not a small next task: it is BUILDING A MODEL OF JS/TS SEMANTICS, and its failure mode is silent under-reporting - a rule for 'who calls whom' that misses a call shape returns a smaller answer that looks correct. The precedent is from the same evening: the executor that lifted the $ ban wrote a term walker that first flagged ITS OWN rules as unsafe, and rules were added until the disagreement with the host oracle reached zero. That worked only because an oracle existed. For JS there is none. MEASURED, and the numbers set the shape of the protection. Over 150 files and 319,448 nodes our corpus uses 108 DISTINCT NODE KINDS, thirteen of them five times or fewer - and that tail is where a miss is quiet: ImportExpression is in it, so a call-graph rule handling only CallExpression drops every dynamic import and no test corpus would show it. But @babel/types declares 252 CONCRETE TYPES, so 144 kinds exist that we have never parsed, and an audit built over the CORPUS would report full coverage while the model is blind to more than half the language. THE PROTECTION IS THEREFORE BUILDABLE AND CHEAP, because the vocabulary is enumerable and the parser enumerates it: assert node_kind(K) for all 252 from @babel/types, declare handled(K, Rule) or ignored(K, Reason) per kind, and require unaccounted[audit](K) :- node_kind(K), not handled(K, _), not ignored(K, _) to be EMPTY. Today it would be 252 - all of them - which is the honest starting point: the audit says nothing has been modelled, and each kind moves across one at a time WITH A REASON. Same discipline as this ledger, where silence is not allowed and a finding is either addressed or dismissed with a reason. A SECOND LAYER, per node rather than per kind: a node of a handled kind that yields no fact is itself a finding, which catches the case where CallExpression counts as handled while the rule misses obj?.method(). ORDER: the coverage audit FIRST, the first normalizing rule second. The other way round, the first rule looks like it works and the price arrives a month later.").


finding(f_grafema_already_solved_the_language_model_problem, insight).
recorded(f_grafema_already_solved_the_language_model_problem, "2026-08-31").
demands(f_grafema_already_solved_the_language_model_problem, decision).
finding_note(f_grafema_already_solved_the_language_model_problem, "Vadim named grafema DSL and grounding it changed the plan. THE ROFL LEDGER CONTAINS ZERO MENTIONS OF GRAFEMA - not in findings, not in docs - which is exactly why I walked into rebuilding, from scratch and badly, a thing that exists next door in production. Grounded from local memory (Enox was down all session): packages/lang-spec is an LLM pipeline corpus -> annotate -> VOCABULARY -> classify-edges -> generate-plugin emitting rule-table.json, where VOCABULARY is defined as the human-reviewed approved node and edge types. That is precisely the problem I measured an hour earlier as 108 corpus node kinds against 252 that babel declares, and there it is a pipeline STAGE rather than a thing to invent. The EDGE-PHASE MODEL is walk / post-file / post-project, derived deterministically from an edge's profile - crossFile to post-project, siblingNodes to post-file, else walk - which is stratification BY WHERE THE DERIVATION RUNS, structurally the same question this whole evening was about, one level up. And the design thesis states the correction Vadim gave me an hour ago as a principle: extracted structure (AST/CONTAINS) is concrete syntax, PLAIN FACTS AND NOT ABSTRACT INTERPRETATION, and only the DERIVED layer is. Also present: .dl rule-packs with depends.dl migrated and proven equivalent on the real corpus at 14.2x, semiring tags as precision/confidence, and coverage-as-demand-diff through why-not. THE TWO PROJECTS HAVE CONVERGED INDEPENDENTLY. What ROFL carries that Grafema does not: rules-as-facts, provenance with why-trees, perspectives as ledgers, the third value EARNED rather than asserted, ticks. What Grafema carries that ROFL does not: the language vocabulary as a pipeline stage, the phase model, a Rust engine, a corpus at scale. And Vadim's phrase - bridging between PERSPECTIVES - is not a metaphor here: two models of one subject, each in its own ledger, with the question of what may cross and on whose declaration, which is the machinery leak[audit] and collects were built for today. THE DECISION IS WHETHER TO BRIDGE OR TO BORROW, and it should not be taken tonight. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THE CONVERGENCE CLAIM: reading _ai/research/datalog-unified-engine-design.md and semantic-graph-as-abstract-interpretation.md and finding the engines answer different questions rather than the same one differently.").


finding(f_a_declared_dirty_scanner_is_a_dated_loan, decision).
recorded(f_a_declared_dirty_scanner_is_a_dated_loan, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_declared_dirty_scanner_is_a_dated_loan, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_declared_dirty_scanner_is_a_dated_loan, "DECIDED by the owner, and it is what keeps a research programme off an engineering critical path. Modelling a language from its AST - see docs/modelling-a-language.md - is a large open programme with an unsolved oracle question. The kernel's minimisation and a native port are neither: they are measured, queued, and were about to be blocked behind it because the scanner they need would have been the programme's first deliverable. TWO WAYS OUT, both legitimate. First, derive from the AST only what is needed now: a scanner answering ONE question completely is not a partial language model, it is a complete answer to one question. Second, and this is the decision, A SCANNER CONTAMINATED WITH SEMANTICS IS ALLOWED PROVIDED THE CONTAMINATION IS DECLARED IN THE ARTEFACT as something that goes away when the language model exists. An undeclared shortcut is debt; a declared one is a dated loan. WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE IS NOT CLEANLINESS BUT QUERYABILITY: a declared contamination is a ROW somewhere, rows can be counted, and the count reaching zero is what 'the language model is built' means measurably. The repository already carries the form - dismissed(F, reason) in this ledger, and the ignored(K, Reason) the audit will use - and the discipline is identical: say what you did and why, so the thing stops being invisible. Three blocking edges were removed when this was recorded, so the port sizing, the policy pre-pass and the split scanner no longer wait on the programme.").

finding(f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task, question).
recorded(f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task, decision).
finding_note(f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task, "Written up as docs/modelling-a-language.md. The owner's aim: model the PROCESS of modelling a language from its AST, so that different languages converge on a common abstract semantics WHERE APPLICABLE, and so that adding a new semantic layer at any moment lights up every place the model still needs describing. THE ONE ARGUMENT THAT MAKES THIS BELONG HERE RATHER THAN ANYWHERE ELSE: adding a layer is ONE FACT. `layer(taint).` plus an unchanged audit rule reports every cell the new layer needs, with no code change at all - which a generated matrix cannot do, because there the layers live in the pipeline. TWO CONSTRAINTS THAT ARE NOT OPTIONAL. The matrix must be DERIVED rather than filled: 252 node kinds by 7 layers is 1764 cells for JavaScript alone and 37 thousand across twenty-one languages, so hand-filling does not solve 'nobody can hold the language model in their head', it renames it to 'nobody can hold the table'. And divergence between languages - Rust lifetimes, Haskell laziness, Lean's dependent types - must be DECLARED with a reason rather than silent. THE MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDES WHETHER THIS IS SCIENCE OR BOOKKEEPING IS THE ORACLE. Every trustworthy piece of work here has had one; when range restriction moved into rules the oracle was the host's own Evaluation.rules[].safe and the two agreed set for set. A language model has none by default, so the rule already recorded applies: if the modelled thing is executable, the oracle is to run it - which means the first layer to model is not the most interesting one but THE ONE WHOSE ORACLE EXISTS. Without that, the matrix becomes a large confident unfalsifiable artefact, which is worse than none. SMALLEST DECISIVE STEP: one layer, two languages, then a third layer added as a single fact - if that lights up the work with no code change the premise holds, and if it does not, two languages is a far cheaper place to learn it than twenty-one.").



finding_note(f_the_split_scanner_prints_where_it_should_assert, "WITNESSED IN ANGER, an hour after the mechanism was built and without anyone looking. The witness recorded against this finding asserted that the policy pre-pass was blocked by the scanner work - one row of blocked_by. Decoupling the research programme from the engineering path removed that edge, and the next witness run reported STALE, 0 rows where 1 was expected, naming the finding whose premise had moved. Nobody was checking; the check found it. That is the two-sided proof the mechanism needed - it had already been shown saying no to a deliberately stale premise and no to a witness naming nothing, and this is the first time it said no about a REAL change, made by its own author, in the same session. The witness was rewritten to the new truth rather than deleted: the premise now is that the pre-pass is READY.").

finding(f_the_matrix_collapses_threefold_and_the_tail_does_not, insight).
recorded(f_the_matrix_collapses_threefold_and_the_tail_does_not, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_matrix_collapses_threefold_and_the_tail_does_not, decision).
finding_note(f_the_matrix_collapses_threefold_and_the_tail_does_not, "The owner's two points - that filling 1764 cells is agent work rather than human work, and that repeated patterns will collapse part of it - are both right, and the collapse is measurable in advance rather than hoped for. MEASURED from babel's own alias declarations: 252 concrete node types fall into 73 equivalence classes by their set of roles; excluding Flow, which is a foreign type system and out of scope, 186 types fall into 63 classes. At seven layers that is 1302 cells by type and 441 BY CLASS - a factor of three, so the work is a long parallel day rather than a swarm of two thousand. BUT 37 OF THE 63 CLASSES HAVE EXACTLY ONE MEMBER - ImportExpression, YieldExpression, TSTypePredicate and their kind - and that tail compresses no further. THREE CONDITIONS WITHOUT WHICH THE SWARM REPRODUCES THE DEBT IT IS MEANT TO AVOID. FIRST, the collapse is a HYPOTHESIS ABOUT BEHAVIOUR and not a proof: TSStringKeyword and TSNumberKeyword share a role set and probably behave identically outside the type layer, but that must be verified per layer by running two members of the class and comparing facts, with a class that disagrees splitting - otherwise the collapse is the same silent under-reporting one storey up. SECOND, one fixture per cell is liveness and a SET is coverage: `if (a) { b() }`, `if (a) b();` and the ternary are three shapes of one cell and a rule can pass the first and miss the rest. THIRD, and this is what decides the cost, 'confirm the rule works as we want' IS NOT AN ORACLE - it is agreement with oneself - unless the fragment is EXECUTED. So the layers split by their oracle: data flow, control flow and the call graph are checkable by running the fragment, scope by whether the compiler resolves a name, types by tsc, modules by the resolver - all one agent each; the STRUCTURE layer has no executable oracle at all, and its only available one is the agreement of TWO INDEPENDENT modellers, where disagreement is worth more than agreement. 441 agents producing 441 confident answers with no cross-check is precisely the shape that produced the tech debt this programme exists to avoid.").

-- The programme has not started: no semantic layer is declared yet. `edb(layer)`
-- is needed for this to be a witness at all - without it the relation does not
-- exist, the query answers 0 with no error, and the guard correctly refuses to
-- read that as satisfied. The day the first layer is declared, this flips.
edb(layer).
witness(f_the_matrix_collapses_threefold_and_the_tail_does_not, "layer(L)", 0).

-- ============ guardrails for the language-modelling loop =====-- Written BEFORE the work, on the owner's instruction and for his reason: a
-- guardrail invented afterwards justifies what was already done. The loop runs
-- hourly at :23; the programme is docs/modelling-a-language.md.
edb(guardrail).
edb(guards).

guardrail(g_oracle_first, "No cell is filled before its ORACLE is named. A layer whose oracle is not executable gets two independent modellers and disagreement is the signal; a layer whose oracle runs gets one. Filling without an oracle produces a large confident unfalsifiable artefact, which is worse than none.").
guardrail(g_collapse_is_a_hypothesis, "The 252-to-63 collapse by alias set is a hypothesis ABOUT BEHAVIOUR. Before a class is treated as one cell, two of its members are run and their facts compared; a class that disagrees SPLITS. Assuming the collapse is the same silent under-reporting the programme exists to prevent, one storey up.").
guardrail(g_one_fixture_is_liveness, "One fixture per cell shows the rule can fire. A SET shows what it covers. `if (a) { b() }`, `if (a) b();` and the ternary are three shapes of one cell.").
guardrail(g_audit_over_babel_not_the_corpus, "Coverage is audited against babel's declared 252 types, never against the 108 the corpus happens to use. A corpus-relative audit reports completeness while the model is blind to 144 kinds, and ImportExpression - dynamic import - is in that blind tail.").
guardrail(g_divergence_is_declared, "Where languages do not converge - Rust lifetimes, Haskell laziness, Lean's dependent types - the divergence is DECLARED with a reason. Silence is not one of the options, exactly as in this ledger.").
guardrail(g_the_matrix_is_derived, "The matrix must be DERIVED, not filled. 1764 hand-filled cells do not solve `nobody can hold the language model in their head`, they rename it to `nobody can hold the table`. What rules cannot decide must be ENUMERABLE.").
guardrail(g_stop_if_it_grows_without_an_oracle, "STOP CONDITION, not advice: if the model starts growing confidently and unfalsifiably, the loop ends. So does it if the night executors return a broken tree, or if the one-fact-per-layer premise is refuted - and the refutation is a RESULT to report, not a failure.").

guards(g_oracle_first, f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task).
guards(g_collapse_is_a_hypothesis, f_the_matrix_collapses_threefold_and_the_tail_does_not).
guards(g_one_fixture_is_liveness, f_the_matrix_collapses_threefold_and_the_tail_does_not).
guards(g_audit_over_babel_not_the_corpus, f_a_language_model_needs_its_coverage_audit_first).
guards(g_divergence_is_declared, f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task).
guards(g_the_matrix_is_derived, f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task).
guards(g_stop_if_it_grows_without_an_oracle, f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task).

witness(f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task, "guardrail(G, T)", 16).

finding(f_the_model_is_an_abstract_interpreter_not_an_interpreter, insight).
recorded(f_the_model_is_an_abstract_interpreter_not_an_interpreter, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_model_is_an_abstract_interpreter_not_an_interpreter, doc).
finding_note(f_the_model_is_an_abstract_interpreter_not_an_interpreter, "The owner's closing thought: a complete language model is an abstract interpreter, and feeding it inputs executes the program while carrying provenance for every conclusion. The frame is right and already established - Grafema's research states the derived graph as declaratively-specified abstract interpretation in Cousot's sense computed as a Datalog fixpoint. TWO PRECISIONS SET THE TARGET, and the loop must take the right one. ABSTRACT INTERPRETATION IS NOT EXECUTION: Cousot's construction runs over an ABSTRACT domain, yielding `x is positive` rather than `x is 7`. Substituting concrete inputs and running is CONCRETE interpretation, and it walks straight into the wall measured this same day - a loop counter is `N is M + 1`, the single place a Datalog fixpoint stops being finite by construction, so THE MODELLED PROGRAM'S NON-TERMINATION BECOMES THE MODEL'S. That is a property and not an obstacle to route around. THE ABSTRACT READING IS WHAT A FIXPOINT IS GOOD AT, because the domain is finite or carries a widening - which is Cousot's trade, precision for termination. So the target is: decide PROPERTIES of JS programs over a finite abstract domain, with a why-tree per conclusion and an honest UNDEFINED where the domain cannot distinguish. THREE THINGS MAKE THAT MORE THAN A DEBUGGER AND TWO ALREADY EXIST HERE: a debugger reports the state while a why-tree reports why the state is what it is, down to axioms; whynot answers why something is NOT the case, which no debugger asks; and the third value is EARNED - every static analyser has a cannot-determine case and almost none say WHY, whereas an UNDEFINED carrying the firing that derived it names the exact place the domain lost a distinction. Semirings add precision and confidence travelling with the conclusion, which Grafema names as an intention and which is running code here. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a finite abstract domain rich enough to be useful on real JS whose fixpoint does NOT terminate, which would mean the trade is not available at this scale and the target has to shrink again.").

finding(f_the_language_model_exists_and_the_prize_is_the_desugaring, insight).
recorded(f_the_language_model_exists_and_the_prize_is_the_desugaring, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_language_model_exists_and_the_prize_is_the_desugaring, decision).
finding_note(f_the_language_model_exists_and_the_prize_is_the_desugaring, "Asked directly whether the language-modelling programme has real novelty, and the answer is checked rather than recalled: NO, and the thing is ten years old. KJS (Park, Stefanescu, Rosu, PLDI 2015) is a COMPLETE formal semantics of JavaScript in the K framework, executable, passing all 2782 core tests of the ECMAScript 5.1 conformance suite; its authors then found the SUITE incomplete, wrote more tests, and used them to find bugs in V8, WebKit and SpiderMonkey. K carries the same for C, Java, Python, Rust, EVM and x86-64. Kythe covers the other half from around 2012 - a language-agnostic graph schema with per-language indexers, which is the cross-language convergence already shipped. TAKING A K SEMANTICS WHOLE DOES NOT WORK, AND THE MIDDLE REASON IS A CATEGORY DIFFERENCE RATHER THAN A DIFFICULTY: a complete execution semantics is Turing-complete of necessity and a stratified fixpoint is not, which is the same wall measured here on `N is M + 1` and which no amount of work crosses. Format is the second reason (K is rewriting logic, adopting it costs the zero-dependency cell) and purpose the third (K answers what a program DOES; the target is what its structure IMPLIES). WHAT IS TAKEABLE IS WORTH MORE THAN THE VERDICT. The case analysis - every syntactic form and what it does - is the matrix's first column filled by people rigorous enough to find engine bugs. THE DESUGARING CORE IS THE PRIZE: K semantics reduce a language to a small core with the reduction EXPLICIT, which is the collapse this programme predicted but JUSTIFIED rather than guessed - the alias estimate of 252 kinds to 63 classes is a hypothesis about behaviour, while a desugaring is a proof that one form unfolds into another, checked against a conformance suite. And test262 is usable as an oracle independently of K: 2782 tests from the standards body beat any fixture set written overnight. CONSEQUENCE, and it redirects the loop: DO NOT INVENT FIXTURES. The first move is to find out whether test262 can be consumed here and whether a published desugaring replaces the guessed collapse - both lookups, both cheaper than one cell of hand-written fixture work. WHAT WOULD REFUTE: a published desugaring that does not survive contact with a real corpus, or test262 proving unusable outside a JS engine, either of which puts the fixture work back on the table.").

guardrail(g_do_not_invent_what_can_be_looked_up, "Before writing a fixture, a taxonomy or a collapse, spend one lookup on whether it exists. Measured 2026-09-01: the complete JS model, its oracle and its justified desugaring all existed, and the programme was an hour from inventing worse versions of all three.").
guards(g_do_not_invent_what_can_be_looked_up, f_the_language_model_exists_and_the_prize_is_the_desugaring).

addressed_by(f_line_ranged_scanners_tax_every_kernel_edit, "scanners/engine_split.ts").

finding(f_a_gate_that_fires_on_movement_is_aimed_at_the_wrong_event, insight).
recorded(f_a_gate_that_fires_on_movement_is_aimed_at_the_wrong_event, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_gate_that_fires_on_movement_is_aimed_at_the_wrong_event, doc).
finding_note(f_a_gate_that_fires_on_movement_is_aimed_at_the_wrong_event, "The scanner now asserts instead of printing and the tax is gone, but the interesting part is HOW it was proved gone, because all-green under mutation reads identically to `the tests stopped checking`. The executor built a DISCRIMINATING PAIR on one file: eleven COMMENT lines inserted - the file merely moves, which is not a classification event - gives 0 red; ONE NEW METHOD at class level, compiling - which IS an event, a unit of code nobody classified - gives 4 red, naming it: `block solveBody absorbs [... brandNewProbe], declared [...] - an unclassified definition inherits a category nobody chose`. THE NEW GUARD IS BETTER AIMED THAN THE ONE IT REPLACES: the line pin fired on MOVEMENT, which is not an event and happens on every edit, while this fires on a NEW UNIT OF CODE, which is an event and happens exactly once. Measured before and after on the same probe: 8 failures to 0, with 15 passing tests becoming 27. AND IT CORRECTED MY NUMBER - I had quoted 11 failures from the previous executor's report; two independent counters agree it is 8. ALSO RECORDED, AND IT IS THE RULE 2 SHAPE: the executor's FIRST counter-control inserted the method inside prepare()'s body where `private` is illegal, the import crashed and all three files went red - red for the wrong reason - and it wrote that down because from the outside a syntax error and a working gate look identical.").

finding(f_i_used_ampersand_instead_of_the_background_flag_twice, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_used_ampersand_instead_of_the_background_flag_twice, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_i_used_ampersand_instead_of_the_background_flag_twice, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_i_used_ampersand_instead_of_the_background_flag_twice, "Twice tonight I launched a full test run as `( ... ) > log 2>&1 &` INSIDE a Bash call instead of setting run_in_background on the tool. The subshell dies when the tool call returns, so the suite is killed mid-way and the log holds a truncated run with no summary line - 18177 bytes where a complete run is about 59000, zero failures reported because the failure summary never printed. The first time I caught it by the byte count and re-ran correctly; the second time, an hour later, I did it again. A REPEAT IS NOT INATTENTION, IT IS A MISSING GATE, and the gate here is cheap because the tell is mechanical: a completed node run ALWAYS ends with the `# tests / # pass / # fail` summary, so a run whose log lacks that line did not finish, whatever its exit code says. That check belongs in the same breath as reading the result, and `0 failures` from a log with no summary is the same defect as an empty grep read as absence - the emptiness is about the capture, not the subject. SECOND ERROR IN THE SAME MINUTE, worth recording together: I launched that run at all while `authorship` was actively editing boot.rofl, which measures a moving tree - the exact mistake paid for this afternoon when two agents wrote into engine.ts at once. Checking ListAgents before a full run costs one call.").

finding(f_adding_a_layer_really_is_one_fact, insight).
recorded(f_adding_a_layer_really_is_one_fact, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_adding_a_layer_really_is_one_fact, doc).
finding_note(f_adding_a_layer_really_is_one_fact, "The smallest step the programme names as decisive has been run, on the first loop tick, and THE PREMISE HOLDS. Setup deliberately tiny - 13 node kinds across two languages, 8 modelled cells and 4 deliberately ignored ones with reasons, in new files facts/js-kinds.rofl and rules/js-model.rofl - because the question is whether the MECHANISM works, not what any cell contains. MEASURED: with two layers the audit reports 26 cells, 12 covered, 14 unaccounted. Adding `layer(scope).` - ONE FACT, nothing else, rules/js-model.rofl byte-identical by md5 afterwards - takes cells to 39 and unaccounted to 27, both growing by EXACTLY 13, one per node kind. TWO CONTROLS, because growth alone proves nothing. A foreign fact (`node_colour(js, red)`) moves neither count, so it is not that any assertion grows the table. And adding a NODE KIND grows cells by exactly the number of layers, 26 to 28, so the matrix is genuinely two-dimensional and both dimensions work - I had only tested one. WHAT THIS SETTLES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT: the enumeration of work is free, which is the single argument for doing this in a relational language rather than in a generated pipeline. The WORK is not free - 441 cells remain 441 cells. The programme's other unsettled question, the oracle, is untouched by this result. Also derived and not asserted: `converges[audit](Layer, Rule, A, B)` reports 8 rows where two languages are modelled by the same rule in the same layer, so cross-language agreement is a consequence of the facts rather than a claim about them.").

-- (a witness was attempted here and removed: see f_a_witness_has_no_world)

finding(f_a_witness_has_no_world, defect).
recorded(f_a_witness_has_no_world, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_witness_has_no_world, decision).
finding_note(f_a_witness_has_no_world, "The witness mechanism caught its author again, on its first application to a new area, and this time it found a real gap in ITSELF. I recorded `witness(f_adding_a_layer_really_is_one_fact, \"layer(L)\", 2)` from the language-model world, where facts/js-kinds.rofl declares two layers. The checker reported STALE at 0, correctly: it loads boot.rofl plus the ledger and its rules, and NOTHING ELSE, so `layer(L)` is empty there. A WITNESS HAS NO WAY TO SAY WHICH WORLD ITS PREMISE IS ABOUT. Two repairs are possible and they are not equivalent. Loading everything into one world is WRONG - different findings are about different file sets, and merging them changes what a query means rather than widening it. Naming the world in the witness - `witness(F, Files, Query, N)` - is right and cheap. NOT DONE, DELIBERATELY: this is a change to the witness FORMAT, which is a design decision rather than a repair, and it was found at one in the morning during an unattended loop. Extending a mechanism unsupervised because the extension looks obvious is the phrase that precedes trouble, and the ledger already carries two findings from tonight about applying a stated principle to my own next move. The bad witness was removed rather than fudged, and the gap is reported. CONSEQUENCE while it stands: findings about anything other than the kernel-plus-ledger world cannot be witnessed at all, which is most of the language-modelling programme.").

finding(f_the_ignored_escape_is_a_bypass_one_fact_wide, defect).
recorded(f_the_ignored_escape_is_a_bypass_one_fact_wide, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_ignored_escape_is_a_bypass_one_fact_wide, decision).
finding_note(f_the_ignored_escape_is_a_bypass_one_fact_wide, "Found by auditing my own tick rather than by a test. The coverage audit is built on the discipline that SILENCE IS NOT AN OPTION - a cell is derived, or handled, or ignored WITH A REASON - and `ignored(Lang, Kind, Layer, Reason)` is an unbounded door: nothing stops the entire matrix being declared ignored with plausible reasons, whereupon `unaccounted[audit]` goes to zero and the audit reports success. A GATE WHOSE BYPASS COSTS ONE FACT. This is the same class this session kept finding in other people's work, authored by me within the hour of stating the principle. Two repairs suggest themselves and neither is free: cap the ratio, so an audit is only green while ignored cells are a minority - which invents a threshold nobody can justify; or require an ignored cell to name a WITNESS for its irrelevance, the way a dismissed finding names a reason that can be argued with - which is the honest form and costs work per cell. A THIRD READING WORTH HOLDING: perhaps ignored SHOULD be countable rather than gated, so the audit reports two numbers - unaccounted and ignored - and a rising ignored count is visible without being forbidden. That matches how this ledger treats dismissed findings, which are counted and never hidden.").

finding(f_babel_is_not_the_language_and_completeness_is_relative, insight).
recorded(f_babel_is_not_the_language_and_completeness_is_relative, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_babel_is_not_the_language_and_completeness_is_relative, decision).
finding_note(f_babel_is_not_the_language_and_completeness_is_relative, "Surfaced by inverting the question - how would this programme certainly fail - and it sharpens the case for test262 beyond what was written in the programme document. THE COVERAGE AUDIT IS BUILT AGAINST @babel/types AND BABEL IS NOT THE LANGUAGE: it is one parser's model of it, and TypeScript's own compiler carries a different AST with different node kinds. So `complete` under this audit means complete RELATIVE TO A TOOL, not relative to a standard, and the 252 concrete types are babel's taxonomy rather than ECMAScript's. That is not a reason to abandon the audit - a parser-relative completeness is still far better than the corpus-relative one it replaced, which was blind to 144 kinds - but it means the audit can be fully green while the model misses a construct babel folds into another node. test262 audits against the STANDARD and is therefore a different KIND of oracle rather than merely a bigger fixture set. A SECOND FINDING FROM THE SAME INVERSION, from a pre-mortem: the most likely way this programme dies is that a swarm fills the cells, a fraction are subtly wrong, nothing distinguishes them, and the model ends up less trustworthy than the ad-hoc scanners it replaced - BECAUSE AN AD-HOC SCANNER IS VISIBLY INCOMPLETE WHILE A FILLED MATRIX LOOKS FINISHED. A filled matrix is more dangerous than an empty one, and that argues for the oracle harder than any argument about correctness.").

finding(f_the_audit_sizes_ignorance_and_does_not_order_it, question).
recorded(f_the_audit_sizes_ignorance_and_does_not_order_it, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_audit_sizes_ignorance_and_does_not_order_it, decision).
finding_note(f_the_audit_sizes_ignorance_and_does_not_order_it, "The coverage audit reports HOW MUCH is unmodelled and says nothing about WHICH CELL MATTERS. 441 cells of truth are as unusable as 441 cells of ignorance if nothing says where to start. The ledger already solved the analogous problem for its own backlog tonight, deriving a queue from blocks/2 rather than narrating one, so the shape of the answer exists: a cell's priority should be DERIVED - from how often the kind occurs in a real corpus, from whether a layer's oracle is executable, from whether other cells depend on it - rather than assigned. Note the tension with the corpus warning: frequency in OUR corpus is exactly the biased signal that would have hidden ImportExpression, so frequency may order the work while never bounding it.").

addressed_by(f_authorship_is_optional_and_that_is_the_hole, "boot.rofl").
addressed_by(f_authorship_is_optional_and_that_is_the_hole, "src/reflect.ts").
addressed_by(f_stratum_of_arity_one_crashes_the_host, "src/api.ts").

finding(f_an_audit_over_who_records_claims_and_authenticates_nobody, insight).
recorded(f_an_audit_over_who_records_claims_and_authenticates_nobody, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_an_audit_over_who_records_claims_and_authenticates_nobody, doc).
finding_note(f_an_audit_over_who_records_claims_and_authenticates_nobody, "Authorship is now mandatory - every asserted fact carries asserted_by, an author-less one carries $anon, and the whole $ prefix is refused as a `who` value so no caller can name itself a kernel principal. Full suite 781 pass 0 fail at 124 s. EVERY FORK WAS DECIDED BY MEASUREMENT AND EACH PRICE IS STATED. Signing $anon WITHOUT granting it authority was rejected because forged[audit] then reddens on nine facts in a bare boot plus sensors - on the first load of any program in the repository - which is a permanently red gate, abolished within a week, leaving the appearance of one; verified by a mutant that removes the grant and watches `honest corpus green` fail. Refusing the whole $ prefix rather than two names costs nothing, measured: 146 literal `who:` values in the corpus and zero beginning with $. Making `who` a mandatory PARAMETER was rejected on scope - 585 call sites, 450 without it. THE PRICE, STATED WITHOUT SOFTENING AND PINNED BY A TEST NAMED FOR IT: anonymity is now VISIBLE BUT NOT JUDGED. A forger who simply omits `who` gets $anon and forged[audit] zero, AND THIS CANNOT BE CLOSED IN THE KERNEL IN PRINCIPLE, because `who` is set by the caller who is also the forger - so no audit over `who` authenticates anyone, it RECORDS CLAIMS. `KNOWN LIMIT: anonymity is recorded, not judged` exists so that limit cannot be mistaken for coverage. WHO JUDGES IS THE LEDGER, NOT THE KERNEL: demands_authorship(P) is host data declared like imports and collects, and unattributed[audit](F, P) names unsigned facts only in the ledgers that asked - silent by default, red where requested. The executor's reason for that shape is worth keeping verbatim: the alternative was a paragraph in the README, and the README is not audited. A THIRD HOLE FOUND AND DELIBERATELY LEFT: rules are never signed at all, because encodeRule writes reflection facts directly and bypasses the signing path, so `who` does not apply to a rule under any value - rule forgery is attributable only when assembled from reflection FACTS, which is the path measured yesterday. Radius bigger than the task, left open with the reason.").

addressed_by(f_the_ignored_escape_is_a_bypass_one_fact_wide, "rules/js-model.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_ignored_escape_is_a_bypass_one_fact_wide, "CLOSED on the next tick, and the repair was tested by trying to use the bypass. The first version had `covered` include `ignored`, so a cell declared ignored vanished from the audit entirely - declare the matrix ignored with plausible reasons and unaccounted goes to zero, reporting success. THE REPAIR IS THE FORM THIS LEDGER ALREADY USES FOR A DISMISSED FINDING: a waived cell is COUNTED AND VISIBLE, never hidden. Three buckets - modelled, waived, unaccounted - partitioning the matrix, so a waiver MOVES a cell between buckets rather than out of sight. MEASURED: 26 cells split 8 modelled, 4 waived, 14 unaccounted, and the sum control holds at 26. Then the attack: 26 facts declaring every cell ignored gives unaccounted 0 - which would have been success under the old shape - while waived reads 26 OF 26, so the audit still tells the truth and the bypass buys nothing. A THIRD NUMBER FELL OUT OF THAT PROBE AND IS A FINDING OF ITS OWN: 8 cells came back DOUBLE-CLAIMED, both modelled and waived. Under the old shape rule order would have resolved that silently; `double_claimed[audit]` now prints it, because a cell claimed both ways is a contradiction ABOUT THE MODEL rather than a fact of the domain.").

finding(f_a_waiver_needs_an_arguable_reason_not_an_atom, question).
recorded(f_a_waiver_needs_an_arguable_reason_not_an_atom, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_waiver_needs_an_arguable_reason_not_an_atom, decision).
finding_note(f_a_waiver_needs_an_arguable_reason_not_an_atom, "Surfaced by auditing the fix rather than the thing fixed. I closed the ignored-bypass by taking the cheapest of three options I had written down an hour before - count the waivers rather than gate them - because it was cheap AND matched a form the ledger already uses for dismissed findings. THE OPTION I DID NOT TEST is the one I had called the honest form: require a waived cell to name a WITNESS for its irrelevance rather than a reason ATOM. The probe proves it matters: 26 cells were waived with the reason `a_because_i_said_so` and nothing objected, so a REASON IS NOT OBLIGED TO BE ARGUABLE. That is the same weakness `dismissed(F, reason)` had in this ledger before the practice of naming a refuter grew up around it. SO THE BYPASS IS NARROWED, NOT CLOSED: it used to hide a cell, now it leaves the cell visible - but nothing stops waiving everything with reasons of the form `because`. THE HONEST CHARACTER OF THE REPAIR, and it should not be overstated: it works SOCIALLY rather than mechanically. An audit reporting 26 waived of 26 LOOKS bad, so a person will look; it does not make the failure impossible. That is weaker than a gate and worth admitting. The undecided question is whether a waiver should carry something checkable - a fixture that demonstrates the construct genuinely cannot participate in that layer - which costs work per cell and is the only version that would make the audit mechanical rather than merely legible.").

guardrail(g_an_oracle_declares_its_own_coverage, "An oracle is an instrument and an instrument has a blind spot. Before a cell is checked against one, the oracle's OWN coverage is declared: what it observes, and what it cannot see even when the model is wrong. An undeclared oracle turns silent under-reporting into confident agreement - the same defect the matrix exists to prevent, one storey up, and harder to see because agreement feels like evidence.").
guards(g_an_oracle_declares_its_own_coverage, f_modelling_a_language_is_a_programme_not_a_task).

finding(f_an_execution_oracle_sees_reachability_not_edges, insight).
recorded(f_an_execution_oracle_sees_reachability_not_edges, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_an_execution_oracle_sees_reachability_not_edges, doc).
finding_note(f_an_execution_oracle_sees_reachability_not_edges, "First cell taken end to end - scanner to rule to an oracle BY EXECUTION - and the run produced disagreement in BOTH directions, one of which turned out to be a defect in the instrument. Setup: scanners/js_ast.ts emits ast_node / ast_child / ast_name and judges nothing (checked - zero occurrences of dataflow, mech, policy, handled in its output, and an empty source yields 3 facts rather than 0, so the walker demonstrably ran); rules/js-dataflow.rofl models ONE cell, value flow through a declarator initialiser, deliberately blind to assignment expressions and to anything but a bare identifier; the oracle runs the fragment with a tagged string and reports which named variables hold the tag. FOUR FIXTURES, NOT ONE, per the guardrail. Results before correction: one hop AGREE, no flow AGREE, `let a = T; let b = a + \"\"` MISSED a->b which was PREDICTED because the rule does not read binary expressions, and `y->x->z` came back as the model OVER-CLAIMING x->z. THE OVER-CLAIM WAS THE ORACLE'S FAULT, NOT THE MODEL'S: the model is right, x does flow to z, and AN EXECUTION ORACLE OVER FINAL VALUES SEES REACHABILITY AND NOT EDGES - in a chain y to x to z all three variables hold the tag and nothing in the values says who handed it on, so a chain and a fan-out are indistinguishable. My declared coverage said the oracle sees value identity of a primitive at top level; it did NOT say the oracle only ever observes flows FROM the source, and that omission produced a confident disagreement pointing the wrong way. Corrected by restricting the comparison to what the instrument can observe: 3 agree, 1 predicted miss, 0 over-claims, with a control showing the oracle is not vacuous - it reports a flow in 3 of 4 fixtures. THE TRANSFERABLE PART: the guardrail written an hour earlier said an oracle must declare its own coverage, and it was not enough to declare it VAGUELY. What the oracle sees has to be stated sharply enough to predict which disagreements are its own, and the cheapest way to find the missing sentence is a fixture whose answer you already know.").


finding(f_the_alias_collapse_is_refuted_it_is_1_27_not_3, insight).
recorded(f_the_alias_collapse_is_refuted_it_is_1_27_not_3, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_alias_collapse_is_refuted_it_is_1_27_not_3, doc).
finding_note(f_the_alias_collapse_is_refuted_it_is_1_27_not_3, "The guardrail written but never exercised - that the collapse of node kinds into classes is a HYPOTHESIS ABOUT BEHAVIOUR and two members must be run and compared - has now been exercised and it REFUTES the number it was written to guard. The number was mine, measured yesterday from babel's alias sets: 186 concrete types outside Flow falling into 63 classes, hence 441 cells at seven layers rather than 1302. THE TEST WAS BUILT TO DISCRIMINATE RATHER THAN TO CONFIRM: one class where collapse MUST fail - Expression+Standardized, holding AssignmentExpression, CallExpression and ArrayExpression, which share an alias set and nothing else - and one where it must hold, TSBaseType, holding sixteen type keywords. And the comparison used is stronger than the fixture method the guardrail names: babel declares NODE_FIELDS for every type, so comparing FIELD SETS is total rather than sampled. RESULT: Expression+Standardized has twelve members and ELEVEN distinct structural signatures - ArrayExpression is [elements], AssignmentExpression is [left, operator, right], CallExpression is [arguments, callee, typeArguments] - so there is essentially no collapse there at all. TSBaseType has sixteen members and three signatures: fourteen genuinely have NO FIELDS and are interchangeable, while TSLiteralType [literal] and TSTemplateLiteralType [quasis, types] are not. I had predicted that second class would come back unified and called the split a false one in advance; THE SPLIT IS CORRECT AND MY EXPECTATION WAS WRONG, which is the method working rather than failing. THE RECOUNT: 63 alias classes become 146 STRUCTURAL classes, so the collapse is 186 to 146, a factor of 1.27 and not 3, and the matrix is 1022 CELLS AT SEVEN LAYERS RATHER THAN 441. WHY BOTH NUMBERS ARE REAL AND ONLY ONE IS USABLE: an alias set says these play the same syntactic ROLE, a field set says these have the same SHAPE, and a rule that walks the AST cannot walk [elements] and [left, operator, right] alike - so cells, which are filled by walking rules, need the structural measure. The role measure would be the right one for a coarse classifier and is the wrong one here.").

guardrail(g_the_document_outranks_the_frozen_prompt, "A loop's prompt is FROZEN at scheduling time and re-injected every tick; the document it points at is editable. So when they disagree the DOCUMENT WINS, and the tick's first duty is to notice the disagreement rather than to obey the prompt. Paid for on tick 5: the prompt still carries `already measured, do not re-derive: 63 classes, 441 cells`, which tick 4 refuted to 146 classes and 1022 cells using the very guardrail the same prompt mandates. A frozen instruction that says `do not re-derive` is the most dangerous kind of stale fact, because it forbids the check that would catch it.").
guards(g_the_document_outranks_the_frozen_prompt, f_the_alias_collapse_is_refuted_it_is_1_27_not_3).

finding(f_convergence_was_a_tautology_over_my_own_typing, defect).
recorded(f_convergence_was_a_tautology_over_my_own_typing, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_convergence_was_a_tautology_over_my_own_typing, doc).
finding_note(f_convergence_was_a_tautology_over_my_own_typing, "The `converges[audit]` relation, written on tick 1 and described in the programme as cross-language agreement DERIVED rather than asserted, was a tautology over my own keyboard. It read handled(A, _, L, Rule), handled(B, _, L, Rule), A != B and reported EIGHT rows - and ALL FOUR named rules exist nowhere in any file. I had typed the same atom into two handled facts and the relation called it agreement. Worse, the check that found this also caught the one I thought was real: `r_declarator_init_flow`, the cell genuinely run against an oracle on tick 3, is ALSO an undefined name - rules/js-dataflow.rofl defines var_flow and decl_binds, not a rule with that id. THE DEFECT IS THAT A FRAMEWORK BUILT BEFORE ITS CONTENT CANNOT TELL A FILLED CELL FROM A NAMED ONE, because a cell refers to its rule by an ATOM and nothing tied the atom to a rule existing. REPAIRED by requiring evidence: `checked(Lang, Kind, Layer, Rule, Oracle, Fixtures)` is recorded only when a cell has actually been run, `verified[audit]` needs it, and convergence needs verification on BOTH sides. The numbers went from a comfortable lie to an uncomfortable truth - 9 claimed modelled, ONE verified, EIGHT claimed-and-not-shown, and ZERO convergences where there had been eight. Gate proven both ways: planting a second checked fact for py takes verified to 2 and convergence to 2. THE GAP BETWEEN CLAIMING AND SHOWING IS NOW ITS OWN NUMBER AND IT IS EIGHT TO ONE. And the pre-mortem written on tick 1 - that a filled matrix is more dangerous than an empty one because it looks finished - was confirmed on my own work within four hours rather than in prediction: the model would have reported nine cells modelled and eight cross-language agreements, and that would have looked like progress.").

finding(f_the_loop_built_the_instrument_and_cannot_build_the_product, decision).
recorded(f_the_loop_built_the_instrument_and_cannot_build_the_product, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_loop_built_the_instrument_and_cannot_build_the_product, decision).
finding_note(f_the_loop_built_the_instrument_and_cannot_build_the_product, "Measured before building further, because five consecutive ticks had each found a defect in the previous tick's scaffolding and the honest question was whether the loop builds the PRODUCT or polishes the INSTRUMENT. THE RATE SETTLES IT: one cell verified against an oracle in five ticks, which is 0.2 per tick, against 1022 cells for JavaScript alone at the structural measure - 5110 hourly ticks, 213 DAYS CONTINUOUS. The loop cannot produce the model, and knowing that cost one tick rather than a week of them. WHAT THE LOOP DID PRODUCE, and it is the thing that makes the rest possible: the instrument. Five ticks built a coverage audit that partitions the matrix into modelled, waived and unaccounted with the sum controlled; catches a claim about a cell that does not exist (orphan_claim, proven by planting one); catches a cell claimed both ways (double_claimed, which surfaced 8 real cases); separates CLAIMED from SHOWN (verified against unverified, currently 1 against 8); and requires cross-language convergence to rest on evidence rather than on matching atoms - which took convergence from 8 tautological rows to 0 honest ones. Plus a dumb AST scanner that judges nothing, and one cell taken end to end against an execution oracle. WITHOUT THAT INSTRUMENT, 1022 cells filled by a swarm would be 1022 confident answers with no way to tell the wrong from the right, which is exactly the failure the tick-1 pre-mortem named and which this session then reproduced twice on its own work. THE REMAINING HIGH-VALUE MOVE IS NOT CELL-FILLING: it is the DESUGARING. A published K semantics reduces the language to a small core with the reduction explicit and checked against a conformance suite, which would cut 1022 to something small AND JUSTIFIED rather than guessed - and the guessed collapse has already been refuted once tonight, from 3x down to 1.27x. That is a lookup-shaped task and a decision about dependencies, so it belongs to the owner rather than to an unattended loop at three in the morning.").

witness(f_the_loop_built_the_instrument_and_cannot_build_the_product, "guardrail(G, T)", 16).

finding(f_i_red_teamed_the_product_and_never_the_process, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_red_teamed_the_product_and_never_the_process, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_i_red_teamed_the_product_and_never_the_process, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_i_red_teamed_the_product_and_never_the_process, "Asked plainly why the language model was not built, and the plain answer is that I spent five hours building a scoreboard instead of playing. Each tick produced something to CHECK the model, then a hole in the checker, then a fix, then a hole in the fix - five ticks, five checkers, ONE actual cell out of 1022. THREE CAUSES, and the third is the one that transfers. Checking is easier than modelling because an audit is a CLOSED task with a visible end, while deciding what is true of CallExpression in the dataflow layer is open and can be wrong in ways nothing catches - so the easy one kept getting picked. My own guardrails rewarded it: every one says check before you build, so following the process honestly kept routing me back to the checker, and THE DISCIPLINE THAT PROTECTS AGAINST SLOPPY WORK PROTECTS EQUALLY AGAINST DOING WORK. And the rate measurement that ended the loop on tick 6 - 0.2 cells per tick against 1022 needed, 213 days - was computable on TICK 1: all three numbers were in hand and I simply never multiplied them. THE OPERATION I APPLIED EVERY TICK WAS `pick the smallest next thing`, and that question CANNOT EVER SELECT A LARGE ONE, so five small steps were never going to sum to a big one and I never asked whether they summed at all. THE CLEAN MISS: on tick 1 I ran a pre-mortem and it was a good one - a filled matrix is more dangerous than an empty one because it looks finished - but I ran it on the PRODUCT and never on the PROCESS. I red-teamed what I was building and never once red-teamed how I was building it. THE RULE THIS BUYS: before a repeating process starts, multiply its measured rate by the target and compare to the time available - and if the loop has no measured rate yet, the FIRST tick's job is to produce one rather than to produce work.").

guardrail(g_multiply_the_rate_before_the_second_tick, "A repeating process must produce its own RATE before it produces its second unit of work: measured units per tick times the target, against the time available. If that product is absurd the loop is the wrong vehicle and the right output is that sentence, not more ticks. Paid for by five ticks of excellent scaffolding and one cell of product, where the three numbers needed were in hand from the first tick and were never multiplied.").
guards(g_multiply_the_rate_before_the_second_tick, f_the_loop_built_the_instrument_and_cannot_build_the_product).

finding(f_two_published_reductions_compose_and_we_model_only_the_core, insight).
recorded(f_two_published_reductions_compose_and_we_model_only_the_core, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_two_published_reductions_compose_and_we_model_only_the_core, decision).
finding_note(f_two_published_reductions_compose_and_we_model_only_the_core, "Told to stop arguing why not and start working out how, and the reframe dissolves the number the whole programme was sized on. I had been reading `a complete execution semantics is Turing-complete and a fixpoint is not` as a wall. It is a wall for COPYING KJS. It is not a wall for the task, because KJS ITSELF DID NOT MODEL 162 ALTERNATIVES - it modelled 84 core ones and wrote 174 REDUCTION RULES, and the reductions are the model of everything else. THE IDEAL FINAL RESULT: the model of JavaScript does not exist, its function is performed by two PUBLISHED reductions that compose, and we model only the nine-category core. THE COMPOSITION, and both halves are executable and neither is ours: Babel's transform plugins take ES2024 and TypeScript down to ES5.1, tested against the whole ecosystem; KJS's js-trans.k takes ES5.1 down to a nine-category core, tested against the ECMAScript conformance suite. The `uncovered surfaces` I had listed as a gap - async, classes, generators, destructuring - are covered by the FIRST half, which turns async into a generator and a generator into a state machine. WHY A REDUCTION IS CHEAPER THAN A CELL, and this is the asymmetry that changes the arithmetic: a semantic cell asks what a construct MEANS in a layer, which is open and needs judgement and can be wrong silently, while a reduction asks whether one form rewrites to another, which is closed and HAS A FREE EXECUTABLE ORACLE - run both forms and compare. The count goes from 146 structural classes times 7 layers, 1022 cells, to NINE CORE CATEGORIES times 7 layers, 63 cells, plus reductions we extract rather than write. THE RISK, sharp and named before building: A REDUCTION PRESERVES MEANING AND DESTROYS STRUCTURE. async to generator to state machine rewrites the call graph beyond recognition, so modelling the reduced form models the TRANSPILED program while a scanner must speak about the SOURCE. A dataflow fact over the desugared form is true of the program; a call-graph fact over it names functions the source does not contain. THE RESOLUTION IS THE ONE THING THIS SYSTEM HAS THAT THE BORROWED HALVES DO NOT: the reduction must carry provenance back to the surface node, and a why-tree does that by construction - model the core, and the derivation maps every answer back to the construct the human wrote.").

finding(f_the_reduction_preserves_node_identity_and_need_not_run, insight).
recorded(f_the_reduction_preserves_node_identity_and_need_not_run, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_reduction_preserves_node_identity_and_need_not_run, doc).
finding_note(f_the_reduction_preserves_node_identity_and_need_not_run, "Vadim made two corrections and the second is a whole move I had missed: line numbers are weak evidence, and BABEL IS OPEN SOURCE SO ITS REWRITE RULES ARE READABLE. I had installed the transform and treated it as a black box to RUN, then built a bridge on source positions - which minification, generated code and several statements per line all break. The rule is 64 lines in @babel/helper-remap-async-to-generator and it says something far stronger: `AwaitExpression` is replaced by `yieldExpression(argument.node)` where the ARGUMENT NODE IS REUSED, and the function itself is MUTATED IN PLACE, `path.node.async = false; path.node.generator = true`. So the correspondence is OBJECT IDENTITY, not position. Positions survived not because babel copied them carefully but because THEY ARE THE SAME OBJECTS. MEASURED by tagging every node before the transform with a non-enumerable id: 19 of 21 survive by identity; all three source functions are the same objects with async flipped false and generator flipped true; both source call nodes are the same objects; and the two that vanish are exactly the two AwaitExpression wrappers, against exactly two YieldExpression gained - one for one, as the rule promises. AND I WAS WRONG AN HOUR EARLIER, judging by the GENERATED TEXT rather than the AST: seeing four functions, scaffolding and `f -> _f.apply` in the printed output I concluded the reduction destroys structure. The scaffolding is added AROUND the original nodes, not substituted for them. FOR THE CALL-GRAPH LAYER ASYNC NEEDS NO REDUCTION AT ALL - the call node is the call node, only the wrapper changes. THE SHARPER IDEAL FINAL RESULT: the reduction does not need to RUN; its function, telling us what a construct means, is performed by READING it. KJS's 174 rules and babel's 64 lines are DOCUMENTATION OF MEANING rather than a pipeline stage - we model the source, and the reductions say how a modern construct differs from a core one. Licence note, in contrast to KJS which has none: both the babel plugin and the helper are MIT.").

guardrail(g_take_the_strongest_available_evidence, "Weigh evidence BEFORE collecting it, not after. Source positions break under minification, generated code and several statements per line; object identity breaks under nothing. Measured 2026-09-01: I built a correspondence on line numbers while the exact answer sat in a 64-line MIT-licensed file I had installed ten minutes earlier. The failure was not `read instead of measure` nor its opposite - all day the correction had run the other way - it was TAKING THE EVIDENCE I KNEW HOW TO RUN RATHER THAN THE STRONGEST ONE AVAILABLE.").
guards(g_take_the_strongest_available_evidence, f_the_reduction_preserves_node_identity_and_need_not_run).

finding(f_every_layer_has_a_found_oracle_and_nothing_was_invented, insight).
recorded(f_every_layer_has_a_found_oracle_and_nothing_was_invented, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_every_layer_has_a_found_oracle_and_nothing_was_invented, doc).
finding_note(f_every_layer_has_a_found_oracle_and_nothing_was_invented, "The last gap closed, and it closed by FINDING rather than by building. TypeScript's prose specification is NOT usable: the last published Language Specification is version 1.8 from 2016, archived, against a compiler now at major version 5-7, and Microsoft's own tracker records that no separate formal spec is maintained - THE COMPILER IS THE SPECIFICATION. So the seventh layer cannot be had from a document. IT CAN BE HAD FROM THEIR REGRESSION SUITE: TypeScript's compiler tests emit baselines that include a `.types` file recording THE TYPE OF EVERY EXPRESSION and a `.symbols` file recording THE SYMBOL OF EVERY IDENTIFIER. That is a maintained, machine-readable corpus of construct-to-type, versioned with the compiler, and it is exactly what test262 is for JavaScript. The `.symbols` half was not being looked for and settles a second layer: which identifier binds to which declaration is the SCOPE oracle. THE COMPLETE PICTURE, six of seven layers with an executable or corpus oracle: dataflow, control flow and the call graph by running the fragment; scope by .symbols; types by .types; modules by the resolver; and structure alone with no executable oracle, where the only one available is the agreement of two independent modellers. THE REDUCTIONS, both found and neither ours: modern syntax to ES5 by Babel, MIT-licensed and readable; ES5 to a nine-category core by KJS, 174 rules, no licence so read-only. AND THE PATTERN THE OWNER NAMED, which is the transferable part: FOUR TIMES THIS EVENING I SAID SOMETHING WAS IMPOSSIBLE IN PRINCIPLE AND EACH TIME THE IMPOSSIBILITY WAS ABOUT ONE METHOD RATHER THAN ABOUT THE TASK - copying K's semantics is impossible and reading it is not; the reduction destroys structure only in the printed text and not in the AST; there is no oracle for a language model except test262 and tsc and the runtime; types are uncovered except that tsc covers them. That is RULE 2 at the level of design, an exhausted method taken for a property of the problem, and it is written in this repository's own rules.").

finding(f_proxy_is_not_a_hard_point_it_is_the_case_the_third_value_exists_for, insight).
recorded(f_proxy_is_not_a_hard_point_it_is_the_case_the_third_value_exists_for, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_proxy_is_not_a_hard_point_it_is_the_case_the_third_value_exists_for, doc).
finding_note(f_proxy_is_not_a_hard_point_it_is_the_case_the_third_value_exists_for, "I called Proxy `a known hard point for every analyser`, which is a stock phrase and was challenged as one. Taken apart, it is wrong in three ways. MEASURED FIRST: the AST node for o.foo is BYTE-IDENTICAL whether o is a plain object, carries a getter, or is a proxy - {type: MemberExpression, object: o, property: foo, computed: false} in every case - so the difficulty is INVISIBLE IN THE SYNTAX and is therefore not a syntax problem at all. WHERE IT ACTUALLY LIVES: knowing whether o IS a proxy at that point, which is points-to, and which is the same KIND of problem as knowing which function a variable holds in f(). Proxy adds nothing new to the kind of difficulty; it widens what points-to must cover. WHAT IT DOES CHANGE, precisely: the UPPER BOUND of the call graph. Call sites are normally enumerable syntactically - CallExpression, NewExpression, tagged templates - and with a proxy ANY member expression is potentially a call site, so the sound over-approximation grows. AND GETTERS DO THE SAME AND PREDATE PROXY BY A DECADE: measured, both a getter and a proxy produce two CallExpressions where a plain object produces one. So the phrase was lazy twice over, naming the newer feature as though it introduced the problem. WHAT OUR DESIGN ACTUALLY SAYS HERE, and it is the point: a two-valued analyser must choose between treating it as a read, which is unsound, and treating every member access as a call, which is useless - and both answers are SILENT about the choice having been made. The third value here is EARNED and carries its firing: `o.foo` is unknown BECAUSE o's provenance contains `new Proxy` at line N, and why-unknown names it. That is not a hard point, it is the case three-valued evaluation was built for, and the contribution is not precision but that the refusal to distinguish becomes EXPLAINABLE rather than quiet.").

finding(f_unknowns_must_be_typed_and_only_one_type_is_irreducible, insight).
recorded(f_unknowns_must_be_typed_and_only_one_type_is_irreducible, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_unknowns_must_be_typed_and_only_one_type_is_irreducible, decision).
finding_note(f_unknowns_must_be_typed_and_only_one_type_is_irreducible, "Vadim's correction, and it is right: points-to IS computable wherever the values on the path do not come from the runtime, because a closed fragment can simply be EXECUTED - which is this session's own rule that if the modelled thing is executable the oracle is to run it. Two qualifications and neither is a wall. Termination: a closed computation may not halt, so it needs a bound, and a run that hits the bound yields unknown - the third value again. And the boundary is itself COMPUTED rather than read off the syntax: `const p = new Proxy(t, h)` where `t = JSON.parse(input)` has a static proxy-ness and a runtime target, so the runtime-dependent frontier is a fixpoint of the same kind - stratified rather than circular, frontier first and everything behind it evaluable. THE DESIGN CONSEQUENCE IS THE PAYLOAD: unknowns must be TYPED BY REASON, and of four types only ONE is irreducible - runtime_dependent, where the value genuinely is not determined until the program runs; against budget_exhausted, not_yet_modelled and diverges, which are all OURS. Today they all print the same word. An audit reporting `247 unknowns` is useless; one reporting `3 irreducible, 244 ours` is a WORK QUEUE. The kernel already carries the machinery - hole reasons budget_exhausted, arith_type_error, arith_zero_divisor exist in src/reflect.ts - so this is a use of it rather than a build. MEASURED on our own kernel, 1407 call sites: 24 percent call a plain identifier, 48 percent a field of a known object or this, 28 percent something computed - so 72 percent have a syntactically determined callee, AND THE 28 IS AN OVERCOUNT because my classifier looks one level deep and filed a.b.c() as computed. The instrument exaggerates the difficulty rather than hiding it. AND THIS CORRECTS MY OWN ENTRY FROM AN HOUR EARLIER: I filed Proxy under `a limit on precision`. It is not. Most proxies are statically resolvable - one whose handler and target are written beside it as literals computes completely - and only those whose target or handler arrives from the runtime are irreducible, a minority of a minority. Both `a known hard point` and `a limit on precision` were lazy. The accurate word is TIER, not wall: compute the runtime-dependent frontier first, everything behind it is decidable, and the frontier is narrow.").

guardrail(g_a_difficulty_claim_names_its_condition, "A claim that something is hard, impossible or limited must name WHAT is hard and UNDER WHAT CONDITION it stops being so. `Points-to is hard` is an impression; `points-to is undecidable behind the runtime-dependent frontier, and that frontier covers under 28 percent of call sites in this kernel` is an assertion and can be refuted. RECORDED WITH ITS OWN LIMITATION, on the owner's judgement: formal notation would not save this, because these claims are not WRONG - they are not precise enough to be wrong, and a formalism would simply refuse them and get something equally empty in return. The only thing that reached them this session was being asked to unfold the mechanism.").
guards(g_a_difficulty_claim_names_its_condition, f_proxy_is_not_a_hard_point_it_is_the_case_the_third_value_exists_for).

finding(f_the_test_suite_caught_none_of_the_catalogue, insight).
recorded(f_the_test_suite_caught_none_of_the_catalogue, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_test_suite_caught_none_of_the_catalogue, doc).
finding_note(f_the_test_suite_caught_none_of_the_catalogue, "Eight failure modes catalogued in docs/failure-modes.md from one fifty-five hour session, every instance measured rather than recalled. SIX OF THE EIGHT ARE ONE SHAPE AT DIFFERENT SCALES: a claim landing where nothing reads it is indistinguishable from an absent claim - an empty grep, a store rolled back by the rejection being measured, a rule id naming no rule, model facts appended to a ledger the model never loads, a waiver whose reason need not be arguable, a frozen loop prompt still carrying a refuted number. In each the system's silence was read as consent. The other two are about the process rather than a claim, and both are an operation applied at the wrong level: a pre-mortem run on the artefact instead of on the loop building it, and a trajectory check owed by the plan but demanded only of the step. WHAT CAUGHT THEM, RANKED BY YIELD, AND THE RANKING IS THE FINDING: first, the owner asking for the MECHANISM - the only thing that reaches mode 5, impressions wearing an assertion's clothes, because those are not wrong and so no gate can fail them; second, applying a mechanism to its own author, which caught the witness's author, the guardrail's author and the audit's author on three separate occasions; third, executors' reports, three corrections each about something asserted rather than measured; FOURTH AND LAST, TESTS, WHICH CAUGHT NONE OF IT. The suite is 781 green and every item in the catalogue lies outside what any test here asserts. That is not an argument against the suite - it guards the kernel and does it well - but it settles what a test suite is FOR, and it is not this.").

finding(f_modelling_a_construct_is_finished_when_its_frontier_is_named, question).
recorded(f_modelling_a_construct_is_finished_when_its_frontier_is_named, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_modelling_a_construct_is_finished_when_its_frontier_is_named, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_modelling_a_construct_is_finished_when_its_frontier_is_named, "The step the owner set: take the AST scanner and model exactly two things, the function call and the file import, watch WHAT THEY DRAG IN, drive it to a stable position, and make NOT MODELLED the default verdict, an abstract class rather than an absence. The question this opens, and it is the one the previous six ticks never asked: WHEN IS A CONSTRUCT FINISHED? The answer this step proposes and must test is that a rule firing is not the finish line. A construct is modelled when every SHAPE that occurs at each of its modelled positions has a verdict, and every shape without a rule carries a TYPED reason. `f(x)` where the callee is a plain identifier is one shape; `o.m()`, `o[k]()`, `(await g())()` are three more, and a rule that answers the first and says nothing about the others reports a smaller call graph that looks correct. That is the exact silent under-reporting Grafema paid three months for. So the deliverable of a modelled cell is TWO things, the rule and the enumerated frontier it does not cover, and the second is what makes the first honest.").

guardrail(g_the_denominator_is_the_grammar_the_work_order_is_the_corpus, "Coverage and priority are two different questions and each has its own source. The DENOMINATOR is the parser's full kind list: measured 2026-08-31, this corpus uses 108 of babel's 252 kinds, so an audit whose denominator is the corpus reports completeness while blind to 144 kinds. The WORK ORDER is the corpus, with counts: what actually occurs, how often, and therefore what to model next. Collapsing the two loses whichever one was dropped, and the loss is silent in both directions. A frontier derived by typing a list from memory is neither of them; it is a memory test, and it disagrees with the corpus precisely where I am blind.").
guards(g_the_denominator_is_the_grammar_the_work_order_is_the_corpus, f_modelling_a_construct_is_finished_when_its_frontier_is_named).

guardrail(g_the_default_verdict_is_positive_typed_and_counted, "The default answer of a coverage model must be a POSITIVE ROW, not an empty result. `? verdict(js, member_expression, callgraph, V)` must answer `not_modelled` and never nothing, because an empty answer is a fact about the query until proven otherwise (RULE 2), and because a model that cannot say `I know that I do not know this` is indistinguishable from one that is complete. Every not_modelled row carries a TYPED reason, and the four types are not equal: `runtime_dependent` is irreducible, while `not_yet`, `budget_exhausted` and `out_of_scope` are ours. `247 unknowns` is useless; `3 irreducible, 244 ours` is a work queue. Positive control, required before the mechanism is claimed to work: print the not_modelled count, model exactly one cell, print it again, and show it moved by exactly one.").
guards(g_the_default_verdict_is_positive_typed_and_counted, f_modelling_a_construct_is_finished_when_its_frontier_is_named).

guardrail(g_a_stability_claim_prints_the_net_frontier_delta, "A claim that modelling has reached a stable position must be capable of being false, and `stable` in the trivial sense is not: once every cell has a default verdict, nothing is ever silent and the claim cannot fail. The number that CAN fail is the NET FRONTIER DELTA: model one cell, count the new unmodelled cells it drags in, subtract the one closed. If that average is at or above one the work DIVERGES and no amount of ticking closes it, and reporting that sentence is a result rather than a failure. Same shape as the rate guardrail that ended the previous loop, applied one level down: there the miss was never multiplying units per tick by the target, here it would be never subtracting what a closed cell opens.").
guards(g_a_stability_claim_prints_the_net_frontier_delta, f_modelling_a_construct_is_finished_when_its_frontier_is_named).

finding(f_the_hard_shapes_are_absent_from_the_corpus_and_still_need_verdicts, insight).
recorded(f_the_hard_shapes_are_absent_from_the_corpus_and_still_need_verdicts, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_hard_shapes_are_absent_from_the_corpus_and_still_need_verdicts, doc).
finding_note(f_the_hard_shapes_are_absent_from_the_corpus_and_still_need_verdicts, "The frontier of the two constructs the owner chose, MEASURED before either was modelled, 155 files and 317278 nodes. Positive control that the run read the whole corpus: it reports 108 distinct babel kinds, the same number measured independently on 2026-08-31. THE CALL, 28365 sites: 37.4 percent a plain identifier callee, 62.5 percent a member expression, and of those 73.2 percent have an IDENTIFIER object and every single one is NON COMPUTED. `o[k]()` occurs ZERO times in 28365 call sites. THIS CORRECTS MY OWN NUMBER FROM THREE HOURS AGO. I reported 28 percent of call sites as computed and flagged it as probably an overcount; it is not an overcount, it is a MISCLASSIFICATION. What my one level deep classifier filed as computed was `a.b.c()` at 10.9 percent and `f().g()` at 10.1 percent, and both are fully static chains resolvable by recursion. The irreducible category was not exaggerated, it was EMPTY. THE IMPORT, 802 declarations and 1730 specifiers: 53.4 percent relative with an extension, 45.1 percent node builtins, 1.5 percent bare, so 98.5 percent resolve without touching node_modules; all four specifier kinds occur; and the DEPENDS versus FLOWS distinction is real and present at 3.5 percent of declarations plus 6.4 percent of specifiers carrying an inline type marker. Five dynamic import sites, four with a string literal and ONE with a computed argument. THE WHOLE IRREDUCIBLE CLASS FOR THE MODULE LAYER IS ONE SITE IN 807. AND THE GUARDRAIL EARNS ITS KEEP IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AT ONCE: the computed callee occurs zero times and STILL NEEDS A VERDICT, because babel's grammar allows it and the denominator is the grammar; and it goes LAST on the work order, because the work order is the corpus. Had I taken the denominator from the corpus the model would report completeness while blind to it; had I taken the work order from the grammar I would have spent the tick on points to analysis for a shape that does not occur.").

finding(f_the_default_verdict_is_explainable_and_that_is_the_whole_difference, insight).
recorded(f_the_default_verdict_is_explainable_and_that_is_the_whole_difference, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_default_verdict_is_explainable_and_that_is_the_whole_difference, doc).
finding_note(f_the_default_verdict_is_explainable_and_that_is_the_whole_difference, "The owner asked for `not modelled` as the DEFAULT, an abstract class rather than an absence, and the property that makes it worth the trouble showed up on the first probe. The coverage model is now a total function: 28 cells partition exactly into 9 modelled, 5 waived, 14 not_modelled, and the 14 split 2 irreducible against 12 ours. Deleting the default rule drives not_modelled to zero and breaks the partition, so the check tests the DEFAULT and not the other two. Adding `layer(scope).` as ONE FACT takes 28 cells to 42 and 14 unknowns to 28, both up by exactly the number of kinds, with no rule edited. THE PART THAT IS NOT BOOKKEEPING: `why verdict[audit](js, yield_expression, dataflow, not_modelled)` returns a DERIVATION - it names the cell, both axioms under it, and BOTH negative premises as finite failure - and `whynot` on a cell that IS modelled names the blocking fact by name rather than shrugging. An `unaccounted` relation could never do this, because it is named for what is MISSING and an empty result can only be approached from the other side. Every static analyser has a cannot determine case and almost none say WHY; this one says which two premises failed and at which axioms. THREE BREAK ATTEMPTS OF MY OWN, from angles the executor's six mutants did not cover, and the first two were KILLED BY A DESIGN I HAD NOT READ CAREFULLY ENOUGH: a `handled` fact naming a kind that does not exist produces a verdict with no cell under it, the partition sum goes to 29 against 28, and `orphan_claim` reports it - a deliberate second independent detector, documented in the rule file, which my probe confirmed rather than refuted. THE RESIDUAL GAP IS THE FAMILIAR ONE: that second detector is claimed in a COMMENT and no arm exercises it, so it is a claim landing where nothing reads it, in a file whose entire purpose is to forbid exactly that.").

finding(f_a_projection_hides_a_contradiction_every_audit_is_right_to_ignore, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_projection_hides_a_contradiction_every_audit_is_right_to_ignore, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_projection_hides_a_contradiction_every_audit_is_right_to_ignore, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_projection_hides_a_contradiction_every_audit_is_right_to_ignore, "Found by the executor on a probe it ran unasked, and left open deliberately for me. The typed unknown carries `unknown_because(Lang, Kind, Layer, Reason)` and NOTHING SAYS THE REASON IS UNIQUE. Two rows for one cell are accepted: `reason[audit]` returns 15 rows over 14 cells, so a cell can be simultaneously `out_of_scope` and `budget_exhausted`. THE PART THAT MAKES IT A CLASS RATHER THAN A TYPO: all three audits are individually CORRECT to stay quiet - both atoms are well typed so `bad_reason` has nothing to say, the cell exists so `orphan_reason` has nothing to say, the cell is not_modelled so `stale_reason` has nothing to say - and the headline number stays right because both buckets PROJECT THE REASON AWAY and still sum to 14. So the contradiction is invisible in exactly the number a reader looks at, and no existing check is wrong. The general shape, which is what transfers: A PROJECTION CAN HIDE A CONTRADICTION THAT EVERY AUDIT IS INDIVIDUALLY RIGHT TO IGNORE. Sibling of `double_claimed[audit]`, which exists precisely because a cell claimed both handled and ignored had to be made visible rather than resolved by rule order; the same discipline was simply not carried to the new relation, which is the other finding of this pair.").

finding(f_the_vocabulary_check_does_not_generalise_and_reflection_is_the_fix, insight).
recorded(f_the_vocabulary_check_does_not_generalise_and_reflection_is_the_fix, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_vocabulary_check_does_not_generalise_and_reflection_is_the_fix, decision).
finding_note(f_the_vocabulary_check_does_not_generalise_and_reflection_is_the_fix, "The structural finding of the tick, handed up by the executor and MEASURED rather than argued. `orphan_claim[audit]` was written by hand against `handled` and `ignored` - four rules, one per relation per vocabulary half - to catch a claim naming a kind or a layer that does not exist. It was written because I had made exactly that typo and the claim EVAPORATED with every count unmoved. Adding one new authored relation over the same vocabulary, `unknown_because(Lang, Kind, Layer, Reason)`, SILENTLY REOPENED THE WHOLE HOLE: with the orphan rules stripped, a fact naming a nonexistent kind AND a nonexistent layer loads, holds in the store, and moves no count, no bad_reason, no stale_reason, no reason. The executor measured that with a positive control before closing it, then closed it by writing `orphan_reason` BY HAND, two more arms. So the check is per-relation and hand-copied, and the next relation over (Lang, Kind, Layer) will have the same hole with nothing to notice. WHAT MAKES THIS WORTH RECORDING RATHER THAN JUST FIXING: the remedy is a SCHEMA, not another copy, and this language can express it where a graph store could not. The sentence wanted is `every authored relation whose arguments include a Kind and a Layer must have both in the vocabulary`, and ROFL is reflective - rules are facts, `premise_pos` and argument positions are queryable - so it is derivable ONCE from the rules themselves rather than restated per relation. That is the same move that took the range restriction check out of the host and into rules, where it agreed set for set with `Evaluation.rules[].safe`. Until that exists, every new authored relation is a fresh silent hole and the only defence is remembering, which is the thing this repository does not accept as a defence.").

addressed_by(f_unknowns_must_be_typed_and_only_one_type_is_irreducible, "rules/js-model.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_default_verdict_is_explainable_and_that_is_the_whole_difference, "test/js-model.test.ts").

finding(f_the_gate_i_wrote_against_invisible_bytes_has_a_hole_one_byte_wide, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_gate_i_wrote_against_invisible_bytes_has_a_hole_one_byte_wide, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_gate_i_wrote_against_invisible_bytes_has_a_hole_one_byte_wide, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_gate_i_wrote_against_invisible_bytes_has_a_hole_one_byte_wide, "Found by an executor IN ITS OWN FILE, and it is a hit on the gate I wrote yesterday against exactly this class. A 0x01 byte reached its test file and PASSED EVERY CHECK: grep found nothing, sed matched nothing, tsc was clean, `npm run textcheck` printed `source text check: clean`, and the test passed, because the byte was a map key separator and was consistent on both sides. Only `od -c` saw it. I VERIFIED IT INDEPENDENTLY BEFORE ACTING, with a positive control: plant a 0x01 inside the word `here` in a tracked source file, and textcheck says clean while `grep -c here` returns ZERO, because the byte splits the word invisibly. THE CAUSE IS A BORROWED CRITERION. `scripts/text_check.ts` bans NUL and only NUL, and it does so because it was written from GIT's binary heuristic - the observable that paid for it was `Binary files differ` killing a review. But the property that actually matters is READABILITY BY A HUMAN AND BY GREP, and on that property 0x01 is exactly as bad as 0x00 while being invisible to the one tool that noticed. So the gate inherited the scope of the incident that produced it rather than the scope of the defect, which is the general shape: A GATE BUILT FROM ONE OBSERVED FAILURE COVERS THAT FAILURE'S SIGNATURE, NOT ITS CLASS. And the reason it stayed hidden is the one this repository keeps re-learning: every instrument that could have seen it is itself text-based, so the byte was invisible to the whole toolchain at once and its absence from every report read as absence from the file. Recorded with the same file's own history in view - text_check rejected its own author on the day it was written, which made it feel finished.").

finding(f_an_id_blind_oracle_cannot_see_the_property_the_cache_depends_on, insight).
recorded(f_an_id_blind_oracle_cannot_see_the_property_the_cache_depends_on, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_an_id_blind_oracle_cannot_see_the_property_the_cache_depends_on, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_an_id_blind_oracle_cannot_see_the_property_the_cache_depends_on, "THE EXECUTOR'S MUTANT SET BEAT MINE, and the way it did is the transferable part. I briefed five mutants for the AST scanner and all five were KILLED. The executor read five for five as a statement about MY FIVE rather than about the gate, and kept inventing mutants until something LIVED. Two did. Reversing the emission order survives because the completeness oracle SORTS. Making the node counter global across scan() calls survives because every signature the oracle compares is ID-BLIND - it compares kind, file, line, field, index and key, deliberately, so that it is not merely restating the scanner's own numbering. THE SURVIVOR THAT WOULD HAVE HURT is the second: `scanners/materialize.ts` caches fact files BY CONTENT HASH, so a scanner whose re-scan of an unchanged file differs by a single byte churns every fact file and moves every golden in the tree. An id-blind oracle CANNOT SEE RE-SCAN DETERMINISM, and re-scan determinism is precisely what the incremental cache depends on. THE STRUCTURE, which is why this is not just a good catch: the oracle's blindness is not an oversight, it is the SOURCE OF ITS STRENGTH - being id-blind is what makes it an independent check rather than a restatement - and the property it therefore cannot see is a real requirement of the system. So a strong oracle has a shadow shaped exactly like its own independence, and the defence is a SECOND check with a different blindness rather than a better single one. Closed by the executor with a determinism test: byte-identical facts on a second scan, root node first, ast_file last, every id declared before any edge names it. All seven mutants now killed. THE BRIEFING LESSON FOR ME: this repository's rule is that a brief must ask the executor to MEASURE WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE. I wrote five mutants and did not ask that question, so the executor asked it unprompted and found the answer in two minutes.").

finding(f_the_widened_gate_caught_a_live_unplanted_defect_within_minutes, insight).
recorded(f_the_widened_gate_caught_a_live_unplanted_defect_within_minutes, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_widened_gate_caught_a_live_unplanted_defect_within_minutes, doc).
finding_note(f_the_widened_gate_caught_a_live_unplanted_defect_within_minutes, "The strongest evidence a gate can produce, and it arrived unasked. `scripts/text_check.ts` was widened from NUL alone to every C0 control plus DEL at about 10:05, and its FIRST RUN on the honest tree came back clean; minutes later, with a second agent still writing, it went RED on a defect nobody planted: SIX LITERAL NUL BYTES in `test/js-modules.test.ts`. THE SAME DEFECT AS 2026-08-30, FROM THE SAME INTENT, BY A DIFFERENT AUTHOR, THIRTY SIX HOURS LATER - a separator written as a literal byte instead of an escape, `join('<NUL>')` and `replace('<NUL>', ' -> ')`. It typechecked, it passed its own tests because the byte was consistent on both sides of every comparison, and grep saw nothing. VERIFIED WITH THREE INSTRUMENTS INDEPENDENT OF THE GATE, because a gate reporting its own first catch is exactly where a second opinion is owed: a python byte scan found 6 NULs with `boot.rofl` as a positive control returning 0, so the probe is known to work; textcheck reported the leftmost with an offset; and GIT ITSELF settled it - `git diff --no-index /dev/null test/js-modules.test.ts` prints `Bin 0 to 30809 bytes, 0 insertions, 0 deletions`. The consequence is PRESENT rather than predicted: git already treats that file as binary and the review of it is already dead. WHAT THIS SETTLES ABOUT GATES: a planted defect proves a check can fire, and only an unplanted one proves it fires at the rate the defect actually occurs. The recurrence interval for this class in this repository is now MEASURED at thirty six hours across two different authors, which is the number that says whether the gate is worth its cost. AND THE EXECUTOR ALSO CLOSED THE EMPTY RESULT TRAP IN THE GATE'S OWN YES ARM, unasked: `clean` and `the walker returned zero files` were the same green, so the CLI now prints `clean (338 files)` and the honest tree arm asserts the walk exceeded 200 files and specifically reached boot.rofl. That is RULE 2 applied to my own script, by someone else.").

finding(f_a_gate_inherits_the_scope_of_its_incident_not_of_its_class, insight).
recorded(f_a_gate_inherits_the_scope_of_its_incident_not_of_its_class, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_gate_inherits_the_scope_of_its_incident_not_of_its_class, doc).
finding_note(f_a_gate_inherits_the_scope_of_its_incident_not_of_its_class, "The generalisation of the 0x01 hole, and it is the part that transfers beyond this file. `scripts/text_check.ts` banned NUL AND ONLY NUL, and the reason is visible in its own header comment: it was written from an observed incident whose signature was `Binary files differ` killing a review, which is GIT'S binary heuristic. So the gate inherited the boundary of the TOOL THAT NOTICED the incident. The property that actually matters is readability by a human and by grep, and on that property 0x01 is exactly as bad as 0x00 while being invisible to the one instrument that had noticed. A GATE BUILT FROM ONE OBSERVED FAILURE COVERS THAT FAILURE'S SIGNATURE, NOT ITS CLASS, and the tell is that its criterion is borrowed from whatever tool produced the first red. THE REMEDY IS A SWEPT BOUNDARY RATHER THAN A WIDER GUESS: the executor planted all thirty three C0 bytes plus DEL one at a time and compared the reported set against the intended set, so what is banned and what is allowed are both MEASURED, and the two allowed bytes print by name. A sampled boundary is an assumption wearing a test's face. AND THE COST OF WIDENING WAS PRICED BEFORE IT WAS PAID: 337 files, zero CR bytes of either kind, so allowing CRLF while banning a lone CR costs nothing today and exists for the next contributor. That order matters - a gate that is red on an honest Windows checkout gets switched off, and then its absence is invisible, which is the Gospriyomka failure this repository names.").

addressed_by(f_the_gate_i_wrote_against_invisible_bytes_has_a_hole_one_byte_wide, "scripts/text_check.ts").
addressed_by(f_an_id_blind_oracle_cannot_see_the_property_the_cache_depends_on, "test/js-ast.test.ts").

finding(f_depends_and_flows_needed_a_third_relation_and_the_marker_lies, insight).
recorded(f_depends_and_flows_needed_a_third_relation_and_the_marker_lies, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_depends_and_flows_needed_a_third_relation_and_the_marker_lies, doc).
finding_note(f_depends_and_flows_needed_a_third_relation_and_the_marker_lies, "Grafema's expensive distinction is DEPENDS is potential, FLOWS is actual, and modelling the file import against a resolver oracle both CONFIRMED it and showed it is one relation short. Confirmed: depends 10, flows 6, GAP 4, so the distinction is not decoration and the four edges that depend without flowing are namable - two type-only imports, one at declaration level and one inline, and two side-effect imports. THE SHORTFALL: depends and flows alone put a TYPE-ONLY import and a SIDE-EFFECT import in the SAME cell, `depends, nothing flows`, and at run time those two are OPPOSITES. A type-only import is erased before the program starts; a side-effect import EXECUTES A FILE. The executor added `evaluates` unplanned because the model would not sit still without it, which is the good kind of discovery - the third relation was forced by the data rather than designed. AND THE MARKER LIES, measured against babel 8 rather than recalled: `import type { T }` produces an ImportDeclaration with importKind `type` whose ImportSpecifier carries importKind `VALUE`. The specifier of a type-only declaration says the OPPOSITE of the truth, so a model reading either marker alone gets one of the two forms wrong, and the two forms are 3.5 percent and 6.4 percent of this corpus. Both must be read. A ONE-DIMENSIONAL READING WOULD HAVE PASSED ITS OWN TESTS. And `evaluates` is itself COMPILER-CONFIG-DEPENDENT: `import { type T } from './x'` erases entirely under default TypeScript and leaves a bare side-effect import under verbatimModuleSyntax, so the fact depends on a build setting rather than on the source - a dependency the kind times layer matrix has no column for.").

finding(f_the_matrix_cannot_hold_the_frontier_that_is_not_a_kind_times_a_layer, insight).
recorded(f_the_matrix_cannot_hold_the_frontier_that_is_not_a_kind_times_a_layer, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_matrix_cannot_hold_the_frontier_that_is_not_a_kind_times_a_layer, decision).
finding_note(f_the_matrix_cannot_hold_the_frontier_that_is_not_a_kind_times_a_layer, "THE FIRST STRUCTURAL LIMIT FOUND IN THE COVERAGE DESIGN ITSELF, and it was found by modelling rather than by inspecting the design. The whole programme is built on cells of node kind times semantic layer, with the invariant that an empty significant cell is a guaranteed gap. Modelling ONE construct produced four frontier items that ARE NOT CELLS and that the matrix therefore cannot hold, cannot count, and cannot report as missing. FIRST AND LARGEST: THE KERNEL HAS NO STRING BUILTINS. Its whole vocabulary is =, !=, is, <, <=, >, >=, so NO RULE CAN DECOMPOSE A PATH, and module resolution needs str_seg, str_char0 and str_scheme supplied by the host. That is a statement about the KERNEL, not about JavaScript, and it binds EVERY language model that ever resolves anything - it is a row about our own engine appearing in the middle of a model of someone else's language. The executor declared it as `host_emitter(js, modules, str_algebra, a_kernel_has_no_string_builtins)`, countable, with the count going to zero being what `the kernel grew a string primitive` would mean. SECOND: WHICH RESOLVER is being modelled at all. The oracle is CJS because `import.meta.resolve` cannot be aimed at a parent without a node flag, and CJS and ESM DIVERGE on extension guessing and directory index - invisible on this corpus because every relative specifier carries an extension, and a real hole on one that does not. THIRD: `evaluates` depends on a COMPILER SETTING rather than on the source. FOURTH: a branch can be DEAD against a corpus - all 371 builtin imports here are node:-prefixed and zero are bare - so a rule can be correct, exercised only by a fixture, and never fire on real input. NONE of these four is a kind times a layer. The matrix's own invariant, that silence is not an option, is silent about every one of them, and the reason is that the matrix has exactly two dimensions while the frontier has more. What to do about it is a decision, not a fix.").

finding(f_the_frontier_delta_counts_vocabulary_growth_and_i_predicted_the_wrong_number, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_frontier_delta_counts_vocabulary_growth_and_i_predicted_the_wrong_number, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_frontier_delta_counts_vocabulary_growth_and_i_predicted_the_wrong_number, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_frontier_delta_counts_vocabulary_growth_and_i_predicted_the_wrong_number, "I pre-registered a prediction before the executors reported, with a falsifier, precisely so I could not move the goalposts: closing two cells would open 10 to 15, more than one opened per one closed, which by my own guardrail means the per-cell process DIVERGES. THE MEASUREMENT IS WORSE THAN I PREDICTED AND THE READING IS DIFFERENT. Modelling ONE construct, the file import, took cells from 28 to 57 and unaccounted from 14 to 36: 29 new cells, 22 newly unaccounted, from closing one. So the raw ratio is 22 to 1 and my number was low by a factor of two. BUT THE DECOMPOSITION REFUTES MY GUARDRAIL RATHER THAN CONFIRMING IT. Of the 22: TEN exist only because the work had to NAME five new node kinds, and naming a kind costs one cell per layer by construction - that is the matrix's arithmetic, not new work. TWELVE are at the modules layer for kinds that mostly do not participate in a module graph at all and close cheaply with an `ignored` reason. Exactly ONE is a real gap needing judgement: py import_from at the modules layer, the same construct in the other language. SO THE GUARDRAIL I WROTE THIS MORNING MEASURES THE WRONG QUANTITY. It counts CELLS, and cells are vocabulary times layers, so ANY growth in vocabulary inflates the delta regardless of how much thinking is left - a model that names more kinds looks like it is diverging while doing exactly the same work. The corrected rule: SPLIT THE DELTA INTO JUDGEMENT CELLS AND ARITHMETIC CELLS, and only the first is a rate. Here that is 1 against 21. THE PREDICTION WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE SIGN, WRONG ABOUT THE MAGNITUDE, AND WRONG ABOUT WHAT THE MAGNITUDE MEANT, and only the pre-registration makes it possible to say the third part honestly.").

finding(f_a_separator_is_where_an_invisible_byte_survives_every_gate, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_separator_is_where_an_invisible_byte_survives_every_gate, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_separator_is_where_an_invisible_byte_survives_every_gate, doc).
finding_note(f_a_separator_is_where_an_invisible_byte_survives_every_gate, "The executor's own diagnosis of its six NUL bytes, and it is better than `a stray keystroke`. A SEPARATOR IS THE ONE PLACE AN INVISIBLE BYTE SURVIVES EVERY GATE A TEST HAS. It typechecks, because a string is a string. It greps as nothing, because grep cannot match across it. And it is CONSISTENT ON BOTH SIDES OF EVERY COMPARISON, so the suite is green - which means a passing test is POSITIVE EVIDENCE THAT THE BYTE DOES NOT MATTER. All three checks the author ran on their own work - tests, tsc, and the model's own eight audit gates - were structurally incapable of seeing it. Same shape as `retainTicks` in CLAUDE.md: invisible by construction to every gate we owned, not inattention. THE FIX WAS ALSO DONE RIGHT and is worth copying: the separator became `|` rather than the space originally intended, because A PATH CAN CONTAIN A SPACE AND CANNOT CONTAIN A PIPE, and it was applied as a byte-level replace rather than by retyping the line, so the defect could not be reintroduced by the same hand that made it. Verified at the byte level, not by grep, which cannot see this class - and `git diff` now reports 624 insertions where it had reported `Bin 0 to 30809 bytes`.").

addressed_by(f_modelling_a_construct_is_finished_when_its_frontier_is_named, "rules/js-modules.rofl").

finding(f_the_model_misses_thirty_percent_of_executed_edges_and_names_every_one, insight).
recorded(f_the_model_misses_thirty_percent_of_executed_edges_and_names_every_one, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_model_misses_thirty_percent_of_executed_edges_and_names_every_one, doc).
finding_note(f_the_model_misses_thirty_percent_of_executed_edges_and_names_every_one, "The call graph modelled against an EXECUTION oracle, V8 stack frames, and the result is the uncomfortable one that makes the design worth having. ORACLE 37 EDGES, MODEL 26, ELEVEN UNSOUND UNDER-REPORTS AND ZERO OVER-APPROXIMATIONS. The model misses thirty percent of the edges the program actually ran. AND EVERY SINGLE MISS IS ATTRIBUTED TO A SHAPE, by the line V8 reported: member on member, member on call, member on this, computed dynamic key twice, computed literal key, optional member, new expression, and two through a plain identifier. Grafema paid three months for silent under-reporting - a rule that misses a call shape returns a smaller answer that looks correct and nothing goes red. A model quietly returning 26 edges with no frontier would have been INDISTINGUISHABLE from a complete one and worthless. TWO ENTRIES IN THAT TABLE ARE WORTH MORE THAN THE OTHER NINE. First, `apply2 to leaf` and `apply2 to mid` are attributed to s_identifier, A SHAPE THE MODEL CLAIMS TO HANDLE: an identifier callee resolves when the identifier names a DECLARATION and does not when it names a PARAMETER, so higher order calls are a hole INSIDE a handled shape and NO SHAPE CENSUS CAN SHOW IT. The classification axis is syntactic and the gap is not. Second, `useClass to Box` is attributed to new_expression, A CALL SITE THAT IS NOT A CallExpression AT ALL, so the callee-shape axis cannot see it because it is a different node kind. Both say the same thing in two registers: THE FRONTIER IS FINER THAN THE CLASSIFICATION, and the census exposed that rather than hiding it. Seventeen shapes over 83 call sites, 16 with a residue, 1 irreducible against 15 ours - and nine further verdicts recorded for shapes the GRAMMAR allows that this corpus does not contain, which is the denominator-from-the-grammar and work-order-from-the-corpus rule carried out unprompted.").

finding(f_every_cell_the_call_graph_added_was_arithmetic_and_none_was_judgement, insight).
recorded(f_every_cell_the_call_graph_added_was_arithmetic_and_none_was_judgement, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_every_cell_the_call_graph_added_was_arithmetic_and_none_was_judgement, decision).
finding_note(f_every_cell_the_call_graph_added_was_arithmetic_and_none_was_judgement, "The second measurement of the net frontier delta, and it settles the correction the first one suggested. Modelling the CALL took js node kinds from 9 to 33, cells from 28 to 76, unaccounted from 14 to 50: PLUS 48 CELLS AND PLUS 36 UNACCOUNTED FROM CLOSING ONE. Raw, that is far worse than the 10 to 15 I pre-registered and worse than the module layer's 22. BUT THE DECOMPOSITION IS EXACT AND IT IS ALL ARITHMETIC: 24 new kinds times 2 layers is exactly 48, so EVERY ONE of the added cells is the matrix charging one cell per layer for a named kind, and NOT ONE of them is a new question. The judgement actually left is not in the matrix at all - it is the 16 shapes with a residue, of which 1 is irreducible and 15 are ours. SO THE UNIT IS WRONG, MEASURED TWICE NOW. Cells are vocabulary times layers, so the delta grows whenever a model NAMES more of the language, which is what progress looks like; a model that named nothing would have a perfect delta and no content. The rate that means anything is JUDGEMENT CELLS PER CLOSED CELL, and across both constructs that is 1 for the module layer and 16 shape residues for the call layer, against 70 cells of arithmetic. AND THE DEEPER POINT, which is a decision rather than a fix: THE CALL GRAPH'S FRONTIER LIVES AT SHAPE GRANULARITY, 17 shapes over 83 sites, WHILE THE MATRIX LIVES AT KIND GRANULARITY. `member_expression` is one kind and six distinct shapes, each with its own verdict. So the coverage matrix and the working frontier are not the same object, and the matrix is the coarser of the two. That is the second structural limit found today by modelling rather than by inspection, the first being the four frontier items that are not cells at all.").

addressed_by(f_the_frontier_delta_counts_vocabulary_growth_and_i_predicted_the_wrong_number, "docs/modelling-a-language.md").

finding(f_my_own_completion_gate_greps_for_the_wrong_reporter_format, pitfall).
recorded(f_my_own_completion_gate_greps_for_the_wrong_reporter_format, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_my_own_completion_gate_greps_for_the_wrong_reporter_format, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_my_own_completion_gate_greps_for_the_wrong_reporter_format, "Small, and the third gate of mine to fail today. After losing a background run to a truncated log I made a rule that a completed node run must end with a printed `# tests / # pass / # fail` and that the grep for it goes in the SAME invocation, so `0 failures` from a log with no summary is impossible. On this run it printed `SUMMARY PRESENT? 0` on a run that had FINISHED PERFECTLY, 854 of 854 and exit 0. THE CAUSE: `npm test` here carries `--test-reporter=spec`, which prints the summary as `INFO tests 854` with an information glyph, while the plain runner prints `# tests 854`. My guard greps for the hash form only, so against the project's own npm script it reports absence ALWAYS. It has therefore never once said yes on the command it exists to guard, which means it has been carrying no information at all - and it failed SAFE, claiming did-not-finish on a finished run, which is exactly why nobody noticed. A gate that is permanently red is abolished; a gate that is permanently red IN THE SAFE DIRECTION is worse, because it is simply ignored while still looking like a check. The fix is to accept either prefix, and the general form is the one already recorded twice today: A GATE INHERITS THE SCOPE OF THE INCIDENT THAT PRODUCED IT. The incident was a bare `node --test`, so the pattern matched a bare `node --test`, and the command actually used in this repository was never checked against it.").

finding(f_i_coin_a_term_and_then_explain_with_the_term_i_just_coined, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_coin_a_term_and_then_explain_with_the_term_i_just_coined, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_i_coin_a_term_and_then_explain_with_the_term_i_just_coined, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_i_coin_a_term_and_then_explain_with_the_term_i_just_coined, "The owner's correction, and he flagged it as SYSTEMATIC rather than as one slip: `the matrix is coarser than the frontier`, `arithmetic cells versus judgement cells`, `a gate inherits the scope of its incident`. Each of those has a real measurement behind it, and each was DELIVERED AS A COINED PHRASE INSTEAD OF AS THE MEASUREMENT. The phrase arrives first, the example never arrives, and the reader is left holding a label whose content only I can see. WHY IT KEEPS HAPPENING, and it is not carelessness: a compressed name is what makes a finding feel FINISHED to its author. Once I can say `the matrix is coarser` I stop doing the work of saying `member_expression is one row in the table and six different jobs in reality, so a tick in that row can mean one job out of six is done`. The second sentence is the finding; the first is a receipt for it. And a receipt is exactly what an author cannot tell apart from the thing, which is the same blindness that makes an author unable to review their own code. IT ALSO DEGRADES THE LEDGER, not just the conversation: a finding_note written in coined vocabulary is unreadable to the next session, and the whole point of the ledger is that it is read by someone who was not here. THE RULE, cheap and mechanical: A NEW TERM MAY NOT APPEAR WITHOUT A CONCRETE INSTANCE IN THE SAME BREATH - one real example with real names from the tree, before the abstraction, not after it. If I cannot produce the instance, the term is not compressing anything, it is standing in for work I have not done. Sibling of the `not X but Y` trigger already recorded: both are cases where a rhetorical move substitutes for content, and BOTH WERE CAUGHT BY THE OWNER AND NEITHER BY ME.").

finding(f_laziness_and_the_third_value_are_mutually_exclusive_today, question).
recorded(f_laziness_and_the_third_value_are_mutually_exclusive_today, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_laziness_and_the_third_value_are_mutually_exclusive_today, decision).
finding_note(f_laziness_and_the_third_value_are_mutually_exclusive_today, "FUTURE WORK, opened by the owner asking why we cannot do what Python does with lazy generators. The premise of the question was wrong in my favour: WE ALREADY DO, in three separate places, and I had to read the engine rather than recall it. First, src/engine.ts around line 120 - a relation whose @now rules are not range restricted is DEMAND BACKED, unfolded at its call sites and never materialised, with a transitive closure so that anything positively depending on it is unfolded too. Second, the semirings - `infinitely many` is a LEGAL CARRIER VALUE rather than an overflow, so a counting fold on a cycle honestly answers infinite, which is exactly Python naming itertools.count rather than evaluating it. Third, hole(Id, budget_exhausted) - a truncation that says why. And SCC detection is implemented in src/semiring.ts, with the precision already recorded that cycle membership implies an infinite count ONLY for a non-idempotent plus, or it cries wolf on every recursive Boolean program including our own reach and sees. WHERE IT ACTUALLY BREAKS, and the engine states it in its own words at src/engine.ts:678: `the alternating fixpoint cannot assume a demand-backed relation - a relation unfolded at its call sites is materialized as a side effect of matching, so no round can hold it fixed`. The well-founded computation needs to run a GENEROUS round and a MEAN round and compare them, which requires the relation to STAND STILL between rounds; a lazily unfolded relation is born as a side effect of matching and cannot be held. SO LAZINESS AND THREE-VALUED EVALUATION ARE MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE IN THIS ENGINE TODAY, and under rounds and stratification laziness already works. THE SAME WALL EXISTS IN PYTHON AND IS SIMPLY NOT DIAGNOSED THERE: `x in itertools.count()` terminates and `x not in itertools.count()` HANGS FOR EVER, because negation demands exhaustion. We pay with a refusal at load time that names the relation; Python pays with a hang. Ours is stricter, not weaker. THE OPEN QUESTION, which is what makes this future work rather than a limit: XSB-style SLG resolution answers it with subgoal completion detection, and our demand relations are typically SHALLOW, so a cheap completion test may be enough to let the two coexist. Nobody has measured how deep they actually go. THAT MEASUREMENT IS THE FIRST STEP AND IT IS CHEAP - it is a property of programs we already have.").

finding(f_string_destructors_are_free_and_constructors_are_not, decision).
recorded(f_string_destructors_are_free_and_constructors_are_not, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_string_destructors_are_free_and_constructors_are_not, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_string_destructors_are_free_and_constructors_are_not, "THE OWNER ASKED FOR A DECISION ON STRING OPERATIONS AND THIS IS IT, with the line drawn where it can be checked rather than argued. The kernel's whole builtin vocabulary is src/reflect.ts:128 - `=`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `is` - so NO RULE CAN TAKE A STRING APART, which is why module resolution had to borrow str_seg, str_char0 and str_scheme from the host as a declared loan. THE DECISION: DESTRUCTORS MAY ENTER THE KERNEL, CONSTRUCTORS MAY NOT. The criterion is the Herbrand universe and it is a proof rather than a preference. A destructor - cut on a separator, first character, prefix before a colon, length, character at an index - yields a SUBSTRING OF A STRING THE PROGRAM ALREADY HOLDS, and the set of all substrings of all strings in a program is FINITE: a string of length L has at most L(L+1)/2 of them. So the universe stays finite and the fixpoint still terminates BY CONSTRUCTION. A constructor - concatenation, repetition - yields a string that was not there, and the result can be fed back in, so the universe is infinite. That is the SAME defect as `N is M + 1`, which is already the ONE place this kernel stops being finite by construction. Destructors add no second such place; a constructor would. PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE: str_seg, str_segs, str_char0, str_scheme, str_len are admissible kernel builtins; str_concat and str_repeat are not, and if one is ever genuinely needed it goes through the host as a declared host_emitter row that the audit can count. TIMING IS SEPARATE FROM FORM AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT DECIDED HERE: the resolver is moving to the host as an OBSERVATION (variant B-plus-trace, chosen by the owner the same day), so the rules-side path parser survives only as a SECOND INDEPENDENT MODEL for cross-checking. If that cross-check turns out to be worth keeping, the destructors are worth adding; if the host observation makes it redundant, the loan can simply stay a loan. The measurement that decides it is the divergence count between the two models, which is being produced now. AND THE TEST THAT MUST ACCOMPANY ANY IMPLEMENTATION IS ALREADY NAMED: a program using only destructors must reach a fixpoint where the term set STOPS GROWING, and the same program with a concatenation must be shown to grow without bound - both directions, or the criterion is decoration.").


finding(f_i_stopped_at_describing_the_decision_and_called_it_made, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_stopped_at_describing_the_decision_and_called_it_made, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_i_stopped_at_describing_the_decision_and_called_it_made, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_i_stopped_at_describing_the_decision_and_called_it_made, "Caught by the owner, in four words and a laugh: are you adding it or waiting for me. I had laid out the shape axis in full - the facts, the applicability declaration, the derived cell rule, the numbers it would move - and then stopped, as though the layout were the work. It is the SAME defect recorded this morning as building the scoreboard instead of playing, in its second form: there the substitute for work was a checker, here it is a DESCRIPTION. Both feel finished for the same reason - a decision written out has the shape of a completed thought, and nothing in the writing distinguishes `decided and queued` from `decided and done`. SELF-CHECK, run rather than assumed, sample = every decision I made in this stretch: FOUR. The resolver went to an executor immediately - fine. String destructors: form decided, TIMING EXPLICITLY DEFERRED with the measurement that would settle it named - fine, because deferral was stated. Laziness: recorded as future work with its first cheap step named - fine, same reason. The shape axis: described and left - THE ONLY MISS, one in four. So the tendency is real and MUCH WEAKER THAN IT FELT the moment it was pointed out, and the three that were fine share one property the miss lacks: EACH SAID OUT LOUD WHAT WAS AND WAS NOT BEING DONE NOW. THE RULE, and it is cheap because it needs no new machinery: a decision stated in an answer gets, in the SAME turn, either an executor or a sentence saying it is deferred and what would un-defer it. The ledger already derives `ready(F)` in rules/findings.rofl:29 - the findings that nothing blocks - and I had not looked at it after recording the decision. Looking at ready after writing a decision is the check; it exists already and I did not run it.").

finding(f_a_number_makes_a_limit_claim_look_unarguable, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_number_makes_a_limit_claim_look_unarguable, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_number_makes_a_limit_claim_look_unarguable, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_number_makes_a_limit_claim_look_unarguable, "FIFTH TIME IN A DAY, and the owner caught it again: `you only tried one approach`. I said a rules-written parser is expressible but impractical for a file, because CYK is cubic and boot.rofl is about 3000 tokens giving 2.7e10 operations. MEASURED AFTERWARDS, BOTH HALVES WERE WRONG. boot.rofl has 409 TOKENS, not 3000 - I divided bytes by four instead of counting, and the file is four fifths comments, which is this repository's own style. And it is 24 GRAMMATICAL UNITS, because a ROFL fact ends in a period, so the input is not one tree of 409 tokens but twenty-four trees whose worst is 32. Sum of n cubed: 187,513. Not 2.7e10. THE TWO ERRORS COMPOUND MULTIPLICATIVELY - seven times wrong on n is 350 times on n cubed, and lumping the units into one tree is another 365 - so the figure that closed the direction was off by about five orders of magnitude. WHY THE EXISTING RULE DID NOT FIRE, and this is the transferable part. The rule I already carry says: before calling something unverifiable, name the method you exhausted and ask whether there is a second. IT DID NOT FIRE BECAUSE I NEVER SAID IMPOSSIBLE - I GAVE A NUMBER. `Impossible` invites the question why; `2.7e10 operations` invites agreement, because a figure wears the clothes of a measurement. A NUMBER IS THE MOST EFFECTIVE WAY TO MAKE AN UNCHECKED ASSUMPTION UNARGUABLE, and it is more dangerous than the bare claim it replaced. THE GATE, and it costs nothing because the machinery exists: A FIGURE OFFERED AS PROOF OF IMPRACTICALITY MUST CARRY THE SAME PROVENANCE A FINDING CARRIES - for each quantity in it, measured or guessed, and WHICH STRUCTURAL ASSUMPTION PRODUCED IT plus what happens when that assumption is dropped. Here: 3000 was guessed (measured 409) and `the whole file is one tree` was an assumption nobody required (dropping it: 365x). This ledger already has `witness(F, Query, N)` - a premise that can be RUN - and a figure in a limit claim deserves exactly that. A number without a witness is an opinion with a digit in it.").

finding(f_splitting_helps_exactly_as_much_as_the_language_is_already_split, insight).
recorded(f_splitting_helps_exactly_as_much_as_the_language_is_already_split, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_splitting_helps_exactly_as_much_as_the_language_is_already_split, doc).
finding_note(f_splitting_helps_exactly_as_much_as_the_language_is_already_split, "Measured across two languages after the owner asked why a file must go through one fixpoint at all. SPLITTING BY GRAMMATICAL UNIT IS NOT A UNIFORM WIN, and the difference between the two cases is the finding. FOR ROFL IT IS TOTAL: a fact ends in a period, so the language is already split by construction. boot.rofl is 24 units, worst 32 tokens; facts/spec.rofl is 361 units of 3665 tokens, worst 13, sum of cubes 436,325 - instant, and there is no wall at all. FOR JAVASCRIPT IT BARELY MOVES: src/engine.ts has 22 top-level statements over 7296 nodes, and ONE of them is 6729 nodes, because a class declaration is a single top-level statement. Sum of cubes 3.05e11 against 3.88e11 for the whole file: a saving of 1.27 times, which is nothing. SO THE RULE IS: SPLITTING BUYS EXACTLY AS MUCH AS THE LANGUAGE IS ALREADY SPLIT, and the owner's instinct about an explicit semicolon is the general form of it - a language with a cheap terminator is a language you can parse in pieces. What this settles for the programme: a rules-written parser for ROFL ITSELF has no complexity problem whatsoever and the only missing piece is string destructors. For JavaScript the cubic method needs a DIFFERENT idea rather than a finer chop, and there are at least two, both already in this engine: TOKEN PER TICK, where a shift-reduce stack carried through @next makes the cost LINEAR and removes the exponent entirely rather than shrinking it - and this repository already runs ticks and already argues that a sense-decide-act loop is acyclic in time; and DEMAND-DRIVEN top-down, which is Earley, where only reachable chart cells are ever built and the engine's demand-backed relations already do exactly that unfolding.").

finding(f_the_environment_is_a_ledger_and_two_of_them_disagree_in_three_places, insight).
recorded(f_the_environment_is_a_ledger_and_two_of_them_disagree_in_three_places, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_environment_is_a_ledger_and_two_of_them_disagree_in_three_places, doc).
finding_note(f_the_environment_is_a_ledger_and_two_of_them_disagree_in_three_places, "Variant B-plus-trace delivered, and the owner's reason for it - the code is local now, how will it resolve in production - turned out to be measurable rather than rhetorical. TWO ENVIRONMENTS DISAGREE ON THREE OF ELEVEN PLACES, and one of the three is the sharp case: `./pick` resolves to `pick.ts` under a TypeScript loader and to `pick/index.js` under plain node - BOTH ANSWER, and they answer DIFFERENTLY. The other two resolve under one environment and not the other. So the environment is not a decorative axis; the same source text has two different module graphs depending on where it runs. AND IT IS A LEGITIMATE LEDGER by our own docs/choosing-perspectives.md, which admits a viewpoint and forbids a status: `in whose book is this entry` answers cleanly - in the book of this configuration - and the mutant that collapsed both environments into one perspective proved the slot is load-bearing rather than ornamental, because `answer_ambiguous[audit]` immediately fired twice: one book, two answers for one place. THE TRACE IS THE PAYLOAD. `why resolves_to[env_plain_node](a.ts:8:./pick, pick/index.js)` returns twenty-eight lines that walk the ACTUAL SEARCH: tried `pick` (a directory), `pick.js` (miss), `pick.json` (miss), `pick.node` (miss), then `pick/index.js` (file), with the mechanism named as index_file. The answer STANDS ON THE FAILED ATTEMPTS and they are in the tree. Under the mutant that records only successful attempts, `why` does not merely get shorter - THE CONCLUSION STOPS HOLDING AT ALL, which is the strongest possible statement that the failures are load-bearing rather than decoration. Thirteen audit gates, each with a planted defect; all six briefed mutants killed. RULES AGAINST HOST: agreement 14, DIVERGENCE 8 - a measured distance to a target rather than an opinion about one, which is exactly what the owner asked for when he said the parser can be brought up to the oracle as future work.").

finding(f_the_case_for_string_destructors_moved_and_the_old_case_weakened, decision).
recorded(f_the_case_for_string_destructors_moved_and_the_old_case_weakened, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_case_for_string_destructors_moved_and_the_old_case_weakened, decision).
finding_note(f_the_case_for_string_destructors_moved_and_the_old_case_weakened, "An honest revision of a decision recorded three hours earlier, forced by a measurement rather than by argument. I decided that destructors may enter the kernel and constructors may not - THAT PART STANDS, and its proof stands: the set of all substrings of a program's strings is finite, so a destructor keeps the Herbrand universe finite while a constructor makes it infinite, the same defect as `N is M + 1` which is already the ONE place this kernel stops being finite by construction. WHAT CHANGED IS THE JUSTIFICATION FOR DOING IT, and it moved in both directions at once. THE OLD REASON WEAKENED. I said the rules-side path parser survives as a second independent model, and destructors are worth adding if that cross-check is worth keeping. It diverges from the host on EIGHT of twenty-two rows, and I read every one: `#sub` needs a package.json imports map, `@babel/parser` needs the node_modules walk, `./pick` and `./onlyts` and `./idx2` need extension guessing and index files. NOT ONE OF THE EIGHT IS BLOCKED ON STRING OPERATIONS. The host already supplies str_seg and str_char0 and they are sufficient; what the rules lack is KNOWLEDGE OF NODE'S SEARCH ALGORITHM and the ability to read the disk. So closing that gap is reimplementing a large living specification, not adding string primitives, and the second model is worth keeping as a CROSS-CHECK rather than as a target. THE NEW REASON IS STRONGER AND CAME FROM THE OWNER'S PARSER QUESTION. Parsing needs no string construction at all - only decomposition - and measured on this tree a rules-written parser for ROFL ITSELF has no complexity problem whatsoever: 361 units and 3665 tokens in facts/spec.rofl give a sum of cubes of 436,325. Rules are already data here; syntax is not. Destructors are the missing third of self-application, and that is a much better reason than a path parser we have now decided not to chase. THE DECISION THIS DEMANDS is therefore no longer `when do we need them for resolution` but `is closing self-application worth a kernel change`, which is a different question and belongs to the owner.").

finding(f_a_single_hop_crossing_licenses_itself_and_the_leak_audit_is_blind_to_it, defect).
recorded(f_a_single_hop_crossing_licenses_itself_and_the_leak_audit_is_blind_to_it, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_single_hop_crossing_licenses_itself_and_the_leak_audit_is_blind_to_it, decision).
finding_note(f_a_single_hop_crossing_licenses_itself_and_the_leak_audit_is_blind_to_it, "FOUND BY AN EXECUTOR AS AN HONEST NEGATIVE - it reported that leak[audit] CANNOT FIRE on its layer rather than reporting the layer green - and it is about the KERNEL, not about that layer. VERIFIED BY ME BEFORE RECORDING, three probes against boot.rofl with nothing else loaded. ONE HOP, `digest[report](X) :- datum[secret](X)`, nothing declared: leak 0, crossing 0 - THE AUDIT IS SILENT. TWO HOPS through a middle ledger, nothing declared: leak 1, crossing 1 - caught. AND THE CONTROL THAT MAKES IT A DEFECT RATHER THAN A DESIGN: the one-hop case with `imports(report, secret)` EXPLICITLY DECLARED reads 0 and 0 as well, so the declared and the undeclared single hop are INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM THE AUDIT. THE MECHANISM IS AT src/reflect.ts:317 - `if (c.head.perspExplicit && pk !== canonTerm(headP)) push bridge_decl` - so writing a rule whose head names a perspective and whose body reads a different one EMITS ITS OWN LICENCE. THE LICENCE IS GRANTED BY THE VERY ACT THAT NEEDS IT. Ten bridge_decl rows exist in a bare boot.rofl for this reason. WHAT IS DEFENSIBLE ABOUT IT, stated so the decision is fair: an explicit head perspective plausibly means the author is deliberately building a bridge, and the audit is then aimed at the accident rather than the intention. But if that is the design, `imports` and `bridge_decl` are DOCUMENTATION rather than checks over single hops, and leak guards only implicit multi-hop walks - which is a far narrower claim than boot.rofl's comment makes, since that comment argues the property is TRANSITIVE and every step separately licensed does not license the walk. THE CLASS IS THE ONE THIS DAY KEEPS PRODUCING, and this is the FOURTH instance: a gate that cannot say no in some class, invisible because the class never produces a red. text_check banned NUL only; orphan_claim did not generalise to a new relation; my run-completion guard matched the wrong reporter format; and now leak is blind to every single-hop crossing by construction. ALL FOUR WERE FOUND BY SOMEONE ELSE. The decision owed: either single-hop crossings must be licensed by something a human WROTE rather than by the shape of the rule, or the audit's own comment must be corrected to say what it actually guards.").

finding(f_node_returns_a_warm_cache_answer_with_an_empty_trace, insight).
recorded(f_node_returns_a_warm_cache_answer_with_an_empty_trace, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_node_returns_a_warm_cache_answer_with_an_empty_trace, doc).
finding_note(f_node_returns_a_warm_cache_answer_with_an_empty_trace, "Three smaller results from the resolver work, kept because each is a fact about an instrument rather than about the subject. FIRST AND BEST: node's `Module._pathCache`, once warm, RETURNS THE ANSWER WITH AN EMPTY SEARCH TRACE, which is byte-for-byte indistinguishable from the empty trace a builtin legitimately produces. That is RULE 2 INSIDE NODE ITSELF - the same output means `nothing was searched because nothing needed searching` and `nothing was searched because the answer was remembered`. The observer would have recorded confident answers standing on no evidence, and every one would have passed the answer_without_trace gate for the wrong reason. Neutralised by clearing the cache per question, and the executor measured that rather than assuming it. SECOND: the environment axis is ONE OF TWO, and the missing one is blocked by a fact about node rather than by preference - export CONDITIONS come from --conditions and are read ONCE PER PROCESS, so varying them requires a child process per environment. Declared in the facts rather than left silent. THIRD: forged[audit] reached 1 through 409 then 9. The 409 were the scanner's own authority being unsigned, the 9 were host tables landing in [main]; both numbers are recorded beside their fixes. A relation that reports four hundred rows on a healthy tree is not a check, it is noise, and it took two rounds to find the actual signal. FOURTH, and it is a self-catch worth copying: two of that executor's own gates rejected their author - `mechanism_unexercised` found that a mis-bounded package.json walk-up meant exports_map NEVER FIRED ONCE, and the first version of its site-removal mutant deleted a line, renumbering every site below it and reporting 8 unseen places for ONE removed import, which would have credited the gate with a precision it had not demonstrated.").

finding(f_the_shape_axis_works_and_moves_the_hole_one_level_deeper, insight).
recorded(f_the_shape_axis_works_and_moves_the_hole_one_level_deeper, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_shape_axis_works_and_moves_the_hole_one_level_deeper, doc).
finding_note(f_the_shape_axis_works_and_moves_the_hole_one_level_deeper, "The third axis is in, as facts and not as code, and it does what it was for. `member_expression` at the call-graph layer was ONE TICK; it is now THIRTEEN ROWS, one per job - s_member_on_ident MODELLED, and twelve not_modelled each carrying its own reason, which are NOT all the same reason: s_computed_dynamic_key is runtime_dependent and therefore irreducible, s_member_on_array is out_of_scope, the other ten are not_yet. Cells 28 to 97 with layers unchanged. THE PREDICTION WAS MADE ON PAPER FIRST, as required, and the interesting half is where it MISSED: fine-grained was predicted exactly at 97 = 19 + 5 + 73, and coarse was predicted 82 = 19 + 5 + 56 while measuring 82 = 21 + 5 + 56 - two more modelled than predicted, because a kind can be modelled through a shape the coarse view collapses away. A prediction that matches teaches nothing; this one located a place where the two granularities are not merely coarser and finer but disagree about what counts as done. THE RESULT THAT MATTERS MOST IS NOT THE SUCCESS. Of the eleven edges the execution oracle saw and the model missed, FIVE land on a not_modelled shape of member_expression - those the new axis explains and enumerates - and ONE LANDS INSIDE ITS SINGLE MODELLED SHAPE, s_member_on_ident. That is the higher-order call: the callee IS a plain identifier, and it names a PARAMETER rather than a declaration. SO REFINING THE AXIS DID NOT REMOVE THE PROBLEM, IT MOVED IT ONE LEVEL DOWN - the hole used to be inside a kind, now it is inside a shape, and the reason is that both axes are SYNTACTIC while this gap is about what a name holds at run time. No further syntactic refinement reaches it; it needs the scope layer, which is a different dimension rather than a finer one.").

finding(f_the_partition_stops_being_a_detector_when_a_third_axis_arrives, defect).
recorded(f_the_partition_stops_being_a_detector_when_a_third_axis_arrives, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_partition_stops_being_a_detector_when_a_third_axis_arrives, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_partition_stops_being_a_detector_when_a_third_axis_arrives, "Reported by the executor as two SLEPT THROUGHs rather than buried, and it is a real regression in a check I had trusted. With two axes the partition - modelled plus waived plus not_modelled equals cell - was a genuine second detector: it broke under a ghost claim and under a deleted default rule, and that is why it earned its own mutant this morning. WITH THREE AXES IT STOPS SEEING TWO WHOLE CLASSES. A shape declared over a kind that does not exist: the partition SLEPT THROUGH, and only new relations orphan_shape and invented_cell caught it. A kind counted BOTH through its shapes and through the none branch: the partition SLEPT THROUGH again, and only double_cell caught it, naming fifteen rows. WHY, and it generalises beyond this table: a partition is an ARITHMETIC identity over one index, and when the index gains a component the identity can stay true while the SET UNDER IT is wrong - fifteen doubled cells and fifteen phantom cells sum exactly as well as thirty honest ones. THE SAME SHAPE AS TODAY'S OTHER FOUR: a check that cannot say no in a class it was never aimed at, invisible because that class never turns it red. It joins text_check banning only NUL, orphan_claim not generalising to a new relation, my run-completion guard matching the wrong reporter format, and leak being blind to single-hop crossings by construction. FIVE IN ONE DAY, EVERY ONE FOUND BY SOMEONE ELSE. THE REPAIR THAT ACTUALLY GENERALISES is the one already recorded as an open decision: derive the vocabulary and disjointness checks ONCE from the rules by reflection, rather than writing orphan_X and double_X by hand for each new relation over the same index. Every axis added will otherwise reopen exactly these two holes, and the next one will be found by an executor too.").

finding(f_my_brief_carried_a_wrong_axis_row_and_the_executor_refused_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_my_brief_carried_a_wrong_axis_row_and_the_executor_refused_it, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_my_brief_carried_a_wrong_axis_row_and_the_executor_refused_it, doc).
finding_note(f_my_brief_carried_a_wrong_axis_row_and_the_executor_refused_it, "I wrote `axis_applies(shape, dataflow)` into the brief as part of a sketch, alongside a sentence saying the sketch was INTENT AND NOT AN ORDER and naming the three properties that must not change. The executor took me at my word, tried the row, and REFUSED IT AS ITS OWN MUTANT 2b: declaring the call-graph shape vocabulary applicable to the dataflow layer takes cells from 97 to 112 AND PRODUCES ZERO ANSWERS, because those shapes classify a CALLEE POSITION and dataflow has no callee position. So the row was not merely unnecessary, it was the exact defect the applicability declaration exists to prevent - a LOCAL axis multiplied across a layer where it is meaningless, which is the arithmetic that produced 48 empty cells earlier today. TWO THINGS THIS PAYS FOR. First, the escape clause was load-bearing: had the brief said `use these rows` the executor would have been correct to obey and the model would carry fifteen meaningless cells with no gate objecting, since I would also not have asked for the mutant that catches it. A brief that names its INVARIANTS and leaves the sketch negotiable is not weaker than a precise one, it is stronger, because the executor can be right where the author was wrong. Second, my own guardrail from this morning - the denominator is the grammar, the work order is the corpus - has a sibling I had not stated: A VOCABULARY IS SCOPED TO THE QUESTION IT ANSWERS, and shape answers `what is in the callee position`, which is a call-graph question and not a dataflow one.").

finding(f_the_string_decision_is_reversed_in_one_half_and_stands_in_the_other, decision).
recorded(f_the_string_decision_is_reversed_in_one_half_and_stands_in_the_other, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_string_decision_is_reversed_in_one_half_and_stands_in_the_other, doc).
finding_note(f_the_string_decision_is_reversed_in_one_half_and_stands_in_the_other, "The executor flagged that two of MY OWN entries now contradict what was just built, and it is right to have flagged it: string destructors are in the kernel, while f_no_string_builtins_and_the_boundary_stays records `DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-31: no string builtins`, closed and addressed_by src/unify.ts, and f_no_string_builtins_moves_why_boundary is dismissed with the reason `decided_boundary_stays`, which no longer holds. NEITHER IS BEING REWRITTEN - a ledger is a journal and a superseded entry is evidence, not an error. WHAT IS REVERSED: the operational half. Destructors exist, `is` reaches them, the why-boundary can move, and the dismissal reason of the second entry has expired. WHAT IS NOT REVERSED, AND IT IS THE MORE VALUABLE HALF: the ARGUMENT that killed my case in August. I had claimed that without string operations a model cannot catch a broken tokenizer, and Vadim's refutation was that RULES REWRITING THE SAME AUTHOR'S UNDERSTANDING OF THE SAME SPEC ARE A DUPLICATE RATHER THAN AN ORACLE - a model re-deriving a field from the raw line uses THE SAME FIELD INDEX, so a wrong index is wrong in both and they agree while both are wrong. That is untouched and it bites the new work directly: A RULES-WRITTEN PARSER FOR ROFL WILL NOT BE AN ORACLE FOR src/parser.ts. Both encode one person's reading of one grammar, and a shared misreading produces agreement. So the justification for today's change must NOT be `now we can cross-check the parser`, and I nearly wrote exactly that. THE JUSTIFICATION THAT SURVIVES is different in kind and was the owner's own: self-application where it is efficient, clean and beautiful AT THE SAME TIME - a why-tree for a parse, a `whynot` for a parse failure, AMBIGUITY AS A COUNTED NUMBER rather than a bug found by testing, and a grammar that is data and therefore auditable by the checks we already own. None of those is a differential check, and none of them needs one. WHAT DIFFERENTIAL VALUE REMAINS IS SMALL AND HONEST: running both parsers over 350-plus real .rofl files finds DIVERGENCES ON CASES THE AUTHOR NEVER HELD IN MIND, which is weaker than an independent oracle and stronger than nothing.").

finding(f_the_budget_counts_steps_and_memory_goes_first, defect).
recorded(f_the_budget_counts_steps_and_memory_goes_first, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_budget_counts_steps_and_memory_goes_first, decision).
finding_note(f_the_budget_counts_steps_and_memory_goes_first, "Found by the executor as a SIDE OBSERVATION while measuring the negative half of the finiteness criterion, and it is a defect in the kernel rather than in its work. The budget is what turns an unbounded computation into an honest `hole(Id, budget_exhausted)`, and it COUNTS STEPS. A string constructor makes terms that GROW IN LENGTH as well as in number, so at a budget of one million the host dies of memory - OOM, rc 134 - BEFORE the step counter reaches its wall. The third value is supposed to be the guarantee that this engine degrades by SAYING something rather than by stopping; here it degrades by being killed, and nothing is said at all. Measured cleanly at smaller budgets, where the mechanism does work as designed: 5k steps gives 2289 terms and budget_exhausted, 10k gives 6952, 20k gives 16397, each one step past the wall and each announcing itself. So the wall is real and it is simply in the wrong units for this class of program. THE CONTROL THAT MAKES THIS A FINDING RATHER THAN A GUESS was inside the same run: with the concatenation patch INSTALLED but the concatenating rule removed, the program is still 19 terms and 89 steps, so what changes behaviour is the RULE and not the instrumentation. RELEVANCE BEYOND STRINGS: this is not a string problem, it is the general case of a term whose SIZE grows, and arithmetic on big integers has the same shape. It does not block the destructors - they cannot grow a term - but it is exactly the class of program a user would reach for on the day a constructor is added through the host.").

finding(f_a_destructor_in_a_comparison_is_silently_false, defect).
recorded(f_a_destructor_in_a_comparison_is_silently_false, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_destructor_in_a_comparison_is_silently_false, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_destructor_in_a_comparison_is_silently_false, "Reported by the executor as a boundary rather than left for someone to trip over: `str_len(S) < 5` NEVER HOLDS, silently. The comparison branch calls the arithmetic evaluator WITHOUT a failure receiver - a documented decision recorded in the README long before destructors existed - so a destructor appearing there yields nothing and emits no hole. The working form is two premises, `N is str_len(S), N < 5`. THIS IS RULE 2 REAPPEARING INSIDE THE LANGUAGE, in the newest thing in it: an empty result that means `the instrument declined` is indistinguishable from one that means `the comparison is false`. The repository has already paid for exactly this once - f_a_type_error_is_not_a_false_premise, where `N is \"hello\" + 1` produced no rows and no diagnostic - and the fix there was to give the evaluator a failure out-parameter and emit hole(rule, arith_type_error). The comparison branch never got the same treatment, and until today nothing could reach it: every operand of a comparison was a plain term or an arithmetic expression that could not fail interestingly. Adding destructors made the branch reachable and therefore made the silence observable. SECOND BOUNDARY FROM THE SAME REPORT, smaller: an operand list takes TERMS, not expressions, so `str_seg(S, Sep, N - 1)` is a PARSE ERROR rather than a wrong answer - which is the good failure mode, and the last segment is written in two steps. Both boundaries are documented in the code; the first is a defect and the second is a limitation.").

finding(f_four_of_fifteen_mutants_came_from_probing_for_blind_zones, insight).
recorded(f_four_of_fifteen_mutants_came_from_probing_for_blind_zones, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_four_of_fifteen_mutants_came_from_probing_for_blind_zones, doc).
finding_note(f_four_of_fifteen_mutants_came_from_probing_for_blind_zones, "The fourth executor in a row to extend its own mutant set past the brief, and the first to say explicitly WHERE the extra ones came from. Six were briefed and all six died. Nine more were invented; five died immediately. FOUR SLEPT THROUGH ON THE FIRST TRY, and all four came from a different question than `what else could I break` - they came from `WHERE IS THIS CHECK STRUCTURALLY UNABLE TO LOOK`. They were: reflection scanning only the RIGHT side of `is`, so an operation on the left is invisible to uses_builtin; every mode list declared [in, in, ...] uniformly, which leaves the audit green while the table LIES about which arguments are outputs; an off-by-one at the far boundary, `i > len` instead of `i >= len`, where the host returns undefined and the operation would have manufactured a term out of nothing; and an empty separator being accepted by two of the five operations. Each was closed by an added assertion, and the final count is 15 of 15 killed. THE TRANSFERABLE PART IS THE QUESTION, not the count. `What else could I break` produces mutants in the region the author was already thinking about, which is why five of nine died on contact and taught nothing. `Where can this check not look` produces mutants OUTSIDE it, and that is where all four survivors were. This repository's rule already says a brief must ask the executor to measure what the oracle cannot see; today it was carried out four times over by an executor asking it of ITSELF, unprompted, and it is the single highest-yield practice observed in this session.").

finding(f_every_operation_that_can_fail_must_be_able_to_say_so_everywhere, decision).
recorded(f_every_operation_that_can_fail_must_be_able_to_say_so_everywhere, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_every_operation_that_can_fail_must_be_able_to_say_so_everywhere, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_every_operation_that_can_fail_must_be_able_to_say_so_everywhere, "THE INVARIANT THE OWNER ASKED FOR, stated so it can be violated: ANY OPERATION THAT CAN FAIL MUST HAVE A WAY TO SAY SO, IN EVERY CONTEXT WHERE IT CAN APPEAR. Today it holds in one context and not in another, and the two are one line apart. `N is str_len(S), N < 5` works; `str_len(S) < 5` is SILENTLY FALSE FOR EVER, because the comparison branch calls the evaluator without a failure receiver while the `is` branch has one and emits hole(rule, reason). WHY THE HOLE EXISTS AT ALL is the part that generalises: the decision to leave comparison without a receiver was made deliberately and documented in the README, and it was HARMLESS AT THE TIME because nothing that could fail interestingly was reachable there - every operand of a comparison was a plain term or arithmetic. Destructors made the branch reachable and thereby made the silence observable. So this is not a bug that was introduced; it is a LATENT hole that a new capability walked into. That is the same shape recorded five times today under a different name - a check inherits the scope of its incident rather than of its class - and it is the sixth instance, this one inside the engine, in a decision I made myself. THE INVARIANT IS THE GENERAL REPAIR: instead of asking `does this branch need a receiver`, require that every context reachable by a failing operation HAS one, and make the requirement testable by enumerating contexts rather than by remembering them. The test that proves it can say no: for each operation and each context, plant an input that must fail and assert a hole is emitted with the right reason - a matrix of operations by contexts, where an empty significant cell is a guaranteed gap, which is the same discipline the language model uses on node kinds.").

blocks(f_a_single_hop_crossing_licenses_itself_and_the_leak_audit_is_blind_to_it, f_every_operation_that_can_fail_must_be_able_to_say_so_everywhere).

finding(f_the_owner_decomposition_relocates_two_of_eleven_misses_out_of_semantics, insight).
recorded(f_the_owner_decomposition_relocates_two_of_eleven_misses_out_of_semantics, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_owner_decomposition_relocates_two_of_eleven_misses_out_of_semantics, decision).
finding_note(f_the_owner_decomposition_relocates_two_of_eleven_misses_out_of_semantics, "The owner proposed a different decomposition from Grafema's seven layers - WHAT is written, HOW it is written, WHAT IT MEANS, the last splitting into relations among the parts, between the expression and its parts, and between the expression and its parent - and doubted the third of those. Tested by laying ALL ELEVEN misses the execution oracle found against it, reading the fixture code rather than recalling it. THE COUNTS: relations among parts 5, expression-through-its-parts 4, context 1, and TWO THAT ARE NOT SEMANTICS AT ALL. THE DOUBT IS NUMERICALLY CONFIRMED AND THE CONCLUSION IS NOT WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE: the parent/context level scores 1 of 11, and the one is `this.get(n)` - but `this` is not a singleton, it is a CLOSED LIST of four, `this`, `super`, `arguments`, `new.target`, plus `self` and implicit receivers in other languages. So that level is not needed as an AXIS, it is needed as an ENUMERATION. An axis is for an open dimension that grows; this one is closed by the grammar and has four members, and an enumeration is an order of magnitude cheaper for the same result. THE LARGEST CLASS IS THE ONE WE DO NOT HAVE AT ALL: four of eleven need the VALUE OF AN EXPRESSION AS A WHOLE, assembled from the values of its parts - `deep.layer.dig(n)`, `mkBox(n).peek()`, `table['pick'](n)`, `ns?.hello(n)`. That is compositionality, and its absence is why `a.b.c()` is a separate uncovered form instead of a recursive application of `a.b()`. A whole day was spent refining the shape axis, and half the hole was never about refinement - it was that an expression HAS NO VALUE and therefore cannot be substituted into the position of its own sub-expression. AND THE RESULT THAT CORRECTS ME DIRECTLY: two of the eleven are not semantic misses at all. `new Box(1)` and `ns?.hello(n)` are NewExpression and OptionalCallExpression, which are simply not in the enumeration of what counts as a call site - level one, WHAT IS WRITTEN. I recorded them this morning as a hole inside a modelled shape; the decomposition caught my own misclassification, and it is the cheapest work on the board: two node kinds added to one rule, no semantics at all.").

finding(f_an_ordinary_three_way_join_kills_the_host_and_the_budget_never_fires, defect).
recorded(f_an_ordinary_three_way_join_kills_the_host_and_the_budget_never_fires, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_an_ordinary_three_way_join_kills_the_host_and_the_budget_never_fires, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_an_ordinary_three_way_join_kills_the_host_and_the_budget_never_fires, "I recorded this as a strings problem and it is not one. The executor observed that a string concatenation kills the host by memory before the step budget fires, and I filed it as a consequence of adding a constructor. WRONG: reproduced with NO constructor of any kind, on a program a beginner would write. `edb(q).` with 200 facts and `tri(X,Y,Z) :- q(X), q(Y), q(Z).` is 200 cubed, eight million facts, and at a budget of FIFTY MILLION steps the process dies with FATAL ERROR heap out of memory, exit 134. The budget was nowhere near exhausted; memory ran out first, and nothing was said. THE THIRD VALUE EXISTS PRECISELY SO THAT THIS ENGINE DEGRADES BY SPEAKING RATHER THAN BY STOPPING, and on the most ordinary shape of Datalog program - a three-variable join over a modest input - it degrades by being killed. `hole(Id, budget_exhausted)` never appears because the counter measures the wrong quantity: steps are a proxy for work and the thing that actually runs out is SPACE. WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND ITS OWN FIX, and this is why it is on the critical path rather than in the backlog: a port to another language inherits this unchanged or worse, because the failure is in the CHOICE OF WHAT TO COUNT and not in the allocator. Porting a kernel whose only guard measures the wrong resource means reproducing the defect in a language where diagnosing it is harder. THE MEASUREMENT THAT SETS THE SIZE OF THE PROBLEM: 200 input facts is not a stress test, it is smaller than several fixtures already in this tree, and three variables in a body is the second example in any Datalog tutorial.").

finding(f_self_licensing_is_not_gone_it_is_now_a_line_someone_typed, insight).
recorded(f_self_licensing_is_not_gone_it_is_now_a_line_someone_typed, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_self_licensing_is_not_gone_it_is_now_a_line_someone_typed, decision).
finding_note(f_self_licensing_is_not_gone_it_is_now_a_line_someone_typed, "The automatic bridge is gone and the numbers say so: bare boot.rofl 9 bridge_decl to ZERO, full tree 122 to ZERO, and the three probes that used to be indistinguishable are now opposite - an undeclared single hop reads leak 1 where it read 0, a declared one reads 0, a two-hop walk still reads its crossings. ONE declaration replaced 122 automatic ones. THE EXECUTOR'S ARGUMENT FOR REMOVING IT IS BETTER THAN MINE AND IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: I reasoned about the inconvenience of a hash rule id; it observed that R JOINED WITH NOTHING - the rule identifier appeared in no other literal of `crossing`, so a `licence on a rule` was always a LICENCE ON A PAIR OF LEDGERS, which is exactly the sentence `imports` speaks. Deleting it loses nothing except one bit, whether the author typed a bracket, and it changes WHO SPEAKS: a rule says what it DOES, a book says what it PERMITS. BUT THE HONEST STATEMENT OF WHAT WAS ACHIEVED IS NARROWER THAN `fixed`, and the executor found it as its own surviving mutant: `imports` IS UNAUTHENTICATED. A program can write its own permission line and leak goes to zero; `forged[audit]` bites only a NAMED author lacking authority, and anonymity is the default across this repository. So self-licensing was not abolished - IT BECAME A LINE SOMEONE TYPED, visible in the source and carried by asserted_by, instead of a line the kernel emitted for every bracket with no author at all. That is a real improvement and it is not the same as a closed class. The open decision is whether a permission must be authenticated, which is the same question already recorded as `anonymity is recorded, not judged`, arriving here from a second direction. Two further survivors, both pre-existing and neither caused nor widened by the change: a ledger reachable only through an argument column, and a host writing content straight into a book under that book's own authority - no rule, so no flow, so leak is silent, and the author is legitimate, so forged is silent.").

finding(f_there_is_no_instrument_for_a_nameless_reader_of_a_named_book, question).
recorded(f_there_is_no_instrument_for_a_nameless_reader_of_a_named_book, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_there_is_no_instrument_for_a_nameless_reader_of_a_named_book, decision).
finding_note(f_there_is_no_instrument_for_a_nameless_reader_of_a_named_book, "Found while closing the corpus after bridges: 38 declarations across 10 programs bring the whole tree back to leak 0, and THIRTY-SIX OF THEM ARE MECHANICAL. Two are not, and I refused them: `imports($var(\"G\"), main)` in goof and `imports($var(\"A\"), world)` in npc. They work, and what they license is THE SPELLING OF A VARIABLE - a rule polymorphic in its head book gets permission because of the letter its variable happens to carry, and renaming that variable silently withdraws the licence. THIS IS THE EXACT MIRROR OF THE CASE `collects` WAS INVENTED FOR. `collects(X)` exists because `imports(To, From)` relates two REGISTERED perspectives, and a rule polymorphic in the SOURCE reads a `$var` that can never be the From of an import - so a gathering rule was a permanent finding whose only remedy was a paragraph in a README. The same hole exists on the other side and has no instrument: a NAMELESS READER of a NAMED book. Left deliberately red and named, because an open gap in the language is better than one closed with the wrong tool - and the wrong tool here would be actively harmful, since a licence keyed to a variable's spelling is worse than no licence: it looks like a decision and is an accident.").

addressed_by(f_bracketing_the_head_silences_the_leak_audit, "src/reflect.ts").

finding(f_i_announced_in_advance_how_i_would_read_an_empty_result, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_announced_in_advance_how_i_would_read_an_empty_result, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_i_announced_in_advance_how_i_would_read_an_empty_result, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_i_announced_in_advance_how_i_would_read_an_empty_result, "Caught by an executor, and it is a sharper failure than the usual one. I ran `find -newermt -12 minutes`, got nothing, and concluded the executor had not started. THE MEASUREMENT WAS CORRECT AND THE INFERENCE WAS WRONG: it had applied everything between 14:09 and 14:12, and my window began after that, so the emptiness meant FINISHED AND THEN BLOCKED, not NOT STARTED. Worse than the ordinary version of this mistake: I had written to the executor, in advance, `silence I will read as not started` - so I did not violate the rule that an empty result is a property of the probe until proven otherwise, I REPEALED IT OUT LOUD, choosing one of two readings before looking. Declaring how you will read an absence converts a genuinely ambiguous signal into a decision already taken, and it feels like clarity while doing the opposite. AND THE EXECUTOR HAD THE CONTROL I LACKED: it checked a file it had NOT touched - examples/blam/blam.rofl, mtime 30 August - to prove that stat distinguishes touched from untouched at all. That is the positive control inside the measurement, on the METHOD rather than on the subject, and it is what turns `nothing changed` from an interpretation into a fact. THE RULE THIS BUYS: a time window is a fact about the WINDOW as much as about the tree, and the cheap repair is to widen it past every plausible start or to name a file that must have changed and check that one first. Never announce in advance how an absence will be read.").

finding(f_main_is_a_namespace_and_a_ledger_at_the_same_time, defect).
recorded(f_main_is_a_namespace_and_a_ledger_at_the_same_time, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_main_is_a_namespace_and_a_ledger_at_the_same_time, decision).
finding_note(f_main_is_a_namespace_and_a_ledger_at_the_same_time, "Found by the bridges executor while closing the corpus, and I did not predict it. With automatic bridging removed, examples/sensors keeps two irreducible rows - `main -> trust` and `main -> verified` - and they are not closable by any honest declaration. THE CAUSE IS THAT [main] DOES TWO JOBS AT ONCE. A relation written without a bracket lands in [main] because that is the DEFAULT, and [main] is also the book that holds a program's principal content. So the flow graph cannot tell `this fact has no ledger of its own` from `this fact belongs to the main ledger`, and a helper relation with no bracket looks exactly like content crossing out of the main book. IT IS NOT A NEW DEFECT AND THAT IS THE INTERESTING PART: the old audit reported the same walk by its FAR end, `main -> verified`, so the walk was always there; removing the automatic bridge only moved the report to where the walk actually STARTS. A fix that named the far end would have been a fix to the symptom. THE NARROW REPAIR EXISTS AND WAS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: give the two helper relations a derived book of their own. The executor refused it on the right grounds - it changes what every program over that layer computes, which is far beyond closing two rows. Same argument it recorded as a caveat against a wider `collects(main)` in the inquiry layer: a declaration broader than the two rules needing it. THE DECISION OWED is whether the default ledger should be DISTINCT from the principal one - a nameless default that no rule may cross out of, against today's [main] which is both - and it is a language decision rather than a repair.").

finding(f_the_probe_i_diagnosed_with_was_itself_refusing_to_run, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_probe_i_diagnosed_with_was_itself_refusing_to_run, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_probe_i_diagnosed_with_was_itself_refusing_to_run, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_probe_i_diagnosed_with_was_itself_refusing_to_run, "The cleanest instance of RULE 2 this repository has produced, and it is mine. I used `find . -newermt \"-5 minutes\"` THREE TIMES today to decide whether the tree was moving, and each time read the empty output as `nothing changed`. THE COMMAND IS A SYNTAX ERROR. Run with a positive control - touch a file, then look for it - it prints `bfs: error: Invalid timestamp` and finds nothing, ever. It was not searching and finding nothing; IT WAS REFUSING, and the refusal went to stderr which my pipelines dropped. Three conclusions built on it, all false: that a full test run was measuring a still tree, that an executor had not started work when it had applied twenty-seven declarations, and just now that no core file had moved when two had been rewritten fourteen seconds earlier. The working form on this machine is `-mmin -5`, and it immediately named five files including the two I had just been told about. WHAT MAKES THIS WORTH RECORDING RATHER THAN JUST FIXING: minutes before running the third one, I WROTE A FINDING ABOUT MISREADING AN EMPTY RESULT - about announcing in advance how I would read silence - and I diagnosed the INTERPRETATION while never once checking THE INSTRUMENT THAT PRODUCED THE SILENCE. The diagnosis was correct one level up and blind on the next, which is exactly the shape it was describing. AND THE EXECUTOR HAD THE DEFENCE I LACKED, twice over: it verified `stat` distinguishes touched from untouched by naming a file it had NOT touched and confirming an old mtime. A positive control on the METHOD, not on the subject. THE MECHANICAL REPAIR, and it costs one line: any command whose EMPTY output will become a conclusion must be run once against a case that MUST produce output, in the same invocation. Not as a habit of mind - as a line in the same command, because the habit is what failed three times in one afternoon while its author was writing about it.").

finding(f_main_carries_the_kernels_own_reflection_and_that_is_ring_zero, insight).
recorded(f_main_carries_the_kernels_own_reflection_and_that_is_ring_zero, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_main_carries_the_kernels_own_reflection_and_that_is_ring_zero, decision).
finding_note(f_main_carries_the_kernels_own_reflection_and_that_is_ring_zero, "The owner asked what [main] actually IS - a question I had skipped by default all session - and pushed the Linux analogy: there is a kernel, there are privilege levels, and user code cannot reach into kernel space AT ALL rather than by permission. Measured rather than reasoned, and the answer is sharper than the two roles already recorded. `src/reflect.ts:641` says the FIRST USE of a perspective registers it under kernel authority; `boot.rofl:248` says the reflection facts - concludes, premise_pos, rule, the program's own structure - are WRITTEN BY THE KERNEL INTO [main]. So [main] carries THREE jobs: it is the kernel's own data, it is the program's principal content, and it is the DEFAULT for any literal written without a bracket. The first and the third together are as if /proc and `the file whose owner nobody set` were the same directory. THIS EXPLAINS THREE THINGS WE HAD MET SEPARATELY AND BLINDLY: why `main -> audit` accounts for seventy-odd of the automatic bridges (audits read the KERNEL'S REFLECTION, not the program's data), why sensors shows `main -> trust` as a leak that no honest declaration can close (a bracket-less helper is indistinguishable from content leaving the main book), and why an export from [main] cannot be meaningfully granted - nobody can say WHAT is being exported. WHAT THE ANALOGY ACTUALLY GIVES, and it is not `add permissions`: in Linux ring 0 is unreachable BY CONSTRUCTION and crossings happen only at a narrow declared point. The ring mechanism ALREADY EXISTS here - `$` marks a kernel principal and a caller is refused the whole prefix, measured at 146 literal `who:` values with zero using it - so the ring is built and THE KERNEL HAS NO BOOK OF ITS OWN. AND THE THREE ROLES THE OWNER NAMED - scope, access control, meaning - are NOT the defect; they sit legitimately on one object the way a Unix file carries content, permissions and a path, because all three follow from one decision to create it there. The defect is the FOURTH role, `no book was named`, which follows from the author's SILENCE rather than from any decision, and is therefore agreed with none of the other three.").

finding(f_export_precedes_import_so_the_check_belongs_at_load, insight).
recorded(f_export_precedes_import_so_the_check_belongs_at_load, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_export_precedes_import_so_the_check_belongs_at_load, decision).
finding_note(f_export_precedes_import_so_the_check_belongs_at_load, "The owner's observation, and it upgrades the repair from a report to a refusal. TODAY'S STATE, stated plainly: we replaced the kernel's automatic self-licence with the PROGRAM'S manual self-licence. `imports(audit, main)` says `the book audit declares that it reads main` - AND MAIN WAS NEVER ASKED. The form changed and the substance did not, which is exactly why the hole survived the fix. THE MISSING HALF IS THE PERMISSION FROM THE SIDE BEING TAKEN FROM: `exports(main, audit)` - main permits audit to read it - and the thing that makes it an authentication rather than another honest-author appeal is that ONLY A HOLDER OF authority OVER main CAN WRITE IT. A program cannot grant itself the right to read someone else's book because it lacks the standing, not because it lacks the honesty. THE OWNER'S SECOND POINT IS THE ONE I HAD NOT SEEN: loading here is RINGED - boot, then rules, then facts - so the source is always loaded before the consumer, which means that when a program declaring `imports(Q, P)` arrives, P already exists and its exports are already stated. THEREFORE THE CHECK BELONGS AT LOAD TIME, NOT IN AN AUDIT. An unresolved import becomes a REFUSAL - the program does not run - which is what a compiler does with an unresolved import, rather than a row in a report someone may not read. `load` already refuses programs for other reasons, so the machinery is present. Both halves are wanted and they do different work: exports is the PERMISSION and protects, imports is the DECLARED INTENT and makes the program readable - with only exports, `I read it by accident` is indistinguishable from `I read it on purpose`. The cost is honest: 27 declarations become 54.").

finding(f_a_mutual_import_makes_sees_cyclic_and_changes_a_counting_verdict, insight).
recorded(f_a_mutual_import_makes_sees_cyclic_and_changes_a_counting_verdict, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_mutual_import_makes_sees_cyclic_and_changes_a_counting_verdict, doc).
finding_note(f_a_mutual_import_makes_sees_cyclic_and_changes_a_counting_verdict, "Found by the bridges executor while closing the corpus, and it checked the result against its own error before reporting it. Declaring BOTH `imports(case, worlds)` AND `imports(worlds, case)` in examples/sus changes the support-cycle count from 24 to 18. The mechanism is not subtle once seen and is invisible before: `sees` is the reflexive TRANSITIVE closure of `imports`, so a mutual pair puts a two-cycle into the support graph, and a counting semiring reports what it sees. THE CONTROL THAT MAKES IT A FINDING RATHER THAN A MISTAKE: it removed each declaration separately - dropping `imports(worlds, case)` gives leak 1, dropping `imports(case, worlds)` gives leak 2 - so BOTH carry real flow and both are true statements about the program. Nothing is over-declared; the cost is inherent. WHAT THIS MEANS BEYOND ONE EXAMPLE: a permission is not free and not purely additive. Writing a second declaration to close a second leak can silently alter what a DIFFERENT audit computes, because both read the same `sees` relation, and a mutual pair is exactly where the two purposes collide. Anyone closing leaks by adding rows should know that a mutual pair is a structural change rather than two independent ones. AND A SIBLING CASE FROM THE SAME SWEEP: examples/heck:41 was a POSITIVE CONTROL asserting that verdict rules DO cross a ledger boundary, and declaring the permission ERASED THE CROSSING AND THEREFORE THE CONTROL. The right repair is to re-aim the control at a pair that still crosses, never to delete it - a declaration can kill the check that was watching whether crossings occur at all, and a repository that closes every leak by declaration ends with no live evidence that the audit can still see one.").

finding(f_the_defect_wrote_false_documentation_about_itself, insight).
recorded(f_the_defect_wrote_false_documentation_about_itself, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_defect_wrote_false_documentation_about_itself, doc).
finding_note(f_the_defect_wrote_false_documentation_about_itself, "Small, and it is a mechanism rather than an anecdote. While repairing fixtures the executor found an assertion in test/flow-closure that had been FALSE BEFORE ANY OF TODAY'S WORK: a comment reading `a declared hop is not a leak`, in a fixture where the hop red->case was declared by NOBODY. It passed because the automatic self-licence silenced it, and the author, seeing green, wrote down the explanation that fitted. SO THE DEFECT DID NOT MERELY HIDE ITSELF - IT CAUSED A TRUE-LOOKING SENTENCE TO BE WRITTEN ABOUT WHY THE SILENCE WAS CORRECT. That sentence would have survived the fix and misled the next reader, and it was found only because someone went into the fixtures rather than only into the rules. THREE OTHER COMMENTS DIED THE SAME DAY FOR THE OPPOSITE REASON - inquiry/ontology, inquiry/perspectives and goof each described the removed mechanism as working - so a single kernel change produced both stale documentation and, retroactively, documentation that had never been true. THE PRACTICAL RULE: when a silent gate is removed, the comments explaining its silences must be read as SUSPECTS, not as context. Green tests teach their authors why they are green, and a gate that cannot say no teaches a false lesson every time someone looks at it.").

addressed_by(f_a_single_hop_crossing_licenses_itself_and_the_leak_audit_is_blind_to_it, "src/reflect.ts").

finding(f_one_edge_of_the_cycle_lives_in_the_host_and_stratification_cannot_see_it, defect).
recorded(f_one_edge_of_the_cycle_lives_in_the_host_and_stratification_cannot_see_it, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_one_edge_of_the_cycle_lives_in_the_host_and_stratification_cannot_see_it, decision).
finding_note(f_one_edge_of_the_cycle_lives_in_the_host_and_stratification_cannot_see_it, "I asked an executor to CHECK my monotonicity argument rather than accept it, and it did both halves: refuted it as stated and replaced it with something sharper. MY CLAIM WAS TOO STRONG. Read positively, gating nothing that grows the store, a host-supplied `store_size` is SAFE - 1830 paths, identical across strata, rounds and naive at every threshold - because the negation phases run after the monotone fixpoint and the veto is decided against the FINAL store. So `a fact that changes during the computation breaks the fixpoint` is false in general and I recorded it as if it were true. THE REAL DEFECT IS WORSE AND NARROWER. Write the veto one actually wants - `path(X,Z) :- path(X,Y), edge(Y,Z), not too_big(big).` with `too_big(big) :- store_size(N), N > k.` - and the SAME program on the SAME input gives TWO DIFFERENT ANSWERS depending on evaluation strategy: at k=800 seminaive says 399 paths and naive says 345; at k=1600, 795 against 749. Silently. And the program is ACCEPTED - unstratified, malformed, breach, undefined_premise and unmoded are all empty - with a positive control in the same run showing a genuine negative cycle IS refused. THE MECHANISM, and it generalises far beyond store size: ONE EDGE OF THE CYCLE LIVES IN THE HOST. `store_size` is edb, so `too_big` depends on nothing derived, and `path` negating it is a textbook stratified program. The actual cycle is path -> (the host counts the store) -> store_size -> too_big -> not -> path, and THE HOP THROUGH THE HOST IS NOT A RULE, so no checker reading dep_neg can ever close it. This holds for ANY host fact computed from the store. Our stratification check is sound over the rules and blind to a cycle that leaves the language and comes back. THE DESIGN WORK NAMED BUT NOT DONE: making `a host fact computed from the store depends on every derived relation` visible to stratification, so that case is REFUSED rather than answered twice.").

finding(f_a_store_size_veto_is_blind_to_the_very_explosion_it_was_proposed_for, insight).
recorded(f_a_store_size_veto_is_blind_to_the_very_explosion_it_was_proposed_for, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_store_size_veto_is_blind_to_the_very_explosion_it_was_proposed_for, decision).
finding_note(f_a_store_size_veto_is_blind_to_the_very_explosion_it_was_proposed_for, "The owner proposed a continuously updated host fact about memory; I converted it to store size on the grounds that a count of facts is deterministic and portable where bytes are not. The executor then made the measurement that settles it, and the answer is not `weaker`, it is BLIND. Wired at the wave boundary with threshold 1000, against the three-way join that kills the host: peakRows 500200, storeSizeEmissions ZERO, largestStoreSizeEverSeen ZERO, tooBigFired ZERO, steps 0. THE STORE IS EMPTY WHEN THE HOST DIES. Eight million partial solutions exist inside the join and NOTHING HAS BEEN CONCLUDED, so a veto over the store's size never gets a number to look at. WHAT THIS SEPARATES, and it is the reason both mechanisms are wanted rather than one: a HOST WALL counting rows held sees THE SEARCH, which is where this program dies and where no rule can see anything because nothing is derived yet; a STORE_SIZE RULE sees THE STORE, which is a different class - a program that legitimately derives eight million facts - and there a why-tree exists and the program itself should decide. AND THE PRICE OF THE ONLY SOUND BOUNDARY: at the tick boundary the veto is ONE TICK LATE by construction, reading the size from BEFORE the tick, so it cannot stop the tick that explodes, only the next one. Same shape as the already-recorded lag in hole(budget_exhausted) and thought_partial. TWO IMPLEMENTATION TRAPS FOUND BY WALKING INTO THEM: the naive version DIVERGES because the emitted fact is itself in the store, so counting inflates the count - 496402 rows and a 4 GB OOM, fixed by excluding the relation from its own count and emitting only on change, 59 emissions for a 60-hop closure; and the executor's own wall DOES NOT CHARGE HOST-INJECTED FACTS, which is why that OOM happened at all. Also small and real: my example was not even writable - the parser refuses a 0-ary head, so a veto relation must carry an argument.").

finding(f_a_measurement_must_certify_its_own_conditions, insight).
recorded(f_a_measurement_must_certify_its_own_conditions, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_measurement_must_certify_its_own_conditions, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_measurement_must_certify_its_own_conditions, "Four attempts to take a baseline today, three of them silently invalid, and the fourth INVALID BUT KNOWN TO BE - which is the whole difference. The failures were all the same shape wearing different clothes: an emptiness read as fact (`find -newermt` is a syntax error on this machine and never searched at all), a silence read as consent (I asked an executor to pause and started the run without waiting for the answer), and a plan resting on a reply that might never come (reports reached me roughly half the time all session - one executor went silent three times running). Each time I treated THE ABSENCE OF A SIGNAL AS INFORMATION. THE REPAIR IS NOT BETTER COORDINATION, IT IS SELF-CERTIFICATION. The run now fingerprints the tree before and after itself, and prints a verdict on its own validity: `find | stat | sort | md5` over sources, excluding the file I write myself. With a positive control in the same command - touch a file, confirm the fingerprint moves - because a fingerprint that cannot change would certify everything. Result on first use: control passed, fingerprint differed, `TREE MOVED - DISCARD`. It caught exactly what it was built for on its first run, and the numbers it produced (929 tests, 899 pass, 30 fail) are now known to be worthless instead of being quietly believed. THE GENERAL FORM, and it is the transferable part: A MEASUREMENT TAKEN UNDER CONDITIONS IT DOES NOT VERIFY IS AN OPINION WITH A NUMBER IN IT. Asking a collaborator to hold still is a promise; comparing a fingerprint is a fact. Everything else in this repository already follows that rule - an oracle enumerates its own sites, a gate carries a planted defect, a witness is a query someone can run - and my own coordination was the last place still running on promises.").

finding(f_a_wall_that_cannot_afford_to_say_it_hit_the_wall, insight).
recorded(f_a_wall_that_cannot_afford_to_say_it_hit_the_wall, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_wall_that_cannot_afford_to_say_it_hit_the_wall, doc).
finding_note(f_a_wall_that_cannot_afford_to_say_it_hit_the_wall, "Two design decisions from the executor closing its own defect, neither of which I asked for and both of which are right. FIRST: the rows that REPORT an inability are counted but exempt from TRIGGERING a refusal. A wall that cannot afford to say it hit the wall is the exact silence the whole mechanism exists to remove - and there is a second reason that is not aesthetic at all, since arithHole is reached FROM the enforcing branch, so enforcing there would recurse. SECOND: the undefined-atom gap is priced WHOLESALE, before a single row is written, and the ground is an invariant already fixed in docs/three-valued-answers.md - the store either NAMES the undefined atoms or says it RAN OUT, never both. A per-row refusal would leave a half-written unknown set sitting beside the hole, which is precisely the forbidden state. Asserted in the test rather than left as a comment. THE DEFECT ITSELF, closed both ways: 4000 unknown rows emitted and 0 charged before, 4000 charged after; at a wall of 5000 the program is REFUSED naming a real rule with zero unknown rows, and at a smaller gap it completes as before. The correction barely moves peak rows - 5999 to 6000 - because the injection happens AFTER the rounds release their charge, which is exactly why no verdict anywhere in the corpus changes. AND HE REFUSED TO FAKE MY CONTROL: I asked for a program where host injection alone reaches the SHIPPED wall, and there is none - the gap is bounded by the generous round which the wall already caps - so he used a lowered wall and said so, rather than adding an emitter that ships nowhere to satisfy the letter of the brief. The 496k figure I quoted at him was his store_size prototype, which does not ship; I had carried it into a requirement without noticing it was not part of the tree.").

finding(f_three_of_his_measurements_lied_and_a_control_caught_all_three, insight).
recorded(f_three_of_his_measurements_lied_and_a_control_caught_all_three, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_three_of_his_measurements_lied_and_a_control_caught_all_three, doc).
finding_note(f_three_of_his_measurements_lied_and_a_control_caught_all_three, "Reported unasked by the executor under the heading METHOD, and it is the same tally I was keeping about myself in the same hour, which makes it a property of the work rather than of either of us. THREE OF ITS MEASUREMENTS LIED TODAY AND A POSITIVE CONTROL CAUGHT ALL THREE - not care, not review. (1) A preload confound attributed five test failures to its own change. (2) A python replacement SILENTLY DID NOT APPLY, so it read 502000 peak rows and began diagnosing a well-founded pathology that does not exist - the real cause was an ordinary two-variable cross product. (3) A shell variable quoted as ONE argument produced two runs that measured NOTHING, from which it wrote `IDENTICAL, none of these are mine` - and it caught that only because the exit code was 1 while zero failure lines were printed. THE REPAIR IT ADOPTED IS THE CHEAPEST POSSIBLE AND IS WORTH COPYING VERBATIM: every probe now PRINTS THE RULES ACTUALLY UNDER TEST AS ITS FIRST LINE. Not a checklist, not a discipline - one line of output that makes `the probe did not apply` visually different from `the probe applied and found nothing`. Set beside my own four failures of the same class in the same hour - a find that was a syntax error, four guessed output formats, a silence read as consent, and a plan resting on a reply that might not come - the count for the session is SEVEN INSTRUMENT FAILURES ACROSS TWO PARTICIPANTS, every one caught by a control or a contradiction and NOT ONE by attention. That ratio is the finding.").

finding(f_demanding_a_reason_per_counter_caught_a_false_reason, insight).
recorded(f_demanding_a_reason_per_counter_caught_a_false_reason, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_demanding_a_reason_per_counter_caught_a_false_reason, doc).
finding_note(f_demanding_a_reason_per_counter_caught_a_false_reason, "I let a census update through on one condition - EVERY MOVED COUNTER GETS A LINE SAYING WHY - and the condition caught something no count could. The executor had told me an hour earlier that POLICY 216 to 218 was the WHOLESALE-GAP decision; I repeated that to the owner and recorded the surrounding work as a model. Forced to write the reason INTO the test, it went and read the scanner's own block table and found its own answer false: the wholesale-gap decision sits inside the alternating fixpoint's MECH block, while the two policy lines are the `edb(unknown)` declaration becoming a CHARGED WRITE, which lands in the admissibility block that runs BEFORE a rule fires. ITS OWN DIAGNOSIS OF WHY IT WAS WRONG IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART, and it is my class of error stated by someone else: `I reasoned it from my diff instead of reading the scanner's own table, and the reading I gave you was plausible and false.` The nearest source instead of the right one - the same shape as dividing bytes by four instead of counting tokens, or reading git diff to learn what an executor touched. THE MECHANISM WORTH KEEPING: a number may be updated, but only against a written reason, and the reason must be read from the thing that PRODUCED the number rather than reconstructed from what changed. A census updated without reasons is not a census, it is an echo - the next reader sees the figure, cannot see the cause, and cannot tell a legitimate shift from an accidental one. Here the requirement did better than that: it made a WRONG cause visible, and both versions now stand in the test, the first answer and what refuted it.").

addressed_by(f_an_ordinary_three_way_join_kills_the_host_and_the_budget_never_fires, "test/budget-space.test.ts").

finding(f_a_program_could_forge_the_audits_own_inputs_anonymously, defect).
recorded(f_a_program_could_forge_the_audits_own_inputs_anonymously, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_program_could_forge_the_audits_own_inputs_anonymously, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_a_program_could_forge_the_audits_own_inputs_anonymously, "The largest security defect found this session, and it was in NO brief - the rings executor found it while measuring what [main] contains. RESERVED forbids a RULE from concluding into concludes / rule / reads_from / writes_to, and says NOTHING ABOUT A FACT. Measured on bare boot.rofl with an ANONYMOUS assert: `reads_from(r_fake, secret). writes_to(r_fake, public).` moves flow 2 to 3, crossing 0 to 1, leak 0 to 1 - AND forged[audit] STAYS AT ZERO. THE PROGRAM UNDER AUDIT WAS WRITING THE AUDIT'S OWN INPUTS, INVISIBLY. The leak audit reads the kernel's reflection about rules - who reads from what, who writes to what - and that reflection lived in [main], the same book any program writes into by default. So a program could manufacture false facts ABOUT ITSELF and make the audit see a graph that does not exist: invent flows that were never written, or - the direction that matters - drown a real crossing among fabricated ones. THE SIGNATURE OF THE HOLE IS THE SAME ONE ALREADY RECORDED ONCE TODAY: signed `mallory` the forgery gives forged 2, ANONYMOUS it gives 0, so anonymity was again the cheaper attack than impersonation. That inversion was found and closed for asserted facts in August; it survived here because the reflection relations are written BY THE KERNEL and nobody asked whether a program could write them too. AFTER THE SPLIT BOTH GIVE 2. And the test that documented the hole is now RED BY INVERSION - second-door.test.ts:129 asserts `an anonymous forgery leaves no authorship trail`, expecting forged[audit] empty, and it now returns THIRTEEN ROWS. A red test whose expectation was the defect is the strongest evidence a repair can produce.").

finding(f_the_four_numbers_changed_the_plan_and_that_is_what_they_were_for, insight).
recorded(f_the_four_numbers_changed_the_plan_and_that_is_what_they_were_for, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_four_numbers_changed_the_plan_and_that_is_what_they_were_for, doc).
finding_note(f_the_four_numbers_changed_the_plan_and_that_is_what_they_were_for, "I wrote into the brief that the first step was four measurements and that a bad reading meant NOT DOING THE WORK. The executor took that literally and the numbers redirected the work rather than cancelling it, which is the outcome a measurement-first brief is actually for. THE NUMBERS: [main] holds 94 to 100 percent of every store; inside it the kernel's share is 66 to 92 percent and NOWHERE the 95 I had assumed; 6292 bare literals across 67 files carry no bracket at all, with 34 files containing not one; and 1145 rules of 1709 read [main] while only THIRTY read the reflection, through 86 literals in total. WHAT EACH NUMBER DECIDED, and this is the reasoning I would not have done: number 2 said splitting by WHAT IS WRITTEN is expensive, because the kernel's share is nowhere dominant. Number 4 said splitting by WHAT IS READ is cheap - 86 mechanical literals. And number 3 said leave the default alone: separating `the program's content` from `no book was named` costs 6292 literals, AND once the kernel's role departs those two are the same role anyway, `the program's book by default`. So the cut went between ring zero and everything else, the default stayed where it was, and the price was ZERO LINES. I had predicted the cut would fall between all three roles; the measurement said two of the three were never distinct once the third left. AND MY OWN ERROR IN THE SAME EXCHANGE: seeing it write test files, I concluded it had gone to cutting without measuring, and said so to the owner. It had measured first and simply not sent the numbers yet. The same shape as everything else today - I read an absence of evidence as evidence, this time about a person.").

finding(f_a_silent_join_failure_wearing_the_shape_of_a_finding, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_silent_join_failure_wearing_the_shape_of_a_finding, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_silent_join_failure_wearing_the_shape_of_a_finding, doc).
finding_note(f_a_silent_join_failure_wearing_the_shape_of_a_finding, "The executor's own phrase and it is the sharpest naming of this class all session: A SILENT JOIN FAILURE WEARING THE SHAPE OF A FINDING. `ruleIdOf` hashes `canonClause`, and a clause's canonical text PRINTS ITS PERSPECTIVE - so once the kernel's reflection moved into its own book, an id computed from the source stopped matching the id stored. MOOT pointed at boot.rofl then found 12 of its rules instead of 20 and REPORTED flow, flows_to, crossing and leak AS UNREACHABLE RELATIONS OF THE META-KERNEL. That output is indistinguishable in form from a real audit result - a list of relations nothing reaches is exactly what MOOT exists to produce - and it was a join quietly matching nothing. THE ONLY THING THAT CAUGHT IT was example-yak, which QUOTES MOOT'S OUTPUT and therefore had a fixed expectation to violate; every other reader of that output would have believed it. THE GENERAL SHAPE, and it is the same one I have been chasing all day from the other side: an instrument's REFUSAL can wear the costume of an ANSWER, and the costume is most convincing when the instrument's normal answer is itself a list of absences. My four cases today were empty output read as fact; this one is worse, because the output was NOT empty - it was populated, plausible, and wrong. The defence that worked was not vigilance but a SECOND READER WITH A FIXED EXPECTATION: one test that pins the content of another tool's report. That is cheap and it is the only thing here that would generalise.").

finding(f_the_bracket_inversion_returned_mirrored, defect).
recorded(f_the_bracket_inversion_returned_mirrored, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_bracket_inversion_returned_mirrored, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_bracket_inversion_returned_mirrored, "Found by the rings executor asking the blind-zone question of its own work, and it is the SECOND appearance of one inversion in one day, mirrored. This morning: WRITING an explicit bracket emitted a self-licence, so `prop[main](P) :- axiom[euclid](P).` was silent while the bracket-less form leaked - typing more made the audit weaker. Now: `shadow[P](R) :- concludes[P](R, _).` with a VARIABLE perspective seats 22 of the program's own facts INSIDE the kernel's book, with forged 0 and leak 0, while THE SAME RULE WITH THE BRACKET WRITTEN IS REFUSED. Typing it is forbidden, not typing it is permitted. THE THREE REASONS IT PASSES, all of them individually correct: the door checks for an explicit `$` head and sees a variable; `forged` reads `asserted_by`, which a DERIVED fact does not carry; and `crossing` requires `A != B`, which a single variable instantiating to one book cannot satisfy. Every guard is right and the composition has a hole - the same shape as the projection that hid a contradiction every audit was individually right to ignore. THE REPAIR IS MEASURED AND FREE: at src/engine.ts:1168 a variable perspective's candidates must exclude facts in `$`-books. Across the corpus 108 body literals read under a variable perspective and 37 read a relation from the kernel's book, and THE INTERSECTION IS EMPTY - with a positive control on each half separately, so the zero is a fact about the corpus rather than about the query. The same filter also closes polymorphic READING of the kernel's book around `imports`. THE LESSON THAT TRANSFERS BEYOND BOTH INSTANCES: a guard keyed on WHAT THE AUTHOR TYPED is bypassed by typing less, and that is not a bug in the guard, it is what keying on syntax means.").

finding(f_an_anomaly_that_favours_you_deserves_the_same_audit, insight).
recorded(f_an_anomaly_that_favours_you_deserves_the_same_audit, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_an_anomaly_that_favours_you_deserves_the_same_audit, doc).
finding_note(f_an_anomaly_that_favours_you_deserves_the_same_audit, "The rings executor found one failure in its BASELINE copy that does not occur in the working tree - a failure in the direction that would have made its own attribution SMALLER. Swallowed silently it would have read `five of the failures are mine` instead of six. IT REPORTED AND INVESTIGATED IT ANYWAY, and that is the finding: an anomaly that favours you deserves exactly the audit an anomaly that hinders you gets, and almost never receives one, because nothing prompts the question. WHAT IT DID INSTEAD OF CALLING IT A FLAKE: rebuilt the copy from scratch and ran that ONE file in isolation, twice, 17 of 17 both times with the exit code read from the process rather than after a pipe. Then named the conditions under which it HAD gone red - last file in a batch of 32, a competing batch of 32 running in another tree, and a test that starts an oracle in a separate process and writes then deletes fixtures on disk - which is the precise class that breaks on CPU starvation and a race for a temp directory. Then checked the causal path: the neutralisation is three edits (a constant, a prefix in the door check, three imports lines) and NONE of them touches the js layer, the scanners or the fixtures, so no path exists. THIS IS THE SECOND TIME TODAY AN EXECUTOR CAUGHT ITS OWN INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN ITS SUBJECT, and in both cases the defence was ISOLATED REPETITION rather than an argument about plausibility. `It is probably a flake` and `it did not reproduce in two isolated runs and here is why it could not have been caused` are different sentences, and only the second is a measurement.").

finding(f_reserved_should_forbid_the_fact_not_only_the_rule, decision).
recorded(f_reserved_should_forbid_the_fact_not_only_the_rule, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_reserved_should_forbid_the_fact_not_only_the_rule, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_reserved_should_forbid_the_fact_not_only_the_rule, "A one-line imperative refusal, and it is the right shape for this because there is no legitimate exception. RESERVED today forbids a RULE from concluding into concludes / rule / reads_from / writes_to, and says nothing about an ASSERTED FACT - so a program could write the reflection the audits read. THE CRITERION THE OWNER PUSHED ME TO STATE, and it is the general one: FORBID WHERE THERE IS NO LEGITIMATE EXCEPTION, REPORT WHERE THERE IS. A crossing between books has legitimate exceptions - declared bridges - so it gets an audit plus a declaration. A program asserting the kernel's reflection has none: the kernel writes it, full stop. So it belongs in `load`, refused at the door with the relation named, the same place and the same shape as the `$`-prefix refusal in checkWho that is already there and already measured. WHY IMPERATIVE RATHER THAN AUDIT, said properly rather than by feel: a refusal works when the violation is LOCAL - this call, this argument, visible where it happens. An audit is needed when the violation is a property of the WHOLE PROGRAM, like a leak three hops long that no single rule shows. Asserting a reserved relation is local, therefore refusable. AND THE COST OF THE IMPERATIVE FORM IS REAL AND ACCEPTED: a refusal CANNOT BE ARGUED WITH. An audit row can be read and answered `yes, deliberate`; a refusal is absolute. That is exactly why it must be reserved for the case with no legitimate exception, and why I should NOT have reached for it as a general instrument. CORRECTION TO MY OWN FRAMING, which is why this is recorded separately from the kernel book: I presented the kernel's own book as the fix for this hole and it is not. The hole closes with this refusal, one line, locally checkable at load. The kernel book solves a DIFFERENT problem - making `imports(audit, kernel)` a true sentence instead of `imports(audit, main)` which conflates reflection with domain data. Both are worth doing; I chained them causally and inflated the second by attaching it to the first.").


dismissed(f_reserved_should_forbid_the_fact_not_only_the_rule, redundant_first_line_already_holds).

finding(f_i_built_a_second_line_for_a_hole_already_closed, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_built_a_second_line_for_a_hole_already_closed, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_i_built_a_second_line_for_a_hole_already_closed, doc).
finding_note(f_i_built_a_second_line_for_a_hole_already_closed, "Built the imperative refusal, tested it 4 of 4 green, and then the full run said 18 failures against 8 before — TEN TESTS BROKEN BY ME. The diagnosis is not a bug in the guard: THE HOLE WAS ALREADY CLOSED, and I did not check before building. MEASURED after temporarily removing my own guard: an anonymous `reads_from(r_fake, secret).` now gives leak 1 and FORGED 2, where before the kernel book existed it gave leak 1 and forged 0. Moving the reflection into `[$kernel]` did it - `$anon` has no standing there, so the forgery is NAMED. My refusal was a second line in front of a first line that works. AND THE COST WAS NOT ZERO, which is what makes this a pitfall rather than a tidy-up: the ten broken tests all assert, deliberately, that FORGED REFLECTION IS ADMITTED AS DATA and caught by the NEXT defence - breach[audit], decodeRules, authorship. That layering is the property second-door and phase3 exist to pin, and a refusal at the door removes the ability to TEST the later lines at all. THE SAME EXECUTOR HAD ALREADY MADE AND UNDONE THIS MISTAKE: rings reported narrowing its door from `any head in KERNEL_BOOK` to `an explicit $ bracket the author typed`, precisely because the wide form killed those tests. I read that sentence, recorded it approvingly, and then built the wide form myself in a different place. THE CHECK I SKIPPED IS ONE COMMAND: remove the guard, run the forgery, read forged[audit]. I ran it only after the tree went red - and by then I had also written 4 tests and a 20-line comment arguing for it. Reverted whole: guard, import, test file. The comparison-sink work in the same session stands and is unrelated.").

dismissed(f_one_edge_of_the_cycle_lives_in_the_host_and_stratification_cannot_see_it, no_instance_and_its_premise_was_dropped).
dismissed(f_the_budget_counts_steps_and_memory_goes_first, no_constructor_exists_to_grow_a_term).

finding(f_three_of_five_queued_items_died_to_the_baseline_question, insight).
recorded(f_three_of_five_queued_items_died_to_the_baseline_question, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_three_of_five_queued_items_died_to_the_baseline_question, doc).
finding_note(f_three_of_five_queued_items_died_to_the_baseline_question, "The gate written an hour earlier - MEASURE THE SUBJECT BEFORE BUILDING THE GUARD - was applied to a queue of five and killed three of them, which is a better yield than it earned when it was written. THE CYCLE THROUGH THE HOST: measured, with a control proving the grep sees `not X(` at all, NOBODY in the corpus negates `unknown`, `hole` or `derived_by`, and nobody even READS them; `store_size` exists nowhere in the shipped tree. The defect is real as a property of the construction and has ZERO instances, and it was explicitly the prerequisite for an expressible size veto that had already been dropped as blind to its own target case. THE TERM-SIZE BLIND SPOT: there is NO string constructor, measured with a control showing the destructors are visible to the same grep, so nothing in the language can grow a term - the defect arrives only WITH the constructor we decided not to add. THE API FLAG: `space` is already exercised in test/budget-space.test.ts and `new Evaluation` is constructed directly in 49 places, so the tuning path exists; adding a flag to `Rofl` would have added a fourth entry to the class of flags this repository already records as invisible-by-construction, and I would have created it myself. WHAT SURVIVED: the README row, which was not merely incomplete but FALSE - it listed three hole reasons where seven exist, so a reader learning the vocabulary would have counted three; and the nameless-reader gap, which is a genuine hole in the language and a DECISION rather than a repair. THE PATTERN WORTH KEEPING: four of the five items were written down while working on something else, and three of the four died the moment someone asked what happens WITHOUT the work. A queue accumulates items at the speed of noticing and discharges them at the speed of measuring, and those are very different speeds.").

addressed_by(f_a_destructor_in_a_comparison_is_silently_false, "test/comparison-sink.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_program_could_forge_the_audits_own_inputs_anonymously, "src/reflect.ts").

finding(f_the_queue_now_refuses_unproven_work, decision).
recorded(f_the_queue_now_refuses_unproven_work, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_the_queue_now_refuses_unproven_work, doc).
witness(f_the_queue_now_refuses_unproven_work, "unproven(F)", 35).
finding_note(f_the_queue_now_refuses_unproven_work, "The owner's instruction after the third instance in one session: CATCH IT AND FORBID EXECUTING ON IT. The pattern caught is mine and it is one step, not three - TAKING A DIAGNOSTIC MECHANISM, ARGUING ABOUT IT IN THE REGISTER OF DEFENCE, AND THEN BUILDING A GUARD FOR IT. Twice the work was reverted: an imperative refusal whose hole another agent had already closed, and a new relation proposed for the meta-kernel to silence two explained rows of a REPORT that blocks nothing. The shared step was never carelessness - it was starting work on a claim NOBODY HAD RUN. THE INSTRUMENT ALREADY EXISTED AND WAS SWITCHED OFF: `witness(F, Query, N)` states a finding's premise as a query anyone can execute, with the number it returned, and scripts/witness_check.ts already checks them. Measured at the moment of writing this: 150 open findings, FIVE with a witness, and of eight open DEFECTS exactly ONE. THE REPAIR IS NOT A RULE ABOUT ATTENTION, IT IS THE QUEUE ITSELF: `unproven(F)` holds for any finding demanding rule_and_test or kernel_test with no witness, and `ready_proven(F)` is `ready` minus those. Record whatever you like - an observation costs nothing and a ledger is a journal - but work is not OFFERED until someone has run the query and written what it returned. MEASURED BOTH WAYS, which is the test this repository applies to every gate: it says NO to 31 items and YES to 118, so it is neither blind nor permanently red. And aimed, not blanket: findings demanding a DECISION pass untouched, because a decision is made by argument and not by a query, and the two open ones about the language pass correctly. It catches the exact case that started this - the forgery finding demanded a kernel test, carried no witness, and I began building on it anyway.").

finding(f_naming_yourself_is_itself_a_forgery, defect).
recorded(f_naming_yourself_is_itself_a_forgery, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_naming_yourself_is_itself_a_forgery, decision).
finding_note(f_naming_yourself_is_itself_a_forgery, "Asked to make `forged[audit]` STOP EXECUTION rather than write a report row, I measured before building and found a blocker that comes first. THE MEASUREMENT: `forged` is 0 on bare boot, on sensors, on the js model, on the ledger and on the inquiry layer - so a refusal looked free. It is free ONLY BECAUSE NOBODY CAN BE NAMED. Load `authority(mybook, alice).` + `p[mybook](x).` with who=alice and forged is 1, and THE ROW NAMED IS THE AUTHORITY DECLARATION ITSELF: `$fact(authority, main, ...)`. The declaration lands in [main] and alice has no standing in [main]. The same program loaded ANONYMOUSLY gives 0. CHICKEN AND EGG, exactly: to write into [main] you need standing in [main]; standing is granted by an `authority` fact; an `authority` fact is written into [main]. So a program that names its author forges on its own preamble, and there is no order of lines that fixes it - I checked authority above the fact, below it, and absent, and all three give 1. THIS INVERTS THE EXPLANATION RECORDED IN boot.rofl, which says anonymity stays the default because `the honest corpus is anonymous end to end`. The corpus is anonymous BECAUSE NAMING YOURSELF IS IMPOSSIBLE, not the other way round, and that is why 146 literal `who:` values live in tests and none in the programs. CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ASK: a refusal on `forged` cannot be added today - it would reject every named program on its preamble, and the current green is the green of an unexercised path rather than of a clean one. THE DECISION OWED, and it is a language decision: either the first namer of a book owns it (as a file's creator owns the file - today `registerPersp` gives every book to `$kernel`), or an `authority` fact ABOUT ONESELF is not a forgery by definition, which is self-licensing of the kind we just removed from bridges, or naming is admitted to be unsupported and `forged` guards a case that cannot occur.").

finding(f_a_natural_ownership_rule_made_the_kernel_non_deterministic, defect).
recorded(f_a_natural_ownership_rule_made_the_kernel_non_deterministic, "2026-09-01").
demands(f_a_natural_ownership_rule_made_the_kernel_non_deterministic, doc).
finding_note(f_a_natural_ownership_rule_made_the_kernel_non_deterministic, "Removing anonymity needed a rule for WHO OWNS A BOOK, and the natural one is `the creator owns it` - a filesystem says exactly that and it gives the right answer on every example I tried. IT MADE THE KERNEL NON-DETERMINISTIC. `registerPersp` fires on EVERY use of a book, so to grant only the creator it has to READ THE STORE and ask whether the book already has a named owner - and the answer depends on which load touched the book first, which is not a property of the program. Measured by test/phase4.test.ts `replay: bit-identical states across 100 runs with shuffled insertion order`: EIGHT distinct states instead of one. I did not catch it; the test did, and I had already written the comment explaining why the rule was correct. THE CORRECTED RULE IS SIMPLER AND FOLLOWS FROM THE TEXT ALONE: `$` books belong to the kernel, every ordinary book belongs to `user`, and a caller that NAMES ITSELF must be granted the book in writing - `authority(book, alice).` No store read, no order dependence, and `forged[audit]` finally means one thing: writing under a name into a book nobody gave you. THE CLASS, and it is what transfers: A RULE THAT NEEDS TO ASK WHAT THE STORE ALREADY CONTAINS IS A RULE ABOUT EXECUTION ORDER, however natural it sounds. Ownership, licensing, defaults - anything the language decides - must be a function of the TEXT. The tell is the store read itself, and it was right there in my own diff.").

finding(f_a_forgery_shutdown_at_load_time_disarms_the_audit, defect).
recorded(f_a_forgery_shutdown_at_load_time_disarms_the_audit, "2026-09-02").
demands(f_a_forgery_shutdown_at_load_time_disarms_the_audit, doc).
finding_note(f_a_forgery_shutdown_at_load_time_disarms_the_audit, "THE OWNER ASKED FOR A SHUTDOWN ON FORGERY AND I BUILT IT ON THE LOAD PATH, which cost 811/701/110 against 948/941/7 without it - and the failure count understates it, because two test FILES stopped loading at all, so 137 tests never ran. Both replacement runs were attested with a tree fingerprint and a control, so the numbers are comparable. TWO DEFECTS, both structural rather than sloppy. ORDER: the check ran BEFORE registerPersp, so the book had no authority rows yet and the message read `written by: nobody` - a NAMED author could therefore never create a book, which is exactly what scanners/spec.ts:179 does, r.load(s.text, who: s.who) per section, 30 such call sites outside tests. CATEGORY, and this one is the transferable half: A LOAD-TIME REFUSAL DISARMS THE AUDIT THAT FINDS THE THING. test/bridges.test.ts:335 plants a forgery on purpose and expects ok, because forged[audit] is proven alive by planting; refuse at load and the forgery cannot be planted, so the gate can never be made red and is indistinguishable from an absent one. THE CORRECT DIVISION IS ALREADY IN THE TREE and I did not look for it first: runtime/admission.ts refuses an unattributed call with the words `anonymity must not bypass the forged audit` - the KERNEL REPORTS, THE HOST APPLICATION STOPS, the way errno and a shell divide the work. A third instrument said the same independently: scripts/kernel_grep.ts refused `forged` as a relation name in src/, because the kernel is not allowed to know the name of the audit. AND THE WHOLE THING WAS UNNECESSARY: forged[audit] measures 0 on boot alone, on every combination of the language model with its facts, and on SPAT. I built a defence against a hole that no measurement showed open, which is the same defect as the retracted imperative refusal earlier the same week.").
addressed_by(f_a_forgery_shutdown_at_load_time_disarms_the_audit, "src/api.ts").

finding(f_the_leak_audit_now_fires_on_a_ledger_polymorphic_head, defect).
recorded(f_the_leak_audit_now_fires_on_a_ledger_polymorphic_head, "2026-09-02").
demands(f_the_leak_audit_now_fires_on_a_ledger_polymorphic_head, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_leak_audit_now_fires_on_a_ledger_polymorphic_head, "SEVEN TESTS ARE RED AND THE CAUSE IS ONE ROW. Over NPC settled at tick 1: crossing 3, gathered 2, leak 1, and the leaking pair is A = world, B = a VARIABLE - the head perspective of a rule polymorphic in the ledger. The two crossings with a variable on the LEFT are gathered; the one with a variable on the RIGHT is not. The four red tests that name it are `a rule uniform in the ledger is no leak, and the audit still bites`, `one polymorphic carry rule carries ten separate journals, and leaks nothing`, `substituting the ledger away derives the same facts, at nine times the rules`, and the two demo audits over NPC and HECK. forged, breach, malformed and undefined_premise are all 0 in the same run, so this is the leak audit alone. WHAT IT DOES NOT BLOCK, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: the language model with its fact files reports leak 0 forged 0, SPAT reports every audit 0, and the tests are green - SPAT 6/6, js-model 28, js-modules 22, js-callgraph 17, js-ast 14, js-resolve 25. THE LATENT RISK IS NAMED AND IS NOT TODAY: rules/js-resolve.rofl is built on exactly this construction, resolve_try[E](Site, K, Path, Outcome) with the ledger standing for the resolver ENVIRONMENT, 29 lines of code carrying [E] or [Env]. It is clean now and it is in the class that broke. NO WITNESS IS WRITTEN because the premise lives in NPC's world and scripts/witness_check.ts runs its query against boot.rofl plus the ledger, where leak[audit] is 0 - a number from a purpose-built probe is not a witness, it is the defect already recorded as a witness has no world.").

finding(f_load_cannot_become_a_language_intent_because_the_author_is_the_callers, idea).
recorded(f_load_cannot_become_a_language_intent_because_the_author_is_the_callers, "2026-09-02").
demands(f_load_cannot_become_a_language_intent_because_the_author_is_the_callers, doc).
finding_note(f_load_cannot_become_a_language_intent_because_the_author_is_the_callers, "ASKED WHETHER load COULD BE A DIRECTIVE OF THE LANGUAGE - an intent a book derives and the host merely executes - and asked to break it. It breaks, on five independent angles, two of them fatal. FATAL ONE, THE AUTHOR SLOT: load is not load(Path), it is load(text, who), and the host CHOOSES the principal per call - scanners/spec.ts loads facts/spec.rofl under a per-section who, then census, then librarian. Make it a book's intent and there are exactly two branches: without a who slot every load becomes user and the whole authorship model collapses to one principal; with a who slot A BOOK CHOOSES THE AUTHOR OF THE FACTS IT LOADS, which is the hole forged[audit] exists to close, reopened through a door the audit does not watch. FATAL TWO, NO READER: load_intent and load_failed occur 0 times in the tree, nobody has asked for conditional loading, and I had refused a kernel journal ten minutes earlier on exactly that ground. Worse, it is the retainTicks class - an opt-in feature is invisible to every gate here BY CONSTRUCTION, so it could never go red. THREE MORE, survivable but real: the ROOT load has no book to derive it, so the host path stays and one operation gets two mechanisms with different audit stories; load_failed cannot be WITHDRAWN because forgetting is not a kernel operation at all (forgetRule lives in examples/loot/demo.ts, host-side), so a failure fact outlives the condition that caused it; and a refusal reason derived from the whole rule set carries global knowledge into one private book through a host write, which the leak audit cannot see because crossing is built from concludes and a host-written fact never appears there. WHAT SURVIVES: the shape for this class is already in the tree and it is not a directive. Loading is a HOST EMITTER - host_emitter(js, modules, node_resolver, a_resolution_is_a_fact_about_the_machine) is the same kind of thing, a declared loan with a countable row. The host loads, the audit counts, the language does not grow.").
dismissed(f_load_cannot_become_a_language_intent_because_the_author_is_the_callers, the_author_slot_belongs_to_the_caller).

finding(f_reify_term_is_not_injective_and_the_language_has_no_quote, defect).
recorded(f_reify_term_is_not_injective_and_the_language_has_no_quote, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_reify_term_is_not_injective_and_the_language_has_no_quote, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_reify_term_is_not_injective_and_the_language_has_no_quote, "MEASURED WITH A CONTROL THAT FIRED, which is the only reason it was caught. `reifyTerm` (src/reflect.ts:432) maps the VARIABLE X to `$var(\"X\")`, and it maps the TERM `$var(\"X\")` to `$var(\"X\")` as well - it recurses through a functor and the two inputs meet. `unreifyTerm` (src/reflect.ts:439) therefore rewrites a literally written `$var(\"X\")` back into a variable, and A RULE CANNOT WRITE DOWN A REIFIED VARIABLE. There is no quote in the language. Probe: a rule concluding `gen_head(C, $lit(C, main, $cons($var(\"X\"), $nil), $now))` derives a term the printer renders `?X#0` - a variable, not the term written. The discriminating pair: `hit` matching `$var(\"X\")` gave 1 AND the control `miss` matching `$var(\"QQQ\")` ALSO gave 1, so the match was against a free variable and told nothing about the name. SECOND HALF, and it is what keeps the sugar layer alive: when the NAME arrives through the body - `vars(\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\").` and a head writing `$var(X)` - the derived fact holds a real `$var(\"X\")` and the printer says so. So a rule may TRANSPORT and CONSTRUCT a reified variable, and may not WRITE ONE AS A LITERAL nor PATTERN-MATCH ONE BY NAME. NO WITNESS: the premise is a property of two host functions and cannot be stated as a query over boot.rofl plus the ledger. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a quoting form, or any construction where a literal `$var(\"X\")` in a rule survives decodeRules as a term.").

finding(f_a_token_cannot_carry_its_own_text, defect).
recorded(f_a_token_cannot_carry_its_own_text, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_a_token_cannot_carry_its_own_text, decision).
witness(f_a_token_cannot_carry_its_own_text, "mode(Op, M)", 14).
addressed_by(f_a_token_cannot_carry_its_own_text, "src/reflect.ts").
finding_note(f_a_token_cannot_carry_its_own_text, "FOUND BY BUILDING RATHER THAN BY READING: a ROFL parser written in ROFL rules runs, and its tokens are PAIRS OF INDICES because they cannot be anything else. THE WHOLE MODED VOCABULARY IS TWELVE ROWS - `=`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `is`, plus `str_char`, `str_len`, `str_pre`, `str_seg`, `str_segs` - and NOT ONE OF THEM TAKES A RANGE. `str_char` answers one character, `str_seg` and `str_pre` cut on a separator that has to occur where the cut is wanted, and no composition of finite-image operations can LENGTHEN a string: extending length is concatenation, which is banned by the finiteness proof already recorded in f_string_destructors_are_free_and_constructors_are_not. SO A RULES-SIDE TOKENIZER CANNOT PRODUCE TOKEN TEXT AT ALL. This is a THIRD wall, independent of the two measured the day before (no string-to-atom, and conclusion_lit being write-protected), and like them it stands on an absence rather than on an argument: `str_sub(S, I, Len)` yields a SUBSTRING of a string the program already holds, so its image is finite and it passes the recorded criterion exactly as the other five destructors do. THE DECISION OWED is whether self-application of the parser is worth `str_sub` plus `atom_of`, which together are the entire remaining cost of a rules-written ROFL parser that can emit a real rule. THE WITNESS IS THE VOCABULARY ITSELF and it goes stale the moment a sixth destructor lands, which is the correct behaviour.").

finding(f_ring_zero_shrinks_by_a_quarter_and_that_is_the_wrong_ledger, insight).
recorded(f_ring_zero_shrinks_by_a_quarter_and_that_is_the_wrong_ledger, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_ring_zero_shrinks_by_a_quarter_and_that_is_the_wrong_ledger, doc).
finding_note(f_ring_zero_shrinks_by_a_quarter_and_that_is_the_wrong_ledger, "THE OWNER ASKED FOR THE EXACT DEPENDENCY behind my claim that a grammar cannot be loaded by itself, and the claim was WRONG AS STATED: ring 0 cannot be empty, but it need not be a ROFL parser - `Rofl.fromSnapshot` (src/api.ts:108) already bypasses the parser entirely. The real question is how small ring 0 gets, and it was measured rather than argued. THE SURFACE IS 28 FEATURES enumerated from src/parser.ts. A working ring 1 - grammar as facts, tokenizer and chart parser as rules, which parses `q(X) :- p(X), r(X, b).` into a tree - USES 20. A saturation walk over every construct ring 1 does not yet handle adds back exactly ONE, escaping, because the character-class table needs `cls(\"\\\"\", quote).` and the reduced parser REJECTS it. So 21 of 28. Dropped: `$`-names, wildcards, negative literals, `* / mod`, parenthesised expressions, PERSPECTIVES and TEMPORAL MARKERS - and the last two are dropped precisely because ring 1 parses text rather than reasoning about a world. THE ORACLE IS EXACT AND FREE: a parser with those seven cut out gives a BYTE-IDENTICAL md5 over the canonical clauses of ring 1 (97848ee2e4bb3c96d3a7707e39b0c88a both sides), and the negative control is real - it rejects boot.rofl on `$`, js-modules.rofl on `[`, findings.rofl on `\\`, and accepts 21 of the corpus 67. CODE: 231 lines to 177, a saving of 54, TWENTY-THREE PER CENT. AND THE NUMBER IS MEASURED ON THE WRONG SIDE OF THE BOOK, which is the finding: ring 0 loses 54 lines while the host GAINS a promotion step and the kernel GAINS two operations that do not exist. As a way to get the parser out of the engine it does not pay. THE JUSTIFICATION THAT SURVIVES is the one recorded on 2026-09-01 - a why-tree for a parse, a whynot for a failure, ambiguity as a counted number - and one of those was OBSERVED rather than promised on the first run: the chart returned a spurious `factnode` at index 14, having correctly seen `r(X, b).` as a fact inside the rule.").

finding(f_the_sugar_layer_needs_no_kernel_change_at_all, insight).
recorded(f_the_sugar_layer_needs_no_kernel_change_at_all, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_sugar_layer_needs_no_kernel_change_at_all, demo).
finding_note(f_the_sugar_layer_needs_no_kernel_change_at_all, "THE OWNER REFRAMED - not replacing the parser, but new vocabulary written as rules - AND THE ECONOMICS INVERT, because a term-level macro NEVER TOUCHES TEXT. Of the three walls only one applies. No string-to-atom is needed: the macro rearranges atoms already present in its input, `closure(reaches, edge).` naming both. No substring-by-range is needed: nothing is tokenised. Only the write protection on `conclusion_lit` stands, and it costs ONE HOST PROMOTER of about twenty lines built from `unreifyLit` and `addClause`, both of which already exist. MEASURED END TO END: one line of user program, `closure(reaches, edge).`, plus five sugar rules emitting `gen_head` and `gen_prem`, gives the full transitive closure - before promotion `reaches(a, d)` is 0 rows, after it the closure is exactly 6, AND THE WHY-TREE WALKS THE GENERATED RULES, naming ra025e2f9 and r537887ae down to `edge[main](a,b) [axiom]`. A macro that is not opaque. Every existing audit applies to a generated rule for free, because a generated rule is an ordinary row of reflection. THE ACCOUNTING, against the parser case: kernel plus zero operations, host plus one promoter ONCE, and every later sugar costs RULES ONLY. TWO THINGS ARE OPEN AND BOTH ARE REAL. PROVENANCE: the promoter installs a rule whose trail says ra025e2f9 with no link back to the `closure(reaches, edge).` that produced it, which for a layer where rules are born from rules is the first thing to fix. HYGIENE: `vars(\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\")` is a manual gensym and two sugars nested will capture each other - reachable with existing operations, since a variable name is a STRING and the destructors are already in the kernel. AND THE DEMO IS NOT SHIPPED, which by this repository's own rule means the capability is invisible to every gate here.").

finding(f_a_contaminated_demo_passed_on_a_relation_boot_already_defined, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_contaminated_demo_passed_on_a_relation_boot_already_defined, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_a_contaminated_demo_passed_on_a_relation_boot_already_defined, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_contaminated_demo_passed_on_a_relation_boot_already_defined, "The first run of the sugar demo used `flow` and `flows_to` AND BOTH ARE ALREADY DEFINED IN boot.rofl, where `flows_to` is the transitive closure of the perspective `flow`. So `edb(flow). flow(a, b).` fed the KERNEL'S OWN relation, the closure was computed by boot's rules and not by the generated ones, and the demo would have been reported as a success while proving nothing at all. What caught it was the NEGATIVE CONTROL BEING IMPLAUSIBLE - `before promotion: reaches(a, d) -> 1 rows`, when the whole point was that it should be 0 - and not any test and not reading the output. Renaming to `reaches` and `edge` gave 0 before and 6 after, which is the result. THE CLASS: a demo of a rule-generating layer is measured by facts appearing, and facts appear for MANY reasons; if the demo's relation name is already populated the instrument reads the wrong source and says yes. THE CHEAP DEFENCE IS ALREADY IN THE RUN and cost nothing: a negative control BEFORE the mechanism is applied, read for plausibility rather than for pass or fail. The general form is this repository's own - a gate that cannot go red certifies everything - narrowed to demos: A DEMO WITHOUT A BEFORE IS NOT A MEASUREMENT. And the collision is not bad luck: `flow`, `flows_to`, `crossing`, `collects` and the rest of boot's vocabulary are exactly the words a graph example reaches for first.").

finding(f_the_book_namespace_is_flat_so_two_authors_share_one_book, defect).
recorded(f_the_book_namespace_is_flat_so_two_authors_share_one_book, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_book_namespace_is_flat_so_two_authors_share_one_book, decision).
witness(f_the_book_namespace_is_flat_so_two_authors_share_one_book, "authority(P, W)", 5).
finding_note(f_the_book_namespace_is_flat_so_two_authors_share_one_book, "THE OWNER PROPOSED SEPARATING AUTHOR FROM PERSPECTIVE - `@alice` with `[tells]` and `[thinks]` under it, one author owning several books - and the model already holds HALF of it. Measured: `authority(tells, alice).` plus `authority(thinks, alice).` loads, `authority(P, alice)` returns both, and `isa[B](bob, W)` returns `tells/jerk` beside `thinks/cute`, two incompatible readings of one subject standing apart because the book differs. A nested proposition rides along as a TERM, `wants[thinks](alice, asks_out(bob, alice))`, with the first-order boundary intact: it is data, and deriving from inside it needs a bridge rule. WHAT DOES NOT HOLD IS THE NAMESPACE. A book is a FLAT ATOM and carries no owner, so `authority(tells, alice).` and `authority(tells, bob).` name ONE book with two writers: `isa[tells](X, Y)` returns alice-said and bob-said rows together, and `authority(tells, W)` lists $kernel, alice, bob and user. Nothing distinguishes whose telling is whose, and `forged[audit]` cannot help because both were granted standing honestly. THE WITNESS SHOWS THE SHAPE IS ALREADY LIVE IN THE HONEST CORPUS: five authority rows over three books, with `main` and `audit` each carrying TWO writers. AND THE OBVIOUS REPAIR IS BLOCKED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, measured: a compound perspective `isa[tells(alice)](bob, jerk).` is refused by the parser with `expected ']', got '('` - the bracket admits an atom or a variable and nothing else - while the flattened `isa[alice_tells](bob, jerk).` is accepted BUT CANNOT BE GENERATED, since building `alice_tells` from `alice` and `tells` is concatenation. So a human may write the flat name by hand, losing exactly the structure the notation existed for, and no rule may mint it. THIS ALSO GIVES f_naming_yourself_is_itself_a_forgery A READING it did not have: when the book is `tells` and the author is `alice` the self-signature on the preamble disappears, because author and book have stopped being one name.").

finding(f_a_book_name_that_carries_its_owner_belongs_to_the_host, idea).
recorded(f_a_book_name_that_carries_its_owner_belongs_to_the_host, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_a_book_name_that_carries_its_owner_belongs_to_the_host, decision).
finding_note(f_a_book_name_that_carries_its_owner_belongs_to_the_host, "Follows from the flat namespace and from the concatenation ban, and the shape it wants is one this repository already owns. TWO WAYS OUT AND THEY COST DIFFERENTLY. FIRST, the host mints the name: rules parse `@alice [tells]` and emit `book_of(alice, tells)`, and the promoter builds the atom. That is a HOST EMITTER - the same declared loan as `host_emitter(js, modules, node_resolver, ...)` - countable by the audit, and the kernel does not grow by one operation nor is the finiteness proof touched. SECOND, a perspective becomes a compound term, which is better on the merits because the owner would then be visible in the book's own name. It is DEEPER THAN THE PARSER: the store keys a perspective by STRING - a snapshot row reads `\"persp\": \"$kernel\"` - so `registerPersp`, the kernel-ledger test and the whole perspective plumbing move with it, not the bracket rule alone. RECOMMENDED AND NOT DONE, because it is a language decision: take the first for now and record the second as the shape the language wants, since a name that carries its owner is the difference between a book and a filename.").

finding(f_i_answered_the_adjacent_question_and_used_the_harder_ones_name, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_answered_the_adjacent_question_and_used_the_harder_ones_name, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_i_answered_the_adjacent_question_and_used_the_harder_ones_name, doc).
finding_note(f_i_answered_the_adjacent_question_and_used_the_harder_ones_name, "Asked whether SYNTACTIC SUGAR could be written as ROFL rules, I built a term-level macro, demonstrated it end to end, priced it, and called it sugar throughout. The owner asked what the sugar actually was and it was NOTHING: `closure(reaches, edge).` is an ordinary fact in the existing grammar, the parser was never touched, not one token is new. I had answered the EASIER ADJACENT QUESTION and signed it with the harder one's name. THE BOUNDARY, measured against the real parser afterwards: everything expressible as a TERM is already accepted - `closure(reaches, edge).`, `define(reaches, transitive_of(edge)).`, `project(r, cols(a, b, c)).`, and notably `macro(\"reaches := edge+\")` - while `reaches := edge+.`, `reaches <- edge, edge.`, `edge(X, *, Z)`, a brace block and a semicolon are all refused. So the free category is NEW VOCABULARY, not new syntax, and it is wide only because ROFL's surface is thin, which is the Lisp bargain rather than a property of this design. THE JOIN, and it is the useful half: a STRING is a legal term, so arbitrary surface syntax travels inside one without any parser change, and the thing that reads it is ring 1 - at which point the cost is exactly `str_sub` and `atom_of` and nothing else. THE CLASS OF THE ERROR is not carelessness. A working demo is enormously persuasive to its author, and the pull is to let it answer the question that was asked rather than the one it answers; the tell was available and I did not look for it - I never asked WHICH TOKEN IS NEW, which takes one grep and would have settled it before the first line of the demo.").

finding(f_a_generic_audit_over_all_relations_is_first_order_after_all, defect).
recorded(f_a_generic_audit_over_all_relations_is_first_order_after_all, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_a_generic_audit_over_all_relations_is_first_order_after_all, doc).
witness_atleast(f_a_generic_audit_over_all_relations_is_first_order_after_all, "in_perspective(F, P)", 1).
finding_note(f_a_generic_audit_over_all_relations_is_first_order_after_all, "I TOLD THE OWNER AN ENTITY AUDIT WAS NOT EXPRESSIBLE AS A RULE, on the ground that a relation name cannot be a variable. He pushed, and the claim was WRONG because it conflated two positions. A relation name in a LITERAL cannot be a variable - that is what keeps the language first-order and it stands. But the reflection puts the name in ARGUMENT POSITION INSIDE A TERM, `$fact(Rel, Book, Args)`, and a variable there is ordinary. MEASURED, six lines and no kernel change: `arg1(Rel, A) :- in_perspective($fact(Rel, P, $cons(A, T)), P2).` plus `unknown_entity(Rel, V) :- closed(Rel), arg1(Rel, V), not thing(V).` over `thing(cat). thing(black). closed(isa). isa(cat, black). isa(dog, brown).` reports exactly `isa/dog` and `isa/brown`. AND `in_perspective` COVERS ANONYMOUS FACTS - nine rows on a program written with no `who:` at all - so the hole that would have made this useless is not there. THE BOUNDARY IS AN ASYMMETRY AND IT IS REAL: READING over all relations is free, WRITING into an arbitrary relation is not, because a rule HEAD demands an identifier. So `same_as(A, B)` can have its missing congruence DETECTED by a generic audit and cannot have it SUPPLIED by one. THE GENERAL CONSEQUENCE, and it is larger than the entity case: every audit of the form `over all facts of all relations` is expressible today. That is the remedy CLAUDE.md prescribes for hand-written twins - orphan_claim[audit] being the recorded instance - and it turns out to hold for FACTS and not only for rules. AS A DEFAULT THE LANGUAGE IS SILENT: a typo in a VALUE (`isa(X, magic) :- isa(X, blak).`) derives 0 rows with undefined_premise, malformed, breach and unmoded all at 0, while the control - a typo in the RELATION - is caught by undefined_premise[audit] naming `iza`. Relations are guarded, entities are not, and the repair is opt-in per relation.").

finding(f_an_optional_strictness_declaration_is_boilerplate_and_wants_sugar, idea).
recorded(f_an_optional_strictness_declaration_is_boilerplate_and_wants_sugar, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_an_optional_strictness_declaration_is_boilerplate_and_wants_sugar, decision).
finding_note(f_an_optional_strictness_declaration_is_boilerplate_and_wants_sugar, "THE OWNER'S READING OF THE ENTITY AUDIT, and it is the argument for the sugar layer stated better than I stated it: the feature is plainly useful and the boilerplate is painful, so this is exactly what sugar is for. He is right and the numbers say so - a guard whose whole content is `closed(isa).` costs FOUR RULES per relation shape, one `argN` walker per argument position plus one `unknown_entity` clause each, and the walkers are mechanical in the arity. The shape is already in the tree twice: `edb` is opt-in per relation and `demands_authorship(P)` is opt-in per ledger, green where nobody asked and red where somebody did. WHAT THIS ADDS is that the generated half is not a policy at all, it is a fold over arity, which is the exact profile of the macro layer measured the same day - a declaration in, rules out, why-tree intact, no kernel change. RECOMMENDED as the FIRST real customer of that layer rather than `closure/2`, because a transitive closure is two rules a person can write by hand and an arity fold is not.").

finding(f_the_rule_syntax_is_provably_sugar, insight).
recorded(f_the_rule_syntax_is_provably_sugar, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_rule_syntax_is_provably_sugar, doc).
finding_note(f_the_rule_syntax_is_provably_sugar, "DEMONSTRATED RATHER THAN ARGUED, and it settles what `:-` actually is. A snapshot was hand-extended with rows for a rule that NEVER PASSED THROUGH THE PARSER - `rule(r_step)`, `concludes(r_step, reaches)`, `conclusion_lit` and two `premise_lit` rows carrying `$lit`, `$cons` and `$var` terms - and restored through `Rofl.fromSnapshot`. It RUNS: `reaches(X, Y)` gives the full closure of a three-edge chain at 6 rows, and the why-tree names `r_step` and `r_base` down to `edge[main](a,b) [axiom]`. src/engine.ts:2 already says the evaluator reads rules ONLY from the reflected store; this is that sentence made visible from outside. CONSEQUENCE: `:-` is an ENCODING over reflection facts, and so are variables (`$var`), perspectives (an argument of `$lit`), tenses (an argument of `$lit`), negation (`$not`) and every builtin (`$builtin`). None of them is a host operation; all of them are terms. THE ONLY THINGS THAT ARE NOT are terms themselves and the act of putting one fact in a book.").

finding(f_i_measured_the_wrong_denominator_for_ring_zero, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_measured_the_wrong_denominator_for_ring_zero, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_i_measured_the_wrong_denominator_for_ring_zero, doc).
finding_note(f_i_measured_the_wrong_denominator_for_ring_zero, "SAME DAY, SAME QUESTION, AND THE SECOND ANSWER IS THREE TIMES THE FIRST. I measured ring 0 by asking WHAT SYNTAX MY RING 1 HAPPENED TO BE WRITTEN IN and got 21 of 28 features, 54 lines, 23 per cent - then read that as `the bootstrap does not pay`. THE DENOMINATOR WAS CIRCULAR: ring 1's source uses whatever I chose to write it in, so the measurement was of my own habits and not of the language. The owner supplied the criterion that is not circular - KEEP ONLY WHAT MAPS DIRECTLY TO A HOST OPERATION AND IS INEXPRESSIBLE OTHERWISE - and under it the answer is 13 of 28 and SIXTY code lines against 231, a saving of 74 per cent, with a working reader that reads exactly terms and facts. The class is one this repository already names: a figure offered as proof of impracticality must say which structural assumption produced it. Mine was `ring 1 is written in ordinary ROFL`, nobody required it, and dropping it moved the answer by a factor of three. THE TELL I MISSED was available in my own output: the seven features I found droppable were perspectives, tenses, wildcards, arithmetic - a list with no principle in it. A principled boundary produces a list you can state in one sentence; an incidental one produces a list you can only enumerate.").

finding(f_ring_zero_under_the_irreducibility_criterion_is_sixty_lines, insight).
recorded(f_ring_zero_under_the_irreducibility_criterion_is_sixty_lines, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_ring_zero_under_the_irreducibility_criterion_is_sixty_lines, decision).
finding_note(f_ring_zero_under_the_irreducibility_criterion_is_sixty_lines, "MEASURED AGAINST A WORKING READER, not estimated. Ring 0 that reads ONLY terms and facts - atoms, integers, negative integers, strings with escapes, `$`-names, nested compounds, argument lists, and `rel(args).` - is SIXTY CODE LINES against src/parser.ts's 231, and it reads a reflection-only program correctly (six rows, including `conclusion_lit/3` and `premise_lit/3` carrying nested `$lit`/`$cons`/`$var`). Thirteen of the twenty-eight surface features survive; the fifteen that go are variables, wildcards, comments, all arithmetic, perspectives, tenses, negation, both builtin forms, the rule form and the body list - EVERY ONE OF THEM AN ENCODING OVER A TERM. THREE COSTS, and they are relocations rather than savings. FIRST, ring 0 must be allowed to write `conclusion_lit`, which is exactly what the load path refuses today, so the write protection becomes `trusted caller` rather than `nobody` and the audit story around it changes. SECOND, ring 1 stops being optional: nobody hand-writes `conclusion_lit(r, 1, $lit(...))`, so the ergonomics of the whole language move into a layer that does not exist yet, where today a person can write ROFL with no layer at all. THIRD, ring 1 cannot be written in its own subset without being hand-encoded, so it SHIPS AS A SNAPSHOT rather than as source - which `Rofl.fromSnapshot` already supports and which is how a self-hosting compiler ships its bootstrap image anyway. ALSO MOVED, not removed: the language-level refusals - `'@next' is not allowed in rule bodies`, `'@init' is not allowed on rule heads` - stop being parser errors and become rules, which makes them auditable and which nobody has written. AND THE LIMIT OF THE CRITERION IS ONE STEP FURTHER THAN THIS: a fact is itself `$fact(Rel, Book, Args)`, so a ring 0 reading ONE form - a term, asserted - would be smaller still, and that is S-expressions.").

finding(f_resolution_policy_is_a_rulepack_and_disagreement_is_countable, insight).
recorded(f_resolution_policy_is_a_rulepack_and_disagreement_is_countable, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_resolution_policy_is_a_rulepack_and_disagreement_is_countable, demo).
finding_note(f_resolution_policy_is_a_rulepack_and_disagreement_is_countable, "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER RESOLVING A PRONOUN COULD BE A RULEPACK, and it can, with three properties an ordinary resolver does not have. MEASURED: two packs in two books, `authority(pack_recency, vendor_a)` and `authority(pack_subject, vendor_b)`, one resolving `she` by most-recent mention and the other by the book's subject. They DISAGREE - bob against alice - and `disputed(she)` fires from a two-line gate. FIRST, every resolution carries its vendor, so the record says who decided and not that the system decided. SECOND, disagreement is a COUNTED ROW rather than a surprise in production: two packs that differ on a pronoun show up in an audit instead of silently producing different downstream answers. THIRD, `why answer(pack_recency, bob)` walks into the negation and the `whynot` prints the FAILED ATTEMPTS - `1 > 2` and `2 > 2` both refused - so what the resolution stands on is visible including what it ruled out. THE GENERAL SHAPE: a resolution policy stops being code inside a translator and becomes an object with an author, a book and a way to be contradicted. THIS ALSO ANSWERS THE COREFERENCE QUESTION from the same conversation - alice's pizza and bob's pizza are two atoms in two books and NOTHING joins them until a third book says `same_as[world](pizza_a, pizza_b)`, which is a legitimate ledger by docs/choosing-perspectives.md because `in whose book is this entry` answers cleanly. The language's job is not to make the assumption but to make it impossible to make SILENTLY.").

finding(f_anaphora_and_relative_time_are_one_reference_problem, insight).
recorded(f_anaphora_and_relative_time_are_one_reference_problem, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_anaphora_and_relative_time_are_one_reference_problem, doc).
finding_note(f_anaphora_and_relative_time_are_one_reference_problem, "`she` and `yesterday` are the same defect wearing two coats, and treating them as one halves the design. Both are references whose ORIGIN is absent from the text - a salient entity, a reference date - both must be resolved outside the language, and both are ruined by the same mistake, which is substituting the resolved value into the record. The repair is one line of policy rather than two mechanisms: `resolves[translator](she, alice).` and `resolves[translator](yesterday, \"2026-09-03\").` - the resolution is a FACT IN THE RESOLVER'S BOOK, so it carries an author, appears in the why-tree, and can be contradicted by the person it is about. WHAT MAKES THE PAIR EXACT is that both are also blocked by the same arithmetic rule: a salience ranking and a date offset are both CONSTRUCTIONS - `today minus one day` yields a date that was not in the program, the same defect as `N is M + 1` - so neither can be computed in the kernel and both come in as data or through a declared host emitter. THE SLOGAN: everything a human fills in automatically becomes a fact in the book of whoever filled it in. The language does not have to guess it; it has to refuse to let it through unsigned.").

finding(f_string_comparison_refuses_loudly_and_that_is_the_whole_date_gap, insight).
recorded(f_string_comparison_refuses_loudly_and_that_is_the_whole_date_gap, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_string_comparison_refuses_loudly_and_that_is_the_whole_date_gap, decision).
finding_note(f_string_comparison_refuses_loudly_and_that_is_the_whole_date_gap, "THE OWNER ASKED FOR A DATE TYPE AND HALF OF IT IS ALREADY HERE. Measured on ISO-8601 strings: `D is str_seg(T, \"T\", 0)` gives `\"2026-09-03\"` and the hour extracts through a second cut, so DECOMPOSITION IS FREE TODAY. Exactly one thing is missing - `TX < TY` on two strings gives 0 rows AND `hole($rule(r), arith_type_error)`, while `=` on strings works and returns 2. I PREDICTED THIS WOULD BE SILENTLY FALSE AND IT IS NOT: it refuses loudly, which is the better of the two and which my own probe caught me on in its last printed line. SO THE WHOLE GAP IS THE FOUR ORDER COMPARISONS. Admitting strings there is the CHEAPEST POSSIBLE EXTENSION by this repository's own criterion, and strictly cheaper than the destructors already admitted: a comparison CREATES NO TERM AT ALL, so the Herbrand universe is not touched by one element and the finiteness proof has nothing to say about it. And then ISO-8601 IS the date type, because it is designed so that lexicographic order coincides with chronological order - no new sort, no new operation, no calendar in the kernel. WHAT STAYS OUT is date arithmetic: `yesterday` constructs a date that was not in the program, which is the `N is M + 1` defect, so relative time arrives as data or through a declared emitter. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a pair of ISO-8601 timestamps whose lexicographic and chronological orders disagree - mixed offsets would do it, which is an argument for normalising to UTC at the boundary rather than for a kernel change.").

finding(f_the_bun_job_fails_at_fourteen_more_than_node, defect).
recorded(f_the_bun_job_fails_at_fourteen_more_than_node, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_bun_job_fails_at_fourteen_more_than_node, doc).
finding_note(f_the_bun_job_fails_at_fourteen_more_than_node, "CI on nextver is red on BOTH runners and they are red at very different depths: node reports 8 failures, bun reports 22 over the same 954 tests - 929 pass, 3 skip - so FOURTEEN FAILURES EXIST ONLY UNDER BUN. The recorded explanation for the branch being red covers seven of them, `f_the_leak_audit_now_fires_on_a_ledger_polymorphic_head`, and says nothing about the other fifteen. The bun-only names are a different family: `DIVERGENCE, environment against environment`, `DIVERGENCE, rules against host`, `WHY: the answer stands on the failed attempts`, `every audit gate reads ZERO on the model as written`, `answer_ambiguous[audit]`, `MUTANT 2`, `MUTANT 4` - the module-resolution work, not the ledger work. CLAUDE.md requires both runners green IN CI and names the bun job as where the second runner is checked, so a persistent fourteen-test gap is a hole in exactly the gate that exists to catch runtime divergence. NOT MINE AND MEASURED SAYING SO: the failed-job log for the previous commit is byte-identical in length for bun (2482) and differs by two for node (1649 against 1647, a timing figure), so both jobs were failing this way before this session touched anything. RECORDED RATHER THAN FIXED, because the cause is unknown and the finding is that NOBODY IS LOOKING - a red job on a branch that is expected to be red stops being read, which is the same defect as a gate permanently wrong in the safe direction.").

finding(f_a_witness_over_a_growing_ledger_drifts_by_construction, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_witness_over_a_growing_ledger_drifts_by_construction, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_a_witness_over_a_growing_ledger_drifts_by_construction, doc).
finding_note(f_a_witness_over_a_growing_ledger_drifts_by_construction, "Caught within one minute of writing it, by the checker, on my own entry. I gave a finding the witness `in_perspective(F, P)` at 1253 to state that fact-level reflection is enumerable - and `in_perspective` counts EVERY FACT IN THE WORLD, which for this checker is boot.rofl plus the ledger. The nine findings I recorded in the same edit moved it to 1290 and the witness was STALE BEFORE THE COMMIT. The defect is not the number, it is the shape: A WITNESS WHOSE QUERY RANGES OVER THE WHOLE STORE MEASURES THE LEDGER'S SIZE AND NOT THE CLAIM, so every unrelated addition breaks it and the repair is a number nobody learns anything from. scripts/witness_check.ts already says this in its own header - a witness measures A CONSEQUENCE, never the code - and a running total is the store's equivalent of a line number. THE FIX IS THE INSTRUMENT ALREADY THERE: `witness_atleast` with 1, which states the load-bearing half - fact reflection is populated at all - and does not move when the ledger grows. THE TEST TO APPLY BEFORE WRITING ONE: ask whether adding an unrelated finding would change the number. If it would, the query is over the wrong thing.").

finding(f_the_front_end_is_already_a_compiler_and_the_snapshot_is_the_object_file, insight).
recorded(f_the_front_end_is_already_a_compiler_and_the_snapshot_is_the_object_file, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_front_end_is_already_a_compiler_and_the_snapshot_is_the_object_file, doc).
finding_note(f_the_front_end_is_already_a_compiler_and_the_snapshot_is_the_object_file, "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER THE PLAN MAKES THE LANGUAGE COMPILED, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT IT ALREADY IS - what the plan does is name the object file. src/parser.ts says it in its own first two lines: text to clause objects, and clause objects are A TRANSIENT PARSE ARTIFACT, the evaluator's source of truth being the reflected store; src/engine.ts:2 says the evaluator reads rules ONLY from there. So the pipeline is source, front end, store rows, evaluator - a compiler front end and an interpreting back end, by definition rather than by analogy. MEASURED over boot.rofl plus the js model, six files: a snapshot taken after loading from source and a snapshot taken after restoring that snapshot are md5-IDENTICAL at a69ab7e215bbabfaf198690ae3a8d462, the image is 1236 KiB, and restoring costs 15.0 ms against 173.0 ms to parse, a factor of 11.5 at five runs and the median. WHAT CHANGES IS NOT THE NATURE BUT THE STATUS OF THE ARTIFACT: save/restore is a REPL convenience today and becomes the way a layer SHIPS, at which point separate compilation is meaningful and the recorded rulepack hazard becomes a LINKER problem - ruleIdOf is a content hash carrying variable names, so two editions of one pack give a shared conclusion two derivations. AND WHAT IS NOT HAPPENING, said plainly because the word invites the claim: there is NO lowering and NO optimisation. The rows in the image are the same rows the parser produces today; 11.5x is the cost of parsing avoided, not execution made faster. The back end remains a fixpoint interpreter.").

finding(f_futamura_names_the_frame_but_the_payoff_is_in_join_specialisation, insight).
recorded(f_futamura_names_the_frame_but_the_payoff_is_in_join_specialisation, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_futamura_names_the_frame_but_the_payoff_is_in_join_specialisation, doc).
finding_note(f_futamura_names_the_frame_but_the_payoff_is_in_join_specialisation, "The owner reached for the Futamura projections - specialise an interpreter to a program and get a compiled program; specialise the specialiser to the interpreter and get a compiler; specialise it to itself and get a compiler generator - and the frame is right while the target is one layer over from where it was aimed. CACHING THE FRONT END IS NOT A PROJECTION. A snapshot is the front end's output stored ahead of time; the 11.5x measured for it is parsing avoided. A projection removes INTERPRETIVE OVERHEAD, which is a different quantity. WHERE THE REAL FIRST PROJECTION SITS: the evaluator is a fixpoint over a RULE SET, so specialising it to a fixed rule set yields a program computing that fixpoint with no rule decoding and no generic join loop. ROFL is unusually well placed for it because the specialiser's input is already canonical data - decodeRules(store) - so nothing needs parsing to specialise. BUT THE PAYOFF IS MEASURED AND IT IS NOT IN DECODING. This repository already recorded where the time burns: an ordinary three-way join kills the host with peakRows at 500200 while THE STORE IS EMPTY, eight million partial solutions living inside the join with nothing concluded. So specialising the decode buys almost nothing and specialising the JOIN PLANS per rule buys everything - and that work, named honestly, is a QUERY PLANNER rather than a Futamura projection. THE FRAME IS STILL WORTH KEEPING, not as a work plan but as a check that the layers are cut in the right places: text to rules, rules to plans, plans to execution, each with its own artifact. The middle row is the only one where specialisation pays and the only one this session did not touch.").

finding(f_an_image_moves_the_audit_surface_off_readable_source, pitfall).
recorded(f_an_image_moves_the_audit_surface_off_readable_source, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_an_image_moves_the_audit_surface_off_readable_source, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_an_image_moves_the_audit_surface_off_readable_source, "Named BEFORE building it, because the class is one this repository has already paid for twice. Shipping a layer as a snapshot - and the owner's next step is baking it into a Rust binary - moves the thing a reviewer reads from a .rofl file to 1236 KiB of JSON that nobody opens. The recorded class is `a gate red on an honest checkout gets switched off, and then its absence is invisible`; the image version is milder and worse: nothing goes red at all, the blob simply stops being read, and a layer whose source has drifted from its image announces itself through BEHAVIOUR rather than through a check. THE DEFENCE IS FREE AND WAS MEASURED WHILE FINDING THIS: the image must be REPRODUCIBLE FROM SOURCE, built in CI and compared, and the comparison is exact - md5 a69ab7e215bbabfaf198690ae3a8d462 from source and from restore, byte for byte, over six files. So the rule to write with the first shipped image: an image is a CACHE and never a source of truth, and the gate that keeps it one is a CI step rebuilding it and diffing. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: an image whose content legitimately differs from a rebuild - a timestamp, a map iteration order, a host version in the header - which would mean the artifact is not deterministic and the gate must compare canonical state rather than bytes. That is worth checking BEFORE the gate is written, since a byte comparison that is flaky will be the first thing switched off.").

finding(f_a_rofl_escape_takes_the_next_character_and_no_escape_makes_a_newline, defect).
recorded(f_a_rofl_escape_takes_the_next_character_and_no_escape_makes_a_newline, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_a_rofl_escape_takes_the_next_character_and_no_escape_makes_a_newline, doc).
finding_note(f_a_rofl_escape_takes_the_next_character_and_no_escape_makes_a_newline, "COST AN HOUR OF DEBUGGING AND THE BUG WAS IN THE HARNESS, NOT THE GRAMMAR. src/parser.ts implements a string escape as `out += src[j + 1]` - take the next character LITERALLY - so `\"\\n\"` is the LETTER n and `\"\\t\"` is the letter t. There is NO ESCAPE THAT PRODUCES A LINE FEED. Measured against the real parser: backslash-n parses to \"n\" at length 1, a LITERAL newline between the quotes parses to a line feed at length 1, and a literal tab likewise. TWO CONSEQUENCES, both paid for. The character-class table for the rules-side parser wrote `cls(\"\\n\", nl).` and thereby declared THE LETTER N to be a newline, which made `kind(21, nl)` true and ended every comment at the first `n` inside it - the comment `-- a comment` stopped at `comme|nt`. What found it was `whynot`, which printed `failed premise: not kind(21,nl) -- blocked: kind(21,nl) holds`; no amount of reading the grammar would have. AND `JSON.stringify` IS THE WRONG ESCAPER FOR ROFL, which is the trap for any host handing source to a program: its `\\n` becomes an `n`, so the whole file arrives with every newline replaced by a letter and the parse fails in a way that looks like a grammar gap. The right one escapes the quote and the backslash and writes everything else, newlines included, as itself. ALSO MEASURED: scripts/text_check.ts allows TAB and LF and reports a lone CR, so a literal line feed in a string is legal in a checked-in file and a literal CR is not - which is why the class table carries exactly one control character and treats everything unclassified as simply not a token character. SMALL AND REAL IN PASSING: the tokenizer does not increment its line counter inside a string literal, so a string containing a newline shifts every later error's reported line by one.").

finding(f_a_front_end_has_three_ways_to_return_a_prefix_and_none_is_a_grammar_bug, defect).
recorded(f_a_front_end_has_three_ways_to_return_a_prefix_and_none_is_a_grammar_bug, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_a_front_end_has_three_ways_to_return_a_prefix_and_none_is_a_grammar_bug, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_a_front_end_has_three_ways_to_return_a_prefix_and_none_is_a_grammar_bug, "A PARSER THAT RETURNS A PREFIX AND CALLS IT A FILE is the silent under-report this repository exists to refuse, and building one produced THREE INDEPENDENT WAYS TO DO IT in an afternoon. None was a grammar bug and none would have been caught by comparing against the host parser, because each returns a WELL-FORMED SMALLER ANSWER. ONE, THE WALK STOPS. The top-level chain goes clause to clause, so the first uncovered construct ends it and every later clause never appears: examples/counter.rofl gave ZERO clauses against the host's three, no error, no diagnostic. Caught by `stuck_at(I) :- top(I), not parses_at(I).`, which names the offset. TWO, THE WALK NEVER STARTS. When the FIRST clause fails there is nothing for the chain to stand on, so `stuck_at` is empty as well: rules/strata.rofl returned zero clauses, zero stuck and no error. Caught by coverage - `uncovered(K) :- tokstart(K), not covered(K).` - every token must lie inside some accepted clause. THREE, THE EVALUATION DOES NOT FINISH, and this is the one no rule could have caught: the parse relations were never computed, so clauses, stuck AND uncovered were all empty together and the answer looked green. Caught by reading `evaluate().partial` and the `hole` relation, which is the kernel's own word for `this answer is not an answer` and which the host had been discarding. THE TRANSFERABLE SHAPE: each gate was blind to the mode discovered after it, and the third was blind to nothing only because it asks about the INSTRUMENT rather than about the answer. A checker that reads only the result cannot tell a small answer from a stopped run.").

finding(f_the_chart_was_cubic_and_the_space_wall_made_it_look_green, defect).
recorded(f_the_chart_was_cubic_and_the_space_wall_made_it_look_green, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_chart_was_cubic_and_the_space_wall_made_it_look_green, doc).
finding_note(f_the_chart_was_cubic_and_the_space_wall_made_it_look_green, "The rules-side parser found the next token by `follows(J, I2) :- tok(I1, J), tokstart(I2), J < I2.` over all pairs of boundaries, then `blocked` over all TRIPLES - cubic in tokens, and written that way because it is the obvious relational spelling of `the least start greater than J`. On rules/strata.rofl, 2207 characters and 123 tokens, it hit the SPACE wall: `evaluate` came back partial with `hole($rule(...), space_exhausted)` and `hole($load(4), budget_exhausted)`, every relation downstream of `nexttok` was empty, and the parse reported zero clauses WITH NO COMPLAINT. THE FIX IS LINEAR AND IS THE SAME IDEA THE LEDGER ALREADY RECORDS FOR JAVASCRIPT - walk one position at a time instead of quantifying over pairs: `scan(J, K) :- tok(I, J), K is J + 1, at(K).` plus `scan(J, K2) :- scan(J, K), not tokstart(K), K2 is K + 1, at(K2).` and `nexttok(J, K) :- scan(J, K), tokstart(K).` MEASURED AFTER: partial false, holes zero, and the token count on that file went from a TRUNCATED 123 to a complete 218 - the cubic version had not merely been slow, it had been silently wrong about how many tokens the file has. WHAT THIS SAYS ABOUT THE WALLS: the space wall is doing its job and the program above it was not listening. A host that ignores `partial` converts a wall into a wrong answer, which is worse than a crash and is exactly why `hole` exists.").

finding(f_ring_one_agrees_with_the_host_parser_on_eight_of_twenty_three, insight).
recorded(f_ring_one_agrees_with_the_host_parser_on_eight_of_twenty_three, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_ring_one_agrees_with_the_host_parser_on_eight_of_twenty_three, demo).
finding_note(f_ring_one_agrees_with_the_host_parser_on_eight_of_twenty_three, "THE FRONT END NOW EXISTS IN THE LANGUAGE AND THE NUMBER IS MEASURED RATHER THAN PROMISED. examples/ring1 tokenizes and parses ROFL with rules; over the 23 corpus files at or under 2.5 KiB, compared against src/parser.ts as canonical clauses: EIGHT IDENTICAL, TEN REFUSED WITH THE OFFSET NAMED, FIVE SILENTLY DIVERGENT. Covered: comments, facts, rules, `not`, perspective brackets, and terms that are atoms, variables, integers or strings. Not covered: builtins and arithmetic, temporal markers, compound terms, wildcards. THE THREE CATEGORIES ARE NOT EQUAL and the middle one is the achievement: a refusal that names the byte offset is a front end saying `I do not know this`, which is what the first version could not do at all. THE FIVE SILENT DIVERGENCES ARE THE ONLY BAD NUMBER and the test pins them with a CEILING rather than a floor, because that is the count that must not grow - they are files where ring 1 returns a DIFFERENT PROGRAM without saying so. AND NO KERNEL CHANGE WAS NEEDED, which was the open question from the morning: a token is a pair of indices, keywords are matched character by character against `str_char`, and the host resolves ranges to text as a declared loan. `str_sub` and `atom_of` were expected to be prerequisites and turned out not to be - for a front end that hands its output to a host promoter, they are conveniences rather than blockers. THE SWEEP IS CAPPED AT 2.5 KiB DELIBERATELY: at 8 KiB it took 75 seconds, more than half the whole suite, and the development loop here is `npm test`.").

finding(f_the_wildcard_number_was_a_backtracking_artifact, defect).
recorded(f_the_wildcard_number_was_a_backtracking_artifact, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_wildcard_number_was_a_backtracking_artifact, doc).
finding_note(f_the_wildcard_number_was_a_backtracking_artifact, "THE SECOND PARSER PAID FOR ITSELF ON ITS FIRST DAY, and in exactly the way that was predicted for it on 2026-09-01: not as an oracle, but as DIVERGENCES ON CASES THE AUTHOR NEVER HELD IN MIND. All five silent divergences between ring 1 and src/parser.ts were one thing, and the bug was in the HOST. `src/parser.ts` mints a fresh clause-local name per wildcard, so two underscores in one clause are two different variables - reading them as one variable called `_` adds a join nobody wrote, which is why `ast_child[code](P, _, _, C)` matters. Ring 1 numbering them 0, 1, 2 STILL disagreed, because THE HOST WAS NUMBERING THEM 1, 3, 5. Measured directly: `a(X) :- p(X, _), q(X, _).` gave `_$1` and `_$3`. The cause is in `bodyElem`: a positive body literal is parsed TWICE - once as an expression, then `this.pos = save` and parsed again as a literal - and each pass consumes the wildcards, so a rewind that restores the POSITION and not the COUNTER leaks the number of backtracks into the name. THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT COSMETIC: `ruleIdOf` is a content hash over the canonical clause INCLUDING variable names - already recorded as the rename leak that gives one shared conclusion two derivations across pack editions - so every wildcard-bearing rule's IDENTITY was a function of the parser's strategy rather than of the program, and any change to how `bodyElem` backtracks would have silently renamed them all. THE FIX IS TWO LINES, saving and restoring `freshCounter` beside `pos`, and the blast radius was measured rather than hoped: exactly two tests went red, both asserting on a quoted `_$1`, plus the README and page that quote the same demonstration verbatim. Corpus agreement went from 8 identical / 5 silently divergent to 13 IDENTICAL AND ZERO SILENT. A CHART PARSER HAS NO BACKTRACKING, which is why it could see this and the host could not - a second implementation finds what the first cannot, precisely where the first has a strategy the second does not share.").

finding(f_the_escaper_belongs_beside_the_unescaper, insight).
recorded(f_the_escaper_belongs_beside_the_unescaper, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_escaper_belongs_beside_the_unescaper, doc).
finding_note(f_the_escaper_belongs_beside_the_unescaper, "ASKED WHAT TO ESCAPE WITH IF NOT JSON, and the honest answer is that the language shipped an unescaper with no escaper, so every caller had to invent one and the nearest thing to hand was wrong. `src/parser.ts` implements `\\X` as `take X literally`, JSON implements `\\n` as a line feed, and the two disagree on precisely the character a source text is full of. `escapeString` now sits in src/parser.ts directly above `parseProgram`, so the pair is visible in one screen; examples/ring1 re-exports it rather than keeping a copy. MEASURED ACROSS THE CORPUS, because the shape of the fix depends on what is actually written: 88 escape sequences inside string literals over 69 files, and they are `\\\"` 82 times and `\\\\` six times. NOTHING ELSE - no `\\n`, no `\\t`, no `\\r`, no `\\0`, `\\x` or `\\u`. So the escaper needs exactly two cases and everything else, newlines included, is written as itself. THE GENERAL RULE THIS IS AN INSTANCE OF: a parser that ships without its printer forces every host to guess the inverse, and the guess will be whichever standard the host language makes convenient. The same applies to any reader here whose writer lives elsewhere.").

finding(f_changing_the_escape_semantics_is_free_today_and_will_not_stay_free, decision).
recorded(f_changing_the_escape_semantics_is_free_today_and_will_not_stay_free, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_changing_the_escape_semantics_is_free_today_and_will_not_stay_free, decision).
finding_note(f_changing_the_escape_semantics_is_free_today_and_will_not_stay_free, "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER TO CHANGE WHAT AN ESCAPE MEANS, and the number that decides it was measured before answering: the corpus contains 88 escape sequences and every one is `\\\"` or `\\\\`. ZERO are `\\n`, `\\t`, `\\r`, `\\0`, `\\x` or `\\u`, so adopting C-style escapes would change the meaning of NOT ONE existing string in 69 files. The change is free TODAY and gets more expensive with every file written, because the cost is proportional to how many `\\n` sequences exist meaning the letter n. THE CASE FOR CHANGING, and it is two things rather than taste. `\\n` silently meaning `n` is a trap every programmer walks into - it cost an hour here, in a character-class table that thereby declared the letter n to be a newline. And there is a REAL EXPRESSIVENESS GAP: a line feed can be written literally between the quotes, but a CARRIAGE RETURN cannot be written at all, because a literal CR is refused by scripts/text_check.ts as a lone CR and no escape produces one. So CRLF data is currently INEXPRESSIBLE in a ROFL string. THE CASE AGAINST is that `take the next character literally` is one sentence and a table of escapes is a table, and this language has kept its vocabulary closed on purpose. A middle option exists: recognise `\\n`, `\\t`, `\\r` and keep `\\X` as X otherwise, which closes the gap and keeps the rule short. NOT DECIDED HERE - it is a language change and belongs to the owner. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THE `it is free` HALF: any `.rofl` outside this repository, since the measurement covers only the corpus in the tree, and a rulepack shipped elsewhere would carry its own strings.").

finding(f_escapes_are_c_style_now_and_the_change_was_a_no_op, decision).
recorded(f_escapes_are_c_style_now_and_the_change_was_a_no_op, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_escapes_are_c_style_now_and_the_change_was_a_no_op, doc).
finding_note(f_escapes_are_c_style_now_and_the_change_was_a_no_op, "DECIDED by the owner and landed the same hour, on a number measured BEFORE the change rather than after: the corpus held 88 escape sequences over 69 files and every one was a quote or a backslash - zero n, t, r, 0, x or u - so C-style escapes could not alter one existing string. THE ORACLE WAS EXACT AND FREE and is the same one the two earlier host-to-rules migrations used: the canonical clauses of all 69 files, md5 31b3b8bbe936c474c64bab9b3a9181b4 before and after, byte for byte. THE TABLE IS FIVE ENTRIES - n, t, r, backslash, quote - and deliberately no xNN, no uNNNN and no NUL: a NUL in a value is not something this language needs and the text gate exists to keep NULs out of files. AN UNKNOWN ESCAPE IS NOW AN ERROR rather than a silent backslash-drop, which is the one place the change is not backward compatible and is the point: dropping it leaves the program running with a value nobody wrote, and the corpus contains none, so refusing costs nothing today and catches every typo from here on. WHAT THIS BUYS BEYOND THE TRAP: a CARRIAGE RETURN IS NOW EXPRESSIBLE. It was not - a literal CR is refused by scripts/text_check.ts as a lone CR and no escape produced one - so CRLF data could not be written in a ROFL string at all. THE BOUNDARY IS SWEPT RATHER THAN SAMPLED, in test/escapes.test.ts: every byte of C0 plus SPACE plus DEL planted one at a time, the writable set asserted BY NAME as exactly TAB, LF, CR and SPACE, and the escaper compared against text_check's own predicate byte for byte - ZERO DISAGREEMENTS OVER 34 BYTES. A ONE-LINE FIX RODE ALONG: the tokenizer did not count lines inside a string literal, so every error after a multi-line string was reported one line early. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a .rofl file outside this repository whose strings use a backslash before any character other than a quote or a backslash - a rulepack shipped elsewhere would carry its own strings and the no-op measurement covers only the corpus in the tree, so such a file would now fail to load rather than load with a different meaning, which is the direction the refusal was chosen for.").

finding(f_round_tripping_is_not_the_same_as_being_writable, insight).
recorded(f_round_tripping_is_not_the_same_as_being_writable, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_round_tripping_is_not_the_same_as_being_writable, doc).
finding_note(f_round_tripping_is_not_the_same_as_being_writable, "The first sweep of the new escapes reported 37 of 37 round-tripping exactly, and that green was HIDING SOMETHING THE CHECK COULD NOT LOOK AT. `escapeString` writes a character with no named escape as ITSELF, so a NUL or an 0x01 came back through the parser perfectly - the pair really is inverse - while the source text it produced carried a raw control byte that scripts/text_check.ts refuses. THE PROPERTY BEING TESTED WAS INVERSION AND THE PROPERTY THAT MATTERED WAS WRITABILITY, and the two are independent: a value can round-trip out of a file nobody can commit. This is the repository's own question - where is this check structurally unable to look - applied to a check written ten minutes earlier, and the answer was `at the file`. THE REPAIR IS IN THE ESCAPER, not in the test: `escapeString` now REFUSES a character it cannot write legally and names it, because the alternative is emitting source that a later gate rejects, which moves the failure away from the code that caused it. And the test now asserts BOTH - the round trip AND that the produced literal contains no byte the gate bans - plus a separate check that the escaper and the gate agree byte for byte over the whole boundary, since the two were written apart and can drift. THE TRANSFERABLE FORM: for any writer whose output is consumed by a checker, `inverse of the reader` is a weaker property than `accepted by the checker`, and a test that asserts only the first will be green on output nobody can use.").

finding(f_a_test_about_escaping_written_with_escaping_is_wrong_twice, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_test_about_escaping_written_with_escaping_is_wrong_twice, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_a_test_about_escaping_written_with_escaping_is_wrong_twice, doc).
finding_note(f_a_test_about_escaping_written_with_escaping_is_wrong_twice, "THREE FAILURES IN ONE HOUR, ALL THE SAME SHAPE, all of them mine and none of them about the language. Generating the character-class table through Python I wrote four backslashes where two were meant, and the file declared a two-character sequence to be the quote class - caught by the parser. Writing the escape test through a Python heredoc, the quote-escapes came out as literal backslash-quote pairs and the file would not lex at all. And a bash heredoc for the boundary sweep was REFUSED BY THE TOOL ITSELF for containing a literal DEL character, which is the exact byte the test was about. THE CLASS: when the subject of a file is escaping and the notation of the file is also escaping, a mistake in the notation looks exactly like a finding about the subject, and there is no reading of the source that separates them - the first version of the character table was WRONG AND PLAUSIBLE for an hour. THE REMEDY THAT WORKED, and it is cheap: test/escapes.test.ts contains NOT ONE BACKSLASH in its own literals. Every sequence is assembled from character codes - `String.fromCharCode(92)` for the backslash - so the notation carries no instance of the thing under test and a typo in one cannot masquerade as a fact about the other. The general form is the one this repository already applies to oracles: an instrument must not be built out of the material it measures.").

finding(f_ring_one_now_agrees_with_the_host_on_every_file_it_sweeps, insight).
recorded(f_ring_one_now_agrees_with_the_host_on_every_file_it_sweeps, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_ring_one_now_agrees_with_the_host_on_every_file_it_sweeps, demo).
finding_note(f_ring_one_now_agrees_with_the_host_on_every_file_it_sweeps, "THE FRONT END WRITTEN IN ROFL NOW AGREES WITH src/parser.ts ON EVERY CORPUS FILE IT SWEEPS: 23 of 23 at or under 2.5 KiB, byte-identical as canonical clauses, with zero refusals and zero silent divergences - up from 13 identical and 10 refused this morning. Added in one pass: perspective brackets holding a VARIABLE as well as an atom, temporal markers, comparison and `is` builtins, arithmetic with precedence and parentheses, `mod`, and compound terms. STILL NOT COVERED, ON PURPOSE AND NOT FOR LACK OF A RULE: a negative integer literal. `X - 1` and `X, -1` are genuinely ambiguous; the host resolves them by parsing greedily from the left and a chart reports both, so matching it needs its own pass. Refusing loudly is the right interim answer and it keeps the `refused and located` gate alive with a REAL subject rather than a planted one - which matters, because that gate's previous subject (a temporal marker) stopped being uncovered and the test went green for the wrong reason. STILL NO KERNEL CHANGE, across the whole grammar: a token is a pair of indices, every keyword is matched character by character against `str_char`, and the host resolves ranges as a declared loan. THE COST IS TIME AND IT IS SUPERLINEAR IN FILE SIZE: measured 7.1 s over 6 files at 900 bytes, 14.5 s over 10 at 1200, 30.9 s over 16 at 1600 and 72.7 s over 23 at 2500, against a whole suite of about 117 s. The suite sweeps at 1200 and the README records the 2.5 KiB number with the line to raise; making the chart cheaper is the next performance question and it is separate from correctness.").

finding(f_eight_hand_written_cases_passed_while_every_real_file_failed, pitfall).
recorded(f_eight_hand_written_cases_passed_while_every_real_file_failed, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_eight_hand_written_cases_passed_while_every_real_file_failed, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_eight_hand_written_cases_passed_while_every_real_file_failed, "MEASURED, AND THE GAP IS THE WHOLE FINDING: eight hand-written cases covering every construct I had just added all passed - facts, rules, comparisons, `is` with arithmetic, a temporal marker on a head and on a rule, a variable perspective, a nested compound term, arithmetic with parentheses and precedence - AND ALL TWENTY-THREE REAL FILES WERE REFUSED. The cause was one rule: a lone dash in code is minus, and the scanner's state BEFORE the first dash of a comment is still `code`, so every `--` produced a spurious minus token. NOT ONE OF THE EIGHT CASES HAD A COMMENT IN IT, and every real file has one. THE CLASS IS NOT `write more cases`. A hand-written case is written from the same understanding as the code, so it exercises what the author was thinking about; the corpus exercises what the author was NOT. This repository already says a mutant set must ask where the check cannot LOOK - the same question aimed at a test set says: what does every real input have that none of my examples has? For a parser the answers are comments, blank lines, unicode in prose, a file that ends without a newline, and a file that is only comments. A SECOND DEFECT FROM THE SAME PASS AND THE SAME SHAPE: a quote inside a comment opened a string, because the first lexer paired quotes across the whole file and found comments separately - a chicken and egg, since a comment is only a comment outside a string. Two corpus files carry a quoted phrase in prose and both were refused at the comment. Neither defect is reachable from any example I would have invented.").

finding(f_cutting_ring_zero_without_an_image_stops_the_system_rather_than_shrinking_it, insight).
recorded(f_cutting_ring_zero_without_an_image_stops_the_system_rather_than_shrinking_it, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_cutting_ring_zero_without_an_image_stops_the_system_rather_than_shrinking_it, doc).
finding_note(f_cutting_ring_zero_without_an_image_stops_the_system_rather_than_shrinking_it, "THE OWNER ASKED WHAT HAPPENS IF RING 0 IS CUT BEFORE THE IMAGE EXISTS, and the answer is sharper than `it would be inconvenient`. Measured with the sixty-line terms-and-facts reader: examples/ring1/charclass.rofl is 83 facts and 0 rules and LOADS; examples/ring1/ring1.rofl is 7 facts and 126 RULES and is refused at line 24, the first `:-`; and boot.rofl is 7 facts and 21 rules and is refused at line 11. THE DATA SURVIVES AND EVERY RULE DIES, THE KERNEL'S OWN PROGRAM INCLUDED - so the system does not degrade, IT DOES NOT START. THE PRICE OF THE ALTERNATIVE IS A NUMBER: hand-encoding those rules as reflection rows is 3 rows per rule plus one per premise, which over 147 rules and 386 premises is 827 ROWS - 104 for boot.rofl and 723 for the grammar - and every `conclusion_lit` among them carries a nested $lit/$cons/$var term written by hand. That is what the image buys, and it is why the order held all session is right: ring 1 working, then the image, then and only then the cut. THE IMAGE NOW EXISTS AND IS MEASURED: 702 KiB, 12.3 ms to restore against 50.5 ms to load from source, a factor of 4.1, and ring 1 parses from it exactly as from source. It is NOT committed - an image moves what a reviewer reads from a .rofl file to 700 KiB nobody opens, which is a class already recorded here, so it stays a cache built on demand and committing one is a separate decision.").

finding(f_an_image_gate_must_compare_content_and_not_bytes, insight).
recorded(f_an_image_gate_must_compare_content_and_not_bytes, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_an_image_gate_must_compare_content_and_not_bytes, doc).
finding_note(f_an_image_gate_must_compare_content_and_not_bytes, "THIS IS THE REFUTER I WROTE INTO f_an_image_moves_the_audit_surface_off_readable_source THIS MORNING, CONFIRMED THE SAME DAY. I said a byte comparison must first be shown deterministic or it would be the first gate switched off, and named `a map iteration order or a host version in the header` as what would break it. MEASURED: two builds of the same recipe in one process are byte-identical, and restoring the image and re-saving is byte-identical - so the snapshot IS deterministic. But loading the SAME THREE FILES IN A DIFFERENT ORDER changes the bytes while leaving the content the same, and the difference is exactly one section. `facts`, `wits`, `firings`, `tickLog` and `tick` are byte-identical; `evals` differs, because it logs HOW the image was built rather than what is in it. `canonicalState()` agrees across both orders, which is the store's own answer to the same question. SO THE GATE COMPARES CONTENT: the snapshot with `evals` removed, which is `imageContent` in examples/ring1/demo.ts, and the test asserts BOTH halves - that the two orders agree on content AND that they DISAGREE on raw bytes, because if the raw bytes agreed the gate would be measuring nothing. THE TRANSFERABLE FORM: an artifact that records its own provenance cannot be compared as bytes, and the fix is not to strip provenance but to name which part of the artifact is the CLAIM and compare that. The load order is part of the recipe either way, and is written down beside the sources.").

finding(f_the_tower_stands_and_its_bottom_step_is_one_hundred_and_seventy_eight_lines, insight).
recorded(f_the_tower_stands_and_its_bottom_step_is_one_hundred_and_seventy_eight_lines, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_tower_stands_and_its_bottom_step_is_one_hundred_and_seventy_eight_lines, demo).
finding_note(f_the_tower_stands_and_its_bottom_step_is_one_hundred_and_seventy_eight_lines, "THE OWNER REJECTED THE SHAPE AND WAS RIGHT, and the diagnosis is precise rather than aesthetic: I wrote ring 1 in FULL ROFL, so loading it needed the full parser, so the only way past the parser was a compiled image - which is not a tower, it is a chicken and egg patched with a binary. A tower requires level N+1 to be WRITTEN IN THE LANGUAGE OF LEVEL N, and I had skipped straight from `terms and facts` to `the whole grammar`. I HAD THE NUMBER THAT SAID SO and misread it: 723 reflection rows was not a cost to avoid, it was the measurement saying A LEVEL IS MISSING. WHAT NOW STANDS. L0 is examples/ring1/l0.ts, 178 lines, and does exactly two things - read terms and facts, and promote a rule written as ONE FACT into the reflection rows the evaluator already executes. L1 is examples/ring1/l1.rofl, derived from L2 by removing exactly what L2's own source does not use - measured, L2's source uses 17 surface features and L1 covers those 17, dropping perspective brackets, temporal markers, wildcards, `* / mod` and parenthesised expressions. Its dense form is 103 facts and 11.1 KiB and it READS: `r(r7, l(wordch, [v(\"I\")]), [l(kind, [v(\"I\"), upper])]).` THE ORACLE IS FREE AND EXACT and it passes: loading the dense form through L0 gives BYTE-IDENTICAL reflection rows to loading l1.rofl through the host parser, 120 conclusion_lit rows either way. THE DIFFERENCE FROM AN IMAGE IS THE WHOLE POINT: 11 KiB of readable facts a reviewer can diff, against 702 KiB of JSON nobody opens - the audit surface stays on text, and the image goes back to being a 4.1x speed-up rather than a condition of starting. AND THE WRITER IS HOST CODE, NAMED: rendering a program into the dense form is the inverse the language does not have, and doing it in ROFL needs a range turned into a name, which is `str_sub` plus `atom_of` - the same two operations measured this morning. The owner's point that self-application is the operation a language model is built for lands exactly on those two.").

finding(f_the_chart_is_quadratic_and_that_is_what_blocks_the_step, defect).
recorded(f_the_chart_is_quadratic_and_that_is_what_blocks_the_step, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_chart_is_quadratic_and_that_is_what_blocks_the_step, doc).
finding_note(f_the_chart_is_quadratic_and_that_is_what_blocks_the_step, "THE TOWER IS CORRECT AND THE BOTTOM STEP IS NOT YET PRACTICAL, and the blocker is cost rather than expressiveness. L1 must parse L2's source, which is 15035 bytes. Measured on prefixes cut at clause boundaries: 401 B in 0.4 s, 906 B in 0.5 s, 1846 B in 2.5 s, 2976 B in 14.2 s, 3946 B in 38.9 s. THE FIT IS NOT STABLE AND I AM SAYING SO RATHER THAN QUOTING THE FLATTERING END: over the whole range the exponent is 2.01, which predicts about 9.5 minutes for the full file, but the LAST TWO POINTS alone give about 3.5, which predicts something closer to half an hour - the small end is dominated by fixed cost and the tail is where the asymptote lives. A run over the full file was still going when this was written. THE SHAPE OF THE COST IS KNOWN AND SO IS THE DIRECTION OF THE FIX. The chart derives every sub-parse of every span, and the top-level chain then discards the ones that do not start where the previous clause ended - so the work is done and thrown away. A ROFL program is ALREADY SPLIT BY CONSTRUCTION, which this ledger recorded on 2026-09-01: a fact ends in a period, boot.rofl is 24 grammatical units of at most 32 tokens, and the sum of cubes over units is five orders of magnitude below the sum over the whole file. PARSING ONE CLAUSE AT A TIME IS THEREFORE NOT AN OPTIMISATION, IT IS THE MEASUREMENT ALREADY IN THE LEDGER, and I built the chart over whole files anyway. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THE FIX: a construct that spans a period, which in this grammar is none, since the period is the clause terminator and appears nowhere else outside a string.").

finding(f_self_application_found_two_defects_on_the_first_pointing, insight).
recorded(f_self_application_found_two_defects_on_the_first_pointing, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_self_application_found_two_defects_on_the_first_pointing, doc).
finding_note(f_self_application_found_two_defects_on_the_first_pointing, "L1 NOW PARSES L2'S OWN SOURCE, byte-identical to src/parser.ts as canonical clauses - 132 clauses over 15890 bytes in 18.4 s - and the FIRST TIME IT WAS POINTED THERE IT FOUND TWO DEFECTS, neither reachable from the corpus. ONE: `is` was excluded from term position as a keyword, so `optok(I, J, is)` - an ordinary atom in an argument - could not be read, and RING 1 COULD NOT PARSE ITS OWN SOURCE. The host recognises `is` and `mod` only where an OPERATOR can stand; only `not` needs excluding from a term, because it opens a negated literal. TWO: the guard on a one-character operator checked the wrong index. `consumed` marks the SECOND character of a two-character form, so a lone `<` was never blocked and `<=` was read as `<` followed by a stray `=` - BOTH PARSES SURVIVED and the clause count came out 137 against the host's 131. The fix is a second guard, `starts_op2`, on the first character. THE POINT IS WHERE THESE CAME FROM. Twenty-three corpus files agreed byte for byte and neither defect showed, because the corpus does not contain `is` in argument position and its `<=` occurrences sat where the extra parse happened not to complete a clause. The GRAMMAR'S OWN SOURCE contains both in quantity. So self-application is not decoration on top of a working parser: it is a test set that no corpus supplies, and it is the one the author cannot bias, because the input is the thing being tested. The owner's reason for wanting the tower - that rendering the model is the operation the model exists for - turns out to have a second edge: the model's own text is its hardest input.").

finding(f_the_clause_is_the_unit_and_the_ledger_said_so_first, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_clause_is_the_unit_and_the_ledger_said_so_first, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_clause_is_the_unit_and_the_ledger_said_so_first, doc).
finding_note(f_the_clause_is_the_unit_and_the_ledger_said_so_first, "A CHART OVER A WHOLE FILE COST MORE THAN FIFTEEN MINUTES AND WAS KILLED WITHOUT FINISHING; the same file parsed ONE CLAUSE AT A TIME takes 18.4 SECONDS, and that figure still rebuilds the entire L1 world for each of 132 clauses. The measured law over prefixes is size^2.01 across the range and closer to size^3.5 over the last two points, because the chart derives every sub-parse of every span and the top-level chain then discards the ones that do not begin where the previous clause ended - the work is done and thrown away. WHAT MAKES THIS A PITFALL RATHER THAN AN OPTIMISATION IS THAT THE LEDGER ALREADY SAID IT, on 2026-09-01, in f_splitting_helps_exactly_as_much_as_the_language_is_already_split: a ROFL fact ends in a period, so the language is split BY CONSTRUCTION, boot.rofl is 24 units of at most 32 tokens, and the sum of cubes over units is five orders of magnitude below the sum over the file. I built the chart over whole files anyway, three sessions later, and rediscovered the wall by walking into it. THE SPLIT IS CHEAP AND LINEAR: a scan carrying the same three states the grammar's own scanner has - code, string, comment - finds the periods that terminate a clause, and nothing else in the language contains one outside a string. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a construct spanning a period, of which this grammar has none. AND THE REMAINING COST IS NOW THE WORLD REBUILD, which is exactly what the image is for: 12.3 ms to restore against 50.5 ms to load, over 132 clauses.").

finding(f_str_sub_and_atom_of_are_in_and_the_boundary_is_swept, decision).
recorded(f_str_sub_and_atom_of_are_in_and_the_boundary_is_swept, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_str_sub_and_atom_of_are_in_and_the_boundary_is_swept, doc).
finding_note(f_str_sub_and_atom_of_are_in_and_the_boundary_is_swept, "DECIDED by the owner and landed with the test the destructor decision named in advance. `str_sub(S, I, L)` is the substring by RANGE the five original destructors could not express - `str_char` answers one character, `str_seg` and `str_pre` cut on a separator that has to occur where the cut is wanted, and no composition lengthens a string - and `atom_of(S)` crosses the last sort boundary. Both are destructors by the recorded criterion and the proof is unchanged: the substrings of a program's strings are finite, so a range-substring adds no term the universe did not already hold, and an atom built from one of finitely many strings is one of finitely many atoms. THE ACCEPTANCE TEST IS THE ONE NAMED ON 2026-09-01 AND IT RUNS BOTH DIRECTIONS: a program deriving every substring of an 8-character seed settles at 36 terms and gives 36 at budgets of 20k, 100k and 500k - the count is a property of the PROGRAM - while the same shape built on `N is X + 1` gives a different count at every budget and reports the wall as a hole. The positive control comes first, because a rule that never fired would also give a constant count. THE BOUNDARY IS SWEPT RATHER THAN SAMPLED: every (I, L) around a three-character string, with the accepted set asserted BY NAME as 0<=I<=len and I+L<=len - ten pairs, the empty substring at the very end legal and one character past it not - and every refusal a named `str_index_error` rather than a silent empty. Modes are written from STR_ARITY without a second table, `mode` is now 14 rows and `unmoded[audit]` is 0. AND THE CAPABILITY IS EXERCISED ON A DEMO'S REAL PATH rather than merely added: examples/ring1 now resolves its own names, which is the rule this repository wrote after `retainTicks` shipped unexercised. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a program using only these destructors whose term set keeps growing with the budget - the fixpoint test would go red and the finiteness proof would be wrong about them, which is the one observation that decides it. A slow program does not refute anything; a count that tracks the budget does.").

finding(f_atom_of_could_make_atoms_nobody_can_write, defect).
recorded(f_atom_of_could_make_atoms_nobody_can_write, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_atom_of_could_make_atoms_nobody_can_write, doc).
finding_note(f_atom_of_could_make_atoms_nobody_can_write, "FOUND BY SWEEPING THE BOUNDARY RATHER THAN BY THINKING ABOUT IT, within a minute of the operation working. `atom_of` as first written accepted ANY string, so it produced atoms with an EMPTY name, atoms that are a single space, atoms with a space in the middle, an atom spelled `123` and an atom `A` - which reads back as a VARIABLE. Every one of those is a term the language could hold and never state: no program can write it, so no program can match it except through the same operation. MEASURED AGAINST WHAT IS ALREADY WRITABLE, because the widening had to be priced rather than assumed: `$kernel` parses as an ordinary atom today - the `$` ban was lifted on 2026-09-01 - so allowing it opens no door that was shut, and it stays allowed. The four kinds above are new and are now refused with their own code, `atom_unwritable`, kept separate from `str_index_error` because the repair is different: the data reached a name that is not a name. THE ORACLE IS THE TOKENIZER ITSELF, not a regex beside it. `atom_of` calls `tokenize` and requires exactly one `ident` token equal to the whole string, so the rule stays true if the tokenizer changes - which is the remedy this repository prescribes for hand-written twins and applies here for the third time. THE GUARD ANNOUNCED ITS OWN MISUSE ON ITS FIRST RUN: ring 1's `tok_name` was written over EVERY token, so it asked for a name from a bracket, a neck, a quoted string and a capitalised variable, and each came back a loud hole instead of a silently wrong atom. The narrowing to ident tokens took one line and would not have been visible at all under the unguarded version.").

finding(f_i_pitched_a_decoupling_and_delivered_a_smaller_one, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_pitched_a_decoupling_and_delivered_a_smaller_one, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_i_pitched_a_decoupling_and_delivered_a_smaller_one, doc).
finding_note(f_i_pitched_a_decoupling_and_delivered_a_smaller_one, "I ARGUED FOR THE TWO OPERATIONS ON A STRUCTURAL CLAIM AND THE CLAIM WAS TOO BIG. The pitch was: with `atom_of`, ring 1 emits reflection-shaped terms, the promoter becomes a copy, and L0 stops depending on the levels above it - measured against 12 grammar shapes the host knew. WHAT ACTUALLY LANDED, measured the same way: the promoter no longer reads the source AT ALL - every `src.slice` in `term`, `lit` and `bodyElem` is gone, and the three that remain are an error message quoting the source for a human and the linear clause splitter, both legitimately host - but THE SHAPE COUNT DID NOT FALL. It is 13 now rather than 12, because a wildcard had to keep its index and gained a shape of its own. So the loan I removed was the SOURCE-READING loan, not the COUPLING, and those are different things that I ran together while arguing for a kernel change. THE FULL DECOUPLING IS STILL REACHABLE AND IS NOW UNBLOCKED: ring 1 would have to emit `$lit`, `$cons` and `$var` directly instead of its own `node`, `atom`, `var` shapes, at which point the promoter knows only the kernel's own seven-name vocabulary and nothing about any grammar. Rules can build `$`-functors - measured on the sugar layer - so nothing stands in the way but the rewrite. WHAT THIS COSTS ME TO SAY: the decision was taken on my argument, and the argument was half right. The operations earn their place on the source-reading loan and on self-application; the decoupling is a further step that I described as if it were this one.").

finding(f_an_evaluation_costs_by_rule_count_not_by_input_size, insight).
recorded(f_an_evaluation_costs_by_rule_count_not_by_input_size, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_an_evaluation_costs_by_rule_count_not_by_input_size, doc).
finding_note(f_an_evaluation_costs_by_rule_count_not_by_input_size, "I WENT LOOKING FOR THE CHART'S COST AND IT IS NOT IN THE CHART. Profiled per clause: the grammar's OWN relations total under 1500 rows - the largest is `step` at 424, `at` and `ch` are one per character - while the store holds 6092 facts, the rest being the reflection of 149 rules and its provenance. AND THE DECISIVE NUMBER IS THE EMPTY ONE: parsing an EMPTY source costs 41 ms with boot.rofl and 24 ms without it. An evaluation costs by the NUMBER OF RULES it schedules, not by the size of the input, and 132 clauses times that constant is where 14.2 seconds mostly goes. FOUR FIXES TRIED AND MEASURED, THREE OF THEM NEGATIVE, recorded because a measured negative is the part that stops the next person repeating it. (1) SAVE THE IMAGE ALREADY EVALUATED so the derived layer restores with it: no change at all - 723 KiB either way, 36 ms against 39 - because the reuse bookkeeping is in-memory and a snapshot does not carry it. (2) ONE WORLD, SWAP THE SOURCE with `retract` instead of restoring an image per clause: WORSE, 170 ms per round against 110, since the retraction costs more than the restore saves. (3) DROP boot.rofl FROM THE PARSING WORLD, which the grammar does not need: the image falls 723 to 493 KiB and the empty-source cost 41 to 24 ms, but a REAL clause moves only 102 to 98 ms - four per cent - so the constant is not simply additive and the win is small. (4) BOUND THE CHART BY CLAUSE NUMBER inside one evaluation - number the tokens per clause and require both ends of a span to share a number, so the pruning the host's splitter does happens in the rules and the 132 evaluations become one: STILL TOO SLOW, killed at 580 seconds without finishing, because `lit0`, `belem` and `clause_at` chain through `nexttok` and were not bounded by the guard I added. REVERTED. WHAT THIS LEAVES: the host-side split at 14.2 s stands as the working answer, and the real lever is engine-level - a per-evaluation cost proportional to the rule count is what makes 132 small parses expensive, and neither the image nor the grammar can do anything about it from where they sit.").

finding(f_the_negation_branch_of_classify_checks_nothing, defect).
recorded(f_the_negation_branch_of_classify_checks_nothing, "2026-09-04").
demands(f_the_negation_branch_of_classify_checks_nothing, decision).
finding_note(f_the_negation_branch_of_classify_checks_nothing, "A NEIGHBOURING BRANCH REPORTED THAT LITERAL ORDER CHANGES THE ANSWER; I reproduced it and found the line. FIRST ATTEMPT DID NOT REPRODUCE, and the report said why in advance: its fixture had no intermediate node with a key of its own, so both readings coincided. Adding ONE FACT - a node owning a DIFFERENT key - separates them. Measured on `par(b,c). par(c,k). own_key(k,k). own_key(c,q).`: `val(X,K) :- par(X,Y), not own_key(X,K), val(Y,K).` gives THREE rows and the same rule with the negation LAST gives FIVE, and `unsafe[audit]` and `malformed[audit]` are 0 for both. `whynot val(c,k)` answers `(no failing premise found within exploration bounds)` - the one instrument that explains absence says the absence has no cause, because the explainer binds the goal's arguments first and so reads the negation with K BOUND, disagreeing with the evaluator. THE ROOT CAUSE IS ONE BRANCH OF ONE SWITCH, src/engine.ts:265 in `classify`: the body walk checks `groundIn` for every element EXCEPT the negative one, whose whole branch is `hasNeg = true;` and nothing else. Positive literals bind, builtins are checked in both directions, the head is checked - the negation is the single case that performs no check at all. So this is not information the engine holds and fails to consult; the check does not exist. THREE REPAIRS AND THEY ARE NOT EQUIVALENT, which is why this is a decision and not a patch: (1) mark such a rule UNSAFE, which does not reject it but makes the relation demand-backed, changing the evaluation strategy rather than the semantics; (2) REORDER the body so a negation follows the positive literals that bind its variables, which is what a standard engine does and which restores the property the language actually wants - literal order must not change the answer; (3) REFUSE the rule, which is the most conservative and most in this repository's manner. NOT FIXED HERE ON PURPOSE: the neighbouring branch has it queued as `w_body_order_is_load_bearing` and owns the change; what this entry adds is the line and the three-way choice.").

finding(f_the_seed_pass_is_the_constant_and_the_delta_loop_is_the_work, insight).
recorded(f_the_seed_pass_is_the_constant_and_the_delta_loop_is_the_work, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_the_seed_pass_is_the_constant_and_the_delta_loop_is_the_work, doc).
finding_note(f_the_seed_pass_is_the_constant_and_the_delta_loop_is_the_work, "THE ENGINE ALREADY HAS THE TWO BIG DATALOG OPTIMISATIONS AND I HAD NOT LOOKED BEFORE CALLING THE COST A MYSTERY. Semi-naive evaluation is the first line of src/engine.ts and `naive` defaults to false; `propagate` already SKIPS a rule none of whose `triggerRels` gained a fact in the previous round; and src/store.ts carries ARGUMENT INDEXES keyed by binding pattern, built above MIN_INDEXED = 16 facts and capped at MAX_PATTERNS = 8. So the answer to `are there optimisations` is that the standard ones are in, and NONE of them stops it being an honest fixpoint - semi-naive computes the same least model by theorem, which is the whole reason it is used. MEASURED where the time actually goes, by patching `fireRule`, `fireRuleFront` and `propagate` on the prototype and parsing through the ring 1 image: EMPTY SOURCE - 149 seed fires, 21.0 ms, then 8 delta fires, 1.2 ms. TINY FACT `a(b).` - 149 seed fires, 15.8 ms, 568 delta fires, 5.4 ms. A REAL CLAUSE from ring1.rofl - 149 seed fires, 25.8 ms, 7098 delta fires, 60.8 ms, 5455 of them through `fireRuleFront`. And the wall splits 10.6 ms to RESTORE the image against 127.4 ms to EVALUATE, over 20 warm rounds. SO THE SEED PASS IS A CONSTANT, NOT THE DOMINANT TERM. `activate` fires EVERY rule once unconditionally - `for (const r of sorted) this.fireRule(r, null, front)` - before the delta loop that would have skipped most of them, and that is about 25 ms of every evaluation whatever the input, which is the whole cost of the empty parse and a fifth of a real one. The remaining four fifths is real chart work in the delta loop. THIS CORRECTS THE SHAPE I GAVE THE PREVIOUS FINDING: `an evaluation costs by rule count` is right about the empty source and WRONG as a description of a real clause, where the input size dominates through the number of delta firings. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a seed pass made conditional - skipping a rule whose positive body relations are all empty - that fails to remove roughly 25 ms per evaluation, which would mean the constant is somewhere else. AND WHAT IT SAYS ABOUT BAKING RINGS INTO A BINARY: freezing the store removes PARSE AND LOAD time, measured at 4.1x on this branch, and leaves both of these terms untouched. The compile-shaped lever that WOULD touch them is specialising the evaluator to a fixed rule set - join plans chosen once instead of a body walked per firing - which is the Futamura direction, not the snapshot.").

dismissed(f_the_seed_pass_is_the_constant_and_the_delta_loop_is_the_work, timing_was_my_instrument).

finding(f_i_measured_a_constant_my_instrument_created, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_measured_a_constant_my_instrument_created, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_i_measured_a_constant_my_instrument_created, doc).
finding_note(f_i_measured_a_constant_my_instrument_created, "I REPORTED A 25 MS SEED CONSTANT AND IT WAS NOT THERE. The number came from wrapping `fireRule` on the prototype and timing 149 calls in the FIRST evaluation of the process - cold, unwarmed, JIT-untouched - and I read a cold-start profile as a steady state. THE FIX FOR THE CONSTANT WAS THEN BUILT AND MEASURED PROPERLY, which is the only reason the error surfaced: `activate` now skips a rule with at least one positive premise all of whose relations are empty (sound, since a positive premise reads the store and the round's frozen assumption is consulted by `negHolds` alone). Seed firings fall 149 to 24 on an empty source and 149 to 44 on a real clause - the COUNTS were right, counts do not depend on the instrument - and A PAIRED A/B IN ONE PROCESS, alternating the two arms 40-60 times each after warmup, medians: empty source 36.7 ms against 36.5, a real clause 96.9 against 98.4. ONE AND A HALF PER CENT, inside the noise, and a cheaper variant of the same check that skips the `relAll` confirmation gives 1.7 and 2.9 - so most of even that is the check paying for itself. THE PATCH WAS REVERTED: it adds a method to the kernel, turns five census gates red (bootstrap-dag's tier-0 line count, three engine-split definition tables, one report headline - the drift gates did exactly their job) and buys two per cent. THE LESSON IS THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE SEED PASS. A wrapper that reports a phase's share of the wall does not prove that removing the phase removes the time: the only proof is to remove it and measure the wall, and to do it in the SAME PROCESS with the arms interleaved, because two processes minutes apart differ by more than the effect. The first baseline I took here, 127.4 ms per clause, and the second, 111.0 ms, differed by 15 per cent with an IDENTICAL tree - the fingerprint attestation this repository already prescribes certifies that the FILES did not move and says nothing about the machine.").

finding(f_a_third_of_a_parse_is_json, insight).
recorded(f_a_third_of_a_parse_is_json, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_a_third_of_a_parse_is_json, decision).
finding_note(f_a_third_of_a_parse_is_json, "PROFILED THE EMPTY PARSE WITH --cpu-prof INSTEAD OF GUESSING AND THE FIXPOINT IS NOT WHERE THE TIME IS. Self time over 200 empty parses: `Store.restore` 23.7 per cent, `Store.snapshot` 12.7, the demo's own `parse` 11.1, garbage collector 10.7, `planReuse` 8.0 - while `matchPremise`, `solveBody`, `conclude` and `unify` together come to under six per cent. THE PER-CLAUSE CONSTANT IS SERIALISATION. Timed inside one parse by wrapping `snapshot`, `clone` and `Store.restore`: a real clause costs 107.6 ms wall, of which snapshot runs TWICE for 19.1 ms and restore TWICE for 17.8 ms - about a THIRD OF THE PARSE spent turning a 6000-fact store into JSON and back. The three round trips are: (1) `fromImage` restores the image, which is the demo's own choice and buys 22 per cent against rebuilding the world; (2) `Rofl.load` takes a ROLLBACK BACKUP with `this.store.clone()`, and `clone` is literally `Store.restore(this.snapshot())` - a full serialise-plus-deserialise of the whole store on EVERY load, 13.0 ms per clause, kept only in case a clause is rejected; (3) the demo reads its results with `JSON.parse(r.save())` rather than with a query. TWO OF THE THREE ARE REMOVABLE AND NEITHER IS AN ENGINE CHANGE. The rollback wants a journal - the keys added by this load, undone on failure - rather than a copy of everything; the demo wants `query` rather than `save`. THIS ALSO CORRECTS WHAT `reuse` COULD BUY: `Store.restore` does not carry `derivedKeys` or `derivedSchedule` (store.ts:731-749), which `clone` copies by hand in its two extra lines (757-758), so a restored image arrives with a full derived layer and no right to keep it. Teaching the snapshot those two fields is a small patch, but `src` changes per clause and every relation downstream of it is refingerprinted anyway, so what it saves is the same charclass-and-boot layer the seed skip was aiming at - and that layer has just been measured at two per cent.").

finding(f_a_copy_taken_to_be_thrown_away_went_out_through_json, defect).
recorded(f_a_copy_taken_to_be_thrown_away_went_out_through_json, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_a_copy_taken_to_be_thrown_away_went_out_through_json, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_copy_taken_to_be_thrown_away_went_out_through_json, "src/store.ts").
finding_note(f_a_copy_taken_to_be_thrown_away_went_out_through_json, "THE GRANULARITY OF THE SNAPSHOT WAS ONE WORLD, AND THREE DIFFERENT JOBS WERE BEING DONE WITH IT. `Store.clone` was `Store.restore(this.snapshot())` plus two lines - a copy meant to be discarded, taken by serialising the entire store to JSON and re-adding every fact through `add`, which rebuilds every key and every run. `Rofl.load` takes one on EVERY load and keeps it only in case a clause is rejected. NOW STRUCTURAL: Maps rebuilt, `FactRec` shallow-copied because `add` mutates `base` in place on an existing record (a base assertion overriding a derived copy, so a shared record would let a write to one store reach the other - which is the one thing a rollback backup must not allow), `Witness` shared because nothing mutates one after it is built, argument indexes dropped exactly as the serialising copy dropped them. MEASURED, arms interleaved in ONE process after warmup: a clone of the ring 1 world's 2902 facts is 0.53 ms against 13.74, TWENTY-SIX TIMES; one clause end to end 112.8 ms against 127.2, 11.4 per cent. THE ACCEPTANCE ORACLE IS THE ONE THE PORT ALREADY OWNS - byte-identical `canonicalState` AND byte-identical `snapshot` against the serialising clone, over three real worlds (boot, boot+strata, ring 1), plus `relAll` key-for-key over every relation. AND THE INDEPENDENCE MUTANTS, because state equality says nothing about sharing: add to the original, remove from the original, edit the clone, undo the original's edits, and flip the `base` flag through `add` on a fact both hold - five, all clean. THREE MORE FOR WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE, which is where the first version was wrong: `derivedKeys` and `derivedSchedule` are in NEITHER oracle (carried, and the Map copied rather than shared), `loose` keys are invisible to `relAll` (carried), and the canon/arrived split is invisible to every read because reads absorb - and THAT is the one that caught the defect.").

finding(f_a_forks_key_order_is_an_accident_of_restore, question).
recorded(f_a_forks_key_order_is_an_accident_of_restore, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_a_forks_key_order_is_an_accident_of_restore, decision).
finding_note(f_a_forks_key_order_is_an_accident_of_restore, "THE STRUCTURAL CLONE'S FIRST VERSION PRESERVED THE ORIGINAL'S ARRIVAL ORDER AND ONE TEST WENT RED - `clone is a fork, and its arrival order is the reference fork's`, in test/store-conformance.test.ts, whose own comment says why: `Store.clone` goes through `restore`, which re-adds in KEY order, so a faithful fork's `allFacts` is in key order and NOT the original's insertion order. The contract is therefore not a property anyone chose. IT IS WHATEVER `restore` HAPPENED TO DO, promoted to a conformance requirement - the same shape this repository already names as a gate inheriting the scope of its incident, one level up: here an IMPLEMENTATION's incidental behaviour became the specification. AND IT IS NOT FREE. The SQLite port renumbers its rows to match, and the test measures the price in the same breath: 20.7 microseconds per fact with the renumbering against 2.92 for `VACUUM INTO` alone, 2.9x, on 200000 facts - with `clone({ renumber: false })` already sitting there as the opt-out that `gives up exactly one thing`. I MATCHED THE CONTRACT rather than change it: the copy now walks `[...facts.keys()].sort()` and fills each run the way `add` does, which costs a sort of the key set (26x rather than 51x against the serialising clone) and keeps every adapter honest. THE QUESTION FOR THE OWNER: is a fork's arrival order part of the language's meaning, or an artefact? If it is an artefact, the reference can preserve the original's order, the port can drop the renumbering and its 2.9x, and the test asserts the property it actually wants - which is that the two agree, whatever the order is. If it is meaning, it should be stated as meaning somewhere other than in a comment about how `restore` works.").

finding(f_read_the_relation_not_the_world, insight).
recorded(f_read_the_relation_not_the_world, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_read_the_relation_not_the_world, doc).
addressed_by(f_read_the_relation_not_the_world, "examples/ring1/demo.ts").
finding_note(f_read_the_relation_not_the_world, "THE FRONT END READ ITS ANSWER BY SERIALISING THE WHOLE WORLD. `parse` ended with `JSON.parse(r.save())` and then one filter per relation over every fact in the snapshot - five relations, five passes, and a JSON document built and reparsed to carry them. MEASURED at 38.7 ms of a 108.5 ms clause: the single largest removable term in the parse, and LARGER THAN THE FIXPOINT IT REPORTS ON, which the profile puts under six per cent. It is now `r.store.relAll(rel)`, and the readers below it did not change a line, because `termToJson` is structure-preserving - a functor is `{k:'f',name,args}` on both sides of it - so the tree walkers that consumed JSON consume terms as they stand. TOGETHER WITH THE STRUCTURAL CLONE, MEASURED IN PAIRS on the self-application gate, alternating patched and HEAD three times because two processes minutes apart differ by more than the effect: 13.4 against 20.6 seconds, 13.1 against 20.2, 14.2 against 20.2 - A THIRD OFF, with the parse byte-identical to src/parser.ts as the ring 1 test asserts. NOTHING IN THE EVALUATOR WAS TOUCHED. What is left of the JSON is the image restore, 9.7 ms per clause, which is the demo's own choice and buys 22 per cent against rebuilding the world - and it too could be a fork of one prototype world rather than a restore, measured at a further 8.5 per cent, which needs a public way to branch a `Rofl` and is not taken here.").

addressed_by(f_the_negation_branch_of_classify_checks_nothing, "src/engine.ts").

finding(f_literal_order_is_no_longer_load_bearing, decision).
recorded(f_literal_order_is_no_longer_load_bearing, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_literal_order_is_no_longer_load_bearing, doc).
addressed_by(f_literal_order_is_no_longer_load_bearing, "test/body-order.test.ts").
finding_note(f_literal_order_is_no_longer_load_bearing, "DECIDED BY THE OWNER - REFUSE AT THE DOOR - AND THE TWO NUMBERS THAT SIZED IT WERE TAKEN FIRST. Over 1965 rules in 71 .rofl files: 409 carry a negation, a safety-driven reorder would move 0 of them, and 0 have a NAMED variable in a negation that no positive premise binds. 46 leave a variable unbound and every one is confined to its own literal - a wildcard by another name, existential by construction - and 9 more are bound by a builtin the plan now waits for. So every program here already writes its negations legally, and the repair moves nothing. `planBody` holds a negation back until every variable it shares with the rest of the rule is bound and emits it at the first point where that is true; `classify` reads the plan, `fireRule` and `fireRuleFront` solve it, and `whynot` walks it - which is what closes the disagreement, since the explainer is top-down and had the head's arguments bound while the bottom-up run did not. The load door refuses a body no order can settle, and refuses ONLY a stuck NEGATION and only when the head is otherwise range-restricted. THE GATE IS A PROPERTY, NOT AN EXAMPLE: all six permutations of the bug's own rule must give the same derived facts, and the fixture is one that discriminates - measured before the fix at ONE row against THREE. MUTATING THE PLANNER TO RETURN THE WRITTEN ORDER KILLS SIX OF THE SEVEN TESTS; the survivor is the wildcard case, which is order-independent by construction and should survive. Suite 987/979/8, the failing set identical to baseline, tree fingerprint unchanged.").

finding(f_a_builtins_position_is_a_choice_and_my_scan_could_not_see_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_builtins_position_is_a_choice_and_my_scan_could_not_see_it, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_a_builtins_position_is_a_choice_and_my_scan_could_not_see_it, doc).
finding_note(f_a_builtins_position_is_a_choice_and_my_scan_could_not_see_it, "I MEASURED A WIDENING AT ZERO AND THE MEASUREMENT HAD A HOLE. The first planner took the FIRST READY element of any kind, so it waited for builtins too, and I priced that over 71 .rofl files: 0 rules moved, 0 newly accepted, 0 newly refused. THE SUITE FOUND WHAT THE SCAN COULD NOT: examples/yak fragment 09 turns on exactly this, and its two programs live in TypeScript strings. `risky(X, Y) :- Y = pair(X, Z), who(X), tag(X, Z).` is NOT range-restricted as written and is therefore demand-backed and unfolded top-down; the same three premises reordered materialise bottom-up. The fragment's whole point is that those are two programs, and a planner that waits for `=` makes them one. SO THE POSITION OF A BUILTIN IS A CHOICE AN AUTHOR ALREADY HAS, documented in this repository, and the answer to `is there syntax that depends on order deliberately` is yes and this is it - alongside the kernel claim, which must be the FIRST clause of the FIRST load. THE PLAN NOW MOVES NEGATIONS ONLY, which closes the widening BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by scan, and that is the better fix for the same reason the scan was the worse instrument: a class closed by argument does not depend on where I looked. WHAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT EARLIER: scanning the .ts files for embedded programs, which is a real gap - rules in this repository live in three places, .rofl files, TypeScript string literals, and snapshots, and only the first is enumerable by a file walk.").

finding(f_a_door_that_refuses_takes_away_the_program_the_audit_needs, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_door_that_refuses_takes_away_the_program_the_audit_needs, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_a_door_that_refuses_takes_away_the_program_the_audit_needs, doc).
finding_note(f_a_door_that_refuses_takes_away_the_program_the_audit_needs, "THE FIRST VERSION OF THE LOAD DOOR REFUSED `negonly(Q) :- not tag(Q).` AND TOOK AN ORACLE DOWN WITH IT. test/head-vars loads that clause ON PURPOSE: it is a range-restriction violation, and the test's claim is that range restriction COMPUTED IN ROFL equals the host's `Evaluation.rules[].safe` set for set. A checker needs a program that violates what it checks, and a door that refuses every violator leaves it with two empty sets to compare - which is a fact about the probe and not about the program, the exact failure mode that test already guards against with its own control. THE REPAIR IS A NARROWING, and it states the door's real job: refuse only what would otherwise pass SILENTLY. A rule whose head is not range-restricted is already unsafe, already reported by that audit and already unfolded top-down where the goal binds; a second and louder verdict adds nothing and costs the audit its material. So the door fires only when the head IS range-restricted and the negation is the only thing left unsettled. GENERAL SHAPE, worth carrying: every new refusal should be checked against the CHECKS that already exist, because a gate and a door can want the same program for opposite reasons.").

finding(f_the_full_decoupling_is_blocked_by_the_concatenation_ban, defect).
recorded(f_the_full_decoupling_is_blocked_by_the_concatenation_ban, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_the_full_decoupling_is_blocked_by_the_concatenation_ban, decision).
finding_note(f_the_full_decoupling_is_blocked_by_the_concatenation_ban, "I RECORDED ON 2026-09-04 THAT THE FULL DECOUPLING WAS UNBLOCKED AND THAT NOTHING STOOD IN THE WAY BUT THE REWRITE. MEASURED, AND THAT IS WRONG. For ring 1 to emit the kernel's own reflected vocabulary the rules would have to BUILD terms the source does not name, and every one of the four constructions is refused: a functor whose name is a variable is a SYNTAX ERROR - `T = N(1, 2)` does not parse - there is no `functor/3` or univ (the seven non-arithmetic operations are `str_char, str_len, str_pre, str_seg, str_segs, str_sub, atom_of`, all destructors), joining two strings gives `arith_type_error`, and a wildcard's name `_$0` is a concatenation. So the block is not effort, it is the SAME PRINCIPLE that bans concatenation: assembling `foo(A, B)` from a name and a list is a CONSTRUCTOR, and the finiteness proof is what forbids it. WHAT IS STILL REACHABLE, and worth doing: the STRUCTURE. `$lit`, `$not`, `$builtin`, `$cons`, `$nil` and `$var` are FIXED functor names a rule can write literally, so nine of the grammar's shapes can go and the promoter would keep exactly six - comp, op, str, int, negint, wild. THE RESIDUE WOULD THEN BE PRINCIPLED RATHER THAN INCIDENTAL: what the host still knows about this grammar is precisely the set of terms the language forbids a rule to construct. THE DECISION IT FORCES: either that residue is the answer and the tower has a floor of six host-built leaves, or the language gains a construction operation - which would have to be paid for against the finiteness proof, and `atom_of` is the precedent for how such a payment is measured.").

finding(f_coverage_over_tokens_cannot_see_a_character_that_makes_none, defect).
recorded(f_coverage_over_tokens_cannot_see_a_character_that_makes_none, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_coverage_over_tokens_cannot_see_a_character_that_makes_none, doc).
addressed_by(f_coverage_over_tokens_cannot_see_a_character_that_makes_none, "examples/ring1/ring1.rofl").
finding_note(f_coverage_over_tokens_cannot_see_a_character_that_makes_none, "RING 1 PARSED `p({a}).` TO `p[main](a)@now` WHILE src/parser.ts REFUSED IT with `expected a term, got '{'`, and did the same for `p(#a).`. A DIVERGENCE IN THE PERMISSIVE DIRECTION, which is the one the corpus oracle cannot catch BY CONSTRUCTION: the sweep compares files the HOST ACCEPTS, so a program ring 1 accepts and the host does not is outside what it can look at - and it is the direction that yields a WRONG PROGRAM rather than no program. Found by asking what ring 1 still refuses and sweeping fifteen constructs against both parsers, which is the question this repository already names as the productive one: where is the check structurally unable to look. THE CAUSE IS ONE LEVEL BELOW THE GRAMMAR. `uncovered(K) :- tokstart(K), not covered(K)` quantifies over TOKEN STARTS, and a character with no class makes no token, so it has no token to be uncovered. charclass.rofl said so in its own header - `everything unclassified is simply not a token character, which is what spaces, tabs and carriage returns need` - and the brace and the hash sat in that same bucket. THE REPAIR STATES THE INVARIANT OVER CHARACTERS: whitespace is now a class of its own (`space` for space, tab and CR, joining the `nl` that comment termination already needed), and `stray(K) :- code_at(K), not white(K), not in_tok(K)` refuses anything else, located at the character. The two dashes of a comment stand in `code` state - the scanner enters `cmt` only after them - so they are named as white too. Corpus 10/10 identical and self-application byte-identical after the change, with the negative control that a tab and a CR between clauses still parse.").

finding(f_a_negative_integer_literal_is_a_position_not_a_token, decision).
recorded(f_a_negative_integer_literal_is_a_position_not_a_token, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_a_negative_integer_literal_is_a_position_not_a_token, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_negative_integer_literal_is_a_position_not_a_token, "examples/ring1/ring1.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_negative_integer_literal_is_a_position_not_a_token, "THE ONE CONSTRUCT RING 1 LEFT UNCOVERED ON PURPOSE IS COVERED, and the ambiguity that justified leaving it was smaller than recorded. MEASURED FIRST: 24 negative integer literals in the 71 .rofl files - goof, npc, sensors, slop and three more - so the gap was real. THE HOST'S RULE IS POSITIONAL, not lexical: `primary()` reads a dash followed by an int as a negative literal, and `expr()` takes a dash as a BINARY operator only after a left operand, so a dash that reaches a primary has none. A chart states that as a production - `term(I, K, negint(S)) :- arithtok(I, I, minus), nexttok(I, J), inttok(J, K), tok_text(J, K, S)` - instead of resolving it by scanning greedily from the left, and no second parse appears because an argument list separates its terms by commas and an expression needs an operator between its operands. THE DIGITS TRAVEL WITHOUT THEIR SIGN and the promoter negates: carrying the text of the whole span would break on `- 1`, which the host reads as -1 because it is token-based and whose span text is not a number. THE GATE'S SUBJECT MOVED FOR THE SECOND TIME, and that is the gate working rather than rotting - it was a temporal marker, then this, and both stopped being uncovered. Its subject now is a character with no class, which cannot be covered by adding a production because the class of characters ROFL does not use is OPEN and the grammar's answer to all of them is one refusal.").

finding(f_the_promoter_now_knows_six_leaves_and_they_are_the_forbidden_ones, decision).
recorded(f_the_promoter_now_knows_six_leaves_and_they_are_the_forbidden_ones, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_the_promoter_now_knows_six_leaves_and_they_are_the_forbidden_ones, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_promoter_now_knows_six_leaves_and_they_are_the_forbidden_ones, "examples/ring1/demo.ts").
finding_note(f_the_promoter_now_knows_six_leaves_and_they_are_the_forbidden_ones, "DECIDED BY THE OWNER - six is small enough - AND LANDED. Ring 1 now emits the KERNEL'S OWN REFLECTED VOCABULARY for everything structural: `$lit`, `$not`, `$builtin`, `$cons`, `$nil`, `$var`. Nine of this grammar's shapes are gone from the host - node, pos, neg, bi, cons, book, bookvar, var, atom - and the table of operator NAMES went with them, because the operator now travels as its own text through `str_sub`. What the promoter reads is `$builtin $lit $not $var comp int negint op str wild`: four kernel names and SIX LEAVES. THE SIX ARE EXACTLY WHAT THE LANGUAGE FORBIDS A RULE TO BUILD, measured rather than asserted: a functor whose name is a variable does not parse, there is no univ, joining two strings is `arith_type_error`, and `_$0` is a concatenation. `comp` and `op` need a functor built from a name, `str` an unescaped string built from an escaped one, `int` and `negint` a number built from digits, `wild` a name built from a rank. So the residue is PRINCIPLED: the host's remaining knowledge of the grammar is the set of constructions the finiteness proof bans. A SEVENTH THING TRAVELS AND IT IS NOT A SHAPE: `$bare` where no `[book]` was written. The reified form has no room for it - `unreifyLit` sets `perspExplicit: true` unconditionally, which is right for a rule read back out of the store, where resolution already happened - and `resolveBook` reads the bit: a kernel-book relation written without a bracket moves to `$kernel`, one written `[main]` is left alone. `canonClause` does NOT print the bit, so every corpus file could agree byte for byte with this wrong, and it has its own gate. ENABLED BY A GAP FOUND ON THE WAY: ring 1 could not read `$`-names at all - `p($kernel).` was refused while the host read it, and `$kernel` stands in boot.rofl - so the front end could never have parsed the kernel's own program. The host's rule is `$` in LEADING position only; stating it in a chart also needed `dollar_before`, because `$` is no word character and the letter after it was a word start too, which returned the inner name as a SECOND clause.").

finding(f_a_mutant_written_in_the_wrong_syntax_passes_for_the_wrong_reason, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_mutant_written_in_the_wrong_syntax_passes_for_the_wrong_reason, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_a_mutant_written_in_the_wrong_syntax_passes_for_the_wrong_reason, doc).
finding_note(f_a_mutant_written_in_the_wrong_syntax_passes_for_the_wrong_reason, "A MUTANT I WROTE THE SAME DAY WAS GREEN FOR A REASON THAT HAD NOTHING TO DO WITH WHAT IT CLAIMED. The negation-order gate asserted that a free PERSPECTIVE variable is refused, with the fixture `[k] q(1). p(X) :- q(X), not [P] r(X).` - and in ROFL the bracket goes AFTER the relation, `rel[book](args)`. So the program did not parse, `load` returned ok:false for a syntax error, and `assert.equal(ok, false)` passed while saying nothing at all. Found only because a second test written in the same wrong syntax hit a parse error loudly instead of quietly. THE SHAPE IS GENERAL AND IT IS WORSE FOR REFUSAL TESTS THAN FOR ANY OTHER KIND: a test asserting that something IS REFUSED passes for every reason a program can fail, including the ones the author did not mean, so it needs a POSITIVE CONTROL THAT THE FIXTURE PARSES. `assert.doesNotThrow(() => parseProgram(fixture))` is one line and it is now there. AND ONCE IT PARSED THE EXPECTATION WAS WRONG TOO: a book variable occurring only inside its own negation is a wildcard by another name - `not r[P](X)` asks whether r(X) holds in ANY book - which is the same rule that leaves `not r(X, _)` alone, so it must NOT be refused. The mutant now states three things instead of one: confined is existential and loads, shared between two negations is refused and names the variable, and bound by a later positive premise is waited for.").

finding(f_the_unit_of_coverage_is_a_construct_not_a_file, decision).
recorded(f_the_unit_of_coverage_is_a_construct_not_a_file, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_the_unit_of_coverage_is_a_construct_not_a_file, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_unit_of_coverage_is_a_construct_not_a_file, "test/example-ring1.test.ts").
finding_note(f_the_unit_of_coverage_is_a_construct_not_a_file, "THE CORPUS GATE SAW 10 FILES OF 70 AND THE CAP WAS THE WRONG DIMENSION. It parsed WHOLE FILES as one chart, which is cubic in file size, so it was capped at 1200 bytes - 10 files, against 70 the host parses and 6855 clauses. TWO MEASUREMENTS MOVED IT. First, the CLAUSE is the unit and the splitter now agrees with the host on the clause count of all 70 files, so cost is linear in clauses rather than cubic in bytes. Second, THE CORPUS HOLDS 6855 CLAUSES AND ONLY 932 DISTINCT SHAPES - a shape being the canonical clause with every leaf replaced by its sort - so parsing 144 facts of one shape says exactly what parsing one of them says. The gate now draws from a pool built out of EVERY file, takes the shortest representative of each shape, and is bounded by a number of SHAPES rather than a number of bytes: 200 of 932, and the result is 200 identical, 0 refused, 0 silent. AND A FREE GATE CAME OUT OF IT: the splitter needs no chart at all, so comparing its clause count against the host parser costs 126 ms over the whole corpus and runs on everything. It went red on its first run - boot.rofl ends with a comment block and the splitter pushed the tail as a part, 29 against 28. A tail with no terminating period is TWO DIFFERENT THINGS: an unfinished clause, which must be handed on so the parse refuses and says where, and comments and whitespace, which are not a clause. The cap that existed for speed was hiding that.").

finding(f_the_charts_cone_is_already_narrow_so_demand_would_buy_nothing_here, insight).
recorded(f_the_charts_cone_is_already_narrow_so_demand_would_buy_nothing_here, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_the_charts_cone_is_already_narrow_so_demand_would_buy_nothing_here, doc).
finding_note(f_the_charts_cone_is_already_narrow_so_demand_would_buy_nothing_here, "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER THE SPARSE-ATTENTION MOVE APPLIES - compute only what cascades into the output of interest - AND ON THIS WORKLOAD THE ANSWER IS NO, MEASURED. Counted the store after parsing one 82-character clause: `parsed` 1, `clause_at` 2, `subparse` 1. The whole discarded cone is ONE ROW. The parse structures are in the tens - lit 4, belem 9, body 22, args 36, term 24, expr 28 - and the largest grammar relation is `step` at 328. The clause splitter already did the pruning that a demand transformation would do, which is why splitting was worth 15 minutes against 18 seconds when it landed. WHERE THE 6185 FACTS ACTUALLY ARE: `derived_by` 1730, `premise_lit` and `has_premise` 436 each, `premise_pos` 294, and six reflection relations at 159 each - the reflection OF THE 159 RULES and its provenance. So the cost is not a wide derivation being narrowed, it is the FIXED cost of scheduling a program against a store whose mass is the program's own reflection. THE IDEA STILL HAS A TARGET HERE AND IT IS PROVENANCE: 1730 `derived_by` rows are written for a parse that never asks `why`, and they are exactly facts nothing cascades from. Measured by patching the write out: 8.2% of a clause. That wants a kernel flag, which is an owner's decision - and this repository's own rule says such a flag is not finished until a demo exercises it, which examples/ring1 would. THE OTHER TARGET IS A DIFFERENT WORKLOAD: a query over the js language model, where a question about one file's exports should not derive the whole call graph. That is where magic sets pay, and it is unmeasured.").

finding(f_a_negative_control_can_stop_discriminating_when_the_recipe_changes, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_negative_control_can_stop_discriminating_when_the_recipe_changes, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_a_negative_control_can_stop_discriminating_when_the_recipe_changes, doc).
finding_note(f_a_negative_control_can_stop_discriminating_when_the_recipe_changes, "THE IMAGE GATE'S NEGATIVE CONTROL WAS `a reordered recipe gives different BYTES and the same content`, and dropping boot.rofl from the parsing world killed it: with two sources a reversed list gives BYTE-IDENTICAL images. The control had been reading a property of the number of loads rather than of the order - what `evals` records is the per-load evaluation, so the third load was what made the log differ. Measured all three ways in one run: reversed order gives identical bytes and identical content; a different BUDGET gives different bytes and identical content. The control is now the budget, which is precisely what `evals` carries. THE GENERAL SHAPE: a negative control is a claim about the instrument, and it can quietly stop being true when the fixture changes for an unrelated reason - here a performance decision. It failed LOUDLY, which is the only reason it was noticed; a control that had merely become weaker would have gone on passing.").

finding(f_ring_one_agrees_on_every_construct_the_corpus_contains, insight).
recorded(f_ring_one_agrees_on_every_construct_the_corpus_contains, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_ring_one_agrees_on_every_construct_the_corpus_contains, doc).
finding_note(f_ring_one_agrees_on_every_construct_the_corpus_contains, "932 OF 932 DISTINCT SHAPES IDENTICAL, 0 REFUSED, 0 SILENT, in 419 seconds. That is every construct the corpus contains, where a shape is the canonical clause with every leaf replaced by its sort - 6855 clauses collapse to 932 of them. THE NUMBER THIS REPLACES was `10 of 70 files, capped at 1200 bytes`, and the gap between the two was not confidence but ARITHMETIC: the old gate parsed whole files as one chart, which is cubic in file size, so it could only afford small files - and small files are not a sample of the language, they are a sample of the FILING. Coverage measured in bytes answers a question nobody asked. WHAT THE GATE IN `npm test` KEEPS is 200 of the 932, and that bound is now a bound on TIME rather than on confidence: the full run exists, is reproducible by raising one constant, and its long tail is slow only because the longest representative is 3734 bytes. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THE CLAIM: a construct outside the corpus. The corpus is 71 files written by this project, so `every construct the corpus contains` is not `every construct the language admits` - and the honest next instrument is a GENERATOR over the grammar rather than a wider sweep over the same files.").

finding(f_policy_that_enforces_cannot_move_and_the_metric_counted_it_anyway, defect).
recorded(f_policy_that_enforces_cannot_move_and_the_metric_counted_it_anyway, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_policy_that_enforces_cannot_move_and_the_metric_counted_it_anyway, doc).
addressed_by(f_policy_that_enforces_cannot_move_and_the_metric_counted_it_anyway, "rules/engine-split.rofl").
finding_note(f_policy_that_enforces_cannot_move_and_the_metric_counted_it_anyway, "THE OWNER ASKED WHICH OF THE EVALUATOR'S 901 LINES COULD BE RULES, and the repository already answered 267 - the POL and POL* blocks - which is the number I was about to run a loop against. IT IS THE WRONG TARGET AND THE FIRST ITERATION FOUND OUT BEFORE MOVING A LINE. `checkUnstratified` reads `unstratified/1` STRAIGHT OUT OF THE STORE: the judgement is already policy-as-data, derived by boot.rofl, and those nine lines are the host reading the answer and refusing. `negPhase` looks the stratum up in a table boot.rofl computed. `roundRules` filters on `safe`. None of them can move, because SOMEBODY HAS TO ACT ON THE ANSWER - and a metric that counted them as movable would have stalled at 46 while looking like failure. SO THE DISTINCTION IS NOW A DECLARED JUDGEMENT AND A QUERY: `role_of(K, decides)` against `role_of(K, enforces)`, one per policy block, emitted beside `cat_of` and `when_of` and counted by the same loan audit - `dirty(engine_split, role, U, language_model)` is 17 rows, and the total contamination goes 98 to 115. Measured: `decides` 221, `enforces` 46, and the two sum to `policy` 267 exactly, which the test asserts so a block cannot lose its role silently. AND THE JUDGEMENT IS DECLARED RATHER THAN INFERRED BECAUSE I TRIED INFERRING IT FIRST: a regex over `this.store.relAll` and `IFACE.` got four of seventeen wrong - it called `hits` an enforcer because the word IFACE appears in it, and `cone` neither, when both compute a closure that is the decision itself. THE LOOP'S TARGET IS THEREFORE 221, NOT 267, and 46 lines are in the host permanently by construction.").

finding(f_every_move_needs_the_same_missing_mechanism_a_present_policy_program, question).
recorded(f_every_move_needs_the_same_missing_mechanism_a_present_policy_program, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_every_move_needs_the_same_missing_mechanism_a_present_policy_program, decision).
finding_note(f_every_move_needs_the_same_missing_mechanism_a_present_policy_program, "ITERATION 2 MEASURED THE ACCEPTANCE AND THEN COULD NOT SPEND IT, and the blocker is one mechanism every move needs. `reads(A, B)` - the relation `planReuse` walks 18 lines to build - is TWO RULES OF ROFL, `dep` from rules/strata.rofl without its tense filter. Measured set for set against the host's own walk over `Evaluation.rules` on five programs: boot.rofl alone 35 pairs, boot+strata 52, boot+findings 51, boot+kernel-policy 64, boot+the grammar 248 - AGREE on every one, with the positive control that the largest is 248 rather than two empty sets. THE MOVE CANNOT LAND BECAUSE A RULE THAT IS NOT LOADED IS NOT A CONSERVATIVE ANSWER, IT IS A WRONG ONE. Measured: 95 files construct a `Rofl` and 22 NEVER LOAD boot.rofl - one of them is this session's own ring 1 parsing world, where dropping boot bought 9.1%. An absent `reads` reads as `no relation reads anything`, which for the reuse plan degrades safely (empty plan means full recomputation, correct and slower, engine.ts:402) and for `negRels` or `readsProvenance` does NOT - absence there reads as `no relation is negated` and `no rule reads provenance`, and the second one ENABLES reuse where it must be off. THREE WAYS OUT, WITH COSTS. (A) boot.rofl becomes a precondition: costs 22 worlds and the 9.1%, and is a real language decision about what a bare store is. (B) THE KERNEL SHIPS THE POLICY PROGRAM ITSELF, the way `bootstrapKernel` already ships 59 facts unconditionally - no precondition and no silent failure, and the tower already has the machinery, since `l1.dense.rofl` is exactly a program carried as diffable rows generated from readable source. Its risk is a METRIC HOLE: rows embedded in src/reflect.ts would lower `decides` without the decision leaving the host, so it only counts if a .rofl file is the source and a gate keeps the rows reproducible from it - which is the gate l1.dense already has. (C) POLARITY, per block: state each rule so that ABSENCE yields the conservative answer. Works for `readsProvenance` (absent means `assume some rule reads provenance`, which disables reuse), does not work for `negRels`. RECOMMENDED (B), because it is the only one that is general, silent-proof and already built once. NOT TAKEN, because what a bare store carries is the owner's decision.").

finding(f_carrying_policy_with_the_kernel_works_and_reprices_every_count, decision).
recorded(f_carrying_policy_with_the_kernel_works_and_reprices_every_count, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_carrying_policy_with_the_kernel_works_and_reprices_every_count, decision).
finding_note(f_carrying_policy_with_the_kernel_works_and_reprices_every_count, "THE OWNER CHOSE (B) - THE KERNEL SHIPS ITS OWN PROGRAM - AND IT WORKS. `policy.rofl` holds `rule_reads(A, B)`, the two rules that replace an 18-line walk in `planReuse`; `bootstrapKernel` installs them through the SAME `encodeRule` path a loaded rule takes, so what the evaluator reads back is indistinguishable from a rule a program wrote. Measured running: a bare store gains 2 rule rows and 26 facts, and `rule_reads` answers about a program the kernel never saw loaded - including rows ABOUT ITSELF, which is what a program carried as data means. TWO OF THE KERNEL'S OWN RULES REFUSED IT FIRST, and both refusals were right. `prepare` rejects a rule concluding into a RESERVED relation, so the head cannot be reserved; and the kernel-ledger ring in `conclude` refuses to DERIVE into a `$` book, so the kernel's own program cannot write into the kernel's own books - a rule the kernel ships is, in the store, indistinguishable from a rule a program wrote, which is the ring working rather than failing. The way through is the one `stratum` already takes: conclude into `[main]` and let the kernel read it, with the same named residue. AND THEN THE PRICE, WHICH NOBODY KNEW WHEN THE DECISION WAS MADE: installing two rules in every store turned 18 tests red against a baseline of 8. Four are ROW AND TERM COUNTS - `MUTANT 4`, `MUTANT 5`, and both sides of the destructor finiteness gate - because every gate here that COUNTS inherits the kernel's own rules as a new baseline, permanently and for every future measurement. Two are the kernel's vocabulary discipline: the grep test reads the embedded program's relation names as kernel source, which they are not. THE CONSUMPTION ALSO DID NOT LAND, and the reuse gate is why. `planReuse` runs BEFORE the evaluation that derives `rule_reads`, so on a store's first evaluation the relation is empty - and an empty reads graph is not `no reuse`, it is fingerprints computed from nothing that evaluation 2 then reuses against. Bailing out instead breaks `a clone carries its fingerprints`. Spending the acceptance therefore needs the policy program EVALUATED FIRST, which is the two-stage bootstrap - step 4 arriving at step 2, because the reuse plan is end-of-run knowledge consumed before-A of the next run. WHAT IS LANDED: policy.rofl, the acceptance as a gate, and the price. WHAT IS NOT: the install, reverted so the failing set stays at 8, and the walk it would replace, which now carries a comment saying what replaces it and why not yet.").

finding(f_a_count_gate_that_reads_the_world_instead_of_the_change, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_count_gate_that_reads_the_world_instead_of_the_change, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_a_count_gate_that_reads_the_world_instead_of_the_change, decision).
finding_note(f_a_count_gate_that_reads_the_world_instead_of_the_change, "THE OWNER SAID COUNT GATES ARE THE WRONG INSTRUMENT AND THAT BEHAVIOUR IS WHAT TO MEASURE, and the measurement splits his point in two. HE IS RIGHT ABOUT THE FORM. Every one of the four that broke when the kernel gained two rules of its own pinned an ABSOLUTE where the claim was about the PROGRAM'S work: 40005 against 40000, 64005 against 64000, and `the STEP counter is still at zero` reading 5. Not one behaviour moved - `run.facts` already counts only the join's own relation and was exactly 40000 both times - and what shifted was `ev.steps`, which counts firings in THE STORE. A gate that reads the world where it means the change goes red for reasons its claim never mentioned, which is the same defect as a gate red on an honest checkout. AND THE REPAIR DOES NOT RESCUE THE DECISION, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. I subtracted a baseline measured on an empty store and SEVEN tests went red instead of four: the kernel's program is not overhead, it costs IN PROPORTION TO THE PROGRAM IT DESCRIBES, because `rule_reads` reads `concludes`, `premise_pos` and `premise_neg` and those grow with every rule loaded. So there is no constant to subtract, and a program's own budget now pays for the kernel's questions about it. THAT IS THE REAL PRICE OF CARRYING POLICY WITH THE KERNEL, and it is larger than the 18 red tests suggested - the tests were only where it became visible. IT ALSO SHARPENS THE NEXT MEASUREMENT: the isolated variant, where the kernel evaluates its policy program in ITS OWN store, removes the proportional cost from the program's budget entirely rather than merely hiding it from the counters. That is now the question, and it is measurable rather than arguable.").

finding(f_the_isolated_policy_store_agrees_and_costs_nothing_the_program_can_see, insight).
recorded(f_the_isolated_policy_store_agrees_and_costs_nothing_the_program_can_see, "2026-09-05").
demands(f_the_isolated_policy_store_agrees_and_costs_nothing_the_program_can_see, doc).
finding_note(f_the_isolated_policy_store_agrees_and_costs_nothing_the_program_can_see, "MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED, which is what the previous iteration asked for. The kernel runs its policy program in ITS OWN store over a copy of the program's reflection - `concludes`, `premise_pos`, `premise_neg` - and reads the answer back. Over four programs: AGREES with the host's own walk on every one, LEAVES THE HOST STORE UNTOUCHED to the fact (the count is identical before and after), and costs 2 to 8 ms, the 8 being boot.rofl plus the whole ring 1 grammar. That removes both halves of the price the previous iteration measured: no user program's counters move, because nothing is added to its store, and no user program's budget pays, because the work happens elsewhere. THE CONE AGREES TOO, and it is three lines: `cone(A, A) :- rule_relation(A).` and `cone(A, C) :- cone(A, B), rule_reads(B, C).` against the host's `for(;;)` growing sets until nothing moves. Measured set for set: 99 pairs over 32 relations on boot.rofl, 132 over 39 with strata, and 2586 PAIRS OVER 114 RELATIONS with the grammar - a positive control no empty comparison could fake. AND THE SCOPE WAS WRONG, WHICH IS THE FINDING. Landing `reads` ALONE trades 18 lines of policy for about 15 lines of orchestration, which trips the guard this loop set for itself - policy must not leave by growing machinery. The orchestration is a FIXED cost: the same 15 lines answer any question the policy program can be asked. So the unit of the move is the whole plan, not one relation: `reads` 18 + `opaqueClosure` 8 + `cone` 11 = 37 lines of policy out for the same 15 in. `hits` (14) stays because fingerprinting is FNV over fact keys, which is mechanism; `opaqueSet` (15) stays until the reflection carries a premise's tense and a store-shape fact flat, which is exactly what its POL* mark has always meant.").

finding(f_the_move_landed_and_the_guard_i_set_myself_was_the_wrong_shape, decision).
recorded(f_the_move_landed_and_the_guard_i_set_myself_was_the_wrong_shape, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_move_landed_and_the_guard_i_set_myself_was_the_wrong_shape, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_move_landed_and_the_guard_i_set_myself_was_the_wrong_shape, "src/engine.ts").
finding_note(f_the_move_landed_and_the_guard_i_set_myself_was_the_wrong_shape, "ITERATION 5 LANDED THE WHOLE PLAN AND THE NUMBERS SAY WHY THE UNIT HAD TO BE THE PLAN. `reads`, `opaqueClosure` and `cone` are gone from src/engine.ts; what stands in their place is `policyAnswer(seed)`, which builds a store of its own, installs the rows of policy.rofl, copies the program's `concludes`/`premise_pos`/`premise_neg` into it, runs an Evaluation and unpacks four relations. MEASURED, full attested suite (tree fingerprint with its control, node): 994 tests, 986 pass, 8 fail - the same 8 as the branch baseline, so no behaviour moved; test/derived-reuse.test.ts is 20 of 20. `decides` 221 -> 200, decisions in the host 12 -> 9, `enforces` unchanged at 46, and the two still sum to `policy` exactly. AND THE COST IS REAL: `mech` 550 -> 598, TOTAL 901 -> 928. Asking a program and unpacking its answer is longer than computing it. MY OWN LOOP GUARD SAID `mech must not rise above 550` AND IT IS VIOLATED, deliberately and reported rather than adjusted quietly. The guard was the wrong shape for the same reason the owner's critique of count gates was right: it pinned an ABSOLUTE where the claim is RELATIONAL. What the loop actually wants is that the host stop deciding, and the honest statement of it is `decides must fall by more than mech rises` - which this iteration fails on the raw numbers (-21 against +48) and passes on the structure, because the +48 is the FIXED orchestration measured in the previous iteration: the same `policyAnswer` answers any further question the policy program can be asked, so the next move pays only for its .rofl clauses. THAT IS A PREDICTION AND IT IS FALSIFIABLE: if the next decision to leave the host raises `mech` again, the orchestration was not fixed and the whole approach is trading host policy for host plumbing at par. THE LADDER MOVED TOO, and it corrected me twice. `policyAnswer` calls no method of `this` at all, so I expected it on the bottom rung; the graph puts it one up, on a single edge keyed `local decl@265:POLICY_BUDGET` - a module constant that lies inside `planBody`'s line range only because a block runs to the next anchor. Real to the instrument, meaningless about the negation planner, and left as measured.").

finding(f_range_restriction_is_expressible_and_the_coverage_measurement_paid_twice, insight).
recorded(f_range_restriction_is_expressible_and_the_coverage_measurement_paid_twice, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_range_restriction_is_expressible_and_the_coverage_measurement_paid_twice, doc).
addressed_by(f_range_restriction_is_expressible_and_the_coverage_measurement_paid_twice, "safety.rofl").
finding_note(f_range_restriction_is_expressible_and_the_coverage_measurement_paid_twice, "ITERATION 6 DOES NOT LOWER `decides` AND SAYS SO. What it lands is `safety.rofl`: RANGE RESTRICTION as rules, the judgement `Evaluation.classify` makes and that every other before-A policy in the kernel rests on. AGREEMENT, measured three ways. The whole corpus - every .rofl file the repository holds, loaded on boot.rofl - is 74 files, 3555 rules, 9 of them unsafe by the host, and ZERO disagreements. A mutant set of 22, one per branch of the host's fold, agrees on every one, 11 safe and 11 unsafe. And the third measurement is the one that paid: DELETING EACH CLAUSE OF safety.rofl IN TURN and asking whether any mutant notices. It killed 18 of 25 on the first run, and the seven survivors were not noise - one was a DEAD CLAUSE (`slot(R, K, pos)`, a groundness question nothing asks of a positive premise, since a positive premise binds rather than being tested), and three were mutants flagged by a DIFFERENT clause than the one they were written for, because the unbound variable also stood in the head. Three new mutants and one deletion later it is 22 of 24, and the two survivors are the `edb` declarations, whose only reader is `undefined_premise[audit]` in boot.rofl, which the isolated store does not hold. THE DESIGN DECISION IS THE STRATIFICATION. `every variable of this operand is already bound` is a universal, and the first version stated it as `not unbound(...)` INSIDE the fold - a negation in a cycle, which needs the alternating fixpoint and cost 2443 ms on the ring 1 grammar. Stating it positively instead, by walking the operand's variables BY INDEX until the walk reaches the slot's arity, keeps the program stratified: 115 ms on the same grammar, 20 times faster, and the default evaluator answers it. TWO INPUTS ARE SEEDED BY THE HOST, `premise_var(R, K, Slot, I, Name)` and `slot_arity(R, K, Slot, N)`, for the reason `opaque_seed` is: a term carries an ARBITRARY FUNCTOR and Datalog cannot destructure one it does not name. Walking a term is mechanism and decides nothing - measured, because the first attempt walked the reified term IN ROFL and got two rules wrong, both of them rules of the walker itself, where a source term `$var(V)` reifies to `$var($var(\"V\"))` and the walk had no clause for a functor it did not know. WHAT IT DOES NOT MODEL, named rather than found: the host also refuses a body whose negation cannot be ordered, and over 3555 rules that case never arises because the load door refuses such a clause first; three attempts to construct one by hand all came back with `stuck` null, which is `planBody` doing its job. NOT CONSUMED YET, and the blocker is a recursion rather than a doubt: `classify` runs inside `prepare`, `prepare` runs in the Evaluation constructor, and asking a program means constructing an Evaluation. The way through is the two-stage bootstrap this repository already owns in `l1.dense.rofl` - the safety of safety.rofl's OWN rules shipped as rows, checked by running the program on itself.").

finding(f_an_unsafe_rule_with_a_variable_perspective_answers_with_the_variable, question).
recorded(f_an_unsafe_rule_with_a_variable_perspective_answers_with_the_variable, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_an_unsafe_rule_with_a_variable_perspective_answers_with_the_variable, decision).
finding_note(f_an_unsafe_rule_with_a_variable_perspective_answers_with_the_variable, "NOTICED WHILE BUILDING THE MUTANT SET, not looked for. `m3[P](A) :- edge(A, _).` loads: the door does not refuse it, `classify` marks it unsafe because the head's PERSPECTIVE variable is bound by nothing, and the demand machinery then unfolds it at the call site. Querying `m3[Q](X)` answers `Q = ?Q, X = x` - a row whose perspective is a VARIABLE, returned to the caller. It is defensible as `for any perspective`, which is what unfolding an unsafe rule at a call site means, and it is also the only place in this system where a perspective - the thing the design notes call a ledger rather than a status - comes back unnamed. Recorded because the owner's open question about what a perspective IS (identity, access control, and the subject a question is asked about, possibly three things) is the one that decides whether this is an answer or a hole. No behaviour changed.").

finding(f_tier_zero_landed_and_my_own_prediction_about_the_cost_was_wrong, decision).
recorded(f_tier_zero_landed_and_my_own_prediction_about_the_cost_was_wrong, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_tier_zero_landed_and_my_own_prediction_about_the_cost_was_wrong, doc).
addressed_by(f_tier_zero_landed_and_my_own_prediction_about_the_cost_was_wrong, "src/engine.ts").
finding_note(f_tier_zero_landed_and_my_own_prediction_about_the_cost_was_wrong, "THE TWO-STAGE BOOTSTRAP WORKS AND TIER 0 IS PAID. `classify` no longer folds over a body tracking bound variables; it reads safety.rofl's verdict, asked once per program in `safetyAnswer`. The recursion that blocked it - classify runs in prepare, prepare runs in the Evaluation constructor, asking a program means constructing an Evaluation - is broken by ONE FLAG, `bootstrap: true`, set on the evaluation of a store that holds one of the kernel's OWN programs. What that rung stands on is the assumption that every rule the kernel ships is range-restricted, and the assumption is not taken on trust: the gate loads policy.rofl and safety.rofl into one store and asks safety.rofl about both, its own rules included, with a planted unsafe clause as the control. MY OWN PREDICTION WAS FALSIFIED, WHICH IS WHY IT WAS WRITTEN DOWN. The last iteration committed to `the orchestration is a FIXED cost, so the next move raises mech by about zero`. Measured: mech 598 -> 632, +34. The orchestration IS fixed - building the store and unpacking the answer is one shared function now, `policyStore` - but each question brings its OWN SEED, and 25 of those 34 lines are the seed: walking every term of every rule for its variables, in order, because a term carries an arbitrary functor and Datalog cannot destructure one it does not name. The corrected statement is narrower and still useful: ORCHESTRATION IS FIXED, SEEDING IS PER-QUESTION. THE METRIC MOVED THE OTHER WAY THOUGH, and by more than lines: `decides` 200 -> 186, host decisions 9 -> 8, and `classify` CROSSED FROM decides TO enforces rather than shrinking away - the judgement is safety.rofl's, and what stands in the host reads a verdict and adds the one case the rules do not model. `enforces` 46 -> 58, and the two still sum to `policy` exactly. THE PRICE IN TIME IS 2.7%, MEASURED A/B: the full suite is 148 s on the baseline tree and 152 s with every Evaluation asking a program about its own rules. That is the memo doing its work - the answer is remembered per RULE SET, and a rule id is a content hash, so two evaluations of the same program ask once. Without it the ring 1 grammar alone would pay 115 ms per evaluation. AND THE LADDER GAINED A RUNG, from four to five: `prepare` used to share a rung with the asking methods and now stands above them, because asking is a step between deciding and using. The relation ladder in scanners/policy_ladder.ts did NOT move, so the two readings now agree on the number of rungs while still disagreeing on the grouping - which is what that test has always been about. Full attested suite: 999 tests, 991 pass, 8 fail, the branch baseline.").

finding(f_two_comments_claimed_a_gate_that_no_test_performed, defect).
recorded(f_two_comments_claimed_a_gate_that_no_test_performed, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_two_comments_claimed_a_gate_that_no_test_performed, doc).
addressed_by(f_two_comments_claimed_a_gate_that_no_test_performed, "test/kernel-policy-program.test.ts").
finding_note(f_two_comments_claimed_a_gate_that_no_test_performed, "src/reflect.ts SAID the copy of policy.rofl it carries is `honest only with the gate that keeps them identical, which test/kernel-policy-program.test.ts is`. The top of that test file said the same thing. MEASURED WHILE ADDING A SECOND SUCH COPY: `grep -rn POLICY_SRC` over the whole repository returns three lines, and all three are in src/. NO TEST READ THE CONSTANT AT ALL. The two texts were in fact byte-identical, so nothing was broken and nothing would have been noticed if it were: this is the same family as the gate that is permanently wrong in the safe direction, one step worse, because the reader of either comment has every reason to believe a check exists. THE TELL, and it generalises: a gate NAMED in prose is not a gate. The repository's own rule about deriving a check once from the rules instead of copying it per relation has a sibling - a check ASSERTED in a comment has to be executable from the comment, or the comment is a promise. NOW GATED, with a planted defect on each side so the comparison is known to be capable of failing, and the same gate now covers safety.rofl.").

finding(f_four_folds_left_and_the_suite_got_faster_for_it, decision).
recorded(f_four_folds_left_and_the_suite_got_faster_for_it, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_four_folds_left_and_the_suite_got_faster_for_it, doc).
addressed_by(f_four_folds_left_and_the_suite_got_faster_for_it, "safety.rofl").
finding_note(f_four_folds_left_and_the_suite_got_faster_for_it, "THE BIGGEST MOVE OF THE BRANCH, and the first one where the host got SMALLER. Four blocks left src/engine.ts in one go, all of them things that rested on the range-restriction verdict the previous iteration made a rules answer: the demand-backed set with its growing-set fixpoint and its memoised trigger closure, the stratum cone's own `for(;;)`, the provenance question and the negated-relation set, each a pair of nested loops over every body. They are clauses of safety.rofl now, asked in the same breath as the verdict, with no new seed at all - which is what the corrected prediction said would happen and this time it held. `decides` 186 -> 84, AND THE ARITHMETIC MATTERS BECAUSE TWO DIFFERENT THINGS HAPPENED IN IT: seventy lines crossed from `decides` to `enforces` (the four blocks became 27 lines of reading), and THIRTY-TWO WERE NEVER POLICY AT ALL - the machinery for asking a kernel program sat inside `planBody`'s block because it sat below it in the file, and it is declared MECH and moved above it now. `enforces` 58 -> 85, exactly the 27. Four decisions remain in the host: where a negation may stand, the opaque seed, the fingerprint hits, the run gate. TOTAL FELL FOR THE FIRST TIME, 962 -> 952, and mech 632 -> 694 is mostly that same 32-line correction. AND THE SUITE GOT 22% FASTER: 115 s against a 148 s baseline measured twice on the same machine with a tree fingerprint. That is not a side effect to shrug at - `closeRel` was a memoised recursion re-run with a fresh `seen` set for every rule of every Evaluation, and the demand fixpoint ran on every construction; both are now one memoised ask per RULE SET. Moving a decision into rules bought speed here because the rules answer is cacheable in a way a fold over a live object graph is not. ACCEPTANCE: the deleted code is kept in test/rule-answers.test.ts as the oracle and compared over 65 corpus files, 3326 rules, zero disagreements on all five answers including the per-rule trigger sets - with the corpus's SEVEN demand-backed relations pinned as a control, because agreement on a corpus that thin is mostly agreement about emptiness. Eight planted mutants carry the demand the corpus does not. THE CYCLE WAS SUPPOSED TO DIFFER AND DOES NOT: the host's `closeRel` stops at a cycle instead of closing over it and caches whatever the first `seen` produced, so it is not the same algorithm as the closure - and on `a -> b -> a` both put the whole cycle in, because the closure is reached from every entry point in turn. Measured, not reasoned. THE LADDER COLLAPSED BACK TO FOUR RUNGS and the policy-ladder's inversion disappeared: `demandSet` and `stratumCone` used to sit on different rungs because one was a loop in `stratumCone` and the other a loop in `prepare`, and they are two readings of one answer now. A disagreement between two units of measurement is not always a fact about the units.").

finding(f_the_copy_gate_caught_me_within_the_hour, insight).
recorded(f_the_copy_gate_caught_me_within_the_hour, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_copy_gate_caught_me_within_the_hour, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_copy_gate_caught_me_within_the_hour, "test/kernel-policy-program.test.ts").
finding_note(f_the_copy_gate_caught_me_within_the_hour, "THE GATE ADDED LAST ITERATION EARNED ITSELF IN THE NEXT ONE. safety.rofl is the source and src/reflect.ts carries a copy, because src/ has no `fs`. I appended fourteen clauses to the file and did not regenerate the constant, and the kernel went on running the old ten-clause program: `demand_rel` came back empty, the demand machinery mis-scheduled, and `test/round-eval-demand.test.ts` HUNG rather than failing. Twenty minutes went into the wrong hypothesis - I probed the policy store for a stratification problem and for a perspective mismatch before printing the inner evaluation's rule list and seeing that ten clauses simply were not there. THE POINT IS NOT THAT I WAS CARELESS, it is what the failure MODE was: a stale copy does not produce a wrong answer with a message, it produces a program that quietly answers a smaller question. The identity gate is a one-line assertion and the previous iteration recorded that two comments had claimed it existed for weeks while no test read the constant. The interval between writing a gate and needing it was under an hour.").

finding(f_a_rule_that_reads_provenance_with_a_free_variable_never_terminates, defect).
recorded(f_a_rule_that_reads_provenance_with_a_free_variable_never_terminates, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_a_rule_that_reads_provenance_with_a_free_variable_never_terminates, decision).
finding_note(f_a_rule_that_reads_provenance_with_a_free_variable_never_terminates, "FOUND WHILE WRITING A MUTANT, NOT LOOKED FOR, AND IT IS PRE-EXISTING. `w(F) :- derived_by(F, _, _).` loaded beside boot.rofl does not come back - measured at 60 s and then confirmed on the branch's BASELINE tree, stashed, with the same result, so it is not this session's work. The shape is a self-feeding loop: every firing emits a provenance record, the rule reads provenance and derives a new `w`, that `w` gets a provenance record of its own, and the head is not ground so each round produces a genuinely new fact. What is wrong is not that the program diverges - a program may - it is that the BUDGET does not stop it, and a budget that does not bound a divergent program is the one thing a budget is for. The mutant was rewritten as `w(x) :- derived_by(_, _, _).`, whose ground head reaches a fixpoint, and the divergent one is left here. NOT INVESTIGATED FURTHER, deliberately: it is a defect in charging rather than in anything this branch has touched, and the owner should decide whether it is this branch's business.").

finding(f_the_kernels_own_program_was_running_without_a_schedule, defect).
recorded(f_the_kernels_own_program_was_running_without_a_schedule, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_kernels_own_program_was_running_without_a_schedule, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_kernels_own_program_was_running_without_a_schedule, "safety.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_kernels_own_program_was_running_without_a_schedule, "A LIVE DEFECT IN WORK LANDED THIS SESSION, found while measuring something else. The kernel evaluates its own program in a store of its own, that store holds boot.rofl's rules not at all, and therefore holds NO `stratum` TABLE - so the strata evaluator ran every negation of safety.rofl in ONE FINAL PASS. That is exactly the failure `examples/wtf` exists to demonstrate, and safety.rofl has nested negation by construction: `mono_rule` negates `unsafe_rule`, which negates `ground` and `binds`. MEASURED, against the round evaluator, which peels its schedule off the decoded rules instead of reading a table: the two disagreed on 3 OF 65 CORPUS PROGRAMS - examples/sensors.rofl, examples/tm.rofl, examples/tm_diverge.rofl - and the relation that moved was `mono_rule`, which feeds the stratum cone the kernel then acts on. NOTHING DOWNSTREAM HAD VISIBLY CHANGED ANSWER: the acceptance test against the deleted host code was green on all 65 before and after. The answer was schedule-dependent, which is enough - a gate that happens to agree is not a gate that must. HOW IT WAS FOUND, and this is the part worth keeping: not by auditing what I had landed, but by extending it. The plan-order rules for `planBody` reported 65 files disagreeing on `stuck`, and the first three hypotheses - budget exhaustion, a perspective mismatch, a wrong existential test - were all wrong. The symptom was in the NEW work and the cause was in the old. THE FIX IS THE DOCUMENTED ARRANGEMENT rather than a workaround: `stratum` is read by the kernel and written by the PROGRAM, so safety.rofl declares its own, twenty facts, one per relation it concludes, taken from `peelRounds` over its own rules. After it: 65 of 65 agree with the round evaluator. THE GATE IS A RE-PEEL, not a comment: test/kernel-policy-program.test.ts peels the program again and compares the declaration set for set, with `peel.rounds >= 3` as the control that the schedule is not one flat layer. And the coverage sweep in test/rofl-safety.test.ts now SKIPS the twenty schedule facts and names that gate, because deleting one of twenty is far below what its mutants can see - a wrong instrument pointed at the right thing is how the last defect got recorded. ONE VISIBLE CONSEQUENCE ELSEWHERE: `readStrata` used to be dead code under the rounds evaluator, and the ablation census asserted the counter never fired. It fires now, on every path, because the table it reads is the kernel's own - the block schedules the kernel's questions rather than the program's answers. AND A PRICE: the suite is 141 s against 115 s, because the inner evaluation now runs three negation phases where it ran one.").

finding(f_the_plan_is_expressible_and_the_corpus_says_almost_nothing_about_it, insight).
recorded(f_the_plan_is_expressible_and_the_corpus_says_almost_nothing_about_it, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_plan_is_expressible_and_the_corpus_says_almost_nothing_about_it, decision).
finding_note(f_the_plan_is_expressible_and_the_corpus_says_almost_nothing_about_it, "`planBody` IS EXPRESSIBLE AND IT IS MEASURED, and the measurement contains its own warning. The rules are 30 clauses: a variable's occurrences element by element, what is bound after step K, and readiness walked BY INDEX so the universal `every variable of this negation is bound or existential` stays positive - the same shape range restriction needed. The plan is then an ORDER, not a sequence: `emit_step(R, J, K)` and `emit_tag(R, J, T)`, and the host sorts by (K, tag, J), which is three lines of mechanism. Against `planBody` over the corpus: 65 files, 3326 rules, 1056 negations, ZERO disagreements on the plan order, on `stuck`, on `stuckVars` and on `headGround`. AND THE NUMBER THAT MATTERS IS THE ONE I ALMOST DID NOT COUNT: **0 BODIES ACTUALLY REORDER**. In the whole corpus `planBody` returns the written body unchanged every single time. So the corpus agreement on the ORDER is agreement about identity - two instruments both returning their input - and it proves nothing about the thing the function exists to do. What it does test is `stuck`, `stuckVars` and `headGround`, which are real and non-vacuous. THE NEXT ITERATION'S ACCEPTANCE THEREFORE CANNOT BE THE CORPUS: it needs a mutant set in which the plan MOVES, one per way a negation can become ready late, and the corpus can only be the negative control. THE OTHER OPEN COST IS THE DOOR. `checkOrderable` runs per clause, before any store exists, and asking a program costs 2.66 ms measured - 444 ms added to loading the ring 1 grammar's 161 rules. `load` parses every clause before adding any, so the ask can be batched per LOAD instead of per clause, which is the shape the move needs and a change to src/api.ts rather than to the kernel. `whynot` asks a third way, about a RENAMED clause that is in no store at all; one ask each, ~3 ms, and it is the reason the answer cannot simply be memoised by rule id.").

finding(f_the_runtime_did_not_shrink_and_i_reported_a_metric_that_moved_instead, defect).
recorded(f_the_runtime_did_not_shrink_and_i_reported_a_metric_that_moved_instead, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_runtime_did_not_shrink_and_i_reported_a_metric_that_moved_instead, decision).
finding_note(f_the_runtime_did_not_shrink_and_i_reported_a_metric_that_moved_instead, "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER THE LANGUAGE AND ITS RUNTIME ARE GETTING SMALLER, AND THE ANSWER IS NO. Measured over this session, from faa6870 to 05a9512: src/*.ts went 3141 to 3409 code lines, of which 223 are the embedded copies of policy.rofl and safety.rofl - so the TypeScript a reimplementer must write went 3141 to 3186, UP BY 45. src/engine.ts went 901 to 952. The kernel's own rules went 31 to 107 lines of .rofl. Tests went 16239 to 16749, +510 in three files. ELEVEN LINES OF GATE PER LINE OF RUNTIME, this session. AND I REPORTED `decides` FOR NINE ITERATIONS WITHOUT ONCE SAYING THAT IT IS NOT THE SIZE. `decides` 221 -> 84 is real and it measures the SURFACE OF JUDGEMENT - how many lines a reimplementer must get semantically right - which fell by 137. But the amount of code went up. The exchange rate, measured: REMOVING 137 LINES OF HOST JUDGEMENT COST 76 LINES OF RULES AND 45 LINES OF NEW HOST MECHANISM. The host does not shrink under this move; it changes character. That is a defensible trade and it is not the trade the branch's title implies, and the difference went unstated for nine iterations because I was watching a number that moved.").

finding(f_the_runtime_cannot_be_made_smaller_by_deleting_because_almost_all_of_it_runs, insight).
recorded(f_the_runtime_cannot_be_made_smaller_by_deleting_because_almost_all_of_it_runs, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_runtime_cannot_be_made_smaller_by_deleting_because_almost_all_of_it_runs, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_runtime_cannot_be_made_smaller_by_deleting_because_almost_all_of_it_runs, "scanners/necessity.ts").
finding_note(f_the_runtime_cannot_be_made_smaller_by_deleting_because_almost_all_of_it_runs, "A SECOND CENSUS, ASKING THE SIZE QUESTION INSTEAD OF THE POLICY QUESTION, AND IT RUNS RATHER THAN READS. `scanners/necessity.ts` takes eight TASKS - programs that use the language for something: evaluate, ticks, explain, persist, refuse, self-apply, well-founded, semiring - and reports, from V8's own coverage, which code lines of src/ each one enters. THE HEADLINE: the MINIMAL task - load a program, evaluate it, ask one question - reaches 2217 of 3409 code lines, 65% OF src/. The other seven capabilities add 588 lines between them, the largest being the semiring fold at 138 and explanation at 125. 604 lines are reached by nothing, and 82 of those are src/repl.ts, which is a tool rather than a runtime. SO THE RUNTIME CANNOT BE MADE SMALLER BY DELETION. Of 322 functions in src/, 283 are entered by some task; the 39 that are not are almost entirely PUBLIC API a caller may use and no task happens to - `excise`, `run`, `holds`, `assert`, `factKeys`, `strataPlan` - which is untasked, not dead. Exactly ONE function in the whole of src/ is referenced nowhere outside its own file: `termEq` in src/unify.ts, three lines, deleted. THE INSTRUMENT'S OWN PROPERTY IS THE POINT: it has no literals to update. The block census pins ~28 hand judgements as numbers and this session spent a measurable share of every iteration re-typing them after the kernel moved; this one is re-run and re-measures. Its test therefore asserts only shapes - every task enters the evaluator, the minimal task reaches between half and nine tenths of src/ - because a number pinned here would reintroduce the maintenance the instrument exists to remove. WHAT IS LEFT AS A CUT is not the runtime: it is the APPARATUS. scanners/{engine_split,bootstrap_dag,policy_ladder,ablate}.ts plus their tests are 1800 code lines measuring 952 lines of src/engine.ts, nearly two to one. `scanners/policy_ladder.ts` (197) and `rules/kernel-policy.rofl` (19) now measure a pack that safety.rofl has become, and test/rule-answers.test.ts is the acceptance that superseded their answer check - but they are referenced from seven files including test/rings.test.ts, so retiring them is a real edit rather than a delete, and it is the owner's call because the measurement is his.").

finding(f_the_scanners_are_not_the_language_and_my_cut_framing_was_wrong, insight).
recorded(f_the_scanners_are_not_the_language_and_my_cut_framing_was_wrong, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_scanners_are_not_the_language_and_my_cut_framing_was_wrong, doc).
finding_note(f_the_scanners_are_not_the_language_and_my_cut_framing_was_wrong, "THE OWNER CORRECTED THE FRAME AND HE IS RIGHT. I proposed cutting scanners/ as an answer to `is the runtime getting smaller`, and scanners/ is not the runtime: nothing there ships, nothing there is a reimplementer's burden, and 1800 lines of measuring apparatus next to 952 lines of evaluator is not a size problem at all. THE REAL COST OF THE APPARATUS IS NOT ITS SIZE, IT IS ITS MAINTENANCE, and that cost is specific and measurable: scanners/engine_split.ts pins about 28 HAND JUDGEMENTS AS NUMBERS, and every iteration this session that moved a line of the evaluator spent a visible share of itself re-typing them. scanners/necessity.ts, written the same day, pins none - it re-runs. So the question about the apparatus is not `how big` but `how many literals does it own`, and the answer for the block census is 28 and for the coverage census is 0. That is the comparison worth keeping; the line count was the wrong measurement of the wrong thing.").

finding(f_the_rofl_parser_agrees_and_is_63000_times_slower, insight).
recorded(f_the_rofl_parser_agrees_and_is_63000_times_slower, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_rofl_parser_agrees_and_is_63000_times_slower, decision).
finding_note(f_the_rofl_parser_agrees_and_is_63000_times_slower, "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER THE LOAD PATH RUNS ON THE ROFL PARSER YET. IT DOES NOT, AND src/ DOES NOT KNOW RING 1 EXISTS - the five mentions in src/ are comments. `Rofl.load` calls `parseProgram` from src/parser.ts, 262 lines of TypeScript reached by every one of the eight necessity tasks. THE PRICE OF MOVING, MEASURED TODAY: boot.rofl parses in 1 ms through the host and 63138 ms through ring 1, and the two AGREE clause for clause (`canon` identical). Sixty-three seconds for twenty-eight clauses. examples/sensors.rofl: 0 ms against 10623 ms. THE SHAPE OF THE COST, AND IT SPLITS IN TWO. A per-clause FLOOR of about 190 ms that does not move with clause length: five bytes and fifty-three bytes both cost ~200 ms. Above roughly sixty bytes a second term appears and it is QUADRATIC: subtracting the floor, 63 bytes cost 41 ms, 125 cost 182, 189 cost 450 - exponent 2.1 and 2.2 across the two steps. THE FLOOR IS NOT AMORTISABLE, and that was worth testing rather than assuming: parsing four clauses in a FRESH world costs 225, 196, 192, 175 ms and in the SAME world 203, 158, 173, 178 - no difference. It is not world construction and it is not something derived-reuse could cache, because the characters change and everything downstream of them is invalidated. It is the engine's throughput running a grammar fixpoint over twenty characters. SO RING 1 IS A PROOF AND A GATE, NOT A REPLACEMENT: it establishes that the grammar is expressible in the language and it agrees with the host on 932 of 932 distinct clause shapes in the corpus. Moving the load path onto it would make every test that loads boot.rofl take a minute - the whole suite runs in 159 s today. WHAT WOULD HAVE TO CHANGE is now nameable rather than vague: the 190 ms floor is an engine-throughput number, so it wants the evaluator to be faster on a small dense program, and the n-squared term is the chart formulation itself. Neither is a defect and neither is close.").

finding(f_the_cache_costs_four_times_the_work_it_saves_none_of, defect).
recorded(f_the_cache_costs_four_times_the_work_it_saves_none_of, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_cache_costs_four_times_the_work_it_saves_none_of, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_cache_costs_four_times_the_work_it_saves_none_of, "rules/parse-cost.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_cache_costs_four_times_the_work_it_saves_none_of, "examples/ring1/demo.ts").
finding_note(f_the_cache_costs_four_times_the_work_it_saves_none_of, "THE OWNER ASKED FOR A MODEL OF THE CODE AND A WHY CHAIN FOR THE SLOWNESS, and building one found a defect the prose had missed for two days. PARSING ONE CLAUSE THROUGH RING 1: 217 ms with derived-reuse on, 42 ms with it off, and the two runs produce 639 FIRINGS EACH and a store of 3737 FACTS EACH. Byte for byte the same answer. The cache saves nothing and costs 175 ms, four times the 42 ms of work it is wrapped around - so on this program it is not an optimisation with a bad case, it is overhead, always. Ring 1's own `fromImage` already passes `reuse: false` and `parseFile` is therefore clean; `world()` does not, so every test and demo that parses through a freshly built world pays it. THE ROOT IS ONE STORE PRIMITIVE. Measured against a relation of 1000 facts and one of 40000: `store.add` costs 934 ns and 1354 ns - flat. `store.relAll` costs 84 us and 6361 us - PROPORTIONAL TO THE RELATION. And `relAll` is on both hot paths: the reuse plan fingerprints a relation by materialising all of it, and the premise match falls back to it whenever `indexProbe` declines. THE SECOND BRANCH IS STRUCTURAL AND THE MODEL CORRECTED ME ON IT. I wrote the rule for `firings are linear in the input` first, and the model refused to derive it: over all ten pairs of the size sweep, firings per byte RISE. So the superlinearity is a product of two growing factors - more firings per byte, and each firing dearer than the last - and only the second is misplaced: a firing should cost what its own premises cost, not what the store happens to hold. It costs the store because `indexProbe` returns null when NO ARGUMENT OF THE PREMISE IS BOUND, which is the leading premise of every chart rule, and the scan is then over a relation the parse itself is filling.").

finding(f_a_model_of_the_code_is_a_program_and_why_is_the_report, insight).
recorded(f_a_model_of_the_code_is_a_program_and_why_is_the_report, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_a_model_of_the_code_is_a_program_and_why_is_the_report, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_model_of_the_code_is_a_program_and_why_is_the_report, "scanners/parse_cost.ts").
finding_note(f_a_model_of_the_code_is_a_program_and_why_is_the_report, "THE SHAPE THE OWNER ASKED FOR AND WHAT IT COST. `scanners/parse_cost.ts` runs ring 1 and writes facts/parse-cost.rofl - 39 lines, no judgement in any of them; `rules/parse-cost.rofl` is the reasoning and measures nothing. Then `why(slow_because(parse, reuse_plan))` walks the derivation back to the two runs that produced the numbers, arithmetic included: `175 is -(217,42)`, `175 > 42`, and under them the four axioms that say the firing counts and the store sizes were equal. THE DIVISION IS THE POINT AND IT IS NOT DECORATION. A prose explanation of a performance problem cannot be re-run; this one is `npm run parsecost` and the model then answers over whatever the code does today. And because the facts and the reasoning are in different files, a number that moves cannot quietly take a conclusion with it - the conclusion either still derives or it does not. THE MODEL REFUSED A HYPOTHESIS, WHICH IS THE STRONGEST THING TO REPORT ABOUT IT. `linear_in_input(firings)` was written before the sweep was read and derived nothing, because the sweep says firings per byte rise. Cross-multiplication rather than division throughout - `L is FB * A, Rr is FA * B, L > Rr` - because a rule may not divide its way to a fraction, and that constraint turned out to make the claims MORE readable rather than less: every comparison is between two integers the reader can check.").

finding(f_the_call_site_is_fixed_and_the_kernel_still_pays_the_fingerprint, decision).
recorded(f_the_call_site_is_fixed_and_the_kernel_still_pays_the_fingerprint, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_call_site_is_fixed_and_the_kernel_still_pays_the_fingerprint, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_call_site_is_fixed_and_the_kernel_still_pays_the_fingerprint, "examples/ring1/demo.ts").
finding_note(f_the_call_site_is_fixed_and_the_kernel_still_pays_the_fingerprint, "`world()` NOW PASSES `reuse: false`, AND HERE IS WHAT THAT BOUGHT, MEASURED BEFORE AND AFTER ON THE SAME MACHINE. One clause through a world: 200 ms -> 40 ms, FIVE TIMES. Building the world: 194 ms against about 350. Building the ring 1 image: 400 -> 249 ms. test/example-ring1.test.ts: 55 s -> 47 s, with IDENTICAL outcomes on all sixteen tests, which is the control that matters - the cache was measured to save zero firings and to leave a byte-identical store, so switching it off must move no answer, and it moves none. `parseFile` is unchanged at 63 s on boot.rofl because it goes through `fromImage`, which has always passed the flag; the corpus sweep is most of that test file, which is why the file improved by 15% rather than by five times. WHAT IS NOT FIXED, and it is the larger half. The kernel still fingerprints EVERY RELATION on every evaluation before discovering it can reuse none of them, and it fingerprints by materialising each relation whole. That is a decision for the owner rather than a bug to patch quietly, because the cheap guard - skip the plan when the previous evaluation reused nothing - is a policy about when an optimisation is worth attempting, and this branch has spent nine iterations establishing that such policies belong in rules rather than in `planReuse`. THE MODEL SAYS SO ITSELF AND STILL DOES AFTER THE FIX: `fixable_now(reuse_plan)` continues to derive, because it is a claim about the cache and not about one call site, and re-running `npm run parsecost` re-measured it at 161 ms of overhead against 42 ms of work.").

finding(f_the_attestation_certifies_the_tree_and_not_the_machine, defect).
recorded(f_the_attestation_certifies_the_tree_and_not_the_machine, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_attestation_certifies_the_tree_and_not_the_machine, doc).
finding_note(f_the_attestation_certifies_the_tree_and_not_the_machine, "THIS NOTE WAS WRONG WHEN FIRST WRITTEN AND THE OWNER CAUGHT IT. It said THE MACHINE GOT SLOWER, on the strength of one experiment - the tree that measured 159 s re-measured at 211 s - and that experiment rules the TREE out without ruling anything else IN. He pushed back: the container is cloud and identical. So it was measured properly. A REGISTER-ONLY LOOP: 1866, 1868, 1875, 1891, 1895 ms over five runs, stable to 1.5%. FOUR COPIES OF IT IN PARALLEL: 2025 ms against 1876 for one, near-perfect scaling on four cores. No cgroup cpu.max at all, 15 GB of 16 free, 30 GB of disk free, load 0.29, no stray process above 3% - and test/example-ring1.test.ts run ALONE takes 49 s against the 47 s it took half an hour earlier. Every dimension I can measure says the machine is the same. THE CLAIM IS RETRACTED. WHAT IS ESTABLISHED IS LESS THAN A CAUSE: per-test durations across the two full runs, matched by NAME, are 988 identical tests, 413897 ms of test time then and 579290 ms now. The work itself takes 40% longer, uniformly - not scheduling, not one slow file. The two new model files cost 1 s of the suite, measured by moving them out; the world() fix is not it either, measured by stashing it. AND A MECHANISM FOR THE SENSITIVITY, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS A CAUSE: my CPU benchmark was register-only and therefore blind to what this suite does. An allocation-heavy loop - 200k map inserts of small objects, forty times over - costs 6081 ms alone and 8680 ms wall when four copies run at once: a 43% penalty per process, against 8% for the ALU loop. THE SUITE IS ALLOCATION-HEAVY AND RUNS FOUR FILES AT A TIME, so its wall clock is set by memory contention, which a single-file run and an ALU benchmark both miss by construction. SO THE INSTRUMENT IS WRONG FOR THE CLAIM, whatever moved. Full-suite wall time varied 159 to 220 s on trees that differ by a comment, and I quoted it as a result four times. What survives is PAIRED, SINGLE-FILE timing taken in one window - ring 1 at 55 s then 47 s across the reuse fix, one clause at 200 ms then 40 ms - because that holds the contention roughly constant instead of assuming it away. Iteration 8 s 115 s against a 148 s baseline was a stashed pair and I still believe it, but it was a FULL-SUITE pair, so it inherits this doubt and wants re-measuring per file before it is quoted again.").

finding(f_the_parser_is_not_the_loader_it_is_the_text_interface, insight).
recorded(f_the_parser_is_not_the_loader_it_is_the_text_interface, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_parser_is_not_the_loader_it_is_the_text_interface, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_parser_is_not_the_loader_it_is_the_text_interface, "scanners/tasks/t9-noparse.ts").
finding_note(f_the_parser_is_not_the_loader_it_is_the_text_interface, "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER src/parser.ts CAN BE THROWN OUT OF THE HOST, and my first answer - `no, ring 1 is 63000 times slower` - was answering a question he had not asked. Ring 1's speed matters if the host must parse AT RUNTIME. It does not, if programs ship COMPILED: `Rofl.save()` already emits a snapshot the host restores without parsing anything, ring 1 already produces exactly that, and a slow compiler in a build step is a different thing from a slow runtime. So the real question is what the host still needs a parser FOR once no program text reaches it, and that is measurable rather than arguable. MEASURED with the coverage census, one task that loads a program and one that restores a snapshot built by a SEPARATE PROCESS: of 262 code lines in src/parser.ts, a loading host enters 220 and a RESTORE-ONLY host still enters 201. NINETEEN LINES, seven percent, are all that is unique to loading program text - and they are not even clause assembly, they are the perspective bracket, `@next`, a negative integer and two body-element branches that this task's query happened not to use. THE REASON IS ONE SENTENCE: A QUERY IS TEXT TOO. `parseLiteral` is called at five sites in src/api.ts - query, holds, why, whynot, retract, excise - against two for `parseProgram`, and it shares the tokenizer, the term parser, the perspective bracket and the escape table with it. The parser is not the loader. It is the language's TEXT INTERFACE, and loading is the smaller half of it. THE MEASUREMENT ALMOST MISSED IT, and how it failed is worth keeping: the first version of the task built its own snapshot in the same process, so it parsed before it restored, and reported that a parser-less host needs 220 of 220 lines. A task that does the thing it is measuring the absence of measures nothing. THE PARSER CAN THEREFORE GO ONLY IF BOTH HAPPEN: programs ship compiled - a build step, and ring 1 can be that compiler - AND queries stop arriving as text, reaching the kernel as reified terms instead. The second is a language decision and a large one: every test, every demo and the REPL ask in text.").

finding(f_the_primitive_already_exists_and_the_kernel_parses_its_own_text, decision).
recorded(f_the_primitive_already_exists_and_the_kernel_parses_its_own_text, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_primitive_already_exists_and_the_kernel_parses_its_own_text, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_primitive_already_exists_and_the_kernel_parses_its_own_text, "scanners/tasks/t10-l0.ts").
finding_note(f_the_primitive_already_exists_and_the_kernel_parses_its_own_text, "THE OWNER ASKED FOR THE BASIC HOST PRIMITIVE THAT WOULD LET THE REST OF THE PARSER LIVE IN ROFL, and the tower already built it: examples/ring1/l0.ts, 138 code lines against the parser's 262, doing exactly two things - READ terms and facts, PROMOTE a rule written as one fact - with no rules, no variables, no perspectives, no tenses and no builtins in the language it reads. His portability argument is the right one: a reimplementer writes L0 and the grammar travels as ROFL, instead of writing 262 lines of surface syntax again. MEASURED, WITH A TASK THAT USES NOTHING ELSE: `loadDense` puts the dense grammar and a dense program in through `addClause`, the answers are read off the store as terms, and no text of the language is parsed by the task at all. It still enters 195 of 262 lines of src/parser.ts. THE SAME TASK WITH NO RULES IN THE STORE ENTERS 27. So 168 of those 195 are not the program's parse at all - they are THE KERNEL PARSING ITS OWN CARRIED PROGRAMS. `kernelProgram(SAFETY_SRC)` and `kernelProgram(POLICY_SRC)` call `parseProgram` the first time any store with rules is evaluated, because I carried policy.rofl and safety.rofl as TEXT this session. The parser is load-bearing for the evaluator now, and I made it so. THE REMEDY IS ALREADY IN THE REPOSITORY AND IS THE SAME PATTERN: `l1.dense.rofl` carries a 49-rule grammar as 51 facts because the reflection form is four rows plus one per premise. Carry the kernel's two programs as PRE-ENCODED ROWS rather than as source, keep the .rofl files as the source, and the identity gate written on 2026-09-06 already keeps the two honest. The kernel would then need no parser to run its own policy. WHAT WOULD STILL STAND: `parseLiteral`, because a QUERY is text - t10 dodges it by reading the store, and every test, demo and the REPL do not. And the speed of the ROFL grammar for loading user programs, which is the place to say the thing this whole thread has been circling: a native parser can be an OPTIONAL CACHE over a definition written in ROFL rather than the definition itself, and the gate that makes that legal already exists - 932 of 932 distinct clause shapes, host against ring 1, identical.").

finding(f_the_kernel_stopped_parsing_itself_and_the_reader_moved_into_it, decision).
recorded(f_the_kernel_stopped_parsing_itself_and_the_reader_moved_into_it, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_the_kernel_stopped_parsing_itself_and_the_reader_moved_into_it, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_kernel_stopped_parsing_itself_and_the_reader_moved_into_it, "src/dense.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_kernel_stopped_parsing_itself_and_the_reader_moved_into_it, "src/kernel-dense.ts").
finding_note(f_the_kernel_stopped_parsing_itself_and_the_reader_moved_into_it, "THE 168 LINES ARE GONE, MEASURED THE SAME WAY THEY WERE FOUND. A host that loads nothing but dense facts entered 195 of src/parser.ts's 262 lines; it enters 27 now, and the 27 are the module's own tables. A host that restores a snapshot and asks in text went 201 to 102, and what remains there is `parseLiteral` -- because a query is text, which is the half this move does not touch. WHAT WAS DONE. policy.rofl and safety.rofl were carried as 223 lines of SOURCE TEXT in src/reflect.ts and parsed by `parseProgram` the first time any store with rules was evaluated. They are COMPILED now, into the dense form the tower already chose: `src/kernel-dense.ts`, 694 bytes for policy and 5240 for safety, generated by scripts/build_kernel_dense.ts from the .rofl files, read by `src/dense.ts`. THE FORMAT WAS A REAL CHOICE AND THE REPOSITORY HAD ALREADY ARGUED IT: the same rows as JSON are 83 KB against 9 KB of ROFL source and nobody opens them; l0.ts makes exactly that argument about 5 KiB of facts against 702 KiB of JSON. Dense is smaller than the source and diffs. AND THE READER IS NOW THE KERNEL'S. `denseTokens`, `denseFacts` and the term decoding moved out of examples/ring1/l0.ts and into src/dense.ts, because the kernel and the tower need the same thing; l0.ts fell from 138 code lines to 15, and what is left of it is the DOOR, which is the whole of what L0 adds over a reader. SIZE, MEASURED: src/ went 3409 to 3400 code lines. The restructuring cost nothing and bought the dependency. THE MOVE FOUND A DEFECT IN L0 that the tower could never have hit. `denseClauses` used a plain fact's arguments RAW instead of decoding them, so `eq_or_is(\"=\")` came back as the FUNCTOR `s(\"=\")` -- and seven corpus programs then computed a different `trigger_of`, which the acceptance test caught immediately. l0.ts had carried that bug since it was written and the tower never reached the case, because a grammar's facts are atoms and integers. One copy of a reader cannot have two behaviours; two copies had one behaviour each. THE GATE GOT STRONGER FOR IT: it used to compare two TEXTS, and it now compares the compiled program against re-compiling the source, and separately reads the shipped program back as CLAUSES and compares those canonically -- which is the check that found the defect.").

finding(f_a_question_is_data_and_the_parser_became_optional, decision).
recorded(f_a_question_is_data_and_the_parser_became_optional, "2026-09-06").
demands(f_a_question_is_data_and_the_parser_became_optional, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_question_is_data_and_the_parser_became_optional, "src/api.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_question_is_data_and_the_parser_became_optional, "test/ask-by-term.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_question_is_data_and_the_parser_became_optional, "src/unify.ts").
finding_note(f_a_question_is_data_and_the_parser_became_optional, "THE OTHER HALF, AND IT WAS ONE LINE PER DOOR. `query`, `holds`, `why`, `whynot`, `retract` and `excise` each began with `resolveBook(parseLiteral(text))`; they take `string | Lit` now and call one private `asked()` instead. Nothing about the text path changed - it parses and then does what it always did - and a host with no parser passes the literal, because A QUESTION IS DATA. MEASURED, the same way the first half was: a host that restores a snapshot and asks in text entered 102 of src/parser.ts's 262 lines; asking with literals it enters 27, THE SAME 27 as the L0-only host. And those 27 are not parsing: they are the type declarations (`Lit`, `Clause`, `BodyElem`, `Temporal`), two error classes and the three constant tables (`PUNCT`, `ESCAPES`, `CMP_OPS`), all of which run at MODULE LOAD. Not one line of a parser body executes. They are entered at all only because this build still offers the text path and `src/api.ts` imports the two entry points as VALUES; TypeScript erases a type-only import, so a build without the text path would not load the module and the number would be zero. THE DENSE FORM CARRIES A QUESTION TOO, and needed no new syntax: a question is a FACT whose arguments may be variables, `close(v(\"A\"), v(\"B\")).`, so `denseLit` is `denseClauses` of one row. One case had to be closed by hand - `r/3` is the RULE form and its body list may be empty, so counting body elements does not tell a fact from a rule; the tag does. THE ACCEPTANCE IS THAT THE TWO PATHS AGREE: thirteen queries over boot.rofl plus sensors, text against literal, rows sorted and errors compared, with a control that at least eight of them ANSWER so the agreement is not between empty sets; the dense path checked separately through query, holds, why and whynot; and retract run in two worlds whose `canonicalState()` is compared BYTE FOR BYTE afterwards, with a control that the retraction moved the store at all. SO THE PARSER IS NOW OPTIONAL BY MEASUREMENT rather than by argument: 235 of its 262 lines are needed only where a person types. What is left to make it optional as a FILE is moving `Lit`, `Clause`, `BodyElem` and `Temporal` out of it -- they are the kernel's data structures and live there only by history -- which is 25 import sites and no behaviour.").

finding(f_the_grammar_is_droppable_as_a_file_and_the_lexis_is_not, decision).
recorded(f_the_grammar_is_droppable_as_a_file_and_the_lexis_is_not, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_grammar_is_droppable_as_a_file_and_the_lexis_is_not, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_grammar_is_droppable_as_a_file_and_the_lexis_is_not, "src/tokens.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_grammar_is_droppable_as_a_file_and_the_lexis_is_not, "docs/dogfood/2026-09-07-grammar-and-lexis.md").
addressed_by(f_the_grammar_is_droppable_as_a_file_and_the_lexis_is_not, "src/unify.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_grammar_is_droppable_as_a_file_and_the_lexis_is_not, "scripts/parser_optional.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_grammar_is_droppable_as_a_file_and_the_lexis_is_not, "test/parser-optional.test.ts").
finding_note(f_the_grammar_is_droppable_as_a_file_and_the_lexis_is_not,
  "THE FOUR TYPES MOVED AND THE FILE FELL AWAY WITH THEM, and the second half of the answer is the one I did not expect. `Lit`, `Clause`, `BodyElem` and `Temporal` lived in src/parser.ts because that is where the first one was ever constructed, and that single accident of history made the GRAMMAR look mandatory to every module that only wanted the shape: five files in src/ imported the parser to name a record. They live in src/unify.ts now, beside the `Term` they are built out of; src/parser.ts re-exports them, so the twenty import sites outside src/ did not move and no behaviour changed. THEN THE LEXIS SPLIT OFF, and this was NOT tidying. src/reflect.ts value-imported `tokenize` and still does - `atom_of` is a kernel builtin, on the rules side, and when a RULE builds a string and asks for an atom the only honest oracle for 'is this a name a program could have written' is the tokenizer itself; a regex beside it would be the hand-written twin this repository has paid for twice. So the lexis is now src/tokens.ts, 78 code lines - tokenizer, escape table, and `escapeString`, which travels with the unescaping because the two are one decision - and src/parser.ts is 174 code lines of pure grammar that NOTHING IN THE KERNEL ENTERS. MEASURED TWO WAYS, AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE THAT ANSWERS THE OWNER'S QUESTION. By the line census: the dense task enters 8 of the grammar's 174 lines and 9 of the lexis's 78, and every one of the 17 is a DECLARATION executed when the module loads - no body runs. That is exactly what the census could already say, and it is NOT the question, because a build ships a module for being imported and not for being run. So: DELETE THE FILE. With src/parser.ts removed and the two text doors in src/api.ts stubbed, the kernel type-checks - exit 0. With src/tokens.ts ALSO removed it fails, with exactly one error, naming src/reflect.ts. Two mutants that answer differently, the second being the control that says the first was not vacuous; scripts/parser_optional.ts runs both and test/parser-optional.test.ts keeps them honest. WHAT THIS BUYS THE PORT, which was the owner's criterion and not compactness: a second host writes the LEXIS (what a name, a number and a string are - 78 lines, and unavoidable because it defines the vocabulary) plus L0 (15 lines, the dense door), and the GRAMMAR travels as ROFL in the tower. It does not rewrite 174 lines of surface syntax, and it can extend the syntax in place because the grammar is a program. WHAT IS STILL TRUE AND SHOULD NOT BE OVERSOLD: this build keeps the text door, so a bundle of the current src/ still contains the grammar; what changed is that dropping it is now a two-line edit at ONE call site instead of impossible.").

finding(f_a_census_with_a_hand_written_file_list_was_already_blind, defect).
recorded(f_a_census_with_a_hand_written_file_list_was_already_blind, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_census_with_a_hand_written_file_list_was_already_blind, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_census_with_a_hand_written_file_list_was_already_blind, "scanners/necessity.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_census_with_a_hand_written_file_list_was_already_blind, "docs/dogfood/2026-09-07-grammar-and-lexis.md").
addressed_by(f_a_census_with_a_hand_written_file_list_was_already_blind, "test/necessity.test.ts").
finding_note(f_a_census_with_a_hand_written_file_list_was_already_blind,
  "THE CENSUS HAD A HAND-WRITTEN FILE LIST AND HAD ALREADY GONE BLIND, one day after it was written. scanners/necessity.ts existed precisely because scanners/engine_split.ts pins ~28 hand judgements as literals and I wanted an instrument with NOTHING TO UPDATE when the kernel moves. It had nine literals: the src/ filenames. src/dense.ts and src/kernel-dense.ts shipped on 2026-09-06 and the census reported on src/ without them - 3132 lines where the directory holds 3416 - and today src/tokens.ts would have vanished the same way. THE FAILURE IS IN THE SAFE DIRECTION, which is why nobody saw it: a missing file makes the total SMALLER and turns nothing red, so the number kept looking like a measurement of src/ while measuring a subset frozen at the hour the scanner was typed. This is the repository's own recorded pattern - a gate inherits the scope of its INCIDENT - and it is the same shape as orphan_claim[audit] being reopened by one new authored relation. The list is a readdirSync of src/ now, in the scanner AND in test/necessity.test.ts, which had a second copy of the same nine literals. AND THE PRICE OF THE BLINDNESS WAS REAL, not hypothetical: 284 of 3416 code lines - 8% of the kernel, including the whole of the dense reader and the compiled programs it reads - were outside every column of the table I used to argue about what the kernel needs.").

finding(f_arrival_order_is_not_meaning_and_the_fork_now_preserves_it, decision).
recorded(f_arrival_order_is_not_meaning_and_the_fork_now_preserves_it, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_arrival_order_is_not_meaning_and_the_fork_now_preserves_it, doc).
addressed_by(f_arrival_order_is_not_meaning_and_the_fork_now_preserves_it, "src/store.ts").
addressed_by(f_arrival_order_is_not_meaning_and_the_fork_now_preserves_it, "adapters/sqlite-store.ts").
addressed_by(f_arrival_order_is_not_meaning_and_the_fork_now_preserves_it, "test/store-conformance.test.ts").
finding_note(f_arrival_order_is_not_meaning_and_the_fork_now_preserves_it,
  "DECIDED BY VADIM 2026-09-07, ON A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN ON THE ARGUMENT. f_a_forks_key_order_is_an_accident_of_restore asked whether a fork's arrival order is part of the language's meaning or an artefact of `restore`. THE WAY TO ANSWER IT WAS NOT TO READ THE FOUR CALL SITES - I did read them and my reading was right but it was still a reading - IT WAS TO REVERSE THE ORDER GLOBALLY AND RUN EVERYTHING. `allFacts` returning `[...facts.values()].reverse()`, full node suite: 1011 tests, 13 fail against the branch baseline of 8. ALL FIVE NEW REDS ASSERT THE ORDER ITSELF and carry the same message, `allFacts ARRIVAL order` - the three sqlite conformance differentials, the well-founded differential, and the fork-order test. NOT ONE FIXPOINT, NOT ONE canonicalState, NOT ONE GOLDEN BYTE MOVED. That is the whole answer, and it is stronger than the code reading because it does not depend on my having found every reader: the only kernel path that sees arrival order is `assumptionOf`, whose consumer `negHolds` returns on the first match, so the array is an EXISTENCE test; every other reader sorts or keys by a unique fact key. WHAT CHANGED. `Store.clone` walks `this.facts` in insertion order instead of `[...keys()].sort()`, so a fork answers in the ORIGINAL's arrival order; `absorb` sorts on read, so `relPersp`/`relAll` are untouched either way. The SQLite port's `renumberByKey` is DELETED, not defaulted off - its 26 lines and the `{ renumber }` option are gone, and `clone()` is `VACUUM INTO` and nothing else. THE PRICES, BOTH MEASURED. The port: 20.7 to 2.92 us per fact at 200000 facts, 7.1x, and the fork now BEATS the in-memory reference instead of losing to it 2.9x. The reference: 1.12-1.47 us per fact sorting against 0.51-0.86 preserving on a shuffled 200k store, three interleaved runs each, about 2x - a sort of the whole key set, paid on every `Rofl.load`, which takes a rollback clone unconditionally. THE TEST NOW ASSERTS WHAT IT WANTED. It read `positive control: a fork IS in key order`, which pinned the accident; it reads `a fork is in its ORIGINAL's arrival order` for both stores plus the control that the original's order is NOT key order, so preserving it is distinguishable from sorting. Full attested suite after: 1011 / 1003 / 8, the same eight as the baseline. THE GENERAL SHAPE, and it is the repository's own rule arriving one level up: an implementation's incidental behaviour became a specification because a test was written FROM the implementation. The cheap way to tell a contract from an accident is to BREAK IT EVERYWHERE and read which reds are about meaning and which are about the break.").
addressed_by(f_a_forks_key_order_is_an_accident_of_restore, "src/store.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_forks_key_order_is_an_accident_of_restore, "adapters/sqlite-store.ts").

finding(f_a_ledger_is_where_you_reason_not_who_you_are_about, decision).
recorded(f_a_ledger_is_where_you_reason_not_who_you_are_about, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_ledger_is_where_you_reason_not_who_you_are_about, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_ledger_is_where_you_reason_not_who_you_are_about, "docs/choosing-perspectives.md").
finding_note(f_a_ledger_is_where_you_reason_not_who_you_are_about,
  "VADIM ENDED A LONG DETOUR IN TWO LINES, and the detour is the finding. Asked what perspectives are, he answered `the context in which this makes sense`, and identity, access and subject are all perspectives. I took SUBJECT literally and asked what `alice's book about tells` would have to be - and then built, in order: a compound perspective term, partial pattern matching in the perspective slot, an order on perspectives, and finally a perspective as a SET of atoms with set inclusion as a free lattice and an inverted index to answer it. His reply: `alice tells and bob tells are ONE perspective, claims`, with `claimed(alice, ...)` and `claimed(bob, ...)`. THE WHOLE EDIFICE WAS SCAFFOLDING AROUND A MIS-MODELLED ENTITY. A perspective is not WHO OR WHAT THE ENTRY IS ABOUT, it is WHO MAY WRITE. Alice and bob are not two standards of truth, they both merely claim; one book, and the claimant is a column. THE REFUTATION WAS IN THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN DOC, in the litmus checklist of docs/choosing-perspectives.md, last item: `Would you ever need two X on one literal? Then at least one of them is not a perspective.` My set proposal is exactly the forbidden thing, offered as elegance, by someone who had cited that file twice in the same conversation. THE RULE WORTH CARRYING, and it is the sharp form of the checklist's FIRST item rather than a new principle: NEED TO REASON INSIDE SOMEONE'S VIEW - derive consequences, keep incompatible conclusions from exploding - AND IT IS A BOOK. NEED ONLY TO RECORD AND COMPARE WHAT WAS SAID - AND THE SPEAKER IS AN ARGUMENT. examples/goof is the first: nine mathematicians, nine ledgers, because nine geometries derive incompatible theorems from incompatible axioms and the point is that it does not explode. Alice and bob calling each other names is the second: `claimed[claims](alice, ...)` and `claimed[claims](bob, ...)` do not contradict, they are different facts about different subjects, so there is nothing to keep apart. AND THE BOUNDARY MUST BE STATED OR THE RULE DOES NOT TRANSFER, because his answer passes close to the doc's OWN anti-pattern `mega-main` - everything in [main] with a claimed_by column, losing forged, leak and paraconsistency. What separates them: mega-main is bad when the column holds the WRITERS, since then the boundary that authority and leak exist to police has been dissolved into data. Here the writer is the registry that keeps [claims], one nameable principal, and `forged[audit]` still guards it exactly as before. NOTHING WAS LOST because there was never a boundary between alice-as-writer and bob-as-writer to lose. COMPOSITION OF PERSPECTIVES NEVER ARISES, and that is the part I would have got wrong for another hour: the two cases are different SITUATIONS, not two halves of one compound context.").

finding(f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming, question).
recorded(f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming, decision).
finding_note(f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming,
  "THE RESIDUE OF f_a_book_name_that_carries_its_owner_belongs_to_the_host, once its motivating example dissolved. That finding wanted a book name to carry its owner - `alice's tells` - and the owner turns out to be either a column or the book itself, so the name needs to carry nothing. WHAT SURVIVES IS A DIFFERENT PROBLEM WEARING THE SAME CLOTHES: two files LOADED TOGETHER both write [main], and mean different things by it. Measured over examples/: [main] appears in aka, ditto, drip, goof, heck, iffy, jopa, loot and more; [record] is in both iffy/statute.rofl and jopa/facts.rofl; [audit] is everywhere. No collision has ever bitten because no two of those programs are loaded into one store - so the need is ANTICIPATED, NOT OBSERVED, and that should be said plainly before anyone builds for it. THE OWNER'S FRAME IS JS, and it is the right one with one correction. JS had exactly this: every script shared one global, `var config` in two files was last-one-wins, and the fix was not to make names structured but to make the DEFAULT SCOPE LOCAL and sharing explicit - IIFE, then CommonJS, then ES modules - while `globalThis` stayed reachable, nameable, and bad form. ROFL ALREADY HAS THE IMPORT HALF: `imports(P, Q)` must be declared to read another book, and f_export_precedes_import_so_the_check_belongs_at_load argues the permission half plus refusal at load. WHAT IT LACKS IS THE FILE-LOCAL DEFAULT: every file writes into whatever book it names, and [main] is the global. AND THE CORRECTION TO THE ANALOGY IS THE INTERESTING PART - THE DAMAGE IS NOT THE SAME. JS loses data, because assignment overwrites. Datalog ACCUMULATES, so two files asserting into one relation is a union, which is usually what you want. The failure mode is therefore not corruption but a SILENTLY WIDENED NEGATION: file A's `not p(X)` now ranges over file B's facts, and nothing says so. That is a much sharper statement of the risk than `flat namespace`, and it names what a fix would have to prevent. THE MECHANISM IS ALREADY PRESENT AND ALREADY USED ONCE: `perspExplicit` records whether the bracket was written, and src/reflect.ts:680 already sends an unbracketed KERNEL relation to [$kernel] instead of [main]. A file-local default is a SECOND use of that flag, not a new mechanism. What it needs decided is what the local book is CALLED - minted from the file name by the host, or anonymous - which is the one genuinely open question here. AND THE SHAPE OF THE FIX IS DECIDED BY THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN CRITERION, applied after the JS parallel was drawn: forbid where there is no legitimate exception, report where there is; a refusal works when the violation is LOCAL, an audit is needed when it is a property of the WHOLE PROGRAM. Two files sharing a book has legitimate exceptions and most of the time the union is what was wanted, and the damage is invisible in either file on its own. SO IT IS AN AUDIT ROW, NOT A SCOPE AND NOT A REFUSAL - something of the shape `relation R is negated in book B and written by more than one loader` - and `leak[audit]` is already an instrument of exactly that class. The file-local default stays available as the second, stronger move if the audit turns out to be red constantly rather than rarely; JS took both, module scope for the default and `use strict` to separate the accidental global from the deliberate one, and `perspExplicit` is this language's copy of that distinction, already recorded and already used once.").
dismissed(f_a_book_name_that_carries_its_owner_belongs_to_the_host, premise_dissolved_owner_is_a_column_or_the_book).

finding(f_the_join_width_is_the_only_quantity_that_can_explode, insight).
recorded(f_the_join_width_is_the_only_quantity_that_can_explode, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_join_width_is_the_only_quantity_that_can_explode, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_join_width_is_the_only_quantity_that_can_explode, "scanners/eval_cost.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_join_width_is_the_only_quantity_that_can_explode, "rules/eval-cost.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_join_width_is_the_only_quantity_that_can_explode,
  "VADIM STOPPED ME MID-OPTIMISATION AND ASKED FOR A MODEL OF THE COMPUTATION THAT ACCOUNTS FOR CARDINALITY, and the model found in one run what an hour of profiling had not. THE MODEL. A rule body is a left-to-right nested loop join: `solveBody` carries an accumulator of partial solutions and at each position calls `matchPremise` ONCE PER ACCUMULATED SOLUTION, replacing the accumulator with everything those calls returned. So per rule and position, width(I) is how many partial solutions arrived, yield(I) how many left, and width(I+1) = yield(I). The work of a firing is the SUM of the widths and the peak is what the space budget watches - and the width is the ONLY quantity here that can explode, because it is a PRODUCT of the fan-outs before it. A fan-out above one at an early position multiplies every position after it; the same premise last is paid once. That is the whole of cardinality explosion and it is a property of body ORDER. MEASURED WITHOUT INSTRUMENTING THE KERNEL: scanners/eval_cost.ts wraps four `Evaluation` methods on the prototype for one run and restores them, so src/ carries no counter and no flag. Counting `matchPremise` calls whose literal is `plan[i].lit` RECOVERS the accumulator width at position i without reaching inside `solveBody`, because the call is made once per accumulated solution - the identity of the literal object is the join key, and `===` is exact where a relation name would not be, since one relation can stand at two positions of one body. WHAT IT SAYS ABOUT RING 1, one 34-byte clause, steady state: 140 rules fired, 9341 accumulator elements, and 51 PER CENT OF THEM IN 77 RULES THAT CONCLUDE NOTHING. Re-derivation, which I had assumed was the waste, is ONE PER CENT - so the model's third branch correctly stopped deriving when the numbers arrived. AND WHICH EDGES ARE WORTH MEASURING IS ITSELF DERIVED, which is the part that keeps the next session honest: `share(R, P)` against the total width, `hot` at five per cent, `noise` below one, and 83 of 140 rules fall under the floor - they cannot be the reason for anything and a profiler pointed at them measures nothing.").

finding(f_i_measured_the_world_build_and_called_it_the_parse, defect).
recorded(f_i_measured_the_world_build_and_called_it_the_parse, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_i_measured_the_world_build_and_called_it_the_parse, doc).
addressed_by(f_i_measured_the_world_build_and_called_it_the_parse, "scanners/eval_cost.ts").
finding_note(f_i_measured_the_world_build_and_called_it_the_parse,
  "CAUGHT WITHIN FIVE MINUTES AND IT WOULD HAVE BEEN A HEADLINE. The first version of scanners/eval_cost.ts ran `parse(src, fromImage(image()))` inside the measured window, so JavaScript evaluated the argument there too and the run included building the world - charclass.rofl plus ring1.rofl, 161 rules - and with it the kernel's ONE-TIME safety check of the grammar. Read off that run, SEVEN OF THE EIGHT HEAVIEST RULES WERE safety.rofl: `ground_upto`, `bound_before` twice, `demand_rel` twice, `stratum_cone`, `blocked_head`, together 34 per cent of all join work, and I was one paragraph from reporting that a third of every parse is the kernel checking itself and that the two-stage bootstrap costs far more than the 2.7 per cent recorded for it. IT WAS AN ARTEFACT OF MEASURING THE BUILD. `safetyMemo` is module-level and keyed by a hash of the rule ids, the rule set does not change when a fact is loaded, so the check runs ONCE PER PROCESS and the recorded 2.7 per cent stands. With the world built and warmed before the wrappers go on: total width 21258 -> 9341, safety.rofl gone from the table entirely, and the top of the list is the grammar's own chart. THE TRAP IS THE ONE THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY NAMES - a task that does the thing it is measuring the absence of measures nothing, recorded when a coverage task built its own snapshot in the same process and so parsed before it restored. Same shape, different door: there the setup was written into the task, here it rode in as a DEFAULT ARGUMENT, which is the same statement wearing punctuation.").

finding(f_half_a_ring1_parse_concludes_nothing_and_one_rule_was_a_fifth_of_it, decision).
recorded(f_half_a_ring1_parse_concludes_nothing_and_one_rule_was_a_fifth_of_it, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_half_a_ring1_parse_concludes_nothing_and_one_rule_was_a_fifth_of_it, doc).
addressed_by(f_half_a_ring1_parse_concludes_nothing_and_one_rule_was_a_fifth_of_it, "examples/ring1/ring1.rofl").
finding_note(f_half_a_ring1_parse_concludes_nothing_and_one_rule_was_a_fifth_of_it,
  "THE MODEL NAMED A RULE AND THE REWRITE PAID 19 PER CENT. `dollar_before(I) :- at(I), 0 < I, J is I - 1, kind(J, dollar), code_at(J).` starts at EVERY position of the source and asks each whether a `$` stands behind it - 101 firings, 1881 accumulator elements, 20 per cent of the parse's whole join work, and ZERO conclusions, because most clauses hold no `$` at all. Written from the rare thing forward - `kind(J, dollar), code_at(J), I is J + 1, at(I)` - it costs one element per `$`: 1881 -> 101, and total width 9341 -> 7561. THE 34-BYTE CLAUSE HID THE WIN AND THAT IS THE LESSON ABOUT THE BENCHMARK RATHER THAN THE CODE. On it the change measured ZERO, because the fixed costs swamp the join: split by stage, one clause is 6.60 ms restoring the image (23%), 18.72 ms in `load` where the fixpoint runs (66%), 3.07 ms asking `hole(R, Reason)` (11%) and 0.02 ms in the explicit `evaluate`, which has nothing left to do. On an 88-byte clause, three interleaved arms: 89.5 ms against 72.8, NINETEEN PER CENT, arms never overlapping. A change that removes a fifth of the work and shows nothing is not a small change, it is a benchmark whose fixed costs are bigger than its variable ones. `planBody` CANNOT DO THIS, and the reason is worth keeping: the arithmetic pins the order, since `J is I - 1` needs I bound, so `at(I)` had to lead. Only INVERTING it to `I is J + 1` frees the rare premise to go first, and that is a rewrite of the rule rather than a permutation of its body - which is why the corpus measurement of `planBody` reordering zero bodies is not the scandal it looks like. AND THE PREVIOUS WHY-CHAIN IS STALE: rules/parse-cost.rofl derives the slowness from `relAll`, measured when the reuse cache was on and fingerprinting whole relations. With the cache off, the evaluator examines 1.4 CANDIDATE FACTS PER PREMISE MATCH - counted, 8938 calls and 12071 candidates - so there is no scan to remove and the index is doing its job. The cost is per-match machinery over many matches, not per-match search.").

finding(f_the_barren_rules_were_a_long_tail_and_the_fix_was_in_a_fertile_one, insight).
recorded(f_the_barren_rules_were_a_long_tail_and_the_fix_was_in_a_fertile_one, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_barren_rules_were_a_long_tail_and_the_fix_was_in_a_fertile_one, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_barren_rules_were_a_long_tail_and_the_fix_was_in_a_fertile_one, "examples/ring1/ring1.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_barren_rules_were_a_long_tail_and_the_fix_was_in_a_fertile_one,
  "WENT LOOKING FOR THE OTHER 76 BARREN RULES AND FOUND THAT BARREN IS THE WRONG SORT KEY. After `dollar_before`, 77 rules still conclude nothing and still hold 40 per cent of all accumulator width on one ring 1 clause - but the heaviest of them is 1.8 per cent and 83 of 140 rules are under 1 per cent each, so there is no second `dollar_before` in that list. THE REASON IS ARITHMETIC ABOUT THE MODEL RATHER THAN ABOUT THE GRAMMAR: `width(R, 0)` EQUALS `fires(R)`, always, because `solveBody` enters position 0 with a single empty solution and calls `matchPremise` once. Summed over the run, position 0 is 3799 of 7561 elements - it IS the firing count, and no permutation of any body can touch it. Only `width(R, I)` for I above zero is reducible by order, and across the 77 barren rules that is 663 elements, 8.8 per cent of the total. So the barren ranking overstates its own prize by a factor of four and a half. RANKING BY REDUCIBLE WIDTH INSTEAD PUTS A RULE THAT CONCLUDES AT THE TOP: `prevword(I) :- at(I), 0 < I, J is I - 1, wordch(J).` - 549 elements, 500 of them at the last premise, for 15 conclusions. It is `dollar_before`'s defect exactly, in the rule that sits two lines above the comment recording it, and it was invisible to `barren` because it is FERTILE. Inverting `J is I - 1` to `I is J + 1` costs 94 elements for the same 15 conclusions - 6.0 per cent of the parse's entire join work in one line. THE LESSON FOR rules/eval-cost.rofl: `wasted(R, N) :- barren(R), width(R, _, N)` counts the position-0 element that every firing must pay, and `worth_measuring(R) :- barren(R), wasted(R, N), N > 100` therefore admits rules where nothing can be done. The quantity that deserves the name is the width at positions above zero, whether or not the rule concludes.").

finding(f_the_relation_the_round_delta_carries_should_lead_not_the_rarest_one, insight).
recorded(f_the_relation_the_round_delta_carries_should_lead_not_the_rarest_one, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_relation_the_round_delta_carries_should_lead_not_the_rarest_one, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_relation_the_round_delta_carries_should_lead_not_the_rarest_one, "examples/ring1/ring1.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_relation_the_round_delta_carries_should_lead_not_the_rarest_one, "src/engine.ts").
finding_note(f_the_relation_the_round_delta_carries_should_lead_not_the_rarest_one,
  "RAREST PREMISE FIRST IS NOT THE RULE, AND THE COUNTEREXAMPLE AND THE EXAMPLE ARE TWENTY LINES APART IN ONE FILE. `wordch(I) :- kind(I, lower), code_at(I).` leads with the rarer relation - nine lowercase letters against thirty-four code positions - and costs 377 accumulator elements. Swapped to `code_at(I), kind(I, lower)` it costs 135. The same swap applied to `punct(I, comma) :- kind(I, comma), code_at(I).` and its six siblings costs 95 elements MORE, and the two commas are rarer still. THE DIFFERENCE IS SEMI-NAIVE EVALUATION, NOT CARDINALITY. `st` is a left-to-right walk that yields ONE fact a round, so `code_at` arrives as a delta of one on every round, and a delta narrows only the position its relation stands at. With `kind` leading, the rule rescans all nine letters each round and probes `code_at` nine times; with `code_at` leading it starts from the single new position. For `punct` the leading scan is two facts either way, so the delta buys nothing and the thirty-four-element scan it introduces is pure loss. SO THE HEURISTIC IS: LEAD WITH THE RELATION THE ROUND'S DELTA ACTUALLY CARRIES, provided it is not much larger than the alternative - and `rarest first` is a static-database rule that a fixpoint does not obey. MEASURED, not reasoned: I predicted the punct swap would win by the same argument that won for wordch, ran it, and it lost by 95. Every reorder in this session was checked against `npm run evalcost` one at a time before being kept, and six of fourteen candidates were reverted for making the model worse - `punct` x7, `neck`, `spanto`, `nexttok`, `lit0`, `prim` - which is a useful base rate for how well this can be reasoned about without running it.").

finding(f_one_body_order_per_semi_naive_version_and_the_delta_only_leads_when_it_is_smaller, insight).
recorded(f_one_body_order_per_semi_naive_version_and_the_delta_only_leads_when_it_is_smaller, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_one_body_order_per_semi_naive_version_and_the_delta_only_leads_when_it_is_smaller, doc).
addressed_by(f_one_body_order_per_semi_naive_version_and_the_delta_only_leads_when_it_is_smaller, "src/engine.ts").
addressed_by(f_one_body_order_per_semi_naive_version_and_the_delta_only_leads_when_it_is_smaller, "test/version-plan.test.ts").
finding_note(f_one_body_order_per_semi_naive_version_and_the_delta_only_leads_when_it_is_smaller,
  "ONE ORDER PER RULE WAS BEING ASKED TO SERVE EVERY VERSION OF IT, AND FIVE RULES WERE PAYING. `propagate` re-fires a rule once per body position whose relation the front carries - the standard semi-naive delta expansion - and every one of those firings is a DIFFERENT join: one position reads a delta and the rest read whole relations. `planBody(r.clause)` was asked ONCE per rule in `prepare` and every version reused that array; only the window moved. Souffle thinks the distinction important enough to put in surface syntax (`.plan 1: (2,1)` schedules version 1 alone) and its profiled scheduler reports a geometric mean 12.07x over untuned orders on DOOP, DDISASM and VPC (LOPSTR 2022, figure read off souffle-lang.github.io/lopstr22.html, not the PDF). PER-VERSION MEASUREMENT FIRST, and it says the gap is real: on one ring 1 clause 63 of 140 fired rules have more than one live delta version and those rules hold 5022 of 6385 accumulator elements. `punct(I, K) :- kind(I, K), code_at(I).` splits into 166 elements for the version whose delta arrives on `code_at` (the whole `kind` bucket rescanned each round) against 37 for the version whose delta arrives on `kind` - one order is right for one of them and wrong for the other, which is exactly the shape a shared plan cannot express. WHAT LANDED: `planVersions` in src/engine.ts computes, per positive body position, the order with that literal first; `fireRule` selects it and `pickVersion` decides per firing whether to use it. Total width 6385 -> 5952 (6.8%) with the SOURCE UNCHANGED - `spanto` 185 -> 79, `punct` 204 -> 105, `nexttok` 169 -> 76 - and no rule got worse. Interleaved paired timing on a 100-byte ring 1 clause, 24 pairs ABABAB in one process under load average 6: B beats A in 24 of 24, ratio 0.810 to 0.979, median 0.927. On a 34-byte clause 18 of 24 (median 0.882) and on a 66-byte one 10 of 24 (median 1.041) - the small benchmark's fixed costs still swamp its join, which is the trap already recorded here. THE HEURISTIC THAT DID NOT WORK IS THE RESULT WORTH KEEPING. `lead with the delta` unconditionally makes the model WORSE: 6385 -> 8485, +33 per cent. It takes 106, 98 and 93 elements off spanto, punct and nexttok and puts 401 back on `args`, 260 on `lit0` and 138 on `prim`. Two guards separate the lists and neither is a safety condition. (1) CONNECTIVITY, static: a five-literal chain whose last literal shares no variable with its first becomes a cartesian product when that last literal leads. (2) CARDINALITY, dynamic: leading with the delta pays only when the delta is smaller than what the shared plan's first position scans, and neither number exists until the fixpoint is running - which is why the decision is made per firing and why Souffle gets the same numbers by profiling. With both, every rule that moves, moves down.").

finding(f_moving_a_literal_earlier_cannot_unbind_anything_so_the_safety_half_refused_nothing, insight).
recorded(f_moving_a_literal_earlier_cannot_unbind_anything_so_the_safety_half_refused_nothing, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_moving_a_literal_earlier_cannot_unbind_anything_so_the_safety_half_refused_nothing, doc).
addressed_by(f_moving_a_literal_earlier_cannot_unbind_anything_so_the_safety_half_refused_nothing, "test/version-plan.test.ts").
finding_note(f_moving_a_literal_earlier_cannot_unbind_anything_so_the_safety_half_refused_nothing,
  "THE SAFETY ANALYSIS REFUSED ZERO OF 1927 CANDIDATE VERSION ORDERS, AND THE REASON IS MONOTONICITY RATHER THAN LUCK. `planBody` is not only a cost heuristic - it decides which elements can run when variables are bound, and `stuck` names one that never can. A per-version order has to satisfy the same analysis, so the worry going in was that forcing the delta first would strand a builtin or a negation. It cannot: pulling ONE literal to the front and keeping everything else in relative order can only bind more variables sooner, so a version plan inherits the shared plan's readiness. Measured over the corpus - 77 .rofl files, 8499 clauses, 2072 rules with a body, 1927 candidate versions - the split is 1290 accepted and 637 refused, and ALL 637 are the cost guard (a delta that would lead a cartesian jump). Stuck negations: 0. Head left unbound: 0. Builtin unready where it lands: 0. WHAT DOES GET REFUSED FOR SAFETY IS THE WHOLE RULE, decided on its SHARED plan: a body that is not run-ready as written gets no version plans at all, because which holes it raises and which solutions it silently drops are properties of the order its author chose. That is examples/yak fragment 09, `risky(X, Y) :- Y = pair(X, Z), who(X), tag(X, Z).`, and the corpus scan above reports ZERO of that class - because the pair lives in a TypeScript string, the same hole the recorded corpus measurement of `planBody` had. It is a fixture in test/version-plan.test.ts instead. AND THE MODE CHECK IS NOT `planBody`'S: `planBody` deliberately leaves a builtin where it was written, so a reordering needs a stricter test than it applies - `orderReady`, which requires `is` to have its right side ground and a comparison both sides, because ARITH_UNBOUND is the one failure `evalBuiltin` swallows and a comparison reached unbound returns null with no hole. A moved builtin would therefore lose rows in silence.").

finding(f_a_lost_semi_naive_window_is_invisible_to_both_oracles_this_repository_owns, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_lost_semi_naive_window_is_invisible_to_both_oracles_this_repository_owns, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_lost_semi_naive_window_is_invisible_to_both_oracles_this_repository_owns, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_lost_semi_naive_window_is_invisible_to_both_oracles_this_repository_owns, "test/version-plan.test.ts").
finding_note(f_a_lost_semi_naive_window_is_invisible_to_both_oracles_this_repository_owns,
  "`frontAt.pos` NAMES A POSITION OF A PARTICULAR PLAN, AND GETTING IT WRONG FAILS TWO WAYS OF WHICH ONLY ONE IS VISIBLE. Once a version has its own body order, the number `fireRuleFront` computes (a position of the SHARED plan) is not the number `solveBody` needs (the delta literal's index in the VERSION's plan). Planted one at a time in test/version-plan.test.ts. NAMING THE WRONG LITERAL restricts a non-delta position to a front of keys from another relation, matches nothing, and DROPS FACTS - 78 rows become 12 - but only on a fixture where that version is the sole route to a new fact; the first fixture tried had the recursion on the first premise, the two versions covered for each other and the mutant came back green. NAMING NO LITERAL (a position past the end) degrades every version into a full scan: the answers are identical, the store is identical - same facts, same support counts, same canonical witnesses - and the ONLY instrument that reports it is an accumulator counter living in a test wrapper. That is a performance defect with no gate in this repository that can see it, which is the recorded class `an opt-in feature is invisible to every check here BY CONSTRUCTION`, arrived at from the other direction. AND THE SAME MUTANT ON A DIFFERENT FIXTURE DOES MOVE PROVENANCE, which is the datum worth carrying: with the recursion through the FIRST premise and a twelve-link chain, a full scan reaches a derivation in an EARLIER round than the delta would and `Store.support` keeps the FIRST firing as the canonical witness, so `canonicalState` differs while every fact stands. Swept at 4, 6, 8, 12 and 20 links: it moves at 12 and 20 and not below, so a small fixture would have reported `provenance is stable` and meant `too small to tell`. THE REAL CHANGE MOVES NOTHING, and that is the point of the permutation: `solveBody` returns one premise reference per body element in the order it solved them and `conclude` builds the firing signature out of that list, so two versions solving one derivation in two orders would sign it twice and grow a second support out of nothing. `perm` puts the list back into the shared order before `conclude` sees it. Checked at 4, 6, 8, 12 and 20 links on two program shapes and over a ring 1 parse of three different clauses: canonicalState and the full snapshot, witnesses and firings included, are byte-identical with per-version plans on and off. Dropping the permutation is the third mutant and canonicalState catches it.").

finding(f_the_rounds_delta_was_one_key_set_so_leading_with_it_walked_the_whole_derived_layer, defect).
recorded(f_the_rounds_delta_was_one_key_set_so_leading_with_it_walked_the_whole_derived_layer, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_rounds_delta_was_one_key_set_so_leading_with_it_walked_the_whole_derived_layer, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_rounds_delta_was_one_key_set_so_leading_with_it_walked_the_whole_derived_layer, "src/engine.ts").
finding_note(f_the_rounds_delta_was_one_key_set_so_leading_with_it_walked_the_whole_derived_layer,
  "THE FRONT WAS RELATION-BLIND, AND THAT IS WHY LEADING WITH A DELTA COULD NOT PAY. `FrontInfo` carried `keys`, every fact key derived last round across every rule, plus a set of relation names. `matchPremise` given that window and a literal with no ground argument cannot use an index, so it took the `[...only].sort()` branch and walked the WHOLE front to find the handful of facts of one relation, filtering the rest away. A delta at position 0 - which is what a per-version plan is for - has no ground argument by construction, so the optimisation and the representation were working against each other: Souffle can schedule a delta first because it has a per-relation `@delta_R`; this front was one bag. SPLITTING IT COSTS ONE MAP LOOKUP PER CONCLUDED FACT AND CHANGES NO ANSWER - the walking branch already filtered by `f.rel === lit.rel` and the index branch already probed one relation - and it is worth measuring on its own, because it is a plain win independent of anything else here. Interleaved paired A/B, 24 pairs ABABAB in one process on a 100-byte ring 1 clause, per-version plans OFF in both arms, load average 6: the per-relation front beats the global one in 23 of 24 pairs, ratio 0.742 to 1.143, median 0.923. JOIN WIDTH DOES NOT SEE THIS AT ALL - it is the same number of `matchPremise` calls either way, only cheaper ones - which is a caveat on the instrument the rest of this session's numbers come from: width counts calls, not what a call walks.").
-- Recorded 2026-09-07 while answering a question the owner asked about
-- parallelism: whether the additions ROFL makes on top of Datalog give a
-- decomposition that plain Datalog lacks. Measurement first, both times.

finding(f_the_ledger_partition_is_widest_where_the_program_is_smallest, insight).
recorded(f_the_ledger_partition_is_widest_where_the_program_is_smallest, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_ledger_partition_is_widest_where_the_program_is_smallest, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_ledger_partition_is_widest_where_the_program_is_smallest, "scanners/ledger_census.ts").
finding_note(f_the_ledger_partition_is_widest_where_the_program_is_smallest,
  "THE HYPOTHESIS WAS GOOD AND THE CORPUS REFUSED IT, WHICH IS THE RESULT. A rule uniform in the ledger instantiates its perspective at both ends together, so the fixpoint over book A and the fixpoint over book B cannot interact unless a rule BRIDGES them - and a bridge is WRITTEN (`imports`, an explicit bracket) rather than discovered in the data, which is a partition of the evaluation the program DECLARES and which no conventional Datalog can state. The store is already laid out along it: idx is Map<rel, Map<persp, KeyRun>> in src/store.ts, so the physical layout already matches the proposed partition. So the question was empirical, and scanners/ledger_census.ts counts it over 2070 rules in 42 programs. STRUCTURE: 79.9 per cent of rules live in exactly one book (62.7 default [main], 15.9 one named book, 1.3 uniform in a book VARIABLE), 17.4 per cent bridge two named books, 2.7 mix a variable with an atom. The bridge graph is tiny and static - fifteen shapes over the whole corpus, led by audit+main 80, main+repo 33, mind+world 28, draft+main 27. AND THE REFUTATION: THE BIG PROGRAMS ARE SINGLE-BOOK. examples/ring1 244 rules, examples/wtf 191, examples/slop 154, examples/spat 131 - all one book, 100 per cent in-book, ZERO bridges. The width is 12 in sus (78 rules), 10 in goof (28 rules), 7 in aka (72). So the partition is widest exactly where the program is smallest, and on the ring 1 parse - the workload that costs 28 ms a clause and is the whole live optimisation thread - it is one, which is no parallelism at all. Two programs (sus, goof) also have a CYCLIC bridge graph, so even where the partition is wide it is not a clean DAG and mutually recursive books are one scheduling unit. WHAT THIS SETTLES: the ledger is a MODELLING device, not a performance one - authors reach for a second book when incompatible theories would otherwise collide (goof's nine geometries), and that motive is uncorrelated with where the time goes. A parallel schedule derived from the book partition would be correct, deterministic and worth almost nothing on this corpus. WHAT IT DOES NOT SETTLE, said because the scanner cannot see it: this reads the SOURCE, so it measures what a program COULD be split into and never what an evaluation SPENT, and a book with two rules counts the same as a book with two hundred. If the partition is ever revisited, the honest instrument is a width-weighted census over scanners/eval_cost.ts rows, not this one.").

finding(f_the_parallel_grain_is_coarser_than_the_join, insight).
recorded(f_the_parallel_grain_is_coarser_than_the_join, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_parallel_grain_is_coarser_than_the_join, decision).
finding_note(f_the_parallel_grain_is_coarser_than_the_join,
  "A CLAIM OF MINE WITHDRAWN BY MEASUREMENT, AND THE WITHDRAWAL IS THE FINDING. Asked whether a Rust port is worth it for the cores, I argued that parallelism needs the tier 3 columnar representation first, because a JS worker cannot share a Map<string, FactRec> and the store is a graph of objects with string keys. THAT IS TRUE ONLY FOR FINE-GRAINED PARALLELISM INSIDE ONE FIXPOINT. Coarse-grained parallelism shares nothing: a worker that owns its whole world needs no shared store, no interned ids and no columnar layout. So the representation argument does not reach the cases that matter, and `parallelism is a reason to port` is withdrawn - the memory gap remains the only standing reason, and it is about a scale tier this engine does not yet reach. WHERE THE GRAIN ACTUALLY IS, four places, none of them the join. (1) FORKS. IFFY and DITTO search a thousand branches; each branch is an independent world with no shared mutable state, and docs/performance-invariants.md puts a realistic 100k-fact fork at 2.2 s, so a thousand-fork search is half an hour sequential. Available in JS today through worker_threads with ZERO risk to the determinism contract, because order within a world never changes and the canonical witness never moves. This is the highest-yield parallelism in the system and it is not in the engine. (2) BATCH, and this is where the ML analogy actually lands - minibatching, not matrix multiplication. parseFile pays `restore` PER CLAUSE, 23 per cent of a parse, which is a fixed per-instance setup cost paid N times over a batch of structurally identical problems (same rules, different data). (3) THE SEMIRING FOLD is the one component where the GPU analogy is an identity rather than a likeness: src/semiring.ts runs AFTER the Boolean fixpoint and by construction does not touch it, and Kleene iteration over a fixed support hypergraph is repeated sparse matrix-vector multiplication over a semiring, which is GraphBLAS's definition. Isolated by design, so no determinism contract is at risk. (4) HOLES MAKE SPECULATION LEGAL. A budget exhausted emits a `hole` FACT, so a partial fixpoint is a legal, auditable answer here where in most engines it is not - which permits parallelism by RACING bounded sub-evaluations rather than by partitioning, with the record saying what was cut. WHAT IS STRUCTURAL AND WILL NOT MOVE: strata are sequential, and rounds within a stratum are sequential because round N+1 consumes round N's front. Only rules within one round of one stratum can fire concurrently, which is invariant I4 and is also where the field's own numbers collapse - Souffle reaches under 25 per cent CPU on long-tail workloads. So the good news is the grain: coarse parallelism survives synchronisation overhead where the field's fine-grained loop parallelism does not. AN OVERCLAIM I MADE AND AM RECORDING AS UNVERIFIED RATHER THAN DELETING: I said no other engine can audit its own schedule, on the argument that strata and cones are computed IN ROFL over the rule graph so a parallel schedule could be derived AND EXPLAINED with a why-tree. I have not checked this. Souffle ships `explain` with proof trees and a profiler that reports the chosen plan, and SQL has EXPLAIN, so `an engine exposes its schedule` is ordinary. The narrow claim that remains untested is whether any engine derives its schedule IN ITS OWN LANGUAGE and can produce a derivation for it. Nobody should repeat the sentence until that is checked.").
finding(f_the_hole_check_is_load_bearing_and_the_query_was_the_wrong_instrument, defect).
recorded(f_the_hole_check_is_load_bearing_and_the_query_was_the_wrong_instrument, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_hole_check_is_load_bearing_and_the_query_was_the_wrong_instrument, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_hole_check_is_load_bearing_and_the_query_was_the_wrong_instrument, "examples/ring1/demo.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_hole_check_is_load_bearing_and_the_query_was_the_wrong_instrument, "test/example-ring1.test.ts").
finding_note(f_the_hole_check_is_load_bearing_and_the_query_was_the_wrong_instrument,
  "THE STAGE SPLIT SAID ONE QUERY OVER AN EMPTY RELATION COSTS 11 PER CENT OF A PARSE, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT THE QUERY NEVER TOUCHED THE RELATION. Two questions were open: is `hole(R, Reason)` doing demand work it need not, and is the check redundant beside `r.evaluate()` returning `partial`. NEITHER. Measured by wrapping `Evaluation.prototype.prepare`, `classify`, `safetyAnswer` and `matchPremise` for one run and restoring them - the scanners/eval_cost.ts pattern, so src/ carries no counter - over nine `query` calls on a finished ring 1 clause: 5.64 ms of 5.81 is `prepare`, NINETY-SEVEN PER CENT, of which 2.30 ms is `classify` re-running `planBody` over 140 bodies and the rest is `decodeRules` reading the reflection back out of the store. `safetyAnswer` is 0.07 (the memo hits). THE MATCH ITSELF IS 0.00 ms. So it is not demand unfolding and it is not a scan: `Rofl.query` builds a FRESH `Evaluation` for every call, and that constructor decodes and plans the whole program before it looks at a single fact. An empty relation is the WORST case for that shape, because the fixed cost is the entire cost. AND THE CHECK IS LOAD-BEARING, established with a positive control rather than by reading: `q(7).  p(X) :- q(Y), X is str_len(Y).` finishes its fixpoint, returns `partial: false`, derives no `p`, and leaves `hole($rule(...), str_type_error)` standing. An `is` that cannot be evaluated is an INABILITY, not a wall - src/engine.ts:1627 calls `arithHole` and carries on, while only the budget and space walls set `partial` - so a front end reading `partial` alone reports an empty parse for a grammar rule that silently failed. THE CHECK STAYS AND THE INSTRUMENT CHANGES: `r.store.relAll(V.hole)` is the same answer without an evaluator. Arms interleaved ABAB in one process over a finished clause, twelve pairs, order flipped each round: query 0.676 ms median, relAll 0.002 - and end to end, three process-level ABABAB rounds on one clause, 10.32 / 10.65 / 10.21 ms against 9.70 / 9.56 / 9.27. THE GENERAL SHAPE, and it is the same one this repository recorded when the demo read its results with `JSON.parse(r.save())` instead of `relAll`: ASKING THE KERNEL A QUESTION COSTS AN EVALUATOR, READING A RELATION DOES NOT, and a host that already knows the fixpoint has run wants the second. The kernel-side fix - caching the prepared `Evaluation` across queries while the store is clean - would pay everywhere rather than at one call site, and is NOT taken here: `Evaluation` carries `steps`, `rows`, `peakRows`, `budget` and `diags` as live per-call state, so reuse needs a reset whose failure mode is a budget silently accumulating across queries. That is a kernel decision, not a parse fix. AND THE CHECK WAS UNGUARDED, which is the answer to `where can this gate not look`, asked of my own change: DELETING THE HOLE CHECK FROM `parse` ENTIRELY AND RUNNING THE FULL NODE SUITE MOVES NOTHING - 1011 / 1001 / 7 / 3 either way, the branch baseline exactly. The test that looks like the guard, `an unfinished evaluation is not a parse`, never calls `parse`, and its assertion is a DISJUNCTION that `partial` alone satisfies, so it is asleep for this door. A gate is now planted in test/example-ring1.test.ts: it plants `holed(N) :- at(I), N is str_len(I).` into the ring 1 world, ASSERTS THE STATE ITSELF before the refusal that depends on it - `partial` false AND `hole` non-empty - and requires `parse` to refuse. Mutant set of two, both killed: `holes` always empty, and the `|| holes.length` disjunct dropped; the old test slept through both.").

finding(f_an_image_is_a_constant_so_restore_it_once_and_fork_it, defect).
recorded(f_an_image_is_a_constant_so_restore_it_once_and_fork_it, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_an_image_is_a_constant_so_restore_it_once_and_fork_it, doc).
addressed_by(f_an_image_is_a_constant_so_restore_it_once_and_fork_it, "src/api.ts").
addressed_by(f_an_image_is_a_constant_so_restore_it_once_and_fork_it, "examples/ring1/demo.ts").
finding_note(f_an_image_is_a_constant_so_restore_it_once_and_fork_it,
  "THE 23 PER CENT `restore` STAGE WAS THE SAME WORLD BEING REBUILT FROM TEXT ONCE PER CLAUSE. `parseFile` called `fromImage(img)` inside its loop, and `img` is a constant for the whole file, so every clause re-ran `JSON.parse` over 545103 bytes and re-`add`ed 2453 facts to arrive at a world identical to the last one. PROFILED FIRST: `Rofl.fromSnapshot` on the ring 1 image is 3.09 ms, of which `JSON.parse` ALONE is 1.71 - fifty-six per cent of the stage is the text, before a fact exists - and the other 1.33 is `add` per fact. `store.clone()` of the same world is 0.20 ms. FIFTEEN TIMES, and the reason is that the clone shares the `Term` objects and never rebuilds a key. AND THE FIGURE IS ABOUT THIS STORE, WHICH IS THE CORRECTION docs/performance-invariants.md ALREADY PAID FOR: it records 21.7-22.5 us/fact to clone a store carrying rules, derived facts and provenance against 5.6-7.2 for a bare one, and this image measures 0.08 us/fact - because it is 2453 BASE facts with ZERO witnesses and ZERO firings, which is the cheap end BY CONSTRUCTION. The number is not a claim about `clone`; it is a claim about forking an image, and an image is base facts by definition. (The doc's figure also predates 2026-09-07, when `clone` stopped sorting the key set.) THE EQUIVALENCE IS CHECKED ON BOTH ORACLES THIS REPOSITORY OWNS, not argued: a fork of the restored image and a second restore of the same text agree byte for byte on `canonicalState` AND on `snapshot`, and `allFactKeys` agrees element for element - which is the arrival order the fork was taught to preserve earlier the same day, and the reason the two coincide at all is that the template ITSELF came from `restore`, so its arrival order IS the snapshot's sorted key order. Isolation checked separately: a write to the fork leaves the template's fact count unmoved. MEASURED, arms interleaved in ONE process, eight clauses per arm, three runs of eight pairs with the order flipped each round: TWENTY-FOUR PAIRED SAMPLES AND B BEATS A IN EVERY ONE, ratios 0.55 to 0.86, median about 0.71 - so 25 to 37 per cent off a whole clause. THE SAVING IS BIGGER THAN THE STAGE, and that is the part worth keeping: `restore` was 23 per cent, but the allocation it did - a 545 KiB string parsed, 2453 fresh term objects, a key rebuilt per fact - was also feeding the collector, and `--cpu-prof` had already put GC at 10.7 per cent of an empty parse. Counted directly, one clause of `parseFile` passed 545103 bytes through `JSON.parse` and now passes the image once for the whole file. WHAT THIS DID NOT FIX: `f_fork_copies_the_whole_store` stays OPEN and is left open knowingly - a fork is still linear in the store, and its own numbers (2000 facts / 12 ms) are stale because they were taken when `clone` was `restore(snapshot())`. Copy-on-write is still the thing IFFY needs and is still not built. WHAT WAS ADDED TO THE KERNEL is one method, `Rofl.fork()`, which is the two lines `excise` had written by hand since it was first shipped; `excise` now calls it, and `parseFile` is the example that EXERCISES it, which is the condition this repository sets for a new capability. THE CHANGE IS COVERED AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, by asking what a fork can get wrong that a timing cannot see - a STALE TEMPLATE. Two mutants over `parseFile`, both killed by a test that already existed: parsing every clause into the SAME world with no fork at all, and forking the PREVIOUS clause's world instead of the template so state carries forward. Both turn `SELF-APPLICATION: L1 parses L2's own source, identically to the host` red, because contamination shows up as a clause that disagrees with the host parser. So the byte-identical oracles say the fork is the same world, and the self-application test says a fork that is NOT the same world is caught.").
addressed_by(f_a_third_of_a_parse_is_json, "examples/ring1/demo.ts").
-- Recorded 2026-09-07 by the ORDER CENSUS: twelve order-perturbing mutants,
-- each run against the full node suite and against scanners/order_census.ts,
-- which digests the fact set, the firing set, the canonical witness, the
-- least-signature witness and canonicalState separately so a red can be
-- attributed to the ANSWER or only to the RECORD of how it was reached.
-- Machine: 8-core Apple M4 Pro, node v24.13.0. Branch baseline 1011 / 1001 /
-- 7 fail / 3 skipped, attested with a tree fingerprint plus its control.

finding(f_the_schedule_moves_the_record_and_not_the_answer, insight).
recorded(f_the_schedule_moves_the_record_and_not_the_answer, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_schedule_moves_the_record_and_not_the_answer, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_schedule_moves_the_record_and_not_the_answer, "scanners/order_census.ts").
finding_note(f_the_schedule_moves_the_record_and_not_the_answer,
  "TWELVE ORDER MUTANTS, ONE SUITE EACH, AND A CENSUS THE SUITE CANNOT SEE. The technique is 3cff6f4's - break the property everywhere rather than read the call sites - extended from one mutant to a SET, because one mutant is liveness and a set is coverage. Perturbed, one at a time: allFacts globally reversed; the active rule list reversed inside propagate; the activate seed order and the active sort inverted; the seminaive front key order reversed; the matchPremise candidate order reversed BEFORE its sort, and separately the sort DISABLED and separately INVERTED; KeyRun arrivals reversed before absorb; relPersp/relAll answering in descending key order; the two phase-A waves swapped (VACUOUS - see f_a_mutant_that_does_not_mutate_proves_nothing); the negation levels run descending (POSITIVE CONTROL); and the staged @next order reversed. THE RESULT, and it is one sentence: on the primary rounds evaluator NO MUTANT MOVED A DERIVED FACT SET OR A FIRING SET. The digests for facts and firings are byte-identical under every mutant except the positive control, which moved spat from 11380 to 13426 facts and made ring 1 fail to parse at all - so the instrument can see a class-(a) break and did not see one. WHAT DID MOVE: the canonical witness and, through it, canonicalState, under exactly three mutants - the active order in propagate, the activate seed order, and an INVERTED matchPremise sort. That is provenance and nothing else: which of several equally true derivations got recorded. THREE MUTANTS WERE PURE CONTROLS AND ALL THREE WERE NO-OPS ON EVERY DIGEST - candidates reversed before the sort, the front key order, and KeyRun arrivals before absorb - which is the load-bearing comment at src/engine.ts:1533 measured rather than believed: the sort is total because `seen` admits one match per key, so candidate order is genuinely unobservable. DISABLING that sort was ALSO a no-op on every digest, so the sort is defensive rather than currently load-bearing; INVERTING it moved the witness on eight of nine worlds. AND THE COMBINATION SAYS WHAT THE SORT ACTUALLY PROTECTS: with the sort removed AND relPersp/relAll answering in DESCENDING key order - the case test/store-conformance.test.ts:195 builds by hand with a reversed-read adapter - the fact set and the firing set are still byte-identical on all nine worlds, the stock evaluator included, and only the witness and canonicalState move. So the sort guards the RECORD, never the ANSWER, and the thirteenth mutant is the one that separates them. THE ONE PLACE THE FACT SET DOES MOVE is the documented fallback: on `evaluator: strata` over examples/wtf, reversing the active order took 8080 facts to 8137 and 2286 firings to 2254. boot.rofl no longer derives stratum/2, so every negation rule there runs in one final pass in canonical rule order, which LIMITS.md already declares outside v0 guarantees; test/example-wtf.test.ts pins two atoms of exactly that wrongness. So the class-(a) region is the one the language already refuses to promise, and the primary path has no such corner because the peel assigns every relation a round from the rules alone.").

finding(f_the_canonical_witness_is_derivable_from_the_data, decision).
recorded(f_the_canonical_witness_is_derivable_from_the_data, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_canonical_witness_is_derivable_from_the_data, decision).
finding_note(f_the_canonical_witness_is_derivable_from_the_data,
  "THE QUESTION: `Store.support` (src/store.ts:602) keeps the FIRST firing to arrive as the canonical witness, which LIMITS.md states as a property of the schedule rather than of the answer. Would selecting the LEAST firing signature instead make the recorded provenance schedule-independent? MEASURED, and the answer is yes, three ways. FIRST, the premise: `firings` accumulates every signature and the census digests that set separately - it is IDENTICAL under every order mutant on the primary evaluator, so the support hypergraph is a function of the data and only the CHOICE among its edges is a function of arrival. SECOND, the prototype: five lines in `support` that pick the least signature among the firings recorded so far. Every world's canonical-witness digest then equals its least-signature digest, and the count of facts whose canonical witness is not the least drops to zero from 7 on spat, 12 on wtf, 77 on goof, 28 on sus, 18 on loot, 14 on sensors and 4 on tm. THIRD, the decisive run: with that change in place, the three order mutants that previously moved the witness - active order in propagate, activate seed order, inverted matchPremise sort - move NOTHING AT ALL, canonicalState included, on every world on the primary path. (A fourth, the phase-A wave swap, also moved nothing and is discounted: f_a_mutant_that_does_not_mutate_proves_nothing shows its second wave is empty in every world measured.) IS IT CHEAP: yes. The exact reading is already there - `witnessesOf` returns the firings sorted by signature, so the least one is element zero - and a production version is one string compare against a stored canonical signature, O(1) per firing where the prototype scans. WOULD THE GOLDENS ACCEPT IT: the full suite goes 7 to 14 failures and ALL SEVEN NEW REDS ARE IN test/store-conformance.test.ts, because adapters/sqlite-store.ts:485 keeps the first witness by its own `SELECT 1 FROM w WHERE key = ?` and the reference no longer does. Not one golden byte, not one why-tree, not one fact count moved anywhere else in 1004 tests. So the price is the same one-line rule in the adapter, and the four store-conformance mutant controls that assert read order and arrival order are INVISIBLE end to end go green again once both stores agree. WHAT IS BOUGHT: a fork, a replay, a re-evaluation and - the reason the question was asked - a parallel firing schedule would all record the same provenance, so canonicalState would stop being a witness to the order in which one host happened to fire rules. WHAT IS LOST, and it is the honest cost: the first firing is a record of WHAT HAPPENED and the least is a CHOICE among what could have happened, which is the same trade performance-invariants.md refuses for Souffle's reconstructed proofs one level down. The difference is that here nothing is recomputed - every firing is already stored - so what changes is only which of them `why` renders by default.").

finding(f_quiescence_compares_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one, defect).
recorded(f_quiescence_compares_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_quiescence_compares_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one, kernel_test).
-- SETTLED as a duplicate: recorded independently by the order-dependence
-- census in the same session as f_quiescence_compared_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one,
-- which carries the fix and the gate. Two agents found one defect.
dismissed(f_quiescence_compares_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one, duplicate_of_the_past_tense_id).
finding_note(f_quiescence_compares_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one,
  "THE ONE REAL ORDER DEFECT THE CENSUS FOUND, and it is four lines. src/api.ts:955-958 decides a tick is quiescent by comparing the CURRENT base layer against the staged facts ELEMENT BY ELEMENT: `curBase` is built with an explicit `.sort()`, `stagedKeys` is `staged.map(f => f.key)` with no sort at all. It works only because src/rounds.ts:304 and src/engine.ts:603 happen to sort the staged map keys on the way out, so the sortedness of one file's comparison is a property of another file's implementation and nothing states it. MEASURED by reversing that one sort: examples/tm.rofl - the 3-state busy beaver, which halts in 13 ticks - stops being detected as quiescent, runs to its 100-tick cap, and grows from 1391 facts to 3509; test/phase4.test.ts:97 goes red on `res.quiescent`. That is a CHANGED ANSWER, not a changed record: a run that terminated no longer terminates. It is also the one finding that bears directly on a parallel port, because a host that stages concurrently would produce exactly this list in exactly this state. THE FIX is to compare sets rather than sequences, or to sort `stagedKeys` where it is built - and the test is the mutant: reverse the staged order in the evaluator and quiescence must still be detected.").

finding(f_a_line_count_test_fires_on_every_mutant_of_any_kind, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_line_count_test_fires_on_every_mutant_of_any_kind, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_line_count_test_fires_on_every_mutant_of_any_kind, doc).
finding_note(f_a_line_count_test_fires_on_every_mutant_of_any_kind,
  "TWO OF TWELVE MUTANTS PRODUCED REDS THAT WERE NOTHING TO DO WITH ORDER. Adding one line to src/engine.ts turned test/bootstrap-dag.test.ts:127 red at 330 against 329 and test/engine-split.test.ts:224 red at 954 against 953; deleting five lines turned them red at 324 and 948, plus a regex on `MECH 693 code`. These are the code-census tests, and they are pinned to exact line counts of the kernel, so they go red for ANY edit to src/ whatever it does. That is correct behaviour for a census and a trap for a mutation study: three of the eleven non-control reds across the whole set were instrument artefacts, and a reader counting reds without reading them would have scored two mutants as order-sensitive that are not. THE RULE, and it is the repository's own gate rule pointed at mutation testing: before attributing a red to the mutant, ask whether the test could have been red for the mere fact that a file changed. The cheap discriminator is a NULL MUTANT - a whitespace-only edit of the same size - and it should be the first entry of any mutant set that edits kernel source rather than wrapping a prototype.").

finding(f_the_parallel_ceiling_is_four_and_the_serial_half_eats_most_of_it, insight).
recorded(f_the_parallel_ceiling_is_four_and_the_serial_half_eats_most_of_it, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_parallel_ceiling_is_four_and_the_serial_half_eats_most_of_it, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_parallel_ceiling_is_four_and_the_serial_half_eats_most_of_it, "scanners/round_shape.ts").
finding_note(f_the_parallel_ceiling_is_four_and_the_serial_half_eats_most_of_it,
  "I4 SAYS STRATA ARE SEQUENTIAL AND PARALLELISM LIVES ONLY INSIDE ONE. scanners/round_shape.ts measures the shape that statement is about, for one ring 1 clause in the steady state, on this machine. THE SCHEDULE. peelRounds settles 82 relations in 7 rounds - layers of 4, 7, 15, 25, 13, 11, 5, 2 - and the evaluation runs EIGHT activate() batches, two monotone waves plus six negation rounds, each of them a hard barrier. Inside those, propagate runs 74 SEMINAIVE ROUNDS that fire something, and a round is a barrier too because round N+1's front is exactly what round N derived. Rules active per round: median 18, p90 47, max 114, with 4 per cent of rounds carrying a single rule and 20 per cent carrying two or fewer. THE CEILING. Charging one accumulator element per matchPremise or negHolds call, the parse's join work is 3108 elements and the CRITICAL PATH - the sum over rounds of the largest single rule's work - is 696. So with unbounded cores, zero synchronisation and perfect balance the fixpoint could go 4.47x; at eight workers 4.01x; at four, 3.22x. AND THE STAGE SPLIT EATS IT. Re-measured here rather than quoted, 20 iterations: one clause is 9.09 ms, of which restore is 3.47 (38 per cent), the load that contains the fixpoint is 4.94 (54), the hole query 0.68 (7) and the explicit evaluate and the term readback round to zero. A perfect 4.47x on the fixpoint alone takes 9.09 ms to 5.25 - 1.73x END TO END, and that is before charging anything for the barriers or for contention on the store. TWO REASONS THE REAL NUMBER IS LOWER STILL. A rule firing in round N can already see facts concluded EARLIER IN THE SAME ROUND, so making the round genuinely parallel means each rule reads a frozen front and the fixpoint takes MORE rounds than 74. And the 4.47x charges nothing for the fact that every conclusion writes the shared store. THE SHAPE IS THEREFORE: a short critical path in the ROUND count, no - 74 rounds and 8 barriers for one clause - and a wide enough round to use maybe four cores, of which Amdahl returns under two. That is the same conclusion the field reports and performance-invariants.md already records: Souffle under 25 per cent CPU on long-tail workloads, RDFox saturating near 12 cores. A faster single core still buys roughly everything.").

finding(f_the_witness_forest_is_stored_and_only_why_renders_one_tree, insight).
recorded(f_the_witness_forest_is_stored_and_only_why_renders_one_tree, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_witness_forest_is_stored_and_only_why_renders_one_tree, doc).
finding_note(f_the_witness_forest_is_stored_and_only_why_renders_one_tree,
  "f_witness_forest_dropped says the structured Witness form is dropped for all but the first firing and asks for a Map<string, Witness[]>. THE STORAGE HALF OF IT IS ALREADY DONE and the note is stale: src/store.ts:601 keeps a structured Witness against EVERY firing signature, `witnessesOf` (src/store.ts:610) returns them all sorted by signature, that method is on the FactStore interface, the sqlite adapter implements it, and test/semiring.test.ts:302 asserts the forest survives snapshot and restore. src/semiring.ts:154 folds over the whole forest, which is what Best-derivation, Polynomial and Counting needed. WHAT REMAINS OPEN IS EXACTLY THE HALF THE ORIGINAL NOTE SAID WAS THE REAL WORK - the output contract, since `why` still renders one tree - so the finding stays open on that and its premise about storage should not be quoted again. Measured while auditing what the canonical witness costs: over eight corpus worlds, 229 of spat's facts, 185 of goof's and 70 of sus's carry more than one firing, so the forest is not a hypothetical.").

finding(f_a_mutant_that_does_not_mutate_proves_nothing, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_mutant_that_does_not_mutate_proves_nothing, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_mutant_that_does_not_mutate_proves_nothing, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_a_mutant_that_does_not_mutate_proves_nothing,
  "ONE OF TWELVE ORDER MUTANTS WAS VACUOUS AND I NEARLY REPORTED ITS SILENCE AS EVIDENCE. Swapping the two phase-A monotone waves - src/rounds.ts:267-268, mirrored at src/engine.ts:564-566 - produced zero new suite failures and a byte-identical census, which reads as `the wave split is not load-bearing`. It is not what happened. Probed by wrapping `activate` on the prototype and recording the size of every batch: THE SECOND WAVE IS EMPTY IN EVERY WORLD MEASURED - spat 25/0 and 105/0, wtf 8/0, goof 8/0 thirteen times, sus 8/0 eleven times, loot 8/0 then 37/0, ring 1 13/0 and 127/0 - and it is still 0/8 with rules/strata.rofl loaded, which is the pack that supplies the stratum table. So the mutant swapped a non-empty array with an empty one and changed nothing because there was nothing to change. TWO CONSEQUENCES. First, the method: a mutant set needs a LIVENESS CHECK PER MUTANT, not only for the set - the question is not `did the suite go red` but `did the mutated code path execute at least once`, and the cheapest form is to count the thing being permuted and refuse to interpret a zero. Second, the code: `late_rule(R) :- concludes(R, Rel), mono_rule(R), stratum_cone(Rel)` in safety.rofl:108 selects monotone rules that conclude into the stratum table's cone, and boot.rofl stopped deriving stratum/2 when the round evaluator became primary - so the split is dead across this whole corpus, and the comment above it claiming it `keeps the firing order, and with it the canonical witness of every fact, identical to the stock run` is a claim nothing here can exercise. It is not wrong; it is unfalsifiable in this tree, which is the same class as a gate that is permanently green in the safe direction.").
-- Recorded 2026-09-07 while measuring whether independent FORKS parallelise on
-- worker_threads (docs/dogfood/2026-09-07-fork-parallelism.md). Artifacts:
-- runtime/fork_pool.ts, runtime/fork_worker.ts, bench/fork_parallel.ts,
-- bench/fork_stages.ts, bench/fork_clone_cost.ts, bench/fork_control.ts.

finding(f_the_repo_has_no_thousand_fork_search_and_iffy_says_why, insight).
recorded(f_the_repo_has_no_thousand_fork_search_and_iffy_says_why, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_repo_has_no_thousand_fork_search_and_iffy_says_why, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_repo_has_no_thousand_fork_search_and_iffy_says_why, "docs/dogfood/2026-09-07-fork-parallelism.md").
finding_note(f_the_repo_has_no_thousand_fork_search_and_iffy_says_why,
  "THE BRIEF SAID 'IFFY AND DITTO SEARCH MANY BRANCHES' AND THE CODE SAYS OTHERWISE, which had to be checked before anything was measured. IFFY DOES NOT FORK AT ALL: `arm[draft](A)` is declared `edb(arm)` at examples/iffy/iffy.rofl:125 and every downstream rule carries the arm as its first argument (`in_force/2` :152, `gained/lost/steady` :185-189), so every arm lives in ONE store and ONE fixpoint. The only Rofl creations in that example are the section 9 cost benchmark, which exists to PRICE a fork rather than to use one, and its README says why in its own numbers - a fork by clone copies 7675 facts where one more arm adds 3673, a ratio of 2.1x. THE ONE PLACE HERE THAT HAD A MANY-ARM SEARCH AND PRICED THE FORK CHOSE NOT TO FORK. DITTO forks ONCE (examples/ditto/demo.ts:408 `repair`, plus one `excise` at :462), not in a loop. THE CENSUS, over examples/, test/, scanners/, runtime/, adapters/, scripts/ and src/: the largest fork-shaped workload in the repository is test/kernel-arity.test.ts:47 driven from :73 at about 800 worlds, then test/phase2.test.ts:67 at 240, test/phase4.test.ts:46 at 100, test/rofl-safety.test.ts:150 and test/rule-answers.test.ts:153 at 70 each, examples/heck/demo.ts:542 at about 68, examples/wtf/demo.ts:574 `runSweeps` at 41, scanners/ablate.ts:247 at 32 per config, examples/blam/demo.ts:364 at 12, examples/npc/demo.ts:418 at 8, examples/nope/demo.ts:418 at 3. SO THE THOUSAND-FORK SEARCH THAT WOULD JUSTIFY THIS WORK DOES NOT EXIST HERE, and the largest that is both many-branch and expensive per branch is 41. AND THE INDEPENDENCE QUESTION, WHICH THE BRIEF CORRECTLY SAID DECIDES EVERYTHING, WAS ANSWERED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: NOTHING IN THIS REPOSITORY PRUNES. There is exactly one best-so-far in the whole census, `best` at examples/heck/demo.ts:539, and it does not prune - every candidate is built and fully evaluated regardless and the `continue` at :546 is decided from that candidate's own sets, so it is a reduction with a deterministic total order, not a bound. TWO SITES ARE SEQUENTIAL AND FOR DIFFERENT REASONS: examples/wtf/demo.ts:632 `sbaFixpoint`, where round n+1 is `fromSnapshot` of round n's RESULT world with a data-dependent break, and examples/loot/demo.ts:655, where the loop body mutates `state.carries`/`state.wounded`/`state.at` and the next leg's world is built from exactly those mutated values - a simulation, not a search. EVERYTHING ELSE IS EMBARRASSINGLY PARALLEL, and the recurring shape is a WRITE-ONLY accumulator (`seen`/`mismatches`/`compared` in wtf, `crashes`/`refusals` in kernel-arity, `states` in phase4, `agree`/`disagreed` in rofl-safety, `held`/`missed` in npc) beside a read-only shared base. TWO CAVEATS BEFORE PARALLELISING ANY OF THEM: examples/iffy/demo.ts:645 and bench/fork_clone_cost.ts:62 create independent worlds in a loop but are STOPWATCHES - the loop exists to take a minimum over repeats, and running the reps concurrently measures contention instead of the thing; and per-branch work varies by three orders of magnitude across the list, so a speedup measured on one says nothing about another.").

finding(f_the_fork_boundary_is_per_worker_not_per_branch, insight).
recorded(f_the_fork_boundary_is_per_worker_not_per_branch, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_fork_boundary_is_per_worker_not_per_branch, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_fork_boundary_is_per_worker_not_per_branch, "runtime/fork_pool.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_fork_boundary_is_per_worker_not_per_branch, "docs/dogfood/2026-09-07-fork-parallelism.md").
finding_note(f_the_fork_boundary_is_per_worker_not_per_branch,
  "THE ARITHMETIC THE BRIEF ASKED FOR, AND THE TERM THAT MATTERS IS NOT THE ONE IT NAMED. A worker_threads worker shares no JS object graph, so the base world must cross as a serialized snapshot - and the fear was that the serialization would dominate a branch. IT DOES NOT, BECAUSE EVERY BRANCH OF A FORK SEARCH STARTS FROM THE SAME WORLD, SO THE SNAPSHOT CROSSES ONCE PER WORKER AND NOT ONCE PER BRANCH. What is per-branch is a 155-byte descriptor in and the answer out. MEASURED IN COUNTS AND BYTES, WHICH DO NOT MOVE WITH THE BOX'S LOAD: one branch of examples/wtf's order sweep is 1466 distinct rule firings over a 6794-fact world; crossing in is 155 B; crossing out is 2398 B of digest, or 762416 B if the acceptance oracle rides along. Crossing per worker is 2533653 B, once. MEASURED IN TIME, on an 8-vCPU Apple M4 Pro (Virtual) with three other agents on it and the load average stated beside every figure: a 2.5 MB string round-trips main->worker->main in 0.32 ms and a 33-byte object in 0.037 ms, against a branch of 83-88 ms at load average 5-9. THE ANSWER SIDE WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN FEARED: running the whole A/B with the 762 KB canonicalState attached and again with only the 2.4 KB digest gives 2.37x and 2.34x at P=4, within the noise - a 762 KB answer per branch is not the limiter. THE CROSSOVER, AND THERE ARE TWO OF THEM. PER BRANCH: the pool costs a branch about 0.04 ms of messaging with a small answer and about 1.3 ms with a 762 KB one, so any branch that runs a fixpoint at all clears it by two to three orders of magnitude, and a branch that is only a `holds` query (examples/npc/demo.ts:418) does not. PER SEARCH, and this is the binding one: pool setup - spawn, module import, one fromSnapshot per worker - is 244-533 ms for one to eight workers, which expressed as a LOAD-INDEPENDENT RATIO is 2.8 to 6.2 BRANCH-EQUIVALENTS, so break-even needs N greater than about setup_in_branches times P/(P-1), which is 7 branches at P=8. A PERSISTENT POOL PAYS THAT ONCE PER PROCESS RATHER THAN ONCE PER SEARCH and the term goes to zero. Stating the setup in branch-equivalents rather than in milliseconds is the move that makes the crossover survive a loaded machine: it is a ratio of two times taken in the same sitting.").

finding(f_allocation_not_cores_is_the_ceiling_on_fork_parallelism, insight).
recorded(f_allocation_not_cores_is_the_ceiling_on_fork_parallelism, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_allocation_not_cores_is_the_ceiling_on_fork_parallelism, doc).
addressed_by(f_allocation_not_cores_is_the_ceiling_on_fork_parallelism, "bench/fork_control.ts").
finding_note(f_allocation_not_cores_is_the_ceiling_on_fork_parallelism,
  "A SPEEDUP BELOW THE CORE COUNT MEANS ONE OF TWO THINGS AND THEY CALL FOR OPPOSITE WORK - either the workload does not parallelise or the box does not - AND ONE CONTROL DOES NOT SEPARATE THEM. Two do. bench/fork_control.ts runs the SAME pool at the same worker counts, interleaved ABABAB, on two synthetic tasks: pure arithmetic with no allocation, and JSON.parse of the same 2.5 MB base snapshot with nothing else. On eight vCPUs, taken within minutes of each other at load average 6-11: ARITHMETIC 5.74x, ALLOCATION 3.78x, THE REAL FORK SEARCH 3.11x at 164 branches and 2.12x at 41. So the box can give 5.7x and the fork search gets 3.1x, and the gap is not the machine - it is that a fork branch is dominated by ALLOCATION: 17 per cent of it is `Rofl.fromSnapshot` parsing 2.5 MB and building 6834 facts, and the 80 per cent that is the fixpoint allocates throughout. Eight of those at once contend for memory bandwidth and for the allocator, and 3.8x is what this box gives that shape. THE PRACTICAL READING FOR I4's WARNING NOT TO PLAN FOR LINEAR SPEEDUP FROM CORES: the warning is right and the reason here is not synchronisation - there is none, the branches share nothing - it is the memory system. WHICH POINTS AT THE SAME PLACE THE MEMORY TIERS DO: a compact fact representation (docs/performance-invariants.md tier 2 and 3) would raise the parallel ceiling as a side effect, because it is allocation per branch that the eight workers are queueing for. AND THE HONEST FORM OF THE HEADLINE IS 3.1x ON 8 CORES, NOT 8x: 0.55x of the loss is pool setup at 41 branches (which a persistent pool removes, taking run-only to 3.4x), and the rest is the allocation ceiling the control names.").

finding(f_a_mutant_that_never_fires_reads_as_a_gate_that_is_blind, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_mutant_that_never_fires_reads_as_a_gate_that_is_blind, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_mutant_that_never_fires_reads_as_a_gate_that_is_blind, rule_and_test).
addressed_by(f_a_mutant_that_never_fires_reads_as_a_gate_that_is_blind, "bench/fork_task_leaky.ts").
finding_note(f_a_mutant_that_never_fires_reads_as_a_gate_that_is_blind,
  "THE MOST IMPORTANT MUTANT IN THE SET REPORTED SURVIVED, AND IT HAD NOT RUN. bench/fork_task_leaky.ts plants the one defect a worker cannot detect about itself - the task mutating its context so branch i+1 sees branch i - and the first version wrote `w.assert(`seen_before(${n})`)` WITH NO TERMINATING DOT. The kernel refused it, `line 1: expected ':-', got 'eof'`, and returned the refusal in a value nobody read. So the leak leaked nothing, the parallel arm matched the sequential arm exactly, and the harness printed SURVIVED against the line `targets: the fork contract itself`. Read literally that says THE DETERMINISM GATE CANNOT SEE A SCHEDULE LEAK, which is the exact opposite of the truth: with the dot added the gate kills it on branch 1. A MUTANT THAT NEVER FIRES IS INDISTINGUISHABLE, IN THE OUTPUT, FROM A GATE THAT CANNOT SEE IT - and it fails in the direction that makes a gate look WEAKER than it is, which is the direction nobody double-checks because it reads as commendable caution. THE FIX IS THE SAME SHAPE AS THIS REPOSITORY'S POSITIVE-CONTROL RULE ONE LEVEL DOWN: a planted defect must ASSERT THAT IT WAS PLANTED. The file now checks `a.ok` and then `w.holds(...)` and throws if either fails, so the mutant cannot report on the gate unless it first proves it changed the world. THE GENERAL RULE: every mutant needs its own positive control, because 'the gate said nothing' has two causes and only one of them is about the gate.").

finding(f_the_structural_clone_made_the_recorded_fork_cost_stale, defect).
recorded(f_the_structural_clone_made_the_recorded_fork_cost_stale, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_structural_clone_made_the_recorded_fork_cost_stale, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_structural_clone_made_the_recorded_fork_cost_stale, "bench/fork_clone_cost.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_structural_clone_made_the_recorded_fork_cost_stale, "docs/performance-invariants.md").
finding_note(f_the_structural_clone_made_the_recorded_fork_cost_stale,
  "THE FOURTH READING OF THE FORK COST, AND IT IS WRITTEN INTO THE SAME LINE OF REASONING AS THE OTHER THREE RATHER THAN BESIDE THEM, because f_a_correction_belongs_inside_the_finding_it_corrects says three open findings that disagree present a puzzle instead of a fact. THE LINE SO FAR: f_fork_copies_the_whole_store measured 8 seconds a fork at 100k facts; f_fork_time_argument_died_with_the_insert_fix re-measured 344 ms and declared the time argument dead; f_i_measured_the_cheapest_fact_and_generalised showed that re-measurement was taken on a BARE store of integer facts and put a realistic store at 21.7-22.5 microseconds a fact, hence about 2.2 seconds for a realistic 100k-fact fork and half an hour for a thousand-fork search. THAT SETTLED READING IS NOW STALE, AND THE REASON IS NAMED RATHER THAN GUESSED: every one of those figures was taken when `clone()` was `snapshot() -> JSON -> restore()`. Commit 3cff6f4 replaced it with a STRUCTURAL copy that walks the fact map directly (src/store.ts:779), and a figure measured through the serialising path is not a figure about this one. RE-MEASURED WITH A BARE-STORE CONTROL IN THE SAME RUN, on stores carrying rules, derived facts, witnesses and firings - 7504 facts of which 6480 derived with 3240 witnesses and 3240 firings, and 38304 facts of which 36080 derived with 18040 witnesses - at load average 8.9: STRUCTURAL CLONE 0.15 and 0.40 microseconds a fact realistic, 0.11 and 0.26 bare; SAVE-PLUS-RESTORE 3.05 and 3.56 realistic, 3.04 and 3.48 bare; a ratio of 9x to 29x between them. THE MACHINE ACCOUNTS FOR SOME OF THE GAP TO 21.7 AND NOT MOST OF IT - a 4-core Xeon at 2.1 GHz against an 8-vCPU M4 Pro is a factor of a few, and the gap is 55x to 150x. CONSEQUENCES, STATED CAREFULLY BECAUSE THIS LINE HAS BEEN WRONG THREE TIMES: a realistic 100k-fact fork costs on the order of 40 ms of CLONE at 0.4 microseconds a fact, so the TIME argument for copy-on-write is weak again and the doc's `2.2 seconds` and `half an hour for a thousand forks` should not be quoted; the MEMORY argument is untouched, because a structural clone is still a full copy in RAM and bytes per fact is still the identified ceiling; and the per-fact cost is NOT flat - it rises 1.7x to 2.6x between 7.5k and 38k facts, which is allocation pressure rather than the index, so an extrapolation to 100k from the 7.5k figure is an underestimate and the 40 ms is a floor. WHAT THIS DOES TO THE RANKED WORK: docs/performance-invariants.md section 5 item 3 ranks copy-on-write third on the strength of the 2.2-second figure, and on the measured stage split of a real fork branch (bench/fork_stages.ts) the whole of `Rofl.fromSnapshot` is 17 per cent and one `store.clone()` is 1 per cent, against 80 per cent in the fixpoint - so a free fork would move a branch from 88 ms to about 75 ms and item 3 should be re-ranked BELOW the memory tiers it was already argued to belong with.").

finding(f_the_front_was_one_key_set_and_that_is_a_defect_not_a_missing_optimisation, insight).
recorded(f_the_front_was_one_key_set_and_that_is_a_defect_not_a_missing_optimisation, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_front_was_one_key_set_and_that_is_a_defect_not_a_missing_optimisation, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_front_was_one_key_set_and_that_is_a_defect_not_a_missing_optimisation, "src/engine.ts").
finding_note(f_the_front_was_one_key_set_and_that_is_a_defect_not_a_missing_optimisation,
  "SEPARATED FROM THE WORK IT ARRIVED WITH, AND IT SURVIVED WHILE THAT WORK DID NOT. Per-delta-version body plans were built on this branch and measured at 6385 -> 5952 join width, minus 6.8 per cent, for NINETY lines of kernel; Vadim declined the trade, and the census agrees with him - the engine is measured by scanners/engine_split.ts and the whole tower argument rests on the kernel being small. But two things arrived in one commit and only one of them was that trade. `FrontInfo` carried a single `keys` set, so `matchPremise` given a window and a literal with NO BOUND ARGUMENT took the `[...only].sort()` branch and walked the WHOLE derived layer of the round to find one relation's facts - it then filtered by `f.rel === lit.rel`, which is the tell: the code already knew it wanted one relation and the structure could not give it one. `FrontInfo` now carries `byRel` beside `keys` and `noteFront` maintains both. SIX lines, PLUMB not POL, and no answer moves - the branch that iterates the window already filtered by relation and the branch that intersects an index probe already probed a single relation. MEASURED ALONE, which is the point of recording this separately: process-level interleaved arms, ten pairs on an 82-byte ring 1 clause at load average 2.4, 22.33 ms -> 21.35, NINE OF TEN PAIRS, ratio median 0.953. About 4.7 per cent for six lines against 6.8 per cent for ninety. It measured 7.7 per cent when it was taken WITH the version plans, and the difference is itself the diagnosis: a version plan leads with its delta, a leading delta has no bound argument by construction, so version plans made this pathology fire more often than the shared plan does. THE GENERAL SHAPE, and it is the reason this is a finding rather than a diff: a defect and an optimisation were measured as one number, the number was judged as a whole, and the defect would have been reverted with the optimisation. When a change bundles a fix with a bet, price them apart before the bet is refused. AND ONE MEASUREMENT WAS SAVED BY ITS OWN CONTROL: the first `wordch` A/B printed 6385 for both arms because BSD sed does not read `\\|` in a basic regular expression, so the rewrite never applied and I was comparing an arm against itself. Three identical numbers where a difference was expected is what caught it; two would have looked like a null result.").

finding(f_wordch_is_only_a_pessimisation_under_a_planner_we_do_not_have, insight).
recorded(f_wordch_is_only_a_pessimisation_under_a_planner_we_do_not_have, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_wordch_is_only_a_pessimisation_under_a_planner_we_do_not_have, doc).
addressed_by(f_wordch_is_only_a_pessimisation_under_a_planner_we_do_not_have, "examples/ring1/ring1.rofl").
finding_note(f_wordch_is_only_a_pessimisation_under_a_planner_we_do_not_have,
  "A DECISION THAT WAS ABOUT TO BE TAKEN ON A NUMBER MEASURED UNDER A CONFIGURATION THAT NO LONGER EXISTS. The per-version planning work reported that `wordch(I) :- code_at(I), kind(I, K)` - a rewrite that had beaten the original by 312 under a shared plan - was 117 WORSE than the original once every rule got a plan per delta version, and offered the revert as a standing decision. It is not one: it is conditional on the planner. With version plans removed, measured three times alternating on this tree, `code_at` leading gives 6385 and `kind` leading gives 6697 - the rewrite is 312 better again, exactly reproducing the shared-plan figure from the other instrument. KEPT, and the conditionality is the finding: a body order is not good or bad, it is good or bad AGAINST A SCHEDULE, and a recorded verdict on a rule's shape must name the schedule it was measured under or it will be applied under another one. This is the same shape as HANDOFF's rule about machine-relative absolutes, one level up: there the hidden variable is the machine, here it is the evaluator's own planner.").

finding(f_quiescence_compared_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one, defect).
recorded(f_quiescence_compared_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_quiescence_compared_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one, kernel_test).
addressed_by(f_quiescence_compared_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one, "src/api.ts").
addressed_by(f_quiescence_compared_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one, "test/phase4.test.ts").
finding_note(f_quiescence_compared_a_sorted_list_against_an_unsorted_one,
  "FIXED. `tickAdvance` decided quiescence by comparing a SORTED array of the tick's base facts element-by-element against an UNSORTED array of the staged next-tick facts. It was correct only because two unrelated places sort on the way out, so the comparison was reading an order neither of its own operands promises. Found by the order-dependence census, which reversed one of those sorts: examples/tm.rofl, the 3-state busy beaver that halts in 13 ticks, stops being detected as quiescent, runs to its 100-tick cap and grows 1391 -> 3509 facts - a program that terminated stops terminating, with no error and no hole. It is also precisely what a concurrent stager would produce, which is why a parallelism census found a bug on the sequential path. THE FIX is `sameKeySet(a, b)` in src/api.ts, sorting both sides or neither; `stagedKeys` is left in arrival order because `tickLog` records it and `canonicalState` reads that. AND THE GATE CAUGHT MY OWN TEST FIRST, which is the part worth keeping. The first version asserted `sameKeySet(['b','a'], ['a','b'])`, planted a one-sided mutant (`const y = b`) - and the suite stayed GREEN, because the right-hand operand in that case is already in order, so a mutant that sorts only the left cannot be seen by it. The defect's own shape is the OTHER direction: left sorted, right as it arrived. Both directions are asserted now and both one-sided mutants die, each with its planting verified before the verdict was read. THE RULE: a test case for an asymmetric defect must be written in the asymmetric direction, and a symmetric-looking assertion can cover exactly half of a mutant space while reading as if it covered all of it - the same failure as a mutant that never fires, one level in.").

finding(f_the_canonical_witness_is_now_the_least_signature_and_the_store_stores_no_pick, decision).
recorded(f_the_canonical_witness_is_now_the_least_signature_and_the_store_stores_no_pick, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_canonical_witness_is_now_the_least_signature_and_the_store_stores_no_pick, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_canonical_witness_is_now_the_least_signature_and_the_store_stores_no_pick, "src/store.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_canonical_witness_is_now_the_least_signature_and_the_store_stores_no_pick, "adapters/sqlite-store.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_canonical_witness_is_derivable_from_the_data, "src/store.ts").
finding_note(f_the_canonical_witness_is_now_the_least_signature_and_the_store_stores_no_pick,
  "DECIDED BY VADIM AND BUILT. The order-dependence census had established the premise: across thirteen order mutants over nine worlds the fact set and the FIRING set are byte-identical every time, and the only thing that moves is which firing is recorded as canonical. That pick was made by one line in `Store.support` - `if (!this.witnesses.has(key)) this.witnesses.set(key, w)` - so the single place where the evaluation's SCHEDULE reached the record was first-arrival-wins. WHAT WAS BUILT IS STRONGER THAN THE PROTOTYPE THAT MOTIVATED IT. The prototype kept the map and maintained it by least signature; this DELETES the map. `witnessOf` is a linear minimum over the fact's firings, `allWitnesses` folds it, `canonicalState` and `snapshot` render it, `restore` ignores the `wits` block it still writes (the block is a rendering of `firings`, which is the source), `clone` copies nothing extra and `advanceTick` holds back only the firing map. The pick is therefore a function of the data BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by an invariant somebody has to maintain, and one Map entry per fact goes away with it. The scan is the right shape and not a compromise: measured across this repository, a fact has 1.0 to 1.9 derivations, so a cache would be caching a one-element answer. MEASURED AFTER. `scanners/order_census.ts` reports `witness == minwit` in every world and `nDiffer` 0 everywhere, against 7 in spat, 12 in wtf, 77 in goof, 28 in sus, 18 in loot, 14 in sensors and 4 in tm before. Three order mutants re-run with the planting verified each time - the rule order inside a propagate round reversed, `matchPremise`'s total sort inverted, and `activate`'s seed order inverted - all IDENTICAL on the primary path, digests included, where the first of them used to move both the witness and `canonicalState`. The one thing that still moves is `wtf/stock`, and it moves its FACT SET (8080 -> 8137): that is the stock `evaluator: strata` path, which LIMITS.md already declares outside v0 guarantees, and it is a different defect wearing this one's clothes. THE COST, STATED BECAUSE IT IS A REAL LOSS AND NOT A FREE WIN: the first firing is a record of what happened and the least is a choice among what could have happened. What defuses it is that nothing is discarded - every firing was already kept and still is - so what changed is only which tree `why` renders by default. WHAT IT BUYS, and this is why it was worth doing rather than merely tidy: order-independence of the whole record becomes a theorem, so a second host can be checked against this one byte for byte, and a reconstruction of a dropped witness now reproduces THE SAME witness rather than a plausible one - which is the objection docs/performance-invariants.md raises against Souffle's store-and-reconstruct trade, and it is now answered. THE ADAPTER WAS THE WHOLE PRICE the prototype predicted: SQLite kept the first witness in its own `w` table, so seven conformance tests would have gone red; `witnessOf` and `allWitnesses` now read the least signature out of `fi`, and conformance is 11/11 with nothing else touched.").

finding(f_two_thirds_of_the_main_cluster_is_stale_and_the_third_resists_the_obvious_fix, insight).
recorded(f_two_thirds_of_the_main_cluster_is_stale_and_the_third_resists_the_obvious_fix, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_two_thirds_of_the_main_cluster_is_stale_and_the_third_resists_the_obvious_fix, decision).
finding_note(f_two_thirds_of_the_main_cluster_is_stale_and_the_third_resists_the_obvious_fix,
  "RE-TAKEN ON THE OWNER'S ASK, AND THE CLUSTER SPLIT. f_main_carries_the_kernels_own_reflection_and_that_is_ring_zero recorded that [main] does THREE jobs - the kernel's own reflection, the program's principal content, and the default for an unbracketed literal - and three symptoms were recorded on it. Two are gone and one is alive. MEASURED. KERNEL_BOOK now holds SEVENTEEN of the twenty-one reserved names, and over spat+goof+wtf+loot+sensors every one of those seventeen lands in [$kernel] and none in [main]: derived_by 7108, asserted_by 907, in_perspective 907, has_premise 1043, premise_lit 1043, premise_pos 735, reads_from 432, rule/concludes/conclusion_lit/conclusion_tense/has_conclusion/writes_to 391 each. What is left in [main] is exactly the four CO-WRITTEN tables, excluded from KERNEL_BOOK on purpose because programs write them by hand: authority 79, edb 227, mode 70, reserved 105. AND THE THIRD JOB IS ALREADY SEPARATED FOR THOSE SEVENTEEN, by a mechanism nobody connected to this finding: `resolveBook` (src/reflect.ts:680) rewrites an UNBRACKETED literal over a KERNEL_BOOK relation to [$kernel], leaving an explicit bracket alone including a wrong one. Measured over 80 .rofl files: 82 literals in rules read a reserved relation and ALL EIGHTY-TWO are written without a bracket - so the corpus never says [$kernel] and never needed to. SYMPTOMS RE-TAKEN: examples/sensors over boot now reports every audit 0, crossing 0 rows, leak 0 rows, where the finding recorded two irreducible rows `main -> trust` and `main -> verified`; bare boot reports crossing 0 and bridge_decl 0, where `main -> audit` was said to account for seventy-odd automatic bridges. WHAT IS ALIVE, unchanged and reproduced exactly: naming yourself is a forgery. `authority(mybook, alice). p[mybook](x).` under who=alice gives forged 1 and the row named is the authority declaration itself, `$fact(authority, main, ...)`; anonymously it is 0. AND THE OBVIOUS FIX IS REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT, which is why this is worth recording rather than acting on. Adding V.authority to KERNEL_BOOK - so the grant lands where the kernel writes - takes forged from 1 to SEVEN under who=alice and from 0 to EIGHT anonymously, because [$kernel] has no standing writers at all, so every honest authority row forges too. That is precisely what the exclusion comment in src/reflect.ts predicts and it is now a number rather than a prediction. Note the shape of the false comfort: test/example-sensors, test/phase2 and test/rofl-safety stayed 12/12 GREEN under a change that made forged eight times worse, because no path in them exercises a named author - the same 'green by construction' this repository has recorded twice already. SO THE PROBLEM IS NOT WHICH BOOK, IT IS THAT THE GRANT OF STANDING IS SUBJECT TO THE STANDING CHECK: in [main] alice has none, in [$kernel] nobody has any, and no third book fixes an ordering problem. Three ways out, all decisions for the owner and none measured yet: exempt authority facts from `forged` (self-licensing, which f_self_licensing_is_not_gone_it_is_now_a_line_someone_typed says is ALREADY the standing position since `imports` is unauthenticated); derive standing from the HOST's `load(text, {who})` so the program's authority line becomes a request rather than a fact, moving the trust root out of program text at the cost of re-meaning 79 corpus facts; or make a claim of standing a claim - `claims(alice, authority(...))` - per the ledger rule settled this same day, so that what is judged is the claim and not the act of writing it.").
finding(f_two_thirds_of_a_floor_is_the_kernel_talking_about_it, insight).
recorded(f_two_thirds_of_a_floor_is_the_kernel_talking_about_it, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_two_thirds_of_a_floor_is_the_kernel_talking_about_it, doc).
addressed_by(f_two_thirds_of_a_floor_is_the_kernel_talking_about_it, "scanners/floor_census.ts").
addressed_by(f_two_thirds_of_a_floor_is_the_kernel_talking_about_it, "rules/floor-census.rofl").
finding_note(f_two_thirds_of_a_floor_is_the_kernel_talking_about_it,
  "A STORE MEASURED BY WHO CAN READ IT, not by relation. Five buckets, and the split is not cosmetic: EXECUTABLE is the three relations decodeRules reads and nothing else (rule, conclusion_lit, premise_lit - src/reflect.ts:813, :818, :833, and `rule/1` is the DRIVING LOOP, so it is three and not two); ABOUT/RULE scales with the program; ABOUT/FACT scales with the DATA and is a different decision that must not be averaged with it; PROVENANCE scales with the fixpoint; WORLD is what the program is about. MEASURED over seven worlds, each built the way its own demo builds it. ring1 unsealed: 4 per cent world, 28 executable, 59 about/rule, 8 about/fact - two thirds of the image is the kernel describing the grammar, and `parseFile` FORKS that store once per clause. The corpus programs are the other shape: spat 38/6/14/3 with 37 per cent provenance, goof 39/4/10/9, wtf 27/13/28/9, loot 16/14/33/21, cram 24/11/26/18. TOP-HEAVY IS DERIVED RATHER THAN EYEBALLED - `about_rule > world` - and it names ring1, wtf, loot and cram. THE TWO HALVES OF ABOUT DIVERGE BY A FACTOR OF EIGHT: on ring1 the per-rule half is 1435 rows and the per-fact half is 186, because `factMetaFacts` fires per ASSERTED fact and a grammar asserts 90 of them; on loot the per-fact half is 21 per cent, larger than on any other world here. Averaging them would have hidden both. AND THE READER CENSUS IS A QUERY, NOT A GREP: every .rofl file is parsed and run through the kernel's own `encodeRule`, so `this rule reads concludes in its body` is answered by the reflection row the kernel would emit. 73 reads over 79 files, in SEVEN files only - boot.rofl 14, safety.rofl 19, policy.rofl 5, rules/strata.rofl 11, rules/kernel-policy.rofl 11, examples/loot 2, examples/cram/flight_log 1. An independent grep-based census by a second agent agreed row for row.").

finding(f_the_kernel_reads_the_rows_it_calls_about_a_program, insight).
recorded(f_the_kernel_reads_the_rows_it_calls_about_a_program, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_kernel_reads_the_rows_it_calls_about_a_program, rule_and_test).
addressed_by(f_the_kernel_reads_the_rows_it_calls_about_a_program, "scanners/floor_census.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_kernel_reads_the_rows_it_calls_about_a_program, "test/sealed.test.ts").
finding_note(f_the_kernel_reads_the_rows_it_calls_about_a_program,
  "THE PREMISE I WAS HANDED WAS FALSE AND THE MEASUREMENT SAYS SO. The brief said the eleven non-executable reflection relations are ABOUT a program rather than FOR running it. Five of them are FOR running it, and the reader is the kernel: `safetyAnswer` (src/engine.ts:628) copies premise_lit, conclusion_lit, has_premise, concludes, conclusion_tense, premise_pos, premise_neg and reserved OUT OF THE USER'S STORE into safety.rofl's isolated store, and `policyAnswer` (src/engine.ts:982) copies concludes, premise_pos and premise_neg into policy.rofl's. An isolated store is not an independent one when it is SEEDED from the store in question. MEASURED BY ABLATION: drop `has_premise` from a ring 1 image and the parse EXHAUSTS ITS BUDGET, because safety.rofl's `bound_before(R, K, V) :- binds_at(R, J, V), K is J + 1, has_premise(R, K).` goes empty, every `is` premise becomes un-ground on the right, every rule using arithmetic is judged unsafe, and the grammar is unfolded top-down instead of run. AND THE MEASUREMENT ONLY WORKS COLD, WHICH IS THE METHOD FINDING: `safetyMemo` is module-level and keyed by a hash of the rule ids (src/engine.ts:251), and dropping rows does not change a rule id - so a WARM ablation reports all twelve about-relations droppable and a COLD one, forced to miss by adding one probe rule per arm, reports has_premise load-bearing. Same store, same code, opposite conclusions, and the difference is a memo. A SECOND INSTRUMENT DEFECT COST AN HOUR OF WRONG READING: the probe rule's own id reached derived facts (`rule_known[main](r...)`), so every arm read DIVERGED including arms that dropped four rows nothing reads; the digest now filters by probe id as well as by name.").

finding(f_a_dropped_audit_row_is_a_clean_bill_and_nothing_says_so, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_dropped_audit_row_is_a_clean_bill_and_nothing_says_so, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_dropped_audit_row_is_a_clean_bill_and_nothing_says_so, rule_and_test).
addressed_by(f_a_dropped_audit_row_is_a_clean_bill_and_nothing_says_so, "test/sealed.test.ts").
finding_note(f_a_dropped_audit_row_is_a_clean_bill_and_nothing_says_so,
  "AN ABLATION THAT LEAVES AN EMPTY AUDIT EMPTY PROVES NOTHING, so each one was planted first and the audit shown to BITE. Five probes on bare boot.rofl, reuse off, each with its own liveness check. `leak[audit]` 3 -> 0 with reads_from and writes_to dropped. `forged[audit]` 1 -> 0 with asserted_by and in_perspective dropped. `unmoded[audit]` 1 -> 0 with uses_builtin dropped. `undefined_premise[audit]` 1 -> 0 with premise_pos dropped. FOUR AUDITS WENT FROM BITING TO REPORTING NOTHING WITH ZERO DIAGNOSTICS ANYWHERE - the store simply stopped holding the evidence and every question about it answered EMPTY, which is the same two characters a clean bill prints. That is the failure examples/ring1 exists to refuse, arriving through the store instead of through a parser. THE FIFTH IS THE INTERESTING ONE AND IT FAILS THE OTHER WAY: dropping `has_conclusion` took `malformed[audit]` from 1 to 22, because `malformed(R) :- has_premise(R, _), not has_conclusion(R, _)` now fires for EVERY rule in the store. So withholding evidence is not uniformly silent - it can also manufacture an alarm on every rule at once, which is a check that gets switched off within a week. THREE INSTRUMENT DEFECTS WERE FOUND WHILE BUILDING THESE FIVE, all of them the same shape: `store.remove` does not set `store.dirty`, so the re-evaluation never ran and every arm reported `still reported`; `new Rofl()` defaults reuse ON, so the derived audit rows were served from the cache built before the ablation; and two probes read 0 in the planted arm for the same reason and would have been reported as dead. A probe that reads the same as a blind gate has to prove it fired first.").

finding(f_a_floor_may_seal_what_it_publishes_and_the_refusal_is_the_mechanism, insight).
recorded(f_a_floor_may_seal_what_it_publishes_and_the_refusal_is_the_mechanism, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_floor_may_seal_what_it_publishes_and_the_refusal_is_the_mechanism, rule_and_test).
addressed_by(f_a_floor_may_seal_what_it_publishes_and_the_refusal_is_the_mechanism, "test/sealed.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_floor_may_seal_what_it_publishes_and_the_refusal_is_the_mechanism, "examples/ring1/ring1.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_floor_may_seal_what_it_publishes_and_the_refusal_is_the_mechanism,
  "`sealed(Body).` IS WRITTEN BY THE PROGRAM AND READ BY THE KERNEL, the same contract `semantics(well_founded).` has, and it names one of three bodies of metadata the kernel keeps ABOUT a program: `rules` (has_conclusion, reads_from, writes_to, uses_builtin), `assertions` (in_perspective, asserted_by), `provenance` (derived_by). WHY A DECLARATION AND NOT AN INFERENCE: dropping rows because nothing currently reads them makes a later audit return EMPTY and look correct, which is the objection that killed the provenance-retention policy on this branch - a guarantee that can be switched off, and a check nobody will notice is off. THE REFUSAL IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM AND THE SAVING IS THE SIDE EFFECT: the kernel writes `hole($sealed(Body), reflection_sealed)` when the declaration arrives, and `Rofl.query` of a withheld relation returns `partial: true` with a `hole` rather than an empty answer. THAT CLOSES THE RECORDED QUERY HOLE (f_a_query_side_gate_cannot_exist, dismissed as retention_not_taken): a RULE that reads provenance is known before the first firing and a QUERY arrives afterwards, so no tick-boundary gate can see one - but a DECLARATION is visible to both, which is the difference that makes this gateable where pruning was not. WHY NOT A SECOND CONSUMER OF `opaque_closed`: policy.rofl's opaque set is the kernel's admission that it cannot promise to REPRODUCE what a relation holds, discovered by the kernel and closed upward through `rule_reads`. Floor sealing is an author's INTENTION about what a rule publishes, closed by nothing - a floor above a sealed one is perfectly describable. Folding them would let a program make `opaque_closed` true by writing a fact, which is the reuse-correctness gate switched off through a door nobody watches. WHAT IT MAY NOT SEAL IS MEASURED RATHER THAN CHOSEN: `rules/floor-census.rofl` derives `sealable(Rel) :- droppable(Rel), not kernel_reader(Rel)` from the ablation and the reader census, and test/sealed.test.ts asserts that the derived set equals the kernel's table element for element. `premise_pos`, `premise_neg`, `concludes` and `conclusion_tense` are droppable on the ring 1 grammar and are EXCLUDED, because both kernel programs read them. `unread/1` - a sealable row nothing reads at all - is EMPTY, so every one of the six costs some reader its subject and the declaration is a trade rather than a tidy-up.").

finding(f_the_reflection_tax_is_real_in_rows_and_invisible_in_time, insight).
recorded(f_the_reflection_tax_is_real_in_rows_and_invisible_in_time, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_reflection_tax_is_real_in_rows_and_invisible_in_time, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_reflection_tax_is_real_in_rows_and_invisible_in_time, "examples/ring1/ring1.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_reflection_tax_is_real_in_rows_and_invisible_in_time,
  "MEASURED ON THE SHIPPED MECHANISM against an UNSEALED TWIN of the same grammar - ring1.rofl with its one `sealed(rules).` clause struck out - interleaved ABABAB in one process, 7 repetitions of a whole 87-clause file, medians, load average 3.87 on 8 cores. STORE: 2450 -> 1994 facts, 18.6 per cent. KEY BYTES: 122434 -> 106418, 13.1 per cent. IMAGE: 544466 -> 478507 bytes, 12.1 per cent. FORK: 0.173 -> 0.141 ms, 18.5 per cent. PARSE PER CLAUSE: 4.559 -> 4.469 ms, 2.0 per cent. THE LAST NUMBER IS THE FINDING AND IT DEFLATES THE PREMISE THIS WORK STARTED FROM: the fork is 3.8 per cent of a clause, so cutting it by a fifth is 0.7 per cent of a clause and the rest is noise. An upper-bound arm - every about-row dropped except `has_premise`, 50 per cent of the store - measured 6.6 per cent on the same harness, so NO MECHANISM OF THIS KIND CAN BUY MORE THAN ABOUT 5 PER CENT OF A RING 1 PARSE. Anyone reaching for this for speed should read `npm run evalcost` instead: 66 per cent of a clause is the fixpoint and 51 per cent of the join width is in rules that conclude nothing. WHAT SEALING IS ACTUALLY FOR is therefore the refusal, not the rows: 456 fewer rows is a real memory and image saving that a long-running host or a shipped artefact may want, and the price is that five boot.rofl audits lose their subject - which the standing `hole` is what makes visible.").

finding(f_a_standing_refusal_makes_every_hole_check_vacuous, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_standing_refusal_makes_every_hole_check_vacuous, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_standing_refusal_makes_every_hole_check_vacuous, doc).
finding_note(f_a_standing_refusal_makes_every_hole_check_vacuous,
  "THE COST OF USING `hole` FOR A DECLARED REFUSAL, found by shipping it. `examples/ring1/demo.ts` refuses a parse when ANY hole stands, and that check is one of the three silent-empty modes the example exists to refuse. Sealing the grammar puts a permanent `hole($sealed(rules), reflection_sealed)` in that world, so the check had to start FILTERING BY REASON - and a check that filters is weaker than one that does not. Two consequences, both named rather than discovered later. First, the REASON ATOM IS LOAD-BEARING and not decoration: without a distinct atom the sentence `the kernel could not finish` and the sentence `the program declined to keep this` are indistinguishable, and every reader that treats holes as failures would either refuse a healthy world or ignore a real wall. That is the argument that justified widening scripts/kernel_grep.ts by one constant. Second, EVERY EXISTING `relCount(hole) > 0` ASSERTION IN A SEALED WORLD IS NOW TRIVIALLY TRUE - test/example-ring1.test.ts has two - so a gate written to catch an unfinished evaluation passes for a reason its author did not intend. Nothing is red today; the class is the one CLAUDE.md already names, a gate whose criterion is borrowed from the first tool that produced it. WHAT WOULD FIX IT PROPERLY: those assertions should count holes whose reason is NOT a declared refusal, the same filter `parse` now applies, and that is a change to a test this session did not own.").

finding(f_a_relation_the_evaluator_regenerates_cannot_be_ablated, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_relation_the_evaluator_regenerates_cannot_be_ablated, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_relation_the_evaluator_regenerates_cannot_be_ablated, rule_and_test).
addressed_by(f_a_relation_the_evaluator_regenerates_cannot_be_ablated, "rules/floor-census.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_relation_the_evaluator_regenerates_cannot_be_ablated,
  "THE INSTRUMENT'S OWN BLIND SPOT, and it was caught by the gate rather than by inspection. `rules/floor-census.rofl` derives `sealable` from the ablation; the kernel's `SEALED_BODY` lists it; test/sealed.test.ts compares the two SET FOR SET and went red on exactly one name - `derived_by`. The rules were right and the table was ahead of its evidence: `derived_by` is DERIVED, so removing it and re-evaluating puts it straight back, and every ablation arm over it reports `not_live` in every world and always will. That is a property of the relation, not a gap that more worlds would close. The fix is a clause that says so - `rederived(Rel) :- in_bucket(Rel, provenance).` and a separate sealability rule for it, whose test is who READS it rather than what breaks without it. THE GENERAL SHAPE: an ablation can only speak about a row the evaluation does not rebuild, and a verdict of `nothing changed` over a rebuilt row is not evidence of anything. The `not_live` verdict is in the emitted facts precisely so that a rule can refuse to draw a conclusion from it - a mutant that did not fire and a gate that cannot see are the same output, one level down from where this branch met that lesson yesterday.").

finding(f_a_number_in_a_comment_had_no_way_to_be_recomputed, defect).
recorded(f_a_number_in_a_comment_had_no_way_to_be_recomputed, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_number_in_a_comment_had_no_way_to_be_recomputed, doc).
finding_note(f_a_number_in_a_comment_had_no_way_to_be_recomputed,
  "`policy.rofl` line 6 says `22 of the 95 worlds in this repository never load boot.rofl`, and test/kernel-policy-program.test.ts restates it verbatim. NEITHER SITE COMPUTES IT and no script in the tree counts worlds - scanners/ledger_census.ts enumerates 42 programs, scanners/policy_ladder.ts has a corpus of 8. A second agent swept every commit from 2026-08-20 to HEAD computing `files constructing a Rofl, of which without boot.rofl` and the pair never reads 95/22: the series goes 21 -> 99 across the squash and then 99 -> 121. THE NUMERATOR REPRODUCES AND THE DENOMINATOR DOES NOT: at the claiming commit c022cf1, test files that construct a `Rofl` and never load boot.rofl were exactly 22 - of 62, not of 95. Today the same metric is 23 of 66 test worlds, or 31 of 121 store-building files. THE ARGUMENT THE SENTENCE CARRIES IS UNHARMED - about a quarter of the worlds here never load boot.rofl, so a rule that is not loaded is a wrong answer rather than a conservative one - and it should be restated with a number that has a script behind it. THE CLASS IS THE ONE THE WITNESS MECHANISM EXISTS FOR: a number written into a comment has no world, and the repository already knows that a premise which cannot be stated as a runnable query does not get a witness. Two more of the same kind were found in passing: examples/loot/loot.rofl:296 claims its four rules are `the only rules in this repository that read derived_by in a rule body` and examples/cram/flight_log.rofl:30 also does; flight_log's own header says `these four rules read derived_by in a body` where one does. And boot.rofl's `imports(main, $kernel)` comment credits `examples/rip 1` in its breakdown of 19 rules, where examples/rip reads no reflection at all today.").

finding(f_the_refusal_covers_one_of_four_doors_into_a_sealed_body, defect).
recorded(f_the_refusal_covers_one_of_four_doors_into_a_sealed_body, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_refusal_covers_one_of_four_doors_into_a_sealed_body, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_refusal_covers_one_of_four_doors_into_a_sealed_body,
  "FOUND BY PROBING MY OWN MECHANISM WHERE IT IS STRUCTURALLY UNABLE TO LOOK, which is the question CLAUDE.md says produces the survivors. `sealed(Body)` makes `Rofl.query` refuse - `partial: true` plus a `hole` row - and it makes the standing declaration a fact. It does NOT reach the other three doors, and the probe says so in their own words. `why writes_to(rbe5a09c1, main)` in a sealed world answers `does not hold; try: whynot ...`, the same sentence it gives for a rule that genuinely does not write that ledger. `whynot` answers `no rule concludes 'writes_to' and no matching base fact exists`, which is FALSE about the world - a rule does write it, the row is withheld - and is exactly the silent-empty shape the declaration exists to remove, arriving one door over. `excise` is untested here and reads the same store. So the refusal covers one of four question forms, and the three uncovered ones do not merely stay quiet, they ASSERT AN ABSENCE. The repair is one shape for all three: the sealed check that stands at the head of `query` (src/api.ts) belongs at the head of `why`, `whynot` and `excise` too, and it should be one predicate rather than four copies - the `orphan_claim[audit]` lesson in CLAUDE.md is that a check written by hand per entry point reopens the moment a fifth is added. `retract` is measured and is FINE: it sweeps the meta rows by key and finds none, which is correct when none were written. NOT REPAIRED IN THIS SESSION AND NOT HIDDEN: the mechanism is honest about the door it guards and silent about three, which is a weaker claim than the design makes and the number to quote is one of four.").
addressed_by(f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming, "boot.rofl").
addressed_by(f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming, "examples/aka/demo.ts").
addressed_by(f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming, "test/widened-negation.test.ts").

finding(f_the_loader_the_kernel_records_is_the_author_not_the_file, decision).
recorded(f_the_loader_the_kernel_records_is_the_author_not_the_file, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_loader_the_kernel_records_is_the_author_not_the_file, kernel_test).
addressed_by(f_the_loader_the_kernel_records_is_the_author_not_the_file, "boot.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_loader_the_kernel_records_is_the_author_not_the_file, "test/widened-negation.test.ts").
witness(f_the_loader_the_kernel_records_is_the_author_not_the_file, "widened[audit](Rel, P)", 0).
witness_atleast(f_the_loader_the_kernel_records_is_the_author_not_the_file, "negated_under(Rel, P)", 1).
witness_atleast(f_the_loader_the_kernel_records_is_the_author_not_the_file, "loader(Rel, P, W)", 1).
finding_note(f_the_loader_the_kernel_records_is_the_author_not_the_file,
  "f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming ASKED FOR AN AUDIT OF THE SHAPE `relation R is negated in book B and written by more than one loader`, and the first thing to settle was whether `loader` is expressible at all. IT IS, WITH NOTHING ADDED TO THE KERNEL, and the answer is `asserted_by`'s second argument. The two premises destructure reifications the kernel already writes: `premise_lit(R, _, $not($lit(Rel, P, _, _)))` carries the negated premise's BOOK, which is the half `safety.rofl`'s `neg_relation` does not have (it is negated-anywhere, book-blind), and `asserted_by($fact(Rel, P, _), Who, _)` carries the writer. safety.rofl already destructures `$builtin` the same way, so this is a second use of a shipped idiom. `perspExplicit` was NOT needed and is not used: it belongs to the file-local-default half of the finding, which was the move deliberately not taken. THREE RULES, NO NEW KERNEL RELATION, NO WIDENING OF scripts/kernel_grep.ts's ALLOWED SET, and no new `imports` line -- `negated_under` and `loader` derive into [main], `widened[audit]` reads them, and boot.rofl's existing `imports(audit, main)` and `imports(main, $kernel)` already license both crossings.\nWHAT IS MISSING, PRICED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. There is no LOAD identity anywhere in the store: src/api.ts counts loads and `$load(N)` reaches only a `hole` id, so the finest grain available is the principal a load named. Two files loaded with no `who` are both `user`, which src/reflect.ts defines as `whoever loaded a file and did not say who they are` -- so by the kernel's own definition they are one writer, and the audit is exactly as sharp as the host's discipline about `who`. Measured over 19 example worlds on this tree, counting only facts that carry an assertion trail: 32 (relation, book) pairs take facts from more than one LOAD, and 22 of those name one principal across every contributing load. The smallest addition that would close it is a fourth argument on `asserted_by` or a companion `asserted_in(F, LoadId)`; both are documented API changes -- `asserted_by` is read at arity 3 by src/reflect.ts's ARITY table, by `forged[audit]` and `unattributed[audit]`, and by the replay path that feeds the trail back to `assertClauses` -- and a new name would have to enter the kernel's closed vocabulary. It was not paid, because nothing in the corpus needs it.\nTHE RING IS SUBTRACTED FROM THE WRITER SET AND THAT IS NOT AN EXEMPTION LIST. Without `Who != $kernel` the audit reports 13 rows over those same 19 worlds, and every one of them is `edb` or `imports` in [main] with exactly two writers, `$kernel` plus one program -- the co-written declaration tables src/reflect.ts already documents as deliberate (`undefined_premise` reads `not edb(Rel)` once and must see both halves), and forcing them apart was already measured there to redden every honest program. With the subtraction, 0. It is written as a subtraction from the writer SET rather than an exemption keyed on the relation because `$kernel` plus alice plus bob is still two files, and a relation-keyed exemption would have gone quiet on exactly that; test/widened-negation.test.ts holds both directions. It is also not an off switch: src/api.ts refuses a caller-supplied `$` principal and the ring can be claimed only by the first clause of the first load, so no program can join `$kernel` to hide its second writer.\nNO WAIVER TABLE IS SHIPPED, on this file's own test -- a declaration that licenses nothing in the corpus derives no row and is a decoration, which is the argument boot.rofl already makes for not writing `collects(audit)`. If one is ever needed it is `collects`-shaped and named in the file. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: an honest program in this repository that goes red, which would mean the union between two named authors is ordinary rather than exceptional and the waiver has to ship after all; the likeliest first instance is a multi-author program in which two differently-signed sections both write `edb(...)`, since boot.rofl negates `edb` in [main] -- that is reported today and the test pins it.").

finding(f_no_two_files_in_this_corpus_ever_widened_a_negation, insight).
recorded(f_no_two_files_in_this_corpus_ever_widened_a_negation, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_no_two_files_in_this_corpus_ever_widened_a_negation, doc).
addressed_by(f_no_two_files_in_this_corpus_ever_widened_a_negation, "test/widened-negation.test.ts").
finding_note(f_no_two_files_in_this_corpus_ever_widened_a_negation,
  "THE PARENT FINDING SAID THE NEED WAS ANTICIPATED AND NOT OBSERVED, AND THAT IS CONFIRMED ON THIS TREE, more sharply than it was stated. Measured over 19 example worlds built by their own demos (aka, bleep, ditto, drip, goof x2, heck, jopa, nope, loot, moot, rip x3, ring1, oops, wtf, iffy x2) at load average 8: `widened[audit]` is 0 in all of them, and so is the intermediate `two distinct non-kernel writers of one relation in one book` -- not one relation anywhere in the corpus is co-written by two named authors, let alone negated. The 0 is a verdict and not an empty table: the same sweep counts 388 `negated_under` rows and 370 `loader` rows across those worlds, so both premises had something to range over.\nAT THE FILE LEVEL, WHICH IS COARSER THAN THE AUDIT AND CATCHES WHAT IT CANNOT, the answer is the same and the exceptions are informative. Counting facts that carry an assertion trail, 32 (relation, book) pairs take facts from more than one LOAD; 14 of those are also negated in the same book, and all 14 are `edb[main]`, `imports[main]` or `authority[main]` -- the kernel's own declaration tables, co-written on purpose. Zero domain relations. The one genuine two-file union of domain relations in the corpus is examples/iffy's statute world, where examples/jopa/facts.rofl and the generated case grid write eight `[record]` relations between them; the parent finding named `[record]` as the collision candidate and it turns out to be a deliberate union under ONE principal, `tribunal_of_fact`, with nothing negated over it. NOT A COLLISION.\nSO THE AUDIT SHIPS WITH A CONSTRUCTED LIVENESS CASE RATHER THAN A CORPUS HIT, and it is in examples/aka because that demo already loads one file per author. Integration suppresses one of its own bridges and reads its list back with `not suppressed[recon](B)`; finance suppresses another id into the same book; b1, an integration bridge nobody in integration touched, silently leaves `in_play`, 8 rows become 7, and `forged[audit]` and `leak[audit]` both stay 0 because both authors were granted the book in writing. That last part is the point: this is what an audit has to say about two writers who are BOTH entitled to be there, which is precisely the case the existing audits cannot reach.").

finding(f_the_forbidden_list_was_three_names_behind_boot_rofl, defect).
recorded(f_the_forbidden_list_was_three_names_behind_boot_rofl, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_forbidden_list_was_three_names_behind_boot_rofl, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_forbidden_list_was_three_names_behind_boot_rofl, "scripts/kernel_grep.ts").
finding_note(f_the_forbidden_list_was_three_names_behind_boot_rofl,
  "NOTICED WHILE ADDING TO IT, AND IT IS THE SHAPE THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY NAMES AS `a gate inherits the scope of its INCIDENT`. scripts/kernel_grep.ts's second check refuses any boot.rofl or appendix relation name appearing as a code identifier in src/, and it enumerates those names ONE BY ONE. The list ends at `undefined_premise`, which is where boot.rofl ended when it was written. Everything appended to boot.rofl since then was outside the check: `demands_authorship` and `unattributed` from the authorship block, and now the three of the widened-negation block. None of the five occurs as a code identifier in src/ today -- `unattributed`, `demands_authorship` and `widened` occur only inside comments, which the check strips -- so the hole cost nothing so far and adding them costs nothing either. THE REMEDY THE REPOSITORY PREFERS IS THE OTHER ONE and is not taken here: derive the list from boot.rofl's own `concludes`/`has_conclusion` rows instead of copying it, which is the same move that took range restriction out of the host and into rules. That is a change to a CI gate and belongs to whoever owns it; the list is brought level meanwhile, and this note is what makes the next omission visible.").

finding(f_three_rules_that_read_a_term_broke_two_hand_kept_shape_assumptions, pitfall).
recorded(f_three_rules_that_read_a_term_broke_two_hand_kept_shape_assumptions, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_three_rules_that_read_a_term_broke_two_hand_kept_shape_assumptions, doc).
addressed_by(f_three_rules_that_read_a_term_broke_two_hand_kept_shape_assumptions, "test/head-vars.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_three_rules_that_read_a_term_broke_two_hand_kept_shape_assumptions, "test/well-founded.test.ts").
finding_note(f_three_rules_that_read_a_term_broke_two_hand_kept_shape_assumptions,
  "ADDING THREE RULES TO boot.rofl COST FOUR TEST FAILURES IN TWO FILES THAT KNEW NOTHING ABOUT THEM, and both were the same mistake in different clothes: a check whose criterion was written against the SHAPES boot.rofl happened to contain at the time. Neither is a defect in the new rules and neither was a re-baseline; both are holes that were there and had nothing to trip them.\nONE. test/head-vars.test.ts carries a walker that computes range restriction inside ROFL and compares it against safety.rofl rule for rule. Its `prem_walk` descends `$cons`, `$lit`'s ARGUMENT LIST, and `$builtin`, and nothing else -- so a head variable bound from inside any other functor is invisible to it. `loader(Rel, P, Who) :- asserted_by($fact(Rel, P, _), Who, _)` and `negated_under(Rel, P) :- premise_lit(R, _, $not($lit(Rel, P, _, _)))` are the first rules in boot.rofl to do that, and the walker called them UNSAFE while safety.rofl called them safe. safety.rofl is right, and the asymmetry is the one src/reflect.ts already records: `premise_var` is SEEDED BY THE HOST because a term carries an arbitrary functor and a Datalog program can only destructure one it names. Fixed by naming the three the reflection actually uses -- `$not`, `$fact`, and `$lit`'s relation and perspective slots -- five lines, and the demo's two genuinely unsafe rules are still flagged by name, which is the control that matters. THE GENERAL FORM: a pure-ROFL reader of the reflection is complete only up to the functors it enumerates, and every new reified shape silently narrows it.\nTWO. test/well-founded.test.ts compares the stratified and well-founded models of examples/wtf fact for fact, minus the rows that CANNOT match because one arm was handed `semantics(well_founded).` and the other was not. That exclusion was keyed on the shapes the declaration takes -- a key starting `semantics[`, or containing `$fact(semantics,`. `loader(Rel, P, Who)` projects an asserted fact's RELATION NAME into an ordinary argument, so the declaration came back as `loader[main](semantics, main, user)` and its provenance row, matching neither pattern. Exactly two rows, both about the declaration, neither about the program. Excluded by name rather than by widening the filter to every key containing the word, because a wider filter hides a real difference the day one exists.\nWHY THIS IS WORTH A FINDING RATHER THAN A DIFF: both checks are of the class this repository already writes about -- a gate inheriting the scope of its INCIDENT -- and both failed in the direction that LOOKS like the new work is wrong. A new relation that reads a term, or that projects a relation name into an argument, is the perturbation that finds them, and there is no third instrument here that would have found either one first.").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-07: THE BACKLOG SWEEP, APPLIED. Four agents read all 219 open
-- findings against the tree and returned verdict tables; this block is those
-- verdicts turned into dispositions. Nothing above this line was edited --
-- a ledger records what turned out to be wrong as well as what turned out to
-- be right, so a stale finding keeps its note and gains a `dismissed`.
--
-- WHAT WAS CHECKED BEFORE APPLYING, because a wrongly-closed finding loses
-- real work permanently and a closed ledger is where nobody looks again: a
-- random sample of 24 of the 134 ADDRESSED verdicts (six per chunk, drawn by
-- seeded shuffle) was re-verified against the tree independently of the
-- report's evidence column, plus every one of the 7 STALE verdicts, since a
-- dismissal is the disposition that cannot be undone by reading. 23 of 24
-- confirmed and all 7 STALE confirmed. See
-- f_the_sweeps_only_error_was_the_disposition_and_not_the_fact.
--
-- THE SEPARATION THIS BLOCK KEEPS: `addressed_by` says THE DEMAND WAS
-- SATISFIED and names the artifact that satisfies it; `dismissed` says THE
-- DEMAND WENT AWAY and names why. Five findings the sweep called ADDRESSED
-- carry neither a `demands` clause nor any nameable artifact -- they are
-- journal entries whose content stands -- and conflating those with a
-- satisfied demand would write a false record, so they are dismissed by name.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


