finding(f_the_sweeps_only_error_was_the_disposition_and_not_the_fact, insight).
recorded(f_the_sweeps_only_error_was_the_disposition_and_not_the_fact, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_sweeps_only_error_was_the_disposition_and_not_the_fact, doc).
finding_note(f_the_sweeps_only_error_was_the_disposition_and_not_the_fact,
  "A NUMBER FOR AN LLM-RUN BACKLOG AUDIT, measured while applying one. Four agents swept 219 open findings against the tree, 55/55/55/54, and returned ADDRESSED 134, STALE 7, NEEDS OWNER 22, STILL OPEN 55, UNCLEAR 1. Before applying any of it: a random sample of 24 ADDRESSED verdicts, six per chunk by seeded shuffle, was re-verified against the tree WITHOUT reading the report's evidence column first, and all 7 STALE verdicts were verified too because a dismissal is the disposition nobody re-reads. TWENTY-THREE OF TWENTY-FOUR CONFIRMED, SEVEN OF SEVEN STALE CONFIRMED. A second, adversarial pass over the four evidence cells that ARGUED a case rather than pointing at a file - `superseded rather than patched`, `superseded upward`, `condition now met` - confirmed all four, though one needed the finding's last sentence read to see that the question it posed had in fact been answered by the mechanism that replaced it. AND A MECHANICAL CHECK OVER ALL 134: every backticked path in every ADDRESSED evidence cell was extracted and stat-ed, and ZERO named a file that does not exist. The claims were about the right tree.\nTHE ONE FAILURE WAS NOT A FALSE CLAIM ABOUT THE WORLD, IT WAS THE WRONG RELATION. Five of the 134 ADDRESSED carry no `demands` clause and name no artifact, because there is nothing to build - a practice lesson, a re-measurement, an observation about doc maintenance. `addressed_by` on one of those writes a false record twice over: it asserts an artifact that does not exist and it erases the difference between THE DEMAND WAS SATISFIED and THE DEMAND WENT AWAY. They are dismissed by name instead. THE SWEEP SAW THIS ABOUT ITSELF AND SAID SO IN ITS OWN SUMMARY - `I've flagged each of those explicitly as no demand so the owner can downgrade them to a plain dismissed if they'd rather keep the distinction sharp` - and two of the four reports independently downgraded verdicts of their own from ADDRESSED to STALE on review, on the grounds that nothing was built and the proposal was abandoned. So the auditor's judgement about ARTEFACTS was reliable at the sample rate and its judgement about WHICH RELATION TO WRITE was the part that needed a second reader, and it knew which was which.\nTHE OPERATIONAL RULE THAT FOLLOWS, and it is the transferable half: ASK A SWEEP FOR THE FACT AND KEEP THE DISPOSITION. `Is the demanded artifact in the tree, at what path` is a question with an oracle - open the file - and 23/24 plus 134/134 paths says an agent answers it well. `Which of addressed_by and dismissed does this deserve` is a judgement about what the ledger MEANS, it has no oracle, and it is where every error in this run lived. A brief that asks for both gets the first for free and the second unchecked.\nAND AN AUDIT'S ANSWER HAS A SHELF LIFE MEASURED IN HOURS HERE. Between the sweep starting and its verdicts being applied - one working day - THREE of its 219 ids had already been settled by commits it could not have seen, two of them ids it had classified NEEDS OWNER because the owner had not yet ruled (`f_the_canonical_witness_is_derivable_from_the_data`, settled by the least-signature commit; `f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming`, settled by the scoping merge), and FOUR new open findings had been recorded that no chunk covered. A sweep is a photograph, so the applier has to diff the ids before writing, and cross-checking the 219 against the ledger is also what proves no verdict names a typo'd id.").

finding(f_model_the_task_in_rofl_because_a_model_can_say_none, decision).
recorded(f_model_the_task_in_rofl_because_a_model_can_say_none, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_model_the_task_in_rofl_because_a_model_can_say_none, doc).
addressed_by(f_model_the_task_in_rofl_because_a_model_can_say_none, "CLAUDE.md").
finding_note(f_model_the_task_in_rofl_because_a_model_can_say_none,
  "DECIDED BY VADIM AS A WORKING METHOD, after a side experiment made the case better than the argument had. THE RULE: before reasoning in prose about a question this system could hold as facts, model it - scanner to facts to rules to report - and put the reasoning in the RULES rather than in the scanner or the summary. WHAT THE MODEL BUYS, stated precisely rather than as `better understanding`: A NEGATIVE OVER A POPULATION. A grep says `no textual tell here` and can never say `none`; a paragraph says `I looked and did not find` and the reader cannot check the looking; a derived relation says `299 of 345 defined functions provably cannot reach the key-spelling machinery` and anyone can re-run it. Every strong result on this branch has that shape - nDiffer 0 across nine worlds, widened[audit] 0 over nineteen with BOTH premises shown to have inputs, the intersection of variable-perspective reads with kernel-book relations EMPTY with a positive control on each half. And a second gain that the metacognitive audit named: a filter's danger is that its criterion is invisible while you look through it, and A RULE BODY IS THE CRITERION, written down and runnable, so the next reader argues with the rule instead of with a conclusion. THE TWO CLEAREST PAYMENTS. Asked for a model of the join instead of more profiling, scanners/eval_cost.ts found in ONE RUN what an hour of profiling had not - half a ring 1 parse concludes nothing and one rule was a fifth of it. And a ten-minute model of the kernel's own call graph - the scanner's `src_call` facts plus a transitive closure to factKey/canonTerm/joinSig/patSig/sigOf - answered a coupling question that a textual census had answered WITH A FLOOR IT ADMITTED TO, and reframed it: the key spelling sits on the PUBLIC API surface (Rofl.query, why, retract, excise, renderWhy are direct callers), not inside the store, which is the sharpest form of the finding that a numeric fact identity changes what the kernel RECORDS rather than how it stores it. THE MODEL ALSO REFUSED A MISTAKE RATHER THAN ANSWERING IT: `spells_key(factKey)` declared an ATOM while src_call carries STRINGS, and the query returned zero rows until the quotes went in. Prose would have accepted the conflation. THREE COSTS, ALL OBSERVED AND ALL WRITTEN INTO THE AGREEMENT: a model is itself a filter and a name-matched call graph conflates an imported canonTerm with a local one and cannot see a call through a callback, so it OVER-answers where a resolved graph would not; a coarse model and a textual census BRACKET the answer, one with a floor and one with a ceiling, so their agreement is worth more than either alone and neither replaces the other; and a model must not be built in the process it measures, which is the recorded trap where seven of the eight heaviest rules came out as the kernel checking itself. THE ANTI-PATTERN is a model nothing is asked of: if no rule reads the facts a scanner writes, it is a report with extra steps.").
-- Recorded 2026-09-07 while making `npm test` cheaper on the wall clock a
-- human sits through. Every number below is CPU measured with
-- `process.cpuUsage()` at each test child's exit, because the box carried two
-- other agents throughout and CPU is load-immune where wall time is not --
-- which is f_the_attestation_certifies_the_tree_and_not_the_machine acted on
-- rather than merely agreed with.

finding(f_node_test_sorts_its_file_list_so_argv_order_cannot_schedule_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_node_test_sorts_its_file_list_so_argv_order_cannot_schedule_it, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_node_test_sorts_its_file_list_so_argv_order_cannot_schedule_it, doc).
finding_note(f_node_test_sorts_its_file_list_so_argv_order_cannot_schedule_it,
  "A PLAN THAT DIED ON A ONE-MINUTE EXPERIMENT, AND THE EXPERIMENT IS THE FINDING. `node --test` parallelises BY FILE with a pool of availableParallelism() workers, and `npm test` hands it `test/*.test.ts`, which the shell expands alphabetically. Instrumented with a `--import` probe that logs each child process start, exit and cpuUsage: on 8 cores, 92 files, 204.2 s of CPU, the longest file is example-ring1 at 21.3 s and IT DID NOT START UNTIL t = 8.2 s, because eight earlier files held every worker. It finished last, at t = 38.8 s of a 38.9 s suite. That is the textbook makespan defect and LPT -- longest processing time first -- is its textbook answer, so a scripts/test_order.ts was written to emit the file list longest first. IT CHANGED NOTHING, and a four-file control said why: `node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/whynot test/ablate test/tick test/admission` runs ablate, admission, tick, whynot. THE RUNNER SORTS ITS FILE LIST AND THE ORDER IT IS GIVEN IS INERT. The ordering script was deleted and package.json put back. WHAT IS LEFT FOR ANYONE WHO WANTS LPT HERE: the only handle on the order is the FILENAME, so a long pole either gets renamed to sort early or gets SPLIT, and splitting is the better answer because it uses the pool instead of the ordering -- see f_the_suite_is_capacity_bound_and_the_one_pole_was_a_file. Also measured and also nothing: NODE_COMPILE_CACHE over the whole suite, warm, 205.9 s of CPU against 204.2 cold. Type stripping and compilation are not where this suite spends.").
addressed_by(f_node_test_sorts_its_file_list_so_argv_order_cannot_schedule_it, "test/example-ring1-self.test.ts").

finding(f_the_suite_is_capacity_bound_and_the_one_pole_was_a_file, insight).
recorded(f_the_suite_is_capacity_bound_and_the_one_pole_was_a_file, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_the_suite_is_capacity_bound_and_the_one_pole_was_a_file, doc).
finding_note(f_the_suite_is_capacity_bound_and_the_one_pole_was_a_file,
  "THE SUITE HAS TWO FLOORS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM WAS EVER THE BINDING ONE. Measured 2026-09-07, 8 uniform cores, per-file CPU from `process.cpuUsage()` at child exit: 92 files, 204.2 s of CPU, so the CAPACITY floor is 204.2/8 = 25.5 s; the longest single file was example-ring1 at 21.3 s, so the MAKESPAN floor was 21.3 s. Observed wall clock 29 to 33 s on a box carrying two other agents. Capacity is the larger of the two and the wall tracks it: the suite runs at about 690 per cent of one core and there is no idle capacity to spread work into. THE CONSEQUENCE FOR PARALLELISM INSIDE A FILE: a worker-thread pool (runtime/fork_pool.ts) moves CPU around without removing any, so on a capacity-bound suite it buys nothing and costs a pool setup -- and the tail where cores DO go idle is about 3 s of a 29 s run, with concurrency falling from 7 to 2 only in the last two seconds. It was not built here, and that is the measurement rather than a preference. WHAT DID PAY, in order of size: (1) SPLITTING THE LONG POLE, because the unit `node --test` schedules is the FILE -- the self-application sweep, 14.1 s of example-ring1 s 18.9, moved to test/example-ring1-self.test.ts and the two now run side by side on node s own pool, longest file 20.3 s -> 16.5 s in-suite and 19.0 -> 14.6 s alone; (2) REMOVING WORK, which lowers the capacity floor itself. Paired ABABAB over three rounds in one sitting, load average 4 to 15: wall 31/30/29 against 29/28/29, paired mean -1.3 s; process CPU 207/213/213 against 205/206/207, paired mean -5.0 s; summed child CPU 193.5/199.5/199.6 against 190.8/191.8/193.2, paired mean -5.6 s. The wall gain is small because the wall is capacity-bound and the CPU removed is 2.5 per cent of the total; the wall clock is worth about one eighth of every CPU second removed, and that is the exchange rate anyone optimising this suite should price against.").
addressed_by(f_the_suite_is_capacity_bound_and_the_one_pole_was_a_file, "test/example-ring1-self.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_suite_is_capacity_bound_and_the_one_pole_was_a_file, "test/js-callgraph.test.ts").

finding(f_build_once_fork_per_case_is_where_a_test_file_wastes_its_cpu, insight).
recorded(f_build_once_fork_per_case_is_where_a_test_file_wastes_its_cpu, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_build_once_fork_per_case_is_where_a_test_file_wastes_its_cpu, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_build_once_fork_per_case_is_where_a_test_file_wastes_its_cpu,
  "THE ONE SHAPE THAT REMOVES WORK RATHER THAN MOVING IT, and its size varies by two orders of magnitude across the files that have it, so it has to be measured per file rather than applied on faith. A test file that builds the SAME world per test can build it once and hand each test a `fork()`. Measured 2026-09-07: test/js-callgraph.test.ts builds boot.rofl, three rule files, two fact files and three babel scans into 25544 facts, TWELVE TIMES for twelve unmutated tests -- buildWorld 511.9 ms, fork 4.9 ms, the fork s first query re-running the fixpoint 37.8 ms, so 512 ms against 43. The file went 10.79 s -> 4.88 s of CPU, a 55 per cent cut and the largest single saving found. The same shape elsewhere is much smaller and the numbers say so: js-model s world() is 71.7 ms against 11.2 for fork-and-evaluate, 4.68 -> 4.48 s including a new gate; rofl-safety loads boot.rofl once per corpus file at 4.40 ms against 0.111 for a fork and also built a second Evaluation per file purely to count rules, 6.70 -> 6.01 s; example-ring1 s shape sweep restored its image per shape at 3.09 ms against 0.20 for a fork, and that test alone went 3.56 -> 1.79 s. example-loot was MEASURED AND LEFT ALONE -- world() is 18.5 ms warm against 4.85 for fork-and-evaluate, about 0.2 s over the whole file, which does not pay for the churn. WHY THE FORK MUST NOT BECOME A SHARED WORLD: `Rofl.query` is not read-only. `ensure` evaluates into the store, and a sealed or budget-cut query writes a `hole` row. AND A CLONE IS ALWAYS DIRTY -- `store.clone()` sets `dirty = true`, so a fork of a settled world re-runs its fixpoint on first use; that is why the saving is 512 against 43 rather than 512 against 5, and why a file whose per-test work is small gains little.").
addressed_by(f_build_once_fork_per_case_is_where_a_test_file_wastes_its_cpu, "test/js-callgraph.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_build_once_fork_per_case_is_where_a_test_file_wastes_its_cpu, "test/js-model.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_build_once_fork_per_case_is_where_a_test_file_wastes_its_cpu, "test/rofl-safety.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_build_once_fork_per_case_is_where_a_test_file_wastes_its_cpu, "test/example-ring1.test.ts").

finding(f_a_shared_world_is_invisible_to_every_test_that_only_reads, defect).
recorded(f_a_shared_world_is_invisible_to_every_test_that_only_reads, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_a_shared_world_is_invisible_to_every_test_that_only_reads, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_shared_world_is_invisible_to_every_test_that_only_reads,
  "ASKED OF MY OWN CHANGE WHERE ITS CHECKS ARE STRUCTURALLY UNABLE TO LOOK, and the answer was the whole file. With `store.clone()` mutated to return the store ITSELF -- a view, not a fork -- all SEVENTEEN tests of test/js-callgraph.test.ts stayed green. They only read, and a shared world is invisible to a reader right up until something writes. So the isolation a build-once-fork-per-case rests on has no witness unless one is written, and four have been: one per file, each asserting that a fork equals a fresh build on `canonicalState()` and on arrival order, that a write to a fork leaves the template s fact count and canonical state unmoved, and that the NEXT case does not see the last one s writes. MUTANT SET OF ELEVEN, WITH LIVENESS CHECKED PER MUTANT, because a mutant that does not mutate proves nothing. KILLED: clone returns `this` (all four gates); clone shares the fact RECORDS instead of copying them (one gate, and only after a fourth claim was added -- `store.add` writes `existing.base = true` and `advanceTick` writes `rec.frozen = true` IN PLACE, so an aliased record is a live hazard and a set-level comparison cannot see it); clone fills its fact map in reverse arrival order (all four); clone drops one real fact (all four); clone drops the firings map (two of four -- the ring 1 image has ZERO firings, so its gate is structurally blind to witnesses and says so); clone drops derivedKeys (one of four); and four test-level mutants, each deleting the `.fork()` from one call site (each killed by its own file s gate). NOT LIVE, and therefore proving nothing rather than surviving: `stratum` facts dropped (none of the three base worlds holds one), sorted-instead-of-arrival keys (arrival order IS sorted order in all three, so the mutation is a no-op), tickLog dropped (empty in all three). SURVIVED HONESTLY: evalLog dropped, which `canonicalState` excludes ON PURPOSE and documents -- the gate is correctly blind there.").
addressed_by(f_a_shared_world_is_invisible_to_every_test_that_only_reads, "test/js-callgraph.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_shared_world_is_invisible_to_every_test_that_only_reads, "test/js-model.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_shared_world_is_invisible_to_every_test_that_only_reads, "test/rofl-safety.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_shared_world_is_invisible_to_every_test_that_only_reads, "test/example-ring1.test.ts").

finding(f_allfactkeys_sorts_so_it_cannot_witness_arrival_order, defect).
recorded(f_allfactkeys_sorts_so_it_cannot_witness_arrival_order, "2026-09-07").
demands(f_allfactkeys_sorts_so_it_cannot_witness_arrival_order, doc).
finding_note(f_allfactkeys_sorts_so_it_cannot_witness_arrival_order,
  "A RECORDED MEASUREMENT THAT CITED THE WRONG INSTRUMENT, found by mutating what it claimed to measure. examples/ring1/demo.ts said of the fork it introduced on 2026-09-06 that `allFactKeys` agrees element for element, WHICH IS THE ARRIVAL ORDER THE FORK WAS TAUGHT TO PRESERVE. It does agree. It says nothing whatever about order: `allFactKeys()` is `[...this.facts.keys()].sort()`, three lines below `allFacts()`, whose own comment says it is arrival order and is DELIBERATELY NOT SORTED. Measured with a mutant that fills a clone s fact map in reverse -- every `allFactKeys` comparison in this repository slept through it; every `allFacts()` comparison killed it immediately. The comment is corrected and the four new isolation gates read `allFacts()`. THE CLASS IS THE ONE CLAUDE.md ALREADY NAMES -- a gate whose criterion is borrowed from whichever call happened to be at hand -- and the tell here is specific and reusable: an ORDER claim asserted through an instrument that sorts is not weak, it is empty, and it reads exactly like a strong one.").
addressed_by(f_allfactkeys_sorts_so_it_cannot_witness_arrival_order, "examples/ring1/demo.ts").
addressed_by(f_allfactkeys_sorts_so_it_cannot_witness_arrival_order, "test/example-ring1.test.ts").

-- ====================================================================-- 2026-09-08 — the red baseline, and what it actually was
-- ====================================================================
finding(f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary, defect).
recorded(f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary, kernel_test).
witness(f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary, "conclusion_tense(R, next)", 2).
addressed_by(f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary, "boot.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary, "test/bridges.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary, "examples/sus/sus.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary,
  "BARE boot.rofl, NOTHING ELSE LOADED, ONE TICK: leak[audit] reports THREE ROWS and all three are boot.rofl's own crossings - $kernel -> audit, $kernel -> main, main -> audit - the very walks its three imports facts declare. The declarations are gone by then. src/api.ts gives an asserted fact scope 'tick' unless its relation is RESERVED or is `semantics` or `sealed`; imports and collects are neither, because they are boot.rofl's host data and not kernel vocabulary, so they are dropped at the boundary with everything else the tick held, and the audit then reports every crossing the program ever declared, on a program nobody changed. MEASURED OVER THE CORPUS, 55 programs - every examples/* world, every rules/*.rofl and facts/*.rofl layer, safety.rofl and policy.rofl - each loaded on boot.rofl and evaluated, then advanced one tick and evaluated again: leak[audit] 17 rows at load and 237 after the tick, with 43 of the 55 clean at load and dirty after it. The floor is 3, this file's own, and it is charged to every program in the tree. THE KERNEL ALREADY KNOWS THIS CLASS AND SAID SO: the comment beside that line in src/api.ts reads `a declaration about HOW the world is kept must not be droppable at a tick boundary, or the world quietly starts keeping again`, written when `semantics` was measured reverting a program to two-valued negation at tick 1 and diverging. examples/npc PAID FOR IT BY HAND, two carry rules with the reason written next to them; examples/sus did NOT, and its imports table was empty from tick 1 onward - which is what test/example-sus.test.ts was catching, from a great distance, as a support-cycle count of 19 against 25, because sees loses the case-worlds two-cycle when the mutual import evaporates. THE FIX IS THE LANGUAGE'S OWN AND NOT THE HOST'S: two clauses appended to boot.rofl, `imports(P, Q) @next :- imports(P, Q).` and `collects(X) @next :- collects(X).`, so it is said once for every program that loads the file. Adding the two names to the timeless list in src/api.ts was refused - the kernel is closed against domain vocabulary on purpose and a program can already say this in one line. AFTER: corpus leak 237 -> 17 after one tick, identical to the load count, and the LOAD count is unchanged, so the audit did not go blind. rules/js-resolve.rofl, the layer named as the latent risk because it is built on the same construction, went from leak 0 at load and 10 after a tick to 0 and 0. Six mutants in test/bridges.test.ts, 13-18, each checked for liveness against a boot.rofl with the clause struck.").

finding(f_carrying_the_import_graph_costs_six_relations_of_reuse, insight).
recorded(f_carrying_the_import_graph_costs_six_relations_of_reuse, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_carrying_the_import_graph_costs_six_relations_of_reuse, decision).
finding_note(f_carrying_the_import_graph_costs_six_relations_of_reuse,
  "THE PRICE OF f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary, measured rather than assumed, and the alternative that would not pay it. src/engine.ts's reuse plan marks a relation opaque when any rule concluding it stages '@next' - `the head stages instead of materialising` - so carrying imports makes imports opaque and everything reading it goes with it. MEASURED, on eight programs (bare boot.rofl, npc, sus, goof, loot, wtf, iffy and the six-file js-* rule stack), by diffing the derivedKeys map with and without the two clauses: the loss is EXACTLY SIX RELATIONS and the same six every time - sees, crossing, leak, gathered, collects_from, collected. Not one relation belonging to any program is among them, and flows_to - the transitive closure, the one that would actually have cost something - is NOT among them either. examples/npc loses nothing at all, because it was already paying this: its own carry rules were in the file. THE WALL-CLOCK PRICE, measured on examples/wtf, the largest program in the corpus, as 40 re-evaluations after a one-fact assertion, six alternating repetitions, min-of-repeats: 1949 ms without the carry and 2068 ms with it, a ratio of 1.06. THE ALTERNATIVE, AND IT IS AN OWNER'S CALL. A kernel interface relation - `timeless(imports).` beside `semantics` and `sealed` - would let a PROGRAM declare that a relation's facts outlive the tick, generically, in kernel vocabulary, without a rule and therefore without opacity. It is a documented API addition with a LIMITS entry and a new name in the closed vocabulary, and it was not taken here because the rules-side fix works today and costs 1.06 on the worst case in the tree. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THE PRICING: a program whose own relations sit downstream of sees or crossing, which would put a program relation on that list of six and change the ratio; nothing in this corpus reads them from a rule body, but the host-side reads in test files are 21 and were counted for a different reason.").

finding(f_a_corpus_sweep_that_only_loads_misses_what_a_runtime_load_makes, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_corpus_sweep_that_only_loads_misses_what_a_runtime_load_makes, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_corpus_sweep_that_only_loads_misses_what_a_runtime_load_makes, rule_and_test).
witness(f_a_corpus_sweep_that_only_loads_misses_what_a_runtime_load_makes, "leak[audit](A, B)", 0).
addressed_by(f_a_corpus_sweep_that_only_loads_misses_what_a_runtime_load_makes, "examples/loot/demo.ts").
finding_note(f_a_corpus_sweep_that_only_loads_misses_what_a_runtime_load_makes,
  "f_there_is_no_instrument_for_a_nameless_reader_of_a_named_book records a sweep that closed the corpus with 38 declarations across 10 programs and brought the whole tree to leak 0. It missed one, and the reason is structural rather than careless: THE SWEEP MEASURED WORLDS AS THEY LOAD, and examples/loot has a crossing that does not exist until a book is READ AT RUNTIME. `venom_sign[codex_of_thorns](T) :- beast[world](T), glows[world](T).` arrives with the codex, reads [world] and writes the book's own ledger, and world -> codex_of_thorns was undeclared - so loot's audits were clean on a bare world and red on a world with books in it, which is the only world the demo is about. It was the fourth of the seven red tests and had nothing to do with variable perspectives. THE FIX IS WHERE THE ACT IS: install() records imports(mind, P) already, and now records imports(P, world) beside it, because installing a book is the act that lets its rules read the world the reader stands in. unload() had to learn the same symmetry - it matched imports facts by their SECOND argument only, so a licence written the other way round would have outlived the book it belonged to, which is exactly the orphan the failing test was named for. THE CLASS: a corpus audit keyed on load time cannot see a program whose rule set grows at runtime, and this repository has exactly one such program. A sweep that reports `the whole tree is at zero` should say which worlds it built.").

-- and the two findings this work settles
addressed_by(f_a_mutual_import_makes_sees_cyclic_and_changes_a_counting_verdict, "test/example-heck.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_leak_audit_now_fires_on_a_ledger_polymorphic_head, "boot.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_leak_audit_now_fires_on_a_ledger_polymorphic_head, "test/bridges.test.ts").

finding(f_one_cause_for_seven_reds_was_four_and_the_dismissed_one_was_the_real_one, pitfall).
recorded(f_one_cause_for_seven_reds_was_four_and_the_dismissed_one_was_the_real_one, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_one_cause_for_seven_reds_was_four_and_the_dismissed_one_was_the_real_one, doc).
addressed_by(f_one_cause_for_seven_reds_was_four_and_the_dismissed_one_was_the_real_one, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_one_cause_for_seven_reds_was_four_and_the_dismissed_one_was_the_real_one,
  "f_the_leak_audit_now_fires_on_a_ledger_polymorphic_head opened `SEVEN TESTS ARE RED AND THE CAUSE IS ONE ROW`, and named the seventh - examples/sus, 19 against 25 - as the one it could not account for. MEASURED, six days later, one test at a time: FOUR causes, and the one the finding set aside was the only one with a defect behind it. (1) sus: boot.rofl's own ledger declarations expire at the tick boundary, corpus-wide, 17 leak rows at load against 237 after one tick - a real defect in a real file, and the 19-against-25 was its shadow, sees losing a two-cycle when a mutual import evaporated. (2) examples/heck: the audits were ALREADY EMPTY and the red was a POSITIVE CONTROL asserting `bridge_decl(R, F, T)` non-empty, a kernel relation that no longer exists and cannot be populated - permanently red and about nothing, and f_a_mutual_import_makes_sees_cyclic_and_changes_a_counting_verdict had already predicted both this erasure and the sus one, and prescribed the repair actually used (re-aim the control at a pair that still crosses). (3) examples/loot: an ordinary missing declaration, world -> codex_of_thorns, visible only after a book is read at runtime. (4) examples/goof and examples/npc, four tests: the recorded LANGUAGE GAP - there is no sentence for a nameless reader of a named book - which the .rofl sources themselves say is left reporting on purpose and which demands(decision). THE ERROR IS NOT THE MISCOUNT. A shared signature - `leak: 1` - was read as a shared cause, and the one failure whose signature did NOT match was set aside as probably separate. It was the same cause as nothing else and the only one that was a defect. THE CHEAP DEFENCE, and it took eight minutes: run the audit on each failing world and PRINT THE ROWS, not the count. Three of the four causes are visible in the first three lines of that output - loot's pair is two NAMED ledgers, heck's leak set is empty, goof's row set differs from npc's - and the fourth needs one tick. AND THE DIAGNOSIS AGED IN A SPECIFIC DIRECTION: the finding's own measurement (crossing 3, gathered 2, leak 1 over NPC at tick 1) reproduced EXACTLY, so it was not wrong about what it looked at, only about what it had not.").

addressed_by(f_the_paired_timing_test_still_flakes, "test/index-arrival.test.ts").

finding(f_a_minimum_is_the_timing_estimator_a_busy_machine_cannot_inflate, insight).
recorded(f_a_minimum_is_the_timing_estimator_a_busy_machine_cannot_inflate, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_minimum_is_the_timing_estimator_a_busy_machine_cannot_inflate, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_minimum_is_the_timing_estimator_a_busy_machine_cannot_inflate, "test/index-arrival.test.ts").
finding_note(f_a_minimum_is_the_timing_estimator_a_busy_machine_cannot_inflate,
  "THE FIX f_the_paired_timing_test_still_flakes ASKED FOR, and the argument for it, because the argument transfers and the code does not. Load, GC and preemption only ever ADD time to a run; none of them can make one finish faster than the work in it. So over k repetitions the SMALLEST reading is the closest one to the work, and the noise lives in the runs that get thrown away - where a single shot has to keep whatever it drew. Interleaving the two sides is the second half: a spike lasting longer than one run is then charged to both and cancels in the ratio instead of landing on one. MEASURED on test/index-arrival.test.ts section 4, the paired arrival-order ratio that was flaking: min of 5 interleaved repetitions, six runs of the file, THREE OF THEM UNDER SIX SPINNING NODE PROCESSES on this machine - 0.75x, 0.81x, 0.87x, 0.94x, 0.99x, 1.02x against a bound of 3. The recorded flake was 7.99x under suite load and 1.41x alone on a tree nobody had changed. AND THE SECOND HALF OF THE REPAIR IS A CONTROL, because a bound nothing can cross is not a bound and this one now reads about 1: the same instrument is pointed at the behaviour being guarded against - per-fact insertion into a sorted array, thirty lines of array handling that touch no kernel file and so cannot rot with it - and reads 8.6x to 10.2x at n = 64000 over the same six runs, against 24x recorded when the defect was live in the store. The guard and its control are measured by the same function, so a flaky instrument shows up in the control rather than only in the guard. A THIRD DEFECT WAS FOUND WHILE DOING IT, in the sibling test: timing `drop` meant rebuilding the layer between repetitions, and the first version timed the rebuild too, which read 1.5x for a reason that has nothing to do with removal. The helper takes a trial that returns ITS OWN elapsed time rather than a closure it wraps, and the honest number is 0.45x to 0.54x.").

finding(f_a_gate_asserted_more_than_clone_promises_and_two_merges_found_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_gate_asserted_more_than_clone_promises_and_two_merges_found_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_gate_asserted_more_than_clone_promises_and_two_merges_found_it, kernel_test).
addressed_by(f_a_gate_asserted_more_than_clone_promises_and_two_merges_found_it, "test/js-model.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_gate_asserted_more_than_clone_promises_and_two_merges_found_it, "test/rofl-safety.test.ts").
finding_note(f_a_gate_asserted_more_than_clone_promises_and_two_merges_found_it,
  "TWO BRANCHES EACH GREEN ALONE, RED TOGETHER, AND NEITHER WAS WRONG. One branch made test files build a world once and fork per case, gating it with `a fork carries the base and nothing else`; the other made boot.rofl carry `imports`/`collects` across the tick boundary. Merged, one gate went red. WHAT IT WAS NOT: the fact set is the same 1728, and `canonicalState` is BYTE-IDENTICAL. Only arrival order moved, from position 1393. WHAT IT WAS: the gate asserted `arrival(world()) == arrival(freshBuild)`, and `store.clone` promises nothing of the kind - it answers `allFacts` in the ORIGINAL's arrival order and says nothing about agreeing with a world someone else built. The stronger form passed by accident until carrying `imports` changed which relations are re-derived, and therefore the order the derived layer is appended in. TWO SEPARATE CORRECTIONS WERE NEEDED and the first was not enough: comparing the fork against its TEMPLATE still failed, because `world()` EVALUATES the fork - `clearDerived` plus re-derivation appends in firing order, which is not the order the template accumulated in. The clone contract is about what a copy CARRIES, not about what a fixpoint REBUILDS, so the assertion is now taken on an unevaluated `BASE.fork()`. THE GATE IS STRONGER, NOT WEAKER, and that was measured rather than hoped: a real reverse-arrival mutant in `Store.clone` kills FOUR gates where it previously killed them through a comparison that also fired on honest change. AND I WALKED INTO THE HOLE THE SAME SESSION HAD JUST RECORDED, twice. First I diagnosed with `allFactKeys()`, which SORTS, and read `same ORDER? true` off an accessor that cannot witness order - the exact defect recorded hours earlier as f_allfactkeys_sorts_so_it_cannot_witness_arrival_order. Then I planted a reverse mutant whose pattern did not match the loop, checked the count, saw `1`, and nearly read a verdict off a comment - the exact defect recorded as `a mutant that does not mutate proves nothing`. Both were caught by the discipline of asserting the planting and re-reading the instrument, and neither by attention. A recorded lesson does not protect its own recorder.").

finding(f_the_tuple_pool_is_one_repair_wearing_a_weighted_average, insight).
recorded(f_the_tuple_pool_is_one_repair_wearing_a_weighted_average, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_tuple_pool_is_one_repair_wearing_a_weighted_average, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_tuple_pool_is_one_repair_wearing_a_weighted_average, "rust/rofl/src/store.rs").
finding_note(f_the_tuple_pool_is_one_repair_wearing_a_weighted_average,
  "HASH-CONSING A FACT'S ARGUMENT TUPLE WAS PROPOSED AS THE BIGGEST STRUCTURAL WIN LEFT IN THE RUST STORE AND IT IS NOT ONE - it is a targeted repair of a single pathological case, and the weighted average hides that completely. MEASURED as an A/B on ONE tree, pool off against pool on, same corpus, same 27 cases, both arms 27/27 byte-identical: weighted over 74073 facts the Rust store goes 370 -> 296 B/fact, which reads as a general twenty per cent and as 2.12x -> 2.65x against JS. EXCLUDING `rip` IT IS 301 -> 299, that is 2.58x -> 2.59x, A WASH. Every bit of the weighted win is one case. WHAT THE ONE CASE IS. `rip` runs `semantics(well_founded)`, and the alternating fixpoint cannot reclaim: a dropped record must stay READABLE for its key to remain spellable, because the numeric identity carries no key, so `clearDerived` cannot free the record arena - 131072 record slots standing for 5678 live facts, 1196.9 B/fact, the ONE case where the Rust engine was WORSE than JS (0.74x). Pooling the tuples takes it to 260.2, minus 78.3 per cent, 3.40x, and the regression is gone. AND THE COST IS REAL AND IN THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION: where sharing is low the pool is pure overhead - sus +5.0 per cent, drip +3.7, spat +2.2, goof +1.4, bleep +1.2 - which is exactly what the agent that started this work said in its last line before an API error ended it. It was right about the mechanism and the conclusion still goes the other way: a one to five per cent loss on five cases buys the removal of the only case this engine loses on, and a case at 0.74x is a case that says the representation does not work. THE GENERAL SHAPE, and it is why this is a finding rather than a diff: A WEIGHTED AVERAGE OVER CASES OF UNEQUAL PATHOLOGY REPORTS THE PATHOLOGY AS A TREND. The honest report of this change is two numbers, not one - what it does to the sick case and what it does to the healthy ones - and either alone is a misreading. Same shape as the isolated-axis rule this repository already records, one level up: there an axis measurement over-predicted an end-to-end win, here an end-to-end average over-generalised a single-case repair.").

finding(f_the_port_oracle_could_not_fail_on_staging_and_now_can, defect).
recorded(f_the_port_oracle_could_not_fail_on_staging_and_now_can, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_port_oracle_could_not_fail_on_staging_and_now_can, kernel_test).
addressed_by(f_the_port_oracle_could_not_fail_on_staging_and_now_can, "scripts/port_corpus.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_port_oracle_could_not_fail_on_staging_and_now_can, "rust/run_corpus.sh").
finding_note(f_the_port_oracle_could_not_fail_on_staging_and_now_can,
  "THE SECOND ENGINE PASSED 27 OF 27 BYTE FOR BYTE AND ITS OWN MUTANT SET SAID WHY THAT WAS CHEAPER THAN IT LOOKED: deleting the `@next` staging path ENTIRELY survived. Seven corpus cases carried `@next` rules and not one of them advanced a tick, so staging was unreachable from outside and an engine that did not implement it at all was indistinguishable from one that did. The agent that built the engine reported this against itself rather than leaving it to be discovered, which is the reason it was cheap to fix. THE FIX is a second family in the corpus: for every world with a `@next` rule OF ITS OWN - more than the two `boot.rofl` now carries for `imports` and `collects`, which would exercise the boundary without exercising the program - the same seed is emitted with three ticks of `tickAdvance` and the canonical state at the end. Seven twins: counter.t3, cram.t3, npc.t3, oops.t3, sus.t3, tm.t3, tm_diverge.t3, from 647 to 6395 facts. Each is admitted only under the same test as the plain cases, that the REFERENCE reproduces it from the seed, so no case is an oracle the first engine fails. MEASURED IMMEDIATELY, which is the whole point: the Rust engine goes from 27/27 to 27 pass and SEVEN FAIL, every one diffing on `tick 3` against `tick 0`. An oracle that cannot fail is not an oracle, and this one had been reported green for a whole sitting. AND A SECOND DEFECT FELL OUT OF THE SAME RUN: the binary accepted `--ticks` and SILENTLY IGNORED IT rather than refusing an unknown flag. Had the ticked cases been added without changing the engine, a harness passing an unrecognised flag and reading a pass would have looked like an implementation that ticks. Same shape as the three `skipped` tests that stood in the node baseline all day because the runner does not forward V8 flags to children - a gate that is silently not running reads exactly like a gate that is passing.").

-- Recorded 2026-09-08 by the second engine, measuring the NEW contract
-- against the old one on all 34 cases plus a 24-mutant set (rust/mutants.sh
-- now reports loose, strict and unit verdicts side by side). The Rust engine
-- passes 34 of 34 under BOTH dumps; these four are what the comparison said.

finding(f_the_loose_contract_is_not_blind_to_the_witness_pick_on_a_well_founded_program, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_loose_contract_is_not_blind_to_the_witness_pick_on_a_well_founded_program, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_loose_contract_is_not_blind_to_the_witness_pick_on_a_well_founded_program, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_loose_contract_is_not_blind_to_the_witness_pick_on_a_well_founded_program,
  "scripts/derivations.ts was adopted on the verified claim that switching the canonical witness from least-signature to first-arrival moves canonicalState on sensors, goof and spat and leaves the derivations dump BYTE-IDENTICAL. Re-measured across all 34 cases by the second engine: it holds on 33 and FAILS ON ONE. The mutant kills the strict dump on 27 cases and the loose dump on exactly one - examples/rip, the corpus's only semantics(well_founded) world - moving 32 lines of its derivations dump. The cause is not the dump: it is that under well-founded semantics the reference itself READS the pick to decide what to KEEP. Store.allWitnesses (src/store.ts:709) builds a table of ONE witness per fact - witnessOf, the least signature - and the alternating fixpoint carries that table across a clearDerived that empties the live one, so on a well-founded program the canonical pick collapses the support hypergraph rather than merely rendering it. Which derivation an engine renders first IS presentation on a stratified program and IS a consequence of derivation on a well-founded one, and the corpus contains exactly one of the latter, which is why the three worlds sampled before adoption all agreed. The blindness claim is right about the intent and needs the qualifier; and the right place to argue about it is whether allWitnesses should keep the whole hypergraph across an alternation round.").

finding(f_the_loose_contract_cannot_see_when_a_derivation_fired, insight).
recorded(f_the_loose_contract_cannot_see_when_a_derivation_fired, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_loose_contract_cannot_see_when_a_derivation_fired, decision).
finding_note(f_the_loose_contract_cannot_see_when_a_derivation_fired,
  "MEASURED BY A MUTANT AIMED AT THE TICK BOUNDARY, and it is the one place the loose dump is weaker than the strict one rather than stronger. src/store.ts:658 reads a staged fact's provenance out before the drop and puts it back after it, so a fact that is staged into the next tick every tick keeps the witness of the FIRST tick that concluded it - Store.support dedups on rule and premises and not on the tick, so the re-firing at each boundary is a duplicate and the original date stands. A mutant that skips the restore moves canonicalState on all 7 ticked cases and leaves the derivations dump BYTE-IDENTICAL on all 7: the only difference is the witness's tick, tm.t3 reading `wit imports[main](audit,$kernel) <- r205f033b@1` correctly and `@3` under the mutant, and sigOf (src/engine.ts:1801) is `ruleId|prem|prem` with no tick in it. So `derivations` records WHICH derivations exist and not WHEN each first fired, which for a temporal kernel is arguably a consequence rather than presentation. It is not a hole in the tick boundary's dating in general - the boundary's own derived_by(Fact, Rule, Tick) rows ARE facts in the dump, and a mutant that dates the crossing to the tick just ended is killed by both - it is specifically the witness's date. One extra field on the `d` line would close it; whether it should is a decision, because a date is also the thing most likely to differ legitimately between two engines that schedule differently.").

finding(f_the_tick_log_is_the_one_key_order_the_loosening_does_not_free, insight).
recorded(f_the_tick_log_is_the_one_key_order_the_loosening_does_not_free, "2026-09-08").
finding_note(f_the_tick_log_is_the_one_key_order_the_loosening_does_not_free,
  "scripts/derivations.ts sorts the tick log AT EXPORT, which frees the order of the LINES - and a tickLog line is `tick N: k1 k2 k3 ...`, one string that embeds both the spelling AND THE ORDER of every key staged at that boundary (src/api.ts:1060). So the loosening does not reach inside the line: a mutant that stages in reversed key order is killed by the loose dump on all 7 ticked cases, exactly as it is by the strict one. This is the whole of what the tick boundary must materialise a key for in a second engine; everything else it does is order-free and can be asked of sets - quiescence, for instance, is sameKeySet (src/api.ts:113) and a store with a numeric identity can answer it by comparing SETS of (relation, perspective, arguments) without spelling anything, which is a strictly better shape than the reference's because it has no order to borrow at all. Worth recording because a porter told `the contract no longer pins key order` will look for the staging sort and find it load-bearing anyway, and the reason is one join(' ') in the reference.").

finding(f_neither_dump_ever_forced_this_ports_spelling_order, insight).
recorded(f_neither_dump_ever_forced_this_ports_spelling_order, "2026-09-08").
finding_note(f_neither_dump_ever_forced_this_ports_spelling_order,
  "the loosening is right and the cost attribution is off by one, measured rather than argued. The decision records that holding a second engine to canonicalState is what forced the Rust store's 8-byte per-fact sortkey and a numeric identity carrying the spelling as an ordering function. Measured over 34 cases and three mutants aimed at exactly that machinery: relPersp answering in ARRIVAL order survives, the run comparator deliberately REVERSED survives, and the sort prefix disabled survives - all three under the STRICT dump as well as the loose one. The reason is that canonicalState sorts its own keys at export too (src/store.ts:719), so the store's internal run order was never observable through either instrument; what asked for it was the PORT CONSTRAINT as written - answer in canonical key order, because relPersp promises it - and no gate this repository owns ever checked that promise. The saving is therefore available under the old contract too, and it is small and now numbered: 79304 distinct argument tuples across the corpus at 8 bytes each is 634432 bytes, 2.35% of the 27.0 MB the 34 worlds cost live, ranging 1.41% (tm) to 3.44% (sus.t3). What the loosening genuinely buys is the FUTURE constraint the decision names - a columnar store that spills to disk cannot maintain a total spelling order over non-resident columns - and that is a design freedom rather than 2.35% of a heap.").

finding(f_the_contract_is_the_consequences_of_derivation_not_the_bytes, decision).
recorded(f_the_contract_is_the_consequences_of_derivation_not_the_bytes, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_contract_is_the_consequences_of_derivation_not_the_bytes, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_contract_is_the_consequences_of_derivation_not_the_bytes, "scripts/derivations.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_contract_is_the_consequences_of_derivation_not_the_bytes, "scripts/port_corpus.ts").
finding_note(f_the_contract_is_the_consequences_of_derivation_not_the_bytes,
  "DECIDED BY VADIM: a second engine owes COMPATIBILITY OF THE CONSEQUENCES OF DERIVATION, not byte-identical state, and those are different things. WHAT THE OLD CONTRACT WAS ACTUALLY BUYING, and it was three things at once: `canonicalState` compares which facts hold, which derivations produced them, AND HOW THE STORE SPELLS AND ORDERS ITS KEYS. Only the first two are consequences of a program. The third is a property of one engine's representation, and holding a second engine to it is not free - it is what forced the Rust store's 8-byte `sortkey` per fact and the design constraint that a numeric identity must carry the key's spelling as an ordering function it never materialises. It also blocks what the owner wants next: COLUMNAR STORAGE WITH SPILL TO DISK AND LOAD-BACK ON DEMAND, which cannot cheaply maintain a total spelling order over columns that are not resident. THE REPLACEMENT WAS BUILT BEFORE THE OLD ONE WAS LOOSENED, because without it we would simply have lost all checking. `scripts/derivations.ts` differs from `canonicalState` in both directions deliberately. STRONGER ON PROVENANCE: it records the WHOLE support hypergraph, every firing of every fact, where `canonicalState` records ONE witness - the least signature - plus a count. Which derivations exist is a consequence of the program; which one an engine renders first is presentation. WEAKER ON PRESENTATION: nothing in it reads the store's own order or a key's collation, and it is sorted AT EXPORT, once, by the reader - so ordering is not a per-fact invariant the writer carries. A key is still spelled, because two engines need a common name for the same fact, but that is a naming for COMPARISON: an engine may materialise a key when asked for the dump and never otherwise. VERIFIED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS BEFORE IT WAS ADOPTED, which is the only reason it can be trusted to be exactly as loose as intended. Blind where it should be: changing the canonical witness pick from least-signature to first-arrival moves `canonicalState` on sensors, goof and spat and leaves `derivations` BYTE-IDENTICAL on all three. Sensitive where it must be: a mutant keeping only the first firing per fact takes goof from 3308 derivations to 1666 and moves the digest. Both dumps are now emitted for all 34 corpus cases, and the strict one is kept beside the loose one rather than deleted - a contract is loosened by measuring what the loosening costs, not by removing the instrument that would have said.").

finding(f_the_store_seam_exists_and_is_not_remotable_and_that_decides_the_storage_question, insight).
recorded(f_the_store_seam_exists_and_is_not_remotable_and_that_decides_the_storage_question, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_store_seam_exists_and_is_not_remotable_and_that_decides_the_storage_question, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_store_seam_exists_and_is_not_remotable_and_that_decides_the_storage_question, "scanners/store_boundary.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_store_seam_exists_and_is_not_remotable_and_that_decides_the_storage_question, "docs/dogfood/2026-09-08-storage-backends.md").
finding_note(f_the_store_seam_exists_and_is_not_remotable_and_that_decides_the_storage_question,
  "THE OWNER ASKED FOR TWO THINGS - reuse a ready-made embedded database instead of hand-writing storage, and design the store so compute can be SEPARATED FROM DATA for later clustering - and one measurement shows they are the same change. `FactStore` is already the seam: the SQLite adapter proves an engine can run over a store it does not own. Whether that seam can cross a PROCESS is arithmetic, not architecture, and `scanners/store_boundary.ts` wraps the prototype for one run to count it. MEASURED on spat, 11591 facts: 232385 store calls, TWENTY PER FACT. add 75250, indexed 48533, argMatches 35961 AT 1.4 ROWS PER CALL, support 34285, get 27394, relPersp 10024 at 9.1 rows, relAll 923 at 31.5. sensors is worse per fact at 39.8. At 50 us of network latency 232385 round trips is 11.6 SECONDS against the 58 ms this world costs today; at 10 us over shared memory it is still 2.3 s. THE CURRENT BOUNDARY IS NOT REMOTABLE AT ANY LATENCY WORTH HAVING. THE KILLER IS `argMatches` AT 1.4 ROWS: it is a point lookup per accumulated solution, so remoting it means shipping the JOIN rather than the lookup - pushing a semi-naive round down to the data. That is predicate pushdown, it is what a columnar engine wants anyway, and it is the RecStep architecture the field has already tried. SO THE TWO REQUESTS CONVERGE: using a database well and separating compute from data both require the same move, from `give me the candidate facts` to `evaluate this join over your data`. AND THE SHORTLIST HAS TWO SHAPES RATHER THAN FIVE SIMILAR ENTRIES. (A) A KV under the CURRENT boundary - redb, fjall, rocksdb - leaves the engine alone and buys spill and durability, buys neither columnar storage nor separation, and its write profile is the deciding detail: 75250 adds for 11591 facts because the derived layer is rebuilt and then dropped WHOLE, which is an LSM profile rather than a B-tree one. (B) Columnar with pushdown - Arrow plus DataFusion, or DuckDB - needs the boundary changed and buys columnar, spill, pushdown and the separation for free, since Arrow is zero-copy across processes and languages by construction. THE WARNING THAT MUST BE PRICED BEFORE (B) IS BOUGHT is already in this repository: differential dataflow measures 37 s and 5.5 GB where Souffle does 0.19 s and 20 MB, because general-purpose engines carry brutal constant factors at small scale, and this system's hot path is a 14 ms parse and a 0.33 ms question to a warm world. The likely answer is therefore not ONE store but a boundary that admits TWO - the in-memory one for small warm worlds, a columnar spilling one for large - which `FactStore` already permits and the SQLite adapter already demonstrates.").

finding(f_a_book_may_be_remote_exactly_when_nothing_is_negated_under_it, insight).
recorded(f_a_book_may_be_remote_exactly_when_nothing_is_negated_under_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_book_may_be_remote_exactly_when_nothing_is_negated_under_it, decision).
addressed_by(f_a_book_may_be_remote_exactly_when_nothing_is_negated_under_it, "scanners/book_locality.ts").
finding_note(f_a_book_may_be_remote_exactly_when_nothing_is_negated_under_it,
  "THE OWNER ASKED HALF IN JEST WHETHER A PROGRAM COULD DECLARE WHICH BOOK IS REMOTE AND WHICH IS LOCAL, AND IT IS THE ANSWER TO THE CLUSTERING QUESTION, THREE QUARTERS BUILT ALREADY. The store is partitioned by perspective by construction - `idx: Map<rel, Map<persp, KeyRun>>`, src/store.ts:343 - so the shard key is not something to introduce, it is what the index already IS. A program declares its books in its own text. And `sealed(Body)` is the standing precedent for a program declaring a STORAGE policy that the kernel reads, refusing with a `hole` FACT rather than an empty answer. THE GATE IS ALREADY DERIVED, AND BY ACCIDENT: `negated_under(Rel, P)` was added to boot.rofl:340 for the widened-negation audit, and it is exactly the predicate that decides locality. Negation is what pins a book local, because `not p[remote](X)` requires proving ABSENCE and absence cannot be proven from a partial view. So the rule is one query rather than new machinery: A BOOK MAY BE REMOTE IFF NOTHING IS NEGATED UNDER IT. MEASURED over six worlds, facts movable under that rule: goof 10 of 11 books and 1427 of 1981 facts, 72 per cent; aka 5 of 7 books but only 87 of 1192 facts, 7 per cent; sus 10 of 13 books and 58 of 1591, 4 per cent, because its three largest - worlds 1079, main 337, case 117 - are all negated; npc 0 per cent; wtf and spat 0 because they are single-book. AND `main` IS NEGATED IN EVERY PROGRAM WITHOUT EXCEPTION, which is not a defect: it is where a program's own content lives and negating your own content is ordinary. SO THE MECHANISM IS RIGHT AND THE DISTRIBUTION DECIDES WHERE IT PAYS - many small independent books with nothing negated under them, which is precisely the case perspectives exist for. goof is nine geometries in nine ledgers and it is the one that works. THE HONEST GAP, stated because the scanner cannot see it: crossing TRAFFIC is unmeasured. `crossing[audit]` reads zero on all six, but only because it fires on UNDECLARED crossings and these programs declare theirs; bytes per semi-naive round across a book boundary needs a walk over `imports`/`sees`, and that is the number that would actually decide the design. It also joins a measurement already recorded: the ledger census found the partition is WIDEST WHERE THE PROGRAM IS SMALLEST, so a book-sharded engine is best exactly where sharding is least needed - and whether the owner's own model books its monorepo widely is a modelling question, not a corpus one.").

finding(f_medium_and_large_are_two_engines_and_negation_draws_the_line, decision).
recorded(f_medium_and_large_are_two_engines_and_negation_draws_the_line, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_medium_and_large_are_two_engines_and_negation_draws_the_line, doc).
addressed_by(f_medium_and_large_are_two_engines_and_negation_draws_the_line, "docs/medium-and-large.md").
finding_note(f_medium_and_large_are_two_engines_and_negation_draws_the_line,
  "DECIDED BY VADIM: the Rust engine is ROFL MEDIUM, and the machinery for billion-scale is ROFL LARGE, a later work. AND THE LINE IS NOT A SIZE THRESHOLD - IT FALLS OUT OF `negated_under`, which was added to boot.rofl for the widened-negation audit and turns out to decide locality: a negation asserts an ABSENCE, an absence cannot be proven from a partial view, so a body of facts may be remote, sharded and spilled exactly when nothing is negated under it. THE TWO WORKLOADS THEN SEPARATE CLEANLY RATHER THAN SITTING ON A SPECTRUM. Code is made of negation - an analysis IS `not` - is rebuilt wholesale rather than appended (75250 adds for 11591 facts, then the derived layer dropped whole) and is queried across the whole graph; so it must be complete where it is read, which means one machine. Runtime data - metrics, traces, messages - is append-only, time-ordered, almost never negated (nobody asks whether there is NO reading, they ask about a window) and queried over windows; so it is admissible remote by the same rule. THE ARITHMETIC THAT SIZES MEDIUM, and it corrected an error I had just made. I measured grafema (1178 files, 227k LOC) and concluded we were over-engineering by three orders. The owner's actual repository is 15000 files. At the measured 9.9 AST facts per line that is 2.9M LOC and 28.7M base facts; at the corpus multiplier of 1.96 to 3.85 stored per base that is 56 to 110M stored; at the Rust engine's 296 B/fact that is 15.5 to 30.4 GB and at the JS kernel's 784 it is 41 to 81 GB. SO THE JS KERNEL DOES NOT FIT THIS WORKLOAD AT ANY POINT IN THE RANGE and the Rust engine fits the bottom and not the top. Medium is a STRADDLE, not a comfortable margin, which is why columnar density stays in Medium and spill is its safety valve rather than its architecture. It also lands on the ladder docs/performance-invariants.md already records from the field: 1-30M facts and 16 GB is that document's own `Medium - must be routine`, and this repository previously measured itself as not reaching it. THE ERROR WORTH KEEPING: I measured A repository rather than HIS repository and generalised, which is the exact defect I had applied to three agents' work the same day - verify the premise before building - and did not apply to my own. Grafema is 3.8 per cent of this machine; the real subject is 50 to 95 per cent of it. WHAT STARTS LARGE is three unmeasured quantities, each worth an order of magnitude, and they are named in the doc rather than assumed: whether metrics are modelled per sample or per aggregate (4.3 billion a month against millions), retention depth, and how many inquiries run at once - a handful being forks in one process, which exists and measures 3.11x on eight cores, and dozens making snapshot isolation a requirement rather than a nicety.").

finding(f_a_persisting_cd_made_me_report_another_worktrees_result_as_the_branchs, defect).
recorded(f_a_persisting_cd_made_me_report_another_worktrees_result_as_the_branchs, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_persisting_cd_made_me_report_another_worktrees_result_as_the_branchs, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_persisting_cd_made_me_report_another_worktrees_result_as_the_branchs, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_persisting_cd_made_me_report_another_worktrees_result_as_the_branchs,
  "I REPORTED A MERGE THAT DID NOT HAPPEN AND A RESULT FROM THE WRONG WORLD, AND EVERY GATE I RAN AGREED WITH ME. The shell's working directory persists between commands here. A block that began `cd /Users/vadim/rofl-rust3 && git commit ...` was followed on the NEXT LINE by `git merge --no-edit -q wip/rust3`, which therefore ran inside rofl-rust3 - already on that branch - and printed nothing, so my `conflicts: ${u:-none}` read `none` and I called it a clean merge into nextver. The `run_corpus.sh` that followed was the AGENT'S worktree reporting its own 34/34, which I attributed to the branch. WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT, and this is the part worth keeping: the full suite then ran in nextver and passed 1047/1047 - PRECISELY BECAUSE the Rust work was not there. `npm test` does not test the Rust engine, so an empty merge and a correct merge are indistinguishable to it. The push that followed was real and pushed the right commits; only the Rust half was missing, silently, for an hour. FOUND BY ASKING A DIFFERENT QUESTION: an inventory of unmerged branches, `git log --oneline nextver..wip/rust3`, printed one commit where it should have printed nothing. THE DEFECT IS RECORDED HERE ALREADY, TWICE OVER. `f_a_witness_has_no_world` is exactly this - a number read from a purpose-built world and written down as if it came from the checker's - and this is its third instance in two days, the other two being a heap measurement that held a reference to what it was freeing and a mutant whose pattern matched nothing. The pattern across all three: I read a SUCCESS LINE without asking which world produced it. THE OPERATIONAL RULE, and it is cheap: a merge is verified by looking for the thing that was merged, not by reading the merge command's silence. `grep -c parse_args rust/rofl/src/bin/rofl_eval.rs` returned 0 in nextver and 11 after the real merge; that check costs one command and does not depend on any tool being honest about its own working directory. Prefer `git -C <path>` over a bare `git` after any `cd` in a multi-worktree tree - the explicit path cannot inherit a mistake.").

finding(f_naming_yourself_is_possible_granting_yourself_is_not, insight).
recorded(f_naming_yourself_is_possible_granting_yourself_is_not, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_naming_yourself_is_possible_granting_yourself_is_not, doc).
addressed_by(f_naming_yourself_is_possible_granting_yourself_is_not, "test/authority-grant.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_naming_yourself_is_itself_a_forgery, "test/authority-grant.test.ts").
finding_note(f_naming_yourself_is_possible_granting_yourself_is_not,
  "f_naming_yourself_is_itself_a_forgery IS WRONG AS STATED AND THE CORRECTION IS ONE LINE OF IDIOM. It recorded that a program naming its author forges on its own preamble and that `there is no order of lines that fixes it`, having tried the grant above the fact, below it, and absent. What it did not try is TWO LOADS, and that is the whole of it: a grant must come from a principal that ALREADY HAS STANDING, and the operator loading anonymously is exactly that principal, because `registerPersp` (src/reflect.ts) grants `authority(book, $kernel)` and `authority(book, user)` for every ordinary book on first use. The reasoning is written there and is a design rather than an accident: an ordinary book is the user's the way a home directory belongs to whoever is logged in, and a caller that NAMES ITSELF claims to be someone other than the user at the keyboard. MEASURED, four cases: a single NAMED load carrying its own grant gives forged 1 - that is self-licensing and it is what the audit exists to refuse; the operator granting anonymously and alice then writing gives forged 0; alice also writing into [main] gives forged 2, because standing is PER BOOK and [main] is the operator's; alice writing a book she was never granted gives forged 1, so the gate bites. The recorded experiment tested self-licensing and read its refusal as impossibility. WHY THE ERROR WAS AVAILABLE, and it is this repository's own rule: the two-load idiom is EXERCISED BY NOTHING. No example and no test performed it, so the only form anyone reaches for is the natural single-load one, which fails - and a capability nothing exercises is invisible to every gate, which is the same structural point already recorded for `retainTicks`. AND THE CEILING IS SHARPER THAN THE HEADLINE, measured and now pinned: THE SANCTIONED PATH AND THE LAUNDERING PATH ARE THE SAME PATH. An anonymous load grants mallory standing in any book; mallory then writes it under her own name; forged is 0 and `leak`, `breach`, `malformed` and `unmoded` are 0 as well. Nothing authenticates an anonymous load, so the operator and an attacker are ONE principal, `user`. `forged` therefore separates `wrote a book nobody gave you` from `wrote a book SOMEONE gave you` and cannot ask who that someone was - the same ceiling `f_self_licensing_is_not_gone_it_is_now_a_line_someone_typed` records for `imports`, now measured for `authority` too. Authenticating `who` is a HOST concern: the kernel records enough and cannot verify. The gate is alive: withdrawing the operator's standing in ordinary books kills both tests.").

finding(f_the_nameless_reader_is_an_export_to_anyone_not_a_new_word, decision).
recorded(f_the_nameless_reader_is_an_export_to_anyone_not_a_new_word, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_nameless_reader_is_an_export_to_anyone_not_a_new_word, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_nameless_reader_is_an_export_to_anyone_not_a_new_word, "boot.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_nameless_reader_is_an_export_to_anyone_not_a_new_word, "test/publishes.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_there_is_no_instrument_for_a_nameless_reader_of_a_named_book, "boot.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_book_namespace_is_flat_so_two_authors_share_one_book, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_nameless_reader_is_an_export_to_anyone_not_a_new_word,
  "THE GAP `f_there_is_no_instrument_for_a_nameless_reader_of_a_named_book` LEFT OPEN IS CLOSED, AND IT COST NO KERNEL VOCABULARY. `imports(To, From)` relates two REGISTERED perspectives; `collects(X)` exists because a rule polymorphic in its SOURCE reads a `$var` that can never be the From of an import. The mirror had no instrument: a rule polymorphic in its HEAD book reads a named one and `$var(\"G\")` can never be the To. Three rows, two programs, and both said in their own comments that the writable workaround `imports($var(\"G\"), main).` was WORSE than the gap, because it licenses the SPELLING of a variable and renaming ?G to ?H revokes it silently. THE SENTENCE IS `exports(A, anyone)`, DECLARED BY THE SOURCE, because the source is the only named party - the same argument that makes `collects` the gatherer's. And it is not kernel vocabulary: `imports`, `collects`, `flow`, `sees`, `crossing` and `gathered` are all `edb` relations of boot.rofl, so this is a program change and `npm run grepcheck` never had to widen. ONE declaration per demo clears everything: goof says `exports(main, anyone).` and npc `publishes(world).`, both at leak 0, and goof never has to license the KERNEL's book because the two-hop `$kernel -> main -> $var(\"G\")` is discharged by the source-side clause. THREE THINGS THE MUTANT SET CHANGED, and none of them was in the design I wrote first. (1) The direct clause `gathered(A, B) :- exported_to(A, B).` is DEAD: `sees(P, P)` is reflexive, so the transitive clause already gives it with X = A. Measured by removing it - goof and npc stay at 0 - and removed rather than kept for symmetry. (2) The transitive premise had to be `sees(X, A)` and NOT `flows_to(A, X)`, and the difference is whether a publication can LAUNDER: goof's own positive control plants an undeclared `euclid -> main` crossing, and under `flows_to` its consequence `euclid -> $var(\"G\")` went silent while only the root stayed named. Measured both ways before choosing. (3) The reader guard `not perspective(B)` is exercised by NOTHING in the corpus, so it took a constructed fixture to make it killable - `publishes(alpha)` with a rule writing the named book `beta` must still leak, and without the guard it is silenced. An unexercised branch reported as such rather than left as a survivor. AND THE THIRD RELATION OF ITS KIND NEEDED THE SAME CARRY: without `exports(A, W) @next :- exports(A, W).` npc reports 20 infinite counts at tick 0 and 21 at tick 3, because the declaration expires at the boundary and the leak it discharges comes back - `f_a_ledger_declaration_expires_at_the_tick_boundary`, found yesterday for `imports` and `collects`, applying immediately to new work.").

finding(f_asserted_by_already_separates_two_authors_of_one_book, insight).
recorded(f_asserted_by_already_separates_two_authors_of_one_book, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_asserted_by_already_separates_two_authors_of_one_book, doc).
addressed_by(f_asserted_by_already_separates_two_authors_of_one_book, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_asserted_by_already_separates_two_authors_of_one_book,
  "`f_the_book_namespace_is_flat_so_two_authors_share_one_book` IS DISMISSED ON THREE MEASURED GROUNDS. ITS FACTUAL CLAIM IS WRONG. It says `nothing distinguishes whose telling is whose` when `authority(tells, alice)` and `authority(tells, bob)` both hold. Measured: with both granted and each writing one fact, `asserted_by` returns `bob $fact(isa,tells,[alice,cute])` and `alice $fact(isa,tells,[bob,jerk])`. The book does not separate them and the TRAIL does, completely. What is true is narrower than what was written: a rule reading `isa[tells](S, W)` sees both, and a rule that wants one reads `asserted_by`. ITS CORPUS EVIDENCE WAS MISREAD. It cites `five authority rows over three books, with main and audit each carrying TWO writers` as the shape being already live. Those two writers are `$kernel` and `user` - the kernel and the operator - which is co-writing by design, and `registerPersp` grants exactly that pair for every ordinary book. Measured over an honest world: books with two or more NAMED authors, ZERO. AND ITS DESIGN QUESTION WAS SETTLED THREE DAYS LATER IN THE OTHER DIRECTION. It asks for compound perspectives so that alice-tells and bob-tells can be different books; `f_a_ledger_is_where_you_reason_not_who_you_are_about` (2026-09-07) decided they are ONE perspective with the claimant as a COLUMN, and recorded the litmus that settles it - would you ever need two X on one literal, and you would: a fact about foo.ts in the [audit] book. So the repair the finding wanted is the thing the later decision refuses.").

finding(f_i_reinvented_export_as_a_one_off_and_the_owner_named_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_i_reinvented_export_as_a_one_off_and_the_owner_named_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_i_reinvented_export_as_a_one_off_and_the_owner_named_it, doc).
addressed_by(f_i_reinvented_export_as_a_one_off_and_the_owner_named_it, "boot.rofl").
finding_note(f_i_reinvented_export_as_a_one_off_and_the_owner_named_it,
  "I BUILT A NEW RELATION FOR A CASE THAT WAS A SPECIAL CASE OF ONE ALREADY NAMED AND WAITING IN THE LEDGER. The gap was real: a rule polymorphic in its HEAD book reads a named one, and `imports(To, From)` cannot name the reader. I invented `publishes(A)` for it, measured it, mutant-tested it and shipped it - and the owner said in one line what it actually was: `besides imports there is export, and an export can be TO ANYONE`. He was right, and `f_export_precedes_import_so_the_check_belongs_at_load` had already been open for days saying exactly that - `exports(main, audit)` is the permission from the side being taken from, and what makes it an AUTHENTICATION rather than an appeal to honesty is that only a holder of `authority` over main can write it. So `publishes(A)` is `exports(A, anyone)` and nothing else, renamed accordingly: one relation whose second argument carries the reader when the reader has a name and `anyone` when it cannot have one. The named half stays unbuilt and is still the owner's decision, along with whether the check becomes a load-time refusal instead of an audit row. WHY I MISSED IT, and it is the useful part: I read the gap as `there is no sentence for a nameless reader` - which is how BOTH demos phrased it in their own comments - and went looking for a sentence about NAMELESSNESS. The demos were describing the symptom in the vocabulary of the mechanism they had, `imports`, and I inherited their framing instead of asking what the missing HALF was. The ledger already held the answer under a name I had listed in the NEEDS OWNER set that same day without connecting it. A backlog is only a memory if you read it against the problem in front of you, not only when tidying it.").

finding(f_anonymity_is_judged_by_a_ledger_and_i_said_it_was_not, defect).
recorded(f_anonymity_is_judged_by_a_ledger_and_i_said_it_was_not, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_anonymity_is_judged_by_a_ledger_and_i_said_it_was_not, doc).
addressed_by(f_anonymity_is_judged_by_a_ledger_and_i_said_it_was_not, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_anonymity_is_judged_by_a_ledger_and_i_said_it_was_not,
  "I MEASURED A CEILING AND DECLARED IT UNAVOIDABLE WITH THE INSTRUMENT SWITCHED OFF. Having found that an anonymous load can grant anyone standing in any book and that the grant is then indistinguishable from an honest one, I wrote that `forged` cannot ask who granted it and that authenticating `who` is a host concern - and checked `leak`, `breach`, `malformed` and `unmoded`, all 0, as if that were the whole audit. The owner: `we forbade that, surely`. Re-measured: with `demands_authorship(main).` declared, `unattributed[audit]` names the laundering grant exactly - `$fact(authority, main, [victimbook, mallory])` - and boot.rofl says in its own comment that the kernel RECORDS anonymity and a LEDGER judges it, with the mechanism deliberately opt-in because withholding standing from `$anon` outright would make every honest program in the corpus red on its first load. So the ceiling is real only where nobody asked for authorship, which is a DECLARATION away and not a property of the design. THE SHAPE OF MY ERROR, which is the reason to record it: I enumerated four audits, found them silent, and read silence as absence. The fifth audit is opt-in, and an opt-in gate is invisible to exactly this kind of survey - the same structural point this repository already records for `retainTicks` and for the three test files that were skipped all day because the runner does not forward a flag. A survey of gates must ask which ones are OFF, not only which ones are quiet. AND THE BOOTSTRAP WRINKLE, worth keeping: `demands_authorship(main).` loaded anonymously is ITSELF reported unattributed, which is the same chicken-and-egg as the authority preamble one level up.").

finding(f_a_rule_can_report_and_only_the_host_can_refuse, insight).
recorded(f_a_rule_can_report_and_only_the_host_can_refuse, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_rule_can_report_and_only_the_host_can_refuse, doc).
witness(f_a_rule_can_report_and_only_the_host_can_refuse,
  "grep -cE '^(sees|crossing|leak|forged|gathered|collects_from|collected|exported_to|exported|unattributed|perspective|flow|flows_to|widened|negated_under|loader)\\b' boot.rofl  vs  grep -c 'isKernelLedger(' src/engine.ts src/api.ts", 21).
finding_note(f_a_rule_can_report_and_only_the_host_can_refuse,
  "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER THE PERMISSION MODEL COULD MOVE INTO THE LEDGER, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT MOST OF IT ALREADY DID - what stayed behind stayed for ONE reason, and naming that reason draws the boundary for everything that comes after. MEASURED: boot.rofl carries 21 rules of the family (sees, crossing, leak, forged, gathered, collects_from, collected, exported_to, exported, unattributed, perspective, flow, flows_to, widened, negated_under, loader), which is the whole JUDGING half, in the language, inspectable, with `why leak[audit](A, B)` printing the tree. TypeScript keeps exactly four things: registerPersp (3 call sites in src/api.ts, grants authority(book, $kernel) and authority(book, user) at first use), isKernelLedger (4 call sites - src/engine.ts:998, 1272, 1547 and src/api.ts:414 - refusing a write into a kernel book and refusing a perspective VARIABLE to bind to one), resolveBook (src/reflect.ts:776, rewriting an unbracketed KERNEL_BOOK literal to [$kernel]), and the KERNEL_BOOK set itself. THE REASON THE FOUR STAYED IS NOT THAT THEY ARE INEXPRESSIBLE. A RULE CAN REPORT AND CANNOT REFUSE. isKernelLedger prevents a write; the write does not happen. An audit row arrives after the write, and for this particular hole the report is worthless by construction - the shadow defect planted 22 facts in [$kernel] via `shadow[P](R) :- concludes[P](R, _)` binding P to $kernel, and a program that has done that and then receives a row in a report HAS ALREADY DONE IT. resolveBook is the same shape one stage earlier: it changes what the program IS before the program is decoded, so nothing downstream can be the one to notice. registerPersp is different and weaker - it is expressible as rules (`first use of a book grants the kernel and the user standing`) and stays host-side for ORDERING, because standing has to exist before the fixpoint that reads it. KERNEL_BOOK is a list and could be facts. So of the four, TWO are host-side by necessity and TWO by habit, and that distinction is the actionable content. THE CRITERION IS NOT NEW HERE AND I SHOULD SAY SO: f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming already recorded `forbid where there is no legitimate exception, report where there is; a refusal works when the violation is LOCAL, an audit is needed when it is a property of the WHOLE PROGRAM`. What is new is that this same criterion IS the ledger/host boundary - it does not merely choose the shape of one repair, it says which sentences can live in boot.rofl at all - and that the boundary as currently drawn was drawn historically rather than by the criterion. AND THERE IS A THIRD LAYER I had been collapsing into the second: src/api.ts states in its own comment that `the kernel REPORTS and the host application DECIDES to stop, the way errno and a shell divide the work`, with the shutdown at the entry points that load a real program and not on the path a test needs open. So the stack is RULES JUDGE -> KERNEL REFUSES -> APPLICATION STOPS, three layers, and an argument about where a check belongs has to name which of the three, not which of two.").

finding(f_a_load_time_refusal_needs_a_gate_that_can_be_made_red, question).
recorded(f_a_load_time_refusal_needs_a_gate_that_can_be_made_red, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_load_time_refusal_needs_a_gate_that_can_be_made_red, decision).
blocks(f_a_rule_can_report_and_only_the_host_can_refuse, f_a_load_time_refusal_needs_a_gate_that_can_be_made_red).
finding_note(f_a_load_time_refusal_needs_a_gate_that_can_be_made_red,
  "THE COST SIDE OF THE THREE-LAYER SPLIT, and it is the reason the export decision has stayed open rather than the reason it should stay open. f_export_precedes_import_so_the_check_belongs_at_load wants an undeclared import to REFUSE the load, and under f_a_rule_can_report_and_only_the_host_can_refuse that is the correct layer: the violation is local to one clause, the source book is always loaded first because loading here is ringed, and a report someone may not read is exactly the failure mode the shadow defect demonstrated. WHAT MAKES IT A DECISION AND NOT A CHORE is that a refusal moves the check OUT of boot.rofl - where it is 21 rules that a program can read, extend and explain with `why` - and INTO TypeScript, where it is a gate that has to be proven alive by planting, and where this repository has already recorded three gates that were switched off, skipped, or unreachable without anyone noticing. The declaration cost the finding priced as 27-to-54 is the small half, and it is itself stale - re-measured the same day from the derived inventory, `imports` is 45 facts over 15 programs, so the price is 45-to-90. The real question the owner has to settle: is an undeclared import a LOCAL violation (refuse, and pay for a host-side gate plus its liveness proof) or a WHOLE-PROGRAM property (audit, and accept that a report can be ignored)? The `not perspective(B)` guard in the export instrument is the warning: it took a constructed fixture to make it killable, because nothing in the corpus exercises it.").

-- REVISITED 2026-09-08 under f_a_rule_can_report_and_only_the_host_can_refuse.
-- The export finding's layer is now named (host-side refusal, not audit row),
-- so what is left of it is the cost decision and nothing else.
blocks(f_a_load_time_refusal_needs_a_gate_that_can_be_made_red, f_export_precedes_import_so_the_check_belongs_at_load).
blocks(f_a_load_time_refusal_needs_a_gate_that_can_be_made_red, f_two_files_writing_one_book_is_scoping_not_naming).
-- Recorded 2026-09-08 by the storage-backend evaluation (branch wip/storage,
-- worktree rofl-storage). Instruments: scanners/store_trace.ts,
-- scanners/round_bytes.ts, rust/storebench, rust/colbench,
-- rust/share_curve.sh. Full account: docs/dogfood/2026-09-08-storage-backends.md

finding(f_the_boundary_count_is_seven_stores_and_the_worlds_own_is_a_fifth_of_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_boundary_count_is_seven_stores_and_the_worlds_own_is_a_fifth_of_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_boundary_count_is_seven_stores_and_the_worlds_own_is_a_fifth_of_it, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_boundary_count_is_seven_stores_and_the_worlds_own_is_a_fifth_of_it, "scanners/store_trace.ts").
finding_note(f_the_boundary_count_is_seven_stores_and_the_worlds_own_is_a_fifth_of_it,
  "`npm run boundary` reports 232385 store calls on spat and TWENTY PER FACT, and that total is summed over SEVEN `Store` INSTANCES of which one is the world's. The instrument wraps `Store.prototype`, so every instance a run touches lands in the same counter, and a run touches more than the world's store: `Evaluation` builds a SCRATCH store per kernel program it consults (`policyStore`, src/engine.ts:232) and `Rofl.load` clones a rollback backup (src/api.ts:282). MEASURED by tagging the instance in the recorder: sensors 30409 calls of which 3664 are the world's across 7 instances; spat 232447 of which 55488 across 8; wtf 185088 of which 33266; goof 130567 of which 102290. So the data path is 4.3 to 4.8 calls per fact on three of the four worlds and 20.9 on goof, not 20 everywhere. AND THE SCRATCH STORES ARE PER PROCESS, NOT PER WORLD: `safetyMemo` (src/engine.ts:220) caches the kernel's policy answers for the life of the process, so the SECOND world evaluated in one node run builds none of them - which also means the two variants of a trace cannot be captured in one process without crediting the second with the first's warm memo. WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE, and it is why this is a correction rather than a reversal: 55488 round trips at 50 us is 2.8 SECONDS against the 58 ms this world costs, and 0.55 s even at 10 us over shared memory. The current boundary is still not remotable. What changes is the size of the claim - the chattiness is a factor of four smaller than recorded, and a fifth of the traffic the headline counted was the kernel auditing itself. THE GENERAL SHAPE: A PROTOTYPE COUNTER COUNTS A CLASS, AND A CLASS CAN HAVE MORE INSTANCES THAN THE QUESTION HAS SUBJECTS. The fix is one line - tag `this` - and it cost nothing to add once the trace had to be REPLAYABLE, because a replay of seven stores' calls merged into one is a workload no store ever saw.").

finding(f_a_recorded_store_trace_is_an_oracle_and_its_two_blind_spots_are_known, insight).
recorded(f_a_recorded_store_trace_is_an_oracle_and_its_two_blind_spots_are_known, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_recorded_store_trace_is_an_oracle_and_its_two_blind_spots_are_known, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_recorded_store_trace_is_an_oracle_and_its_two_blind_spots_are_known, "scanners/store_trace.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_recorded_store_trace_is_an_oracle_and_its_two_blind_spots_are_known, "rust/storebench").
finding_note(f_a_recorded_store_trace_is_an_oracle_and_its_two_blind_spots_are_known,
  "A STORE BENCHMARK THAT IS NOT ALSO A CONFORMANCE GATE MEASURES THE WRONG FIXPOINT. `scanners/store_trace.ts` records every `FactStore` call of one run WITH ITS ANSWER, and `rust/storebench` replays the trace against a candidate and checks the answer at the call: every `add` verdict (was the fact new), every `get`, every `relPersp`/`relAll`/`relCount` row count, every `indexed` answer, every `support` verdict, and `argMatches` under the port's own asymmetry - a LARGER answer conforms and a smaller one does not (src/store.ts:243). The final gate is a digest of the sorted live fact set, which is exactly `scripts/derivations.ts`'s contract and not `canonicalState`'s. Seven backends agree on all four worlds with zero mismatches over 3664 to 102014 calls each. THEN THE INSTRUMENT WAS ASKED WHERE IT CANNOT LOOK, which is the question this repository records as the only one whose mutants survive. Six planted in the columnar backend: the layer drop taking base facts KILLED (2778 row mismatches, 3011 facts lost); `add` never promoting a derived fact to base SURVIVES; `support` reporting every firing as new KILLED (666); `argMatches` under-answering by one KILLED (4169); `relPersp` answering in REVERSE order SURVIVES; `indexed` always false KILLED (8447). THE TWO SURVIVORS ARE DIFFERENT ANIMALS AND THAT IS THE FINDING. Reverse order survives BY DESIGN - the derivations contract deliberately does not require an order, so a survivor there is a statement about the CONTRACT and confirms the loosening from the other side. Base promotion survives because THE WORKLOAD NEVER PRODUCES IT: no fact in any of the four traces is derived and then asserted base before a layer drop, so `src/store.ts:349` is unreachable from this corpus and a backend could drop it silently. A replay is only as strong as the run it replays, and the honest way to say so is to name the path the corpus does not reach.").

finding(f_columnar_is_the_answer_and_the_measurement_that_says_so_is_density_not_speed, decision).
recorded(f_columnar_is_the_answer_and_the_measurement_that_says_so_is_density_not_speed, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_columnar_is_the_answer_and_the_measurement_that_says_so_is_density_not_speed, kernel_change).
addressed_by(f_columnar_is_the_answer_and_the_measurement_that_says_so_is_density_not_speed, "rust/storebench/src/column.rs").
addressed_by(f_columnar_is_the_answer_and_the_measurement_that_says_so_is_density_not_speed, "rust/storebench/src/colfile.rs").
finding_note(f_columnar_is_the_answer_and_the_measurement_that_says_so_is_density_not_speed,
  "TIER 3 OF THE MEMORY DECISION WAS BUILT AND MEASURED, and it is the first tier that pays what it was predicted to. `docs/performance-invariants.md` ranks columnar per-relation storage last and calls it `the only tier that approaches the field's numbers`; tier 1 landed, tier 2 measured 1.02-1.13x. MEASURED HERE over 1159100 real facts - spat's live set tiled 100 times over disjoint argument vocabularies, same program, more data: `rofl::store::Store` 215.7 B/fact, a struct-of-arrays columnar store 31.4 B/fact, SIX AND A HALF TIMES DENSER; with one firing per fact 240.8 against 58.2, four times. At 5795500 facts: 193.8 against 32.8. AND IT IS ALSO FASTER, which was not predicted: replaying the recorded trace it is 1.7-2.0x quicker than the store it replaces on all four worlds (spat 5.8 -> 2.9 ms, goof 6.5 -> 3.6, wtf 3.9 -> 2.2, sensors 0.4 -> 0.3), conformant on every call, because a group is a flat `Vec<u32>` and a probe is one open-addressed lookup with no per-fact object to chase. WHAT ACTUALLY COSTS THE BYTES, twice measured and twice a surprise: (1) the IDENTITY TABLE, not the data - a `HashMap<hash, Vec<row>>` measured 125.6 B/fact against 12 bytes of argument, four fifths of a `columnar` store being one heap allocation per bucket; open addressing at 8 bytes a slot takes it to 31.4. (2) PROVENANCE, once facts are cheap - one firing per fact adds 26.8 B/fact to the columnar store, 84% of what a fact itself costs, where the same firing adds 25.1 to a store whose facts are seven times heavier. Columnar makes facts cheap and thereby makes provenance the majority of the bill, which is the same reversal interning produced when it made names cheap. (3) AND THE VOCABULARY DWARFS BOTH: 2080100 distinct argument names cost 391 MB, 338 B/fact at this tiling - MORE THAN THE FACTS - which is exactly the split this repository already calls the worst possible one and exactly the split a code corpus has.").

finding(f_the_sharing_curve_is_the_criterion_and_only_the_mapped_family_passes_it, decision).
recorded(f_the_sharing_curve_is_the_criterion_and_only_the_mapped_family_passes_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_sharing_curve_is_the_criterion_and_only_the_mapped_family_passes_it, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_sharing_curve_is_the_criterion_and_only_the_mapped_family_passes_it, "rust/share_curve.sh").
finding_note(f_the_sharing_curve_is_the_criterion_and_only_the_mapped_family_passes_it,
  "ONE SERVER, ONE USER, MANY AGENTS - and a fork is a full copy in RAM, so at Medium's 15.5-30.4 GB two agents already exceed a 32 GB machine and four are 122 GB. The requirement that follows is not `spill under pressure` but `the base is mapped and shared, and each agent holds only its private delta`, and whether a candidate meets it is a CURVE rather than a property of its README. MEASURED with macOS's own physical-footprint accounting, which counts a process's dirty and compressed pages and NOT the clean file-backed pages it shares with the page cache - a resident-set figure cannot answer the question at all, because it counts a mapped page in every process that maps it. Base: 5795500 real facts. N = 1, 2, 4, 8 processes each opening it read-only and running 200000 point lookups. MAPPED COLUMNAR FILE, 201 MB base: 16 MB per process at every N, and the 16 MB is the HARNESS's own probe sample - the base contributes nothing measurable. Probe 0.165 to 0.257 us. LMDB via heed, 342 MB base: 16 MB per process at every N, probe 0.590 to 0.611 us. ROCKSDB read-only, 34 MB base: 51 MB per process AND LINEAR - 8 processes cost 408 MB, twelve times the base, because a block cache and its decompressed blocks are private by construction. That is the axis the LSMs lose on and it has nothing to do with their write throughput. REDB AND FJALL DO NOT REACH THE CURVE AT ALL: both refuse a second process outright (`Locked`, and a panic in `Database::create`), so N agents cannot share one base under either at any cost. AND THE MAPPED BASE DOES NOT LOSE THE WARM QUESTION, which was the risk worth measuring: the same point lookup is 0.180 us against an IN-HEAP columnar store and 0.165 us against the mapped file, and 0.378 us against `rofl::store::Store` in heap. A MAPPED COLUMNAR BASE IS AS FAST AS AN IN-HEAP ONE AND COSTS ONE COPY FOR TWELVE AGENTS. Caveats stated: redb was opened read-write with a standing write transaction where a reader would use `begin_read`; fjall's base was never compacted and its probe is 266 us with a 1.9 s open, which a major compaction would improve; and fjall leaves a lock file that blocks reopening after a killed holder.").

finding(f_a_general_purpose_columnar_engine_is_three_orders_off_at_the_size_this_system_runs, insight).
recorded(f_a_general_purpose_columnar_engine_is_three_orders_off_at_the_size_this_system_runs, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_general_purpose_columnar_engine_is_three_orders_off_at_the_size_this_system_runs, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_general_purpose_columnar_engine_is_three_orders_off_at_the_size_this_system_runs, "rust/colbench").
finding_note(f_a_general_purpose_columnar_engine_is_three_orders_off_at_the_size_this_system_runs,
  "THE WARNING THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY RECORDS WAS PRICED AND IT IS WORSE THAN THE RECORD SUGGESTS. `docs/performance-invariants.md` cites differential dataflow at 37 s and 5.5 GB where Souffle does 0.19 s and 20 MB. MEASURED HERE on the operation a pushdown boundary would actually ship - one semi-naive round, `next(x,y) :- edge(x,z), delta(z,y)`, with the resident relation indexed BEFORE the clock starts and only the round's front handed over, which is the most favourable division of labour the columnar engines can be given. AT SPAT'S REAL MEDIAN FRONT of 111 tuples over 2000 edges: hand-written hash join 0.0016 ms, polars 0.198 (279x), datafusion 0.542 (764x), duckdb 0.757 (1067x). At 10000 tuples over 200000 edges: 9.3x, 35x, 7.1x. AT 500000 TUPLES OVER 10 MILLION EDGES the general engines are STILL BEHIND - datafusion 3.1x, duckdb 5.4x, polars 1.4x - against a single-threaded hash join, while they are parallel. THERE IS NO CROSSOVER IN THE MEASURED RANGE. In absolute terms: spat runs 39 semi-naive rounds, so DataFusion's per-round floor alone is 21 ms against the 58 ms the whole world costs today, and DuckDB's is 30 ms. SO SHAPE B IS DEAD AS AN ENGINE CHOICE and alive only as a FORMAT choice: what a columnar layout buys here is density and a shareable mapping, measured elsewhere at 6.5x and flat-in-N, and what a columnar QUERY ENGINE buys is a constant factor this workload cannot pay. Crate versions: datafusion 54.1.0, duckdb 1.10505.0 bundled, polars 0.51, arrow 58.4.0.").

finding(f_the_remotable_boundary_is_the_round_and_it_is_fourteen_kilobytes_wide, insight).
recorded(f_the_remotable_boundary_is_the_round_and_it_is_fourteen_kilobytes_wide, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_remotable_boundary_is_the_round_and_it_is_fourteen_kilobytes_wide, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_remotable_boundary_is_the_round_and_it_is_fourteen_kilobytes_wide, "scanners/round_bytes.ts").
finding_note(f_the_remotable_boundary_is_the_round_and_it_is_fourteen_kilobytes_wide,
  "THE SEAM FINDING LEFT A NEGATIVE RESULT AND THIS IS THE POSITIVE ONE. A semi-naive round is a barrier - round N+1's front is exactly what round N derived (src/engine.ts:1178) - and the evaluator installs a NEW `FrontInfo` per round (engine.ts:1217), so object identity IS the round number and `FrontInfo.byRel` is already the delta grouped the way a pushdown would ship it. MEASURED by `scanners/round_bytes.ts` over four worlds. SPAT: 39 non-empty rounds, front median 111 tuples, p90 1355, max 5419; 4.7 relations per front; 14182 BYTES PER ROUND at an interned width of 4 bytes a relation, 4 a perspective and 4 an argument, 31062 at the width of the key strings the current boundary would have to send; 540 KB for the whole evaluation. sensors 22 rounds and 2441 B/round; wtf 88 rounds and 3374; goof 35 rounds and 3949. THE COMPARISON THAT DECIDES IT: the current boundary ships 61578 candidate rows on spat ONE CALL AT A TIME, 3.2 rows per front tuple, and 55488 round trips at 50 us is 2.8 seconds. THIRTY-NINE round trips at 50 us is 2 MILLISECONDS against a 58 ms world. So the round IS remotable and the call is not, by a factor of fourteen hundred in round trips. BUT NOTHING SHOULD BE BUILT ON IT YET, and the reason is in `docs/medium-and-large.md` rather than in these numbers: a negation needs completeness, an analysis is made of `not`, and `main` is negated in every program measured. The remotable boundary is identified and priced; the workload that could use it is Large's, not Medium's. What Medium takes from this is narrower and immediate: A ROUND IS THE UNIT AT WHICH THIS ENGINE'S DATA ACCESS IS BATCHABLE AT ALL, which is also the unit a columnar layout wants and the reason the two requests converge.").
-- ============ 2026-09-08: the permission model, in one place ============

finding(f_the_permission_family_derives_from_one_anchor, insight).
recorded(f_the_permission_family_derives_from_one_anchor, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_permission_family_derives_from_one_anchor, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_permission_family_derives_from_one_anchor, "docs/books-and-permission.md").
addressed_by(f_the_permission_family_derives_from_one_anchor, "rules/permission-model.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_permission_family_derives_from_one_anchor, "test/permission-doc.test.ts").
finding_note(f_the_permission_family_derives_from_one_anchor,
  "THE BOOK-AND-PERMISSION STORY LIVED IN TWELVE PLACES AND THE ANSWER TURNS OUT TO BE ONE CLOSURE OVER ONE ANCHOR. `perspective(P) :- authority(P, _).` is boot.rofl's sentence for `a book exists`; three closure rules over boot.rofl's own clauses - a boot-owned relation that READS a family relation, one that a family relation READS, and two that read the SAME evidence - derive the family EXACTLY, 20 relations, with no list of names anywhere. THE CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES IT A CLAIM RATHER THAN A NET, and it is live: 5 relations of boot.rofl fall OUTSIDE - `rule_known`, `malformed`, `breach`, `unmoded`, `undefined_premise`, the four structural audits and their helper, which read the SHAPE of a rule and never a book. If the closure ever swallows them the criterion has gone slack and `outside` says so; test/permission-doc.test.ts asserts it is non-empty. THE THIRD LINK IS THE ONE I WOULD NOT HAVE GUESSED: without `co_read` - two relations reading the same evidence - the authorship pair (`demands_authorship`, `unattributed`) and the widened-negation trio (`negated_under`, `loader`, `widened`) are three DISCONNECTED components, because they touch the book graph only through the kernel's reflection. They share `asserted_by` with `forged` and that shared premise is what makes them one machinery. THREE NEGATIVES A GREP CANNOT PRODUCE, all derived: (1) `demands_authorship` is declared, audited and documented and NO .rofl program in this repository writes it - one test does; (2) all SIX audit rows are TERMINAL, read by no rule anywhere in boot.rofl or the corpus, and `terminal_non_audit` is 0, so nothing else in the family is dead weight; (3) `authority` is NOT boot.rofl's relation at all - it is RESERVED kernel vocabulary that boot.rofl only reads, one of the four RESERVED names deliberately kept out of KERNEL_BOOK so a program can co-write it, and that is why the standing question in §5.4 has no book that fixes it.").

finding(f_a_doc_gate_must_re_derive_both_sides_or_it_is_stale_by_construction, insight).
recorded(f_a_doc_gate_must_re_derive_both_sides_or_it_is_stale_by_construction, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_doc_gate_must_re_derive_both_sides_or_it_is_stale_by_construction, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_doc_gate_must_re_derive_both_sides_or_it_is_stale_by_construction, "test/permission-doc.test.ts").
finding_note(f_a_doc_gate_must_re_derive_both_sides_or_it_is_stale_by_construction,
  "THE FIRST DESIGN OF THE INVENTORY GATE WOULD HAVE BEEN GREEN FOREVER. The obvious shape is: the scanner writes facts/permission-inventory.rofl, the rules derive `doc_missing` over it, the test asserts empty. That test reads a GENERATED file - so a relation added to boot.rofl by someone who did not run `npm run perminv` is invisible to it, exactly the way scripts/kernel_grep.ts's hand-copied relation list fell three names behind boot.rofl and cost nothing only by luck. The gate therefore re-derives BOTH SIDES from source on every run: it parses boot.rofl live and re-scans the document's tables live, and never opens the generated facts for the set comparison. THE MUTANT SET, six, each naming its target: (1) a fifth declaration plus an audit reading it, added to boot.rofl with no doc row - red, naming both; (2) a doc row deleted - red, naming it; (3) a doc row for a relation boot.rofl does not have - red on the other direction; (4) a family relation renamed in boot.rofl - red BOTH ways, which a size check would sleep through; (5) NEGATIVE CONTROL, a new STRUCTURAL audit in the register of `breach` added to boot.rofl - must stay GREEN, because the family is not `everything in boot.rofl`, and this is the mutant that would catch a slack criterion; (6) THE SURVIVOR, the prose of a row rewritten to say the opposite of the truth - GREEN, because the gate compares SETS OF NAMES and every claim, every number and every `what it cannot see` in that document is outside it. Recorded rather than left implicit: the numbers in the document are dated for exactly that reason. PROOF OF LIVENESS ON THE REAL FILES, not only in memory: two clauses appended to boot.rofl on disk turned the gate red naming `revokes` and `revoked`, and it went green again on restore.").

finding(f_the_anonymous_principal_was_renamed_and_the_comments_were_not, defect).
recorded(f_the_anonymous_principal_was_renamed_and_the_comments_were_not, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_anonymous_principal_was_renamed_and_the_comments_were_not, doc).
finding_note(f_the_anonymous_principal_was_renamed_and_the_comments_were_not,
  "`ANON_WHO` IS `'user'` AND ABOUT TWENTY-FIVE COMMENT SITES STILL SAY `$anon`. Found while writing docs/books-and-permission.md §4 from boot.rofl's own comments, which is the point: boot.rofl:180 says `a call that named no author is signed $anon (src/reflect.ts)` and boot.rofl:202's OWN RULE reads `asserted_by(F, user, _)`, so the file contradicts itself four lines apart and the rule is the one that is right. Measured: `src/reflect.ts` exports `ANON_WHO = 'user'`, and a bare boot.rofl gives `authority(main, W)` = {`$kernel`, `user`} with no `$anon` anywhere. Stale sites: boot.rofl 3 (fixed here), src/reflect.ts 11, src/api.ts 3, tests 8 - and the test ones are mostly HISTORICAL, `\"`$anon` used to stand here\"`, which is correct prose about a rename and must not be swept. THE CLASS, and it is why this is worth a finding rather than a quiet edit: a constant was renamed and the twenty-five paragraphs explaining it were not, so a reader assembling the model from comments assembles a model of the previous version. That is the same disease as the twelve-places problem this session exists to close, one level down - the comments are the twelve places for the KERNEL. NOT SWEPT HERE: src/ comments are a separate piece of work and this task owns documentation and inventory, not the kernel's prose. The three in boot.rofl are fixed because boot.rofl is the file the model is derived from.").

finding(f_the_carry_costs_eight_relations_of_reuse_not_six, insight).
recorded(f_the_carry_costs_eight_relations_of_reuse_not_six, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_carry_costs_eight_relations_of_reuse_not_six, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_carry_costs_eight_relations_of_reuse_not_six, "docs/books-and-permission.md").
finding_note(f_the_carry_costs_eight_relations_of_reuse_not_six,
  "`f_carrying_the_import_graph_costs_six_relations_of_reuse` WAS OUT OF DATE ON THE DAY IT WAS WRITTEN, and by exactly the work that shipped beside it. It measured the two carry clauses for `imports` and `collects` and found six relations going opaque: `sees`, `crossing`, `leak`, `gathered`, `collects_from`, `collected`. `exports(A, W) @next :- exports(A, W).` landed the same day as the THIRD clause of the same kind, and it takes `exported_to` and `exported` with it. RE-MEASURED by diffing `store.derivedKeys` with and without all three clauses, over bare boot.rofl plus npc, sus, goof, loot, wtf, iffy and the six-file js-* rule stack: EIGHT, and the same eight every time. What the original finding got right survives unchanged: `flows_to` - the transitive closure, the one that would actually cost something - is still NOT among them; no relation belonging to any program is; examples/npc loses only TWO because its own carry rules for `imports` and `collects` were already in the file, so its exposure is exactly the one clause it had not written; and examples/loot has an empty reuse plan either way. The wall-clock half (wtf, 1949 ms against 2068, ratio 1.06) was NOT re-measured. THE GENERAL SHAPE: a price measured over a mechanism with N instances is a price per instance, and the next instance is added by someone who does not know the number exists. Stating it as `six relations` rather than `every relation downstream of the carried ones` is what made it go stale rather than merely grow.").

finding(f_the_main_cluster_re_measurement_was_itself_half_wrong, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_main_cluster_re_measurement_was_itself_half_wrong, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_main_cluster_re_measurement_was_itself_half_wrong, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_main_cluster_re_measurement_was_itself_half_wrong, "docs/books-and-permission.md").
finding_note(f_the_main_cluster_re_measurement_was_itself_half_wrong,
  "A RE-MEASUREMENT THAT DECLARED TWO OF THREE SYMPTOMS DEAD WAS RIGHT ABOUT ONE AND WRONG ABOUT THE OTHER, and the wrong half is the one that would have closed a defect. `f_two_thirds_of_the_main_cluster_is_stale_and_the_third_resists_the_obvious_fix` (2026-09-07) states four checkable claims. Re-taken 2026-09-08, claim by claim. HOLDS: KERNEL_BOOK holds 17 of the 21 RESERVED names, the four left out being `authority`, `edb`, `mode` and `reserved`. HOLDS: bare boot.rofl reports `crossing` 0 and `leak` 0. HOLDS, and is the live third: naming yourself is a forgery, reproduced exactly by test/authority-grant.test.ts case A. FALSE: `examples/sensors over boot now reports every audit 0, crossing 0 rows, leak 0 rows`. Measured, sensors.rofl on bare boot.rofl: leak 4 - `main -> trust`, `main -> verified`, `$kernel -> trust`, `$kernel -> verified` - and crossing 8. Measured under BOTH readings of the sentence, alone and in the `spat+goof+wtf+loot+sensors` set the finding names, identical either way; and measured against boot.rofl at the last four commits, identical at every one, so it is not a regression introduced since. THE TWO ROWS `f_main_is_a_namespace_and_a_ledger_at_the_same_time` calls irreducible are still there and sensors.rofl still says so in its own comments; the other two are the SAME walk one hop further back, exposed when the reflection moved into [$kernel]. So the defect is LIVE and its symptom is twice the recorded size. STALE IN A SMALLER WAY, worth naming because this repository already records the class: the finding cites `resolveBook (src/reflect.ts:680)`; the function is at 775 today. boot.rofl's own comment block warns that `facts/spec.rofl` cites it by line number and that inserting above unfounds nine duties - the same defect, on the other side, in a finding written by someone who had just read that warning. Cite the FUNCTION.").

finding(f_three_worlds_still_leak_at_load_and_nothing_says_so, defect).
recorded(f_three_worlds_still_leak_at_load_and_nothing_says_so, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_three_worlds_still_leak_at_load_and_nothing_says_so, decision).
addressed_by(f_three_worlds_still_leak_at_load_and_nothing_says_so, "examples/iffy/statute.rofl").
addressed_by(f_three_worlds_still_leak_at_load_and_nothing_says_so, "examples/cram/flight_log.rofl").
-- SETTLED 2026-09-08. Two of the three were declarable and are declared:
-- `imports(main, record).` in statute.rofl clears BOTH its rows, because
-- `sees` is transitive through the existing `imports(audit, main)`, and
-- `imports(log, $kernel).` in flight_log.rofl clears cram's. Both worlds now
-- report `crossing` 0 and `leak` 0 under the scanner's own protocol; suite
-- 1061/1061, both demos unchanged. The third is sensors and it is LEFT
-- FIRING, which is the disposition its own file already argued for.
--
-- THE TEST THAT SEPARATED THEM, and it is the reusable part: not whether a row
-- can be silenced but whether the SENTENCE IS TRUE about the program.
-- flight_log.rofl exists to read the kernel's provenance -- its header says so
-- at length, and prose is not a premise, which is the whole reason `imports`
-- is a relation and not a comment. statute.rofl exists to adapt a corpus held
-- in [record]. Sensors' crossing is an ARITHMETIC HELPER that happens to live
-- in [main], so `imports(verified, main)` would assert a content read that
-- never happens -- a false declaration written to make an audit quiet, which
-- is worse than the row. Its repair is the default-book decision
-- (docs/books-and-permission.md 5.1) and stays open there.
finding_note(f_three_worlds_still_leak_at_load_and_nothing_says_so,
  "THE CORPUS IS NOT AT `leak` ZERO, AND TWO OF THE THREE WORLDS DO NOT MENTION IT ANYWHERE. Derived by scanners/permission_inventory.ts, which loads each examples/ world on bare boot.rofl - every .rofl file in the directory, sorted - evaluates once at tick 0 and asks each audit IN THE BOOK IT LANDS IN. 26 worlds built; `leak[audit]` non-empty in three: examples/sensors 4 rows, examples/iffy 2 (`record -> audit`, `record -> main`), examples/cram 1 (`$kernel -> log`). Sensors is DECLARED OPEN in its own comments and is the standing example of the [main] default-book decision. iffy and cram say nothing at all: no comment, no declaration, and no test that reads the audit, so these two rows have been standing unexplained since the automatic bridge was removed. `f_there_is_no_instrument_for_a_nameless_reader_of_a_named_book` recorded a sweep that `brought the whole tree to leak 0` and `f_a_corpus_sweep_that_only_loads_misses_what_a_runtime_load_makes` found ONE it missed; this is three more, and the reason is the same class one step earlier - a sweep that reports `the whole tree is at zero` must say WHICH WORLDS IT BUILT and print the rows rather than the count. THE DECISION OWED is per world and small: either the crossing is real and wants an `imports` line, or it is the [main] default-book case and wants a comment saying so, which is what sensors did. THE INSTRUMENT'S OWN LIMIT, stated: this loads sources only, so a world whose rule set grows at runtime is measured before its books exist - examples/loot is exactly that and reads clean here.").

finding(f_a_demo_may_conclude_into_a_boot_relation_name, insight).
recorded(f_a_demo_may_conclude_into_a_boot_relation_name, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_demo_may_conclude_into_a_boot_relation_name, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_demo_may_conclude_into_a_boot_relation_name, "docs/books-and-permission.md").
finding_note(f_a_demo_may_conclude_into_a_boot_relation_name,
  "TWO DEMOS CONCLUDE INTO boot.rofl VOCABULARY AND ONLY THE BOOK KEEPS THEM APART. Derived as `name_collision` while building the permission inventory, and found because the first version of the model keyed corpus reads on the RELATION alone and therefore reported `exported` as being consumed by a rule, which is false. examples/aka concludes `crossing[recon]/1` against boot.rofl's `crossing[main]/2`; examples/ditto concludes `exported[main]/3` against boot.rofl's `exported[audit]/1`. Neither collides TODAY: a relation is keyed by name AND book, and in both cases the books differ. Nothing prevents the next one from being written without a bracket, and `breach[audit]` does not refuse it - it refuses a rule concluding into a RESERVED relation, and none of boot.rofl's own vocabulary is reserved. THE MEASUREMENT LESSON IS THE TRANSFERABLE HALF: a model over relation NAMES in a language whose relations are keyed by (name, book) over-answers, silently, in the direction of `something reads this`, which is the direction that hides dead weight. Keying on the pair turned `terminal` from 5 to 6 and made `terminal_non_audit` empty, which is the statement worth having.").

finding(f_the_line_anchor_trap_fired_twice_in_one_session_and_the_gate_caught_both, insight).
recorded(f_the_line_anchor_trap_fired_twice_in_one_session_and_the_gate_caught_both, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_line_anchor_trap_fired_twice_in_one_session_and_the_gate_caught_both, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_line_anchor_trap_fired_twice_in_one_session_and_the_gate_caught_both, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_line_anchor_trap_fired_twice_in_one_session_and_the_gate_caught_both,
  "`facts/spec.rofl` CITES BOTH boot.rofl AND README.md BY LINE NUMBER, and adding a paragraph to either unfounds every citation below it. boot.rofl warns about this in its own text - twice, in two appended blocks, with `appending is what costs nothing` as the remedy - and the warning is about boot.rofl ONLY. Both halves fired in one session while writing the permission document. (1) A ten-line pointer at the head of boot.rofl moved nine duties anchored at 154-163; caught by hand, before running anything, only because the warning was in the paragraph directly above the insertion point, and fixed by appending at the end with the reason written there. (2) A four-line growth in a README paragraph at line 179 moved THIRTEEN citations anchored between 237 and 358; NOT caught by hand, because nothing in README.md says it is cited, and caught by test/spec-coverage.test.ts in the full run - three tests red, `every citation resolves at the line it names` plus its positive control plus the deliberate-removal arm. Repaired by rewriting the replacement to be exactly line-neutral, ten lines for ten, and verified by comparing README.md's line count and all thirteen anchored lines against HEAD. TWO THINGS WORTH KEEPING. The gate is real and it works on a file whose vulnerability is undocumented, which is the harder half. And the fix that is NOT available is `append instead` - a README paragraph belongs where the subject is, so the constraint here is line-neutrality rather than position, and a contributor has no way to know that without running the suite. The remedy this repository prefers is the one it already names for kernel_grep: anchor on CONTENT rather than on a number. `duty[boot](b_leak_empty, guaranteed, \"boot.rofl\", 155, ...)` could cite the text of the line it means.").

finding(f_the_biggest_number_i_reported_was_the_benchmarks_own_string_vector, defect).
recorded(f_the_biggest_number_i_reported_was_the_benchmarks_own_string_vector, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_biggest_number_i_reported_was_the_benchmarks_own_string_vector, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_biggest_number_i_reported_was_the_benchmarks_own_string_vector, "facts/findings.rofl").
witness(f_the_biggest_number_i_reported_was_the_benchmarks_own_string_vector,
  "storebench base column <trace> 500 --probe 200000, before and after the interner was replaced", 1963).
finding_note(f_the_biggest_number_i_reported_was_the_benchmarks_own_string_vector,
  "I READ ONE LINE OF A BENCHMARK'S OUTPUT AS A FACT ABOUT THE ENGINE AND MADE IT THE HEADLINE. The storage evaluation printed `10400500 names cost 1963.5 MB = 338.8 B/fact on top` beside a fact store measured at 31.4 B/fact, and I reported to the owner that THE VOCABULARY DWARFS THE DATA TENFOLD IN ANY LAYOUT and that the name table was the largest number on the table and the first work to do. It is not the engine's vocabulary. `scale::tiled_names` builds a `Vec<String>` of 10.4M synthetic names as the benchmark's OWN scaffolding, and the 1963 MB is the RSS delta of building that vector - the crate's comment says as much four lines above the print, explaining that the vocabulary is measured separately so that charging it to the fact representation would not be `measuring the benchmark's own string vector`, which is exactly what I then did with it. PROVED BY THE THING THAT SHOULD HAVE MOVED AND DID NOT: `rofl::term::Interner` was replaced wholesale - `Vec<Rc<str>>` plus `HashMap<Rc<str>, Sym>` became an arena plus an open-addressed table of ids - and the same command reprinted 1963.6 MB, unchanged to the decimal. A number that cannot move when the thing it supposedly measures is rewritten was never measuring it. WHAT THE ENGINE'S VOCABULARY ACTUALLY COSTS, measured directly (term.rs `vocabulary_cost_at_scale`, ignored by default): 10M names of 33 bytes = 587.2 MB = 58.7 B/name, against 189 B/name if the benchmark's figure had been the engine's. On the port corpus the interner was 9.7 percent of the store on spat, not ten times it. THE SHAPE OF THE ERROR, which is why this is a defect and not a note: three tiles down in an agent's report, a number in a units-per-fact form that INVITES comparison with the number beside it, and I compared them without asking what allocated the bytes. The repository already records the same step twice this week - the store-boundary count summed over seven Store instances, and a leak sweep reporting a count instead of the rows. A per-unit figure is a RATIO OF TWO MEASUREMENTS, and the denominator being shared does not make the numerators commensurable.").

finding(f_an_interner_pays_three_overheads_per_name_and_an_arena_pays_none, insight).
recorded(f_an_interner_pays_three_overheads_per_name_and_an_arena_pays_none, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_an_interner_pays_three_overheads_per_name_and_an_arena_pays_none, doc).
addressed_by(f_an_interner_pays_three_overheads_per_name_and_an_arena_pays_none, "rust/rofl/src/term.rs").
witness(f_an_interner_pays_three_overheads_per_name_and_an_arena_pays_none,
  "rust/target/release/rofl-eval --bytes facts/port-corpus/spat.seed.json, field h.syms over field syms", 40).
finding_note(f_an_interner_pays_three_overheads_per_name_and_an_arena_pays_none,
  "THE MOVE THIS FILE ALREADY MADE FOR FUNCTORS, MADE FOR NAMES, AND IT IS THE SAME SENTENCE: a table keyed by the thing holds a second handle to the thing, so the copy is ADDED rather than saved. `Heap.cons` records that for functors in its own comment and cites src/unify.ts:22 for the JS side; the name table was still `Vec<Rc<str>>` plus `HashMap<Rc<str>, Sym>`, where the `Rc` had already been introduced to stop the two halves holding two copies of the BYTES - and it did, leaving three per-name overheads standing that no `Rc` can remove: a 16-byte fat pointer in the vector, a 16-byte refcount header on a separate allocation, and a 28-byte map slot. MEASURED BEFORE, over the port corpus: 87.0 B/sym on spat, 84.5 wtf, 92.1 goof, 91.5 sensors, against name text averaging sixteen bytes - so more than four fifths of the table was not the names. AFTER, same instrument, same worlds: 40.0, 39.6, 27.9, 32.1 - and these are honest totals rather than better estimates, because all three allocations are now single and `bytes()` no longer has a per-name malloc rounding hiding under it. Live allocator bytes fell 5.4, 4.5, 7.5 and 11.3 percent on the four worlds, and bytes per fact from 269.4 to 254.8 on spat. THE SHAPE: one `String` arena of concatenated bytes, one `Vec<u32>` of end offsets, one open-addressed `Vec<u32>` of ids at 0.7 load, no per-name allocation and nothing to drop. The arena is a `String` and not a `Vec<u8>` deliberately - `&buf[a..b]` is then a SAFE O(1) slice with a char-boundary check, where `Vec<u8>` would force either a full revalidation on every `name()` or the only `unsafe` block in the library. AND WHAT PROVES IT, because the conformance corpus interns about three thousand names and never reaches the fifth rehash: four unit tests, of which the load-bearing one walks 100000 names through eleven doublings and requires every id to still name its own string and every re-intern to find it rather than append. 34 of 34 on both oracles, unchanged, which is what a representation change is supposed to look like.").

finding(f_the_density_table_had_two_instrument_defects_and_both_favoured_the_candidate, defect).
recorded(f_the_density_table_had_two_instrument_defects_and_both_favoured_the_candidate, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_density_table_had_two_instrument_defects_and_both_favoured_the_candidate, doc).
addressed_by(f_the_density_table_had_two_instrument_defects_and_both_favoured_the_candidate, "rust/storebench/src/scale.rs").
finding_note(f_the_density_table_had_two_instrument_defects_and_both_favoured_the_candidate,
  "THE STORAGE RECOMMENDATION RESTED ON `columnar is 6.5x denser than the store we have`, AND THE INSTRUMENT THAT PRODUCED IT WAS BROKEN IN TWO PLACES, BOTH IN THE CANDIDATE'S FAVOUR. Found by re-running the arms before starting the rewrite they justify. DEFECT ONE, THE MEASURE. `rss()` shells out to `ps`, and resident set size is not monotonic: at the 3.4 GB working set these arms reach, macOS evicts clean pages while the process still holds them, so a delta across a build can come back NEGATIVE and `saturating_sub` floors it. Caught by the arm reporting `0.0 MB / 0.0 B/fact` on a run whose predecessor reported 1123.0 MB - and confirmed by two runs of the identical `tiled_names` call reporting 1963.5 MB and 1726.4 MB for the same 10.4M strings, a 12 percent spread on one line of code. Replaced with a counting global allocator, the same instrument `rofl-eval --bytes` already used on the engine side; every figure below is now identical across repeated runs. DEFECT TWO, THE WINDOW. `drop(names)` - releasing 1.8 GB of benchmark vocabulary - stood INSIDE the measured window on the columnar arm and outside it on the native one. Under an exact allocator that is a free larger than the fill and the arm reads 0.0; under `rss()` it read about 190 MB, because resident bytes do not fall when an allocator frees, and that 190 MB became the numerator of `6.5x`. Moved above the window. AND THE THIRD THING, which is not a defect but an unlike comparison: the native arm HOLDS A VOCABULARY and the columnar arm has none - it works in the trace's own symbol ids and cannot render a name at all. Charging one arm for an interner and not the other is not a property of columnar layout. THE CORRECTED TABLE, part by part, 5795500 facts, deterministic: native store proper 507.6 MB = 87.6 B/fact (recs 100.7, tups 134.2, args 134.2, by_key 67.1, idx 37.8, wit 33.6) plus interner 302.0 MB; columnar 167.1 MB = 28.8 B/fact and no vocabulary. So the fact representation is 3.0x, not 6.5x, and with the same vocabulary charged to both it is 1.7x overall. AND WITH PROVENANCE ON, which is the configuration a real store runs in: native 943.8 MB = 162.9 B/fact against columnar 301.3 = 52.0, a ratio of 3.1 that agrees with the dry run - and the single largest line on the native side becomes the witness store at 469.8 MB (81.1 B/fact) against the columnar arena's 134.2 (23.2), a linked node per firing against an arena plus an open-addressed set. THE DECISION THIS CHANGES: 3.0x on the fact representation is still worth having and no longer obviously worth a rewrite of the engine's store before the cheaper parts of the same gap are taken - see f_two_thirds_of_the_columnar_gap_is_a_wider_word_and_a_cached_sort_key.").

finding(f_two_thirds_of_the_columnar_gap_is_a_wider_word_and_a_cached_sort_key, insight).
recorded(f_two_thirds_of_the_columnar_gap_is_a_wider_word_and_a_cached_sort_key, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_two_thirds_of_the_columnar_gap_is_a_wider_word_and_a_cached_sort_key, decision).
finding_note(f_two_thirds_of_the_columnar_gap_is_a_wider_word_and_a_cached_sort_key,
  "ONCE THE DENSITY TABLE WAS MEASURED PART BY PART RATHER THAN AS ONE TOTAL, THE 3.0x GAP DECOMPOSED, AND MOST OF IT IS NOT ABOUT COLUMNS. Native 87.6 B/fact against columnar 28.8, same 5795500 facts, same workload. TWO LINE ITEMS CARRY IT. (1) `Tuples.args` is `Vec<Term>` and a `Term` is a TAGGED 64-BIT WORD; the columnar arm stores a bare `u32` symbol id. That is 2x on the argument payload for a store whose arguments in this workload are all atoms - 134.2 MB against 90.5 - and it is the price of terms that can also be strings, numbers, variables and functors in the same slot. (2) `Tuples.sks` is EIGHT BYTES PER DISTINCT TUPLE, 134.2 MB here, and the columnar arm has no counterpart at all. It is a CACHE and nothing else: `cmp_args` compares two `u64` prefixes and falls back to rendering both argument lists when they tie or are non-ASCII, so deleting it costs speed in the comparator and nothing in the answer. THE THIRD ITEM IS SLACK, not design: `recs` reports 100.7 MB where 5795500 twelve-byte records need 69.5, because a `Vec` doubles - so roughly a fifth of three of these arrays is capacity nobody asked for, recoverable with `shrink_to_fit` at a fixpoint. WHY THIS IS A DECISION AND NOT A CHORE: the sort key exists to make the canonical key order cheap, and this repository has already MEASURED that the order is observable at exactly two sites and that a mutant answering in arrival order survives 27 of 27 corpus cases (f_key_spelling_reaches_the_answer_at_two_sites). A cache for a comparator that nothing observable depends on, costing 23 B/fact including slack, is the cheapest thing on this table to question - and it can be tested in an afternoon against the corpus, where a store rewrite cannot.").

finding(f_i_proposed_deleting_a_cache_that_is_worth_four_times_its_weight, defect).
recorded(f_i_proposed_deleting_a_cache_that_is_worth_four_times_its_weight, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_i_proposed_deleting_a_cache_that_is_worth_four_times_its_weight, doc).
addressed_by(f_i_proposed_deleting_a_cache_that_is_worth_four_times_its_weight, "rust/rofl/src/store.rs").
witness(f_i_proposed_deleting_a_cache_that_is_worth_four_times_its_weight,
  "cargo test -p rofl --lib --release -- --ignored --nocapture merge, with and without Tuples::sks", 4).
finding_note(f_i_proposed_deleting_a_cache_that_is_worth_four_times_its_weight,
  "I NOMINATED `Tuples.sks` AS THE CHEAPEST THING ON THE TABLE TO DELETE, WITH TWO ERRORS IN THE NOMINATION AND A THIRD IN THE INSTRUMENT THAT WOULD HAVE CONFIRMED IT. ERROR ONE, ARITHMETIC. I put the sort key at the whole 134.2 MB of the `tups` line. That line is `ends*4 + sks*8 + cons*4`, so the sort key is HALF of it - 67.1 MB - and on the port corpus 4.7 percent of the store rather than the 23 B/fact I quoted. Split into `tup_ends`, `tup_sks` and `tup_cons` so the next reader cannot make the same attribution. ERROR TWO, THE MEASUREMENT THAT AGREED WITH ME. Deleting it outright: 34 of 34 on both oracles, memory down 3.0 to 4.7 percent, and evaluation time UNCHANGED - 22.16 ms against 21.53 on spat, 7.65 against 8.22 on wtf, which is noise in both directions. Read as a result that would have been `a cache nothing needs`. THE CORPUS CANNOT SEE IT, and the reason is structural rather than bad luck: `absorb` sorts each round's arrivals and merges them into the group's canonical run, so `cmp_args` is on the EVALUATION path, and the corpus's groups hold six to twelve facts. A comparator's cache is priced by GROUP SIZE, and every world here is below the size where sorting costs anything. THE INSTRUMENT THAT REACHES IT, built rather than argued about (`canonical_merge_at_group_size`, ignored by default, 20 rounds of arrivals with a read forcing the merge): group 10000 - 2.6 ms with the key, 10.9 without, 4.2x; group 100000 - 21.9 against 106.0, 4.8x; group 1000000 - 265.0 against 1079.9, 4.1x. The key costs 12.5 to 17.2 percent of the store in that shape and buys FOUR TO FIVE TIMES on the merge. Kept, and the proposal withdrawn. AND THE REGIME IS THE POINT: the whole storage line of work is about a repository with 15k files, where `defines(File, Symbol)` is ONE group with millions of rows. A measurement taken at group size 12 and applied to group size 1000000 is not a small extrapolation, it is a different question. This is the third instrument defect in two days on the same body of work - after a benchmark's own string vector read as the engine's, and an rss delta with a free inside the window - and all three share one shape: A NUMBER MEASURED IN ONE REGIME, QUOTED IN ANOTHER.").
